/* SQE1 January 2027 — consolidated bank */
QUESTIONS.push(...[{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0735","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Case management — relief from sanctions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party misses a procedural deadline carrying a sanction and applies for relief. The breach is serious and significant, there is no good reason, but the party argues that other circumstances should nevertheless be considered.\n\nWhich analytical framework should the court apply?","options":{"A":"Relief must automatically be refused whenever a breach is serious.","B":"Relief must automatically be granted whenever the default was accidental.","C":"The court considers only whether the opponent suffered financial loss.","D":"The overriding objective is irrelevant to sanctions.","E":"The CPR 3.9 discretion applying the three-stage Denton approach: seriousness/significance, reason for default, and all the circumstances including efficient litigation and compliance."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Denton requires the court to assess seriousness and significance, why the default occurred, and then all circumstances so as to deal justly with the application, giving particular weight to efficient, proportionate litigation and enforcement of rules and orders.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that relief must automatically be refused whenever a breach is serious. The controlling rule here is: Relief from sanctions is a structured discretionary exercise rather than an automatic consequence of any single factor.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that relief must automatically be granted whenever the default was accidental. The controlling rule here is: Relief from sanctions is a structured discretionary exercise rather than an automatic consequence of any single factor.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the court considers only whether the opponent suffered financial loss. The controlling rule here is: Relief from sanctions is a structured discretionary exercise rather than an automatic consequence of any single factor.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the overriding objective is irrelevant to sanctions. The controlling rule here is: Relief from sanctions is a structured discretionary exercise rather than an automatic consequence of any single factor.","E":"Correct. Denton requires the court to assess seriousness and significance, why the default occurred, and then all circumstances so as to deal justly with the application, giving particular weight to efficient, proportionate litigation and enforcement of rules and orders."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.9; Denton v TH White Ltd [2014] EWCA Civ 906.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relief from sanctions is a structured discretionary exercise rather than an automatic consequence of any single factor.","common_trap":"Turning one Denton stage into an inflexible outcome.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"00f525e184326ba1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0736","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — specific disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party reasonably believes its opponent has failed to disclose a clearly identified class of documents falling within the applicable disclosure obligation. Ordinary requests have not resolved the issue.\n\nWhich procedural remedy may be appropriate?","options":{"A":"An application for specific disclosure or inspection under CPR Part 31 where that regime applies.","B":"An application for default judgment regardless of the defence.","C":"A charging order over the opponent's documents.","D":"A witness summons directed to the judge.","E":"Automatic striking out without any court order."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where CPR Part 31 applies, the court may order specific disclosure, including disclosure of specified documents or classes, or a search to the extent stated in the order.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where CPR Part 31 applies, the court may order specific disclosure, including disclosure of specified documents or classes, or a search to the extent stated in the order.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that an application for default judgment regardless of the defence. The controlling rule here is: Specific disclosure is a targeted case-management remedy for deficiencies in a party's disclosure obligations.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a charging order over the opponent's documents. The controlling rule here is: Specific disclosure is a targeted case-management remedy for deficiencies in a party's disclosure obligations.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a witness summons directed to the judge. The controlling rule here is: Specific disclosure is a targeted case-management remedy for deficiencies in a party's disclosure obligations.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that automatic striking out without any court order. The controlling rule here is: Specific disclosure is a targeted case-management remedy for deficiencies in a party's disclosure obligations."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 31.12.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Specific disclosure is a targeted case-management remedy for deficiencies in a party's disclosure obligations.","common_trap":"Confusing a disclosure remedy with judgment or enforcement mechanisms.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec98fa317c981eaa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0737","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — single joint experts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In a modest technical dispute, the court considers that proportionate expert evidence can be given by one expert instructed by both parties.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Each party has an absolute right to its own expert in every case.","B":"The court may direct that evidence on an issue be given by a single joint expert, whose overriding duty remains to the court.","C":"A single joint expert acts as the parties' advocate rather than assisting the court.","D":"Expert evidence never requires court permission.","E":"The parties may privately agree that the expert need not be independent."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR Part 35 gives the court power to direct use of a single joint expert. Like all experts, that expert's overriding duty is to help the court on matters within expertise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that each party has an absolute right to its own expert in every case. The controlling rule here is: Proportionality can justify one jointly instructed expert without altering the expert's independence and duty to the court.","B":"Correct. CPR Part 35 gives the court power to direct use of a single joint expert. Like all experts, that expert's overriding duty is to help the court on matters within expertise.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a single joint expert acts as the parties' advocate rather than assisting the court. The controlling rule here is: Proportionality can justify one jointly instructed expert without altering the expert's independence and duty to the court.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that expert evidence never requires court permission. The controlling rule here is: Proportionality can justify one jointly instructed expert without altering the expert's independence and duty to the court.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the parties may privately agree that the expert need not be independent. The controlling rule here is: Proportionality can justify one jointly instructed expert without altering the expert's independence and duty to the court."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 35.1, 35.3 and 35.7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Proportionality can justify one jointly instructed expert without altering the expert's independence and duty to the court.","common_trap":"Assuming joint instruction converts an expert into a shared partisan representative.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"551beab073d98532"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0738","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — independence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A major commercial client tells a solicitor that future instructions will be withdrawn unless the solicitor gives advice that understates a clear legal risk. The solicitor knows the requested advice would not reflect independent professional judgment.\n\nWhich is the best professional response?","options":{"A":"Client commercial importance permits the solicitor to alter legal advice to retain the relationship.","B":"Independence applies only when acting before a court.","C":"The solicitor must preserve independence and should not allow commercial pressure from the client to distort professional judgment.","D":"A solicitor must follow every client instruction concerning the content of legal advice.","E":"The duty of independence belongs only to law-firm managers."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Solicitors must act with independence and in the client's best interests while maintaining honesty and integrity. Client pressure cannot justify advice the solicitor does not professionally consider sound.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that client commercial importance permits the solicitor to alter legal advice to retain the relationship. The controlling rule here is: A client's right to make decisions does not entitle it to dictate the solicitor's independent legal assessment.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that independence applies only when acting before a court. The controlling rule here is: A client's right to make decisions does not entitle it to dictate the solicitor's independent legal assessment.","C":"Correct. Solicitors must act with independence and in the client's best interests while maintaining honesty and integrity. Client pressure cannot justify advice the solicitor does not professionally consider sound.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a solicitor must follow every client instruction concerning the content of legal advice. The controlling rule here is: A client's right to make decisions does not entitle it to dictate the solicitor's independent legal assessment.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the duty of independence belongs only to law-firm managers. The controlling rule here is: A client's right to make decisions does not entitle it to dictate the solicitor's independent legal assessment."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles, including independence, honesty and integrity; SRA Code of Conduct.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A client's right to make decisions does not entitle it to dictate the solicitor's independent legal assessment.","common_trap":"Confusing client autonomy over objectives with control over the solicitor's professional judgment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d3536b3d14f01f14"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0739","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — client instructions and legality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client instructs a solicitor to implement a transaction that the solicitor knows would be unlawful. The client insists that the solicitor must follow instructions because the client bears the commercial risk.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A solicitor must carry out every client instruction once a retainer exists.","B":"Client consent converts unlawful conduct into lawful conduct.","C":"Only a court may tell a solicitor not to implement an unlawful transaction.","D":"The solicitor must not facilitate unlawful conduct merely because the client instructs it and should advise appropriately about the legal position.","E":"The solicitor should conceal the illegality if doing so advances the client's interests."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Professional duties to uphold the rule of law, act with integrity and avoid misleading or improper conduct constrain the means by which client objectives may be pursued.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a solicitor must carry out every client instruction once a retainer exists. The controlling rule here is: Solicitors pursue lawful client objectives within professional and legal boundaries; instructions do not override those boundaries.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that client consent converts unlawful conduct into lawful conduct. The controlling rule here is: Solicitors pursue lawful client objectives within professional and legal boundaries; instructions do not override those boundaries.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that only a court may tell a solicitor not to implement an unlawful transaction. The controlling rule here is: Solicitors pursue lawful client objectives within professional and legal boundaries; instructions do not override those boundaries.","D":"Correct. Professional duties to uphold the rule of law, act with integrity and avoid misleading or improper conduct constrain the means by which client objectives may be pursued.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the solicitor should conceal the illegality if doing so advances the client's interests. The controlling rule here is: Solicitors pursue lawful client objectives within professional and legal boundaries; instructions do not override those boundaries."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles; SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Solicitors pursue lawful client objectives within professional and legal boundaries; instructions do not override those boundaries.","common_trap":"Treating the duty to act in a client's interests as a duty to execute unlawful instructions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bf3bf88a252fb2a3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0740","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Terms — collateral contracts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Before a buyer signs a main written contract, the seller makes a clear promissory assurance that induces entry into the main contract. The assurance is not included in the main written terms, but the buyer argues it formed a separate enforceable promise supported by entry into the main transaction.\n\nWhich doctrine may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Any pre-contract statement is automatically incorporated into the main contract.","B":"A collateral contract requires no intention to create legal relations.","C":"The parol evidence rule makes collateral contracts legally impossible.","D":"A collateral contract is the same as a deed of variation executed after performance.","E":"A collateral contract may arise if the requirements for a separate contractual promise are established."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A sufficiently promissory pre-contract assurance can, in appropriate circumstances, support a collateral contract where the main contract or entry into it supplies consideration and the other formation requirements are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that any pre-contract statement is automatically incorporated into the main contract. The controlling rule here is: Some pre-contract promises may have contractual force through a distinct collateral contract rather than incorporation into the main document.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a collateral contract requires no intention to create legal relations. The controlling rule here is: Some pre-contract promises may have contractual force through a distinct collateral contract rather than incorporation into the main document.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the parol evidence rule makes collateral contracts legally impossible. The controlling rule here is: Some pre-contract promises may have contractual force through a distinct collateral contract rather than incorporation into the main document.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a collateral contract is the same as a deed of variation executed after performance. The controlling rule here is: Some pre-contract promises may have contractual force through a distinct collateral contract rather than incorporation into the main document.","E":"Correct. A sufficiently promissory pre-contract assurance can, in appropriate circumstances, support a collateral contract where the main contract or entry into it supplies consideration and the other formation requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"City and Westminster Properties (1934) Ltd v Mudd [1959] Ch 129; collateral-contract principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Some pre-contract promises may have contractual force through a distinct collateral contract rather than incorporation into the main document.","common_trap":"Assuming every representation is either a term of the main contract or legally irrelevant.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f6d341244c3e36dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0741","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Exclusion clauses — UCTA reasonableness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business contracts on its written standard terms and seeks to exclude liability for breach of a contractual obligation. The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 applies and subjects the clause to a reasonableness requirement.\n\nWho ordinarily bears the burden of showing that the term satisfies the statutory reasonableness requirement?","options":{"A":"The party seeking to rely on the term.","B":"The party challenging the term in every case.","C":"The court clerk.","D":"No party, because reasonableness is presumed conclusively.","E":"The Competition and Markets Authority alone."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under UCTA, where a contract term must satisfy the reasonableness requirement, the party claiming that the term satisfies that requirement bears the burden of proof.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under UCTA, where a contract term must satisfy the reasonableness requirement, the party claiming that the term satisfies that requirement bears the burden of proof.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the party challenging the term in every case. The controlling rule here is: A party relying on a clause controlled by UCTA must establish statutory reasonableness where the Act requires it.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the court clerk. The controlling rule here is: A party relying on a clause controlled by UCTA must establish statutory reasonableness where the Act requires it.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that no party, because reasonableness is presumed conclusively. The controlling rule here is: A party relying on a clause controlled by UCTA must establish statutory reasonableness where the Act requires it.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the Competition and Markets Authority alone. The controlling rule here is: A party relying on a clause controlled by UCTA must establish statutory reasonableness where the Act requires it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, s 11(5).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A party relying on a clause controlled by UCTA must establish statutory reasonableness where the Act requires it.","common_trap":"Assuming the weaker or challenging party always bears the burden of disproving reasonableness.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"71a0145cc5d8a43c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0742","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Discharge — accord and satisfaction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A creditor and debtor genuinely dispute the amount due. They agree that the debtor will make an immediate payment of a specified amount in full and final settlement of the disputed claim, and the debtor pays it.\n\nWhich contractual concept most directly explains discharge of the disputed claim if the settlement is binding?","options":{"A":"Frustration, because payment makes performance impossible.","B":"Accord and satisfaction: the agreement to settle coupled with consideration satisfying the agreed compromise.","C":"Rescission for misrepresentation automatically.","D":"The postal rule.","E":"Contributory negligence."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A binding compromise of a disputed claim can operate through accord and satisfaction: accord is the agreement to accept substituted performance and satisfaction is the consideration or performance supporting discharge.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that frustration, because payment makes performance impossible. The controlling rule here is: A genuine compromise can itself supply consideration and settle disputed rights.","B":"Correct. A binding compromise of a disputed claim can operate through accord and satisfaction: accord is the agreement to accept substituted performance and satisfaction is the consideration or performance supporting discharge.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that rescission for misrepresentation automatically. The controlling rule here is: A genuine compromise can itself supply consideration and settle disputed rights.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the postal rule. The controlling rule here is: A genuine compromise can itself supply consideration and settle disputed rights.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that contributory negligence. The controlling rule here is: A genuine compromise can itself supply consideration and settle disputed rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Accord and satisfaction principles; Callisher v Bischoffsheim (1870) LR 5 QB 449.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A genuine compromise can itself supply consideration and settle disputed rights.","common_trap":"Applying the rule on bare part-payment of an undisputed debt without recognising consideration furnished by compromise of a bona fide dispute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"18f9d0377a9371e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0743","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — standard of care for children","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A 12-year-old causes accidental injury while engaging in an ordinary activity. Negligence is alleged.\n\nAgainst what standard is the child's conduct ordinarily assessed?","options":{"A":"The standard of the most careful adult imaginable.","B":"Children can never be liable in negligence.","C":"The standard of a reasonable child of the same age, rather than the ordinary reasonable adult standard.","D":"The child's own subjective belief is the sole test.","E":"The standard applicable to a qualified professional."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"For negligence, a child's conduct is generally measured against the standard reasonably expected of an ordinary child of the same age, reflecting age rather than imposing the ordinary adult standard.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the standard of the most careful adult imaginable. The controlling rule here is: The objective standard is adapted to the defendant child's age.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that children can never be liable in negligence. The controlling rule here is: The objective standard is adapted to the defendant child's age.","C":"Correct. For negligence, a child's conduct is generally measured against the standard reasonably expected of an ordinary child of the same age, reflecting age rather than imposing the ordinary adult standard.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the child's own subjective belief is the sole test. The controlling rule here is: The objective standard is adapted to the defendant child's age.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the standard applicable to a qualified professional. The controlling rule here is: The objective standard is adapted to the defendant child's age."},"primary_legal_authority":"Mullin v Richards [1998] 1 WLR 1304.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The objective standard is adapted to the defendant child's age.","common_trap":"Either granting complete immunity to children or applying an unmodified adult standard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a49fc7282fd90a6d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0744","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — professional advice and Bolam/Bolitho","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professional defendant followed a practice supported by a responsible body of professional opinion. The claimant argues that the supporting opinion is internally illogical and incapable of withstanding rational analysis.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any body of professional support conclusively defeats negligence regardless of logic.","B":"The court must substitute the claimant's preferred practice whenever experts disagree.","C":"Professional negligence is strict liability.","D":"Professional support is important under Bolam, but the court can reject expert opinion that does not withstand logical analysis under Bolitho.","E":"Bolam applies only to criminal proceedings."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Bolam recognises responsible professional practice, while Bolitho confirms that the court is not bound to accept a body of opinion if it cannot withstand logical analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that any body of professional support conclusively defeats negligence regardless of logic. The controlling rule here is: Professional opinion must be responsible and rationally defensible; its mere existence is not always conclusive.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the court must substitute the claimant's preferred practice whenever experts disagree. The controlling rule here is: Professional opinion must be responsible and rationally defensible; its mere existence is not always conclusive.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that professional negligence is strict liability. The controlling rule here is: Professional opinion must be responsible and rationally defensible; its mere existence is not always conclusive.","D":"Correct. Bolam recognises responsible professional practice, while Bolitho confirms that the court is not bound to accept a body of opinion if it cannot withstand logical analysis.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that bolam applies only to criminal proceedings. The controlling rule here is: Professional opinion must be responsible and rationally defensible; its mere existence is not always conclusive."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee [1957] 1 WLR 582; Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1998] AC 232.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional opinion must be responsible and rationally defensible; its mere existence is not always conclusive.","common_trap":"Treating professional custom as legally immune from judicial evaluation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6a26d1b61eb182ce"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0745","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — public interest defence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A publisher makes a defamatory statement on a matter of public interest. The publisher cannot prove every factual allegation true but argues that, after appropriate investigation, it reasonably believed publication was in the public interest.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Truth under section 2 necessarily, even though truth cannot be established.","B":"Absolute privilege applies to all journalism.","C":"Consent is presumed whenever the subject is a public figure.","D":"There is no defence unless every allegation is proved true.","E":"The public-interest defence under section 4 of the Defamation Act 2013."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 4 provides a defence where the statement was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest, assessed in all the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that truth under section 2 necessarily, even though truth cannot be established. The controlling rule here is: Public-interest protection is distinct from proving substantial truth of the imputation.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that absolute privilege applies to all journalism. The controlling rule here is: Public-interest protection is distinct from proving substantial truth of the imputation.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that consent is presumed whenever the subject is a public figure. The controlling rule here is: Public-interest protection is distinct from proving substantial truth of the imputation.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that there is no defence unless every allegation is proved true. The controlling rule here is: Public-interest protection is distinct from proving substantial truth of the imputation.","E":"Correct. Section 4 provides a defence where the statement was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest, assessed in all the circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public-interest protection is distinct from proving substantial truth of the imputation.","common_trap":"Assuming inability to establish truth necessarily defeats every defence available to responsible public-interest publication.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6939d980779e0d73"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0746","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — procedural exclusivity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks to challenge the lawfulness of a public authority's decision and obtain a public-law quashing remedy, but attempts to avoid the judicial-review procedure without a legitimate reason.\n\nWhich principle is relevant?","options":{"A":"Public-law challenges seeking judicial-review remedies should ordinarily use the judicial-review procedure, subject to recognised exceptions and modern procedural flexibility.","B":"A claimant may always bypass judicial review whenever a limitation period is inconvenient.","C":"Public-law decisions can be quashed only by Parliament.","D":"The CPR contains no special procedure for judicial review.","E":"Every claim involving a public body must be brought exclusively as a private contract action."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The procedural exclusivity principle associated with O'Reilly v Mackman seeks to prevent abuse of process by bypassing judicial-review safeguards, though later authority recognises important qualifications and the modern CPR context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The procedural exclusivity principle associated with O'Reilly v Mackman seeks to prevent abuse of process by bypassing judicial-review safeguards, though later authority recognises important qualifications and the modern CPR context.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a claimant may always bypass judicial review whenever a limitation period is inconvenient. The controlling rule here is: A claimant ordinarily should use the designated public-law procedure when the substance is a judicial-review challenge.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that public-law decisions can be quashed only by Parliament. The controlling rule here is: A claimant ordinarily should use the designated public-law procedure when the substance is a judicial-review challenge.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the CPR contains no special procedure for judicial review. The controlling rule here is: A claimant ordinarily should use the designated public-law procedure when the substance is a judicial-review challenge.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that every claim involving a public body must be brought exclusively as a private contract action. The controlling rule here is: A claimant ordinarily should use the designated public-law procedure when the substance is a judicial-review challenge."},"primary_legal_authority":"O'Reilly v Mackman [1983] 2 AC 237; CPR Part 54; subsequent procedural-exclusivity authorities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A claimant ordinarily should use the designated public-law procedure when the substance is a judicial-review challenge.","common_trap":"Treating procedural form as wholly optional where it would evade public-law safeguards.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"030c1b122c187a88"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0747","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — Convention rights and Parliament","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court concludes that an unambiguous provision of primary legislation cannot be interpreted compatibly with a Convention right without contradicting a fundamental feature of the statutory scheme.\n\nWhich remedy may be available in an appropriate case to a court empowered to grant it?","options":{"A":"Automatic invalidation of the Act under section 3.","B":"A declaration of incompatibility under section 4, which does not itself invalidate or disapply the primary legislation.","C":"A declaration that the legislation never existed.","D":"An order requiring Parliament to repeal the Act within 24 hours.","E":"A preliminary reference requiring the CJEU to annul the Act."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where compatible interpretation under section 3 is not possible, specified higher courts may make a declaration of incompatibility under section 4. The declaration does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that automatic invalidation of the Act under section 3. The controlling rule here is: The HRA preserves parliamentary legislation while providing a formal mechanism to identify incompatibility.","B":"Correct. Where compatible interpretation under section 3 is not possible, specified higher courts may make a declaration of incompatibility under section 4. The declaration does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a declaration that the legislation never existed. The controlling rule here is: The HRA preserves parliamentary legislation while providing a formal mechanism to identify incompatibility.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that an order requiring Parliament to repeal the Act within 24 hours. The controlling rule here is: The HRA preserves parliamentary legislation while providing a formal mechanism to identify incompatibility.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a preliminary reference requiring the CJEU to annul the Act. The controlling rule here is: The HRA preserves parliamentary legislation while providing a formal mechanism to identify incompatibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, ss 3–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The HRA preserves parliamentary legislation while providing a formal mechanism to identify incompatibility.","common_trap":"Confusing a section 4 declaration with constitutional strike-down of primary legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eecff0d5edb32e3e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0748","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — distinguishing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A lower court is faced with an earlier binding authority whose ratio would normally govern, but a legally material factual feature necessary to the earlier reasoning is absent from the present case.\n\nWhat technique may allow the court not to apply the earlier ratio?","options":{"A":"Overruling the higher court.","B":"Ignoring the precedent because the judge disagrees with it.","C":"Distinguishing the precedent on materially different facts.","D":"Repealing the precedent by statutory instrument.","E":"Declaring the earlier judgment unconstitutional."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A court can distinguish a precedent where legally material differences mean the earlier ratio does not govern the present facts. A lower court cannot simply overrule a binding higher-court decision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that overruling the higher court. The controlling rule here is: Distinguishing works through the scope of the earlier ratio, not disagreement with its correctness.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that ignoring the precedent because the judge disagrees with it. The controlling rule here is: Distinguishing works through the scope of the earlier ratio, not disagreement with its correctness.","C":"Correct. A court can distinguish a precedent where legally material differences mean the earlier ratio does not govern the present facts. A lower court cannot simply overrule a binding higher-court decision.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that repealing the precedent by statutory instrument. The controlling rule here is: Distinguishing works through the scope of the earlier ratio, not disagreement with its correctness.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that declaring the earlier judgment unconstitutional. The controlling rule here is: Distinguishing works through the scope of the earlier ratio, not disagreement with its correctness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Doctrine of precedent; distinguishing.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Distinguishing works through the scope of the earlier ratio, not disagreement with its correctness.","common_trap":"Confusing legitimate factual distinction with impermissible refusal to follow binding authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6f26a0c7c3c0c22c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0749","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — alterations after execution","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After validly executing a will, a testator writes a new beneficiary's name into a legacy clause. The alteration itself is not executed with the statutory will formalities and the original wording remains apparent.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any handwritten alteration automatically changes a will.","B":"Alterations after execution never require witnesses.","C":"The entire will is automatically revoked by any unauthenticated alteration.","D":"A post-execution alteration generally has no effect unless executed with the required formalities, subject to statutory rules; the original wording may therefore remain operative if apparent.","E":"An executor may choose whichever wording seems fairer."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 21 addresses alterations made after execution. An alteration generally has no effect unless executed in the manner required for a will, subject to the statutory treatment of the original wording and authenticated alterations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that any handwritten alteration automatically changes a will. The controlling rule here is: Changing testamentary text after execution ordinarily requires compliance with testamentary formalities.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that alterations after execution never require witnesses. The controlling rule here is: Changing testamentary text after execution ordinarily requires compliance with testamentary formalities.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the entire will is automatically revoked by any unauthenticated alteration. The controlling rule here is: Changing testamentary text after execution ordinarily requires compliance with testamentary formalities.","D":"Correct. Section 21 addresses alterations made after execution. An alteration generally has no effect unless executed in the manner required for a will, subject to the statutory treatment of the original wording and authenticated alterations.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that an executor may choose whichever wording seems fairer. The controlling rule here is: Changing testamentary text after execution ordinarily requires compliance with testamentary formalities."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Changing testamentary text after execution ordinarily requires compliance with testamentary formalities.","common_trap":"Assuming handwriting by the testator alone is enough to amend an already executed will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5ee9ffe4625034fd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0750","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — appropriation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives wish to satisfy a beneficiary's £200,000 share by transferring an estate asset of equivalent agreed value to that beneficiary rather than selling it and paying cash.\n\nWhich administrative power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The doctrine of ademption.","B":"A disclaimer by the beneficiary.","C":"Automatic intestacy.","D":"A secret trust.","E":"The personal representatives' power of appropriation, subject to the statutory conditions and relevant consents."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives have a statutory power to appropriate estate assets in or towards satisfaction of beneficiaries' interests, subject to the governing statutory requirements, valuation and consent rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the doctrine of ademption. The controlling rule here is: Appropriation can satisfy an entitlement in specie rather than requiring every estate asset to be sold.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a disclaimer by the beneficiary. The controlling rule here is: Appropriation can satisfy an entitlement in specie rather than requiring every estate asset to be sold.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that automatic intestacy. The controlling rule here is: Appropriation can satisfy an entitlement in specie rather than requiring every estate asset to be sold.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a secret trust. The controlling rule here is: Appropriation can satisfy an entitlement in specie rather than requiring every estate asset to be sold.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives have a statutory power to appropriate estate assets in or towards satisfaction of beneficiaries' interests, subject to the governing statutory requirements, valuation and consent rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 41.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appropriation can satisfy an entitlement in specie rather than requiring every estate asset to be sold.","common_trap":"Confusing administration of an existing beneficial entitlement with failure or renunciation of a testamentary gift.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"71ac7d420de62a7c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0751","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — annual exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes a lifetime gift that is a transfer of value and wants to know whether part of it can be covered by the ordinary annual IHT exemption, assuming no other complication.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"The IHT legislation provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers up to the statutory amount, with limited carry-forward of unused exemption to the following tax year.","B":"Every lifetime gift is fully exempt from IHT.","C":"The annual exemption applies only to gifts made by will.","D":"Unused annual exemption can be carried forward indefinitely.","E":"The annual exemption is available only to companies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The IHT annual exemption shelters qualifying lifetime transfers up to the statutory amount and permits unused exemption from the immediately preceding year to be used subject to the statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The IHT annual exemption shelters qualifying lifetime transfers up to the statutory amount and permits unused exemption from the immediately preceding year to be used subject to the statutory rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that every lifetime gift is fully exempt from IHT. The controlling rule here is: The annual exemption is a limited lifetime-gift relief rather than a blanket exemption.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the annual exemption applies only to gifts made by will. The controlling rule here is: The annual exemption is a limited lifetime-gift relief rather than a blanket exemption.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that unused annual exemption can be carried forward indefinitely. The controlling rule here is: The annual exemption is a limited lifetime-gift relief rather than a blanket exemption.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the annual exemption is available only to companies. The controlling rule here is: The annual exemption is a limited lifetime-gift relief rather than a blanket exemption."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 19.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The annual exemption is a limited lifetime-gift relief rather than a blanket exemption.","common_trap":"Assuming the exemption is unlimited or can accumulate indefinitely.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4512baa364cfd77f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0752","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — client ledger records","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives and pays client money through a general client account. It keeps only the bank statement and does not maintain records showing transactions attributable to each individual client.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A pooled client-account bank statement is the only record the SRA requires.","B":"The firm's accounting records must appropriately identify client-money transactions and balances for each client or matter; a bank statement alone is insufficient.","C":"Individual client ledgers are prohibited because client accounts are pooled.","D":"Records need be created only if a client complains.","E":"Accounting records can be destroyed immediately after completion."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require accurate, contemporaneous accounting records, including client ledgers showing receipts, payments and balances attributable to individual clients or matters, together with cash books and reconciliations as applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a pooled client-account bank statement is the only record the SRA requires. The controlling rule here is: Pooling money in the bank does not remove the obligation to maintain matter-specific accounting records.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require accurate, contemporaneous accounting records, including client ledgers showing receipts, payments and balances attributable to individual clients or matters, together with cash books and reconciliations as applicable.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that individual client ledgers are prohibited because client accounts are pooled. The controlling rule here is: Pooling money in the bank does not remove the obligation to maintain matter-specific accounting records.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that records need be created only if a client complains. The controlling rule here is: Pooling money in the bank does not remove the obligation to maintain matter-specific accounting records.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that accounting records can be destroyed immediately after completion. The controlling rule here is: Pooling money in the bank does not remove the obligation to maintain matter-specific accounting records."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Pooling money in the bank does not remove the obligation to maintain matter-specific accounting records.","common_trap":"Confusing a bank's record of the aggregate account with the firm's obligation to account separately for each client's money.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2233f022a7894cb9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0753","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trusts — certainty of objects","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a discretionary trust 'for such of my relatives and dependants as my trustees select'. The class can be tested person by person to determine whether any given individual is or is not within it.\n\nWhich certainty test is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Every potential beneficiary must be listed by name.","B":"A discretionary trust requires complete enumeration of every object.","C":"For a discretionary trust, conceptual certainty is assessed by whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not a member of the class.","D":"The test is identical to certainty of subject matter.","E":"Administrative difficulty and conceptual uncertainty are necessarily the same issue."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"McPhail v Doulton adopted the 'is or is not' test for certainty of objects in discretionary trusts. Conceptual certainty is distinct from evidential difficulty and from questions of administrative workability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that every potential beneficiary must be listed by name. The controlling rule here is: A discretionary class need not be completely enumerated if its membership criterion is conceptually certain.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a discretionary trust requires complete enumeration of every object. The controlling rule here is: A discretionary class need not be completely enumerated if its membership criterion is conceptually certain.","C":"Correct. McPhail v Doulton adopted the 'is or is not' test for certainty of objects in discretionary trusts. Conceptual certainty is distinct from evidential difficulty and from questions of administrative workability.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the test is identical to certainty of subject matter. The controlling rule here is: A discretionary class need not be completely enumerated if its membership criterion is conceptually certain.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that administrative difficulty and conceptual uncertainty are necessarily the same issue. The controlling rule here is: A discretionary class need not be completely enumerated if its membership criterion is conceptually certain."},"primary_legal_authority":"McPhail v Doulton [1971] AC 424; Re Baden (No 2) [1973] Ch 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A discretionary class need not be completely enumerated if its membership criterion is conceptually certain.","common_trap":"Applying the fixed-trust complete-list approach to every discretionary trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"04e13b7217266f94"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0754","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — maintenance for minors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trust property is held for a minor beneficiary with an interest to which the statutory maintenance power applies. Income is available and the trustees consider paying some towards the minor's education.\n\nWhich statutory power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The trustees must accumulate all income until age 25 in every trust.","B":"Trustees may use capital only and never income for a minor.","C":"The beneficiary must first become a trustee.","D":"The Trustee Act power to apply income for a beneficiary's maintenance, education or benefit, subject to the trust terms and statutory conditions.","E":"Maintenance payments are possible only under a court order."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Trustee Act 1925 gives trustees statutory powers, subject to the trust instrument and legislation, to apply income for the maintenance, education or benefit of a minor beneficiary.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the trustees must accumulate all income until age 25 in every trust. The controlling rule here is: Trustees can have statutory flexibility to use trust income for a minor's benefit before full entitlement to receive it personally.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that trustees may use capital only and never income for a minor. The controlling rule here is: Trustees can have statutory flexibility to use trust income for a minor's benefit before full entitlement to receive it personally.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the beneficiary must first become a trustee. The controlling rule here is: Trustees can have statutory flexibility to use trust income for a minor's benefit before full entitlement to receive it personally.","D":"Correct. The Trustee Act 1925 gives trustees statutory powers, subject to the trust instrument and legislation, to apply income for the maintenance, education or benefit of a minor beneficiary.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that maintenance payments are possible only under a court order. The controlling rule here is: Trustees can have statutory flexibility to use trust income for a minor's benefit before full entitlement to receive it personally."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 31, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustees can have statutory flexibility to use trust income for a minor's benefit before full entitlement to receive it personally.","common_trap":"Assuming minority necessarily requires all income to remain untouched until adulthood.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cd65fedf5f687ece"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0755","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Land registration — restrictions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A registered title contains a Form A restriction because the property is held by co-owners on trust. A sole surviving registered proprietor proposes to sell for capital money.\n\nWhat is the restriction principally designed to prevent?","options":{"A":"Any sale of the land by two trustees.","B":"The creation of all legal mortgages.","C":"Beneficiaries from ever receiving sale proceeds.","D":"Registration of changes of address.","E":"Registration of a disposition by a sole proprietor under which capital money arises unless the requirements for overreaching are addressed, thereby protecting trust interests through the registration machinery."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A Form A restriction reflects the existence of a trust of land and restricts registration of dispositions by a sole proprietor where capital money arises, helping ensure the two-trustee mechanism relevant to overreaching.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that any sale of the land by two trustees. The controlling rule here is: A restriction regulates the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered; it does not itself create the beneficial interests.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the creation of all legal mortgages. The controlling rule here is: A restriction regulates the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered; it does not itself create the beneficial interests.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that beneficiaries from ever receiving sale proceeds. The controlling rule here is: A restriction regulates the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered; it does not itself create the beneficial interests.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that registration of changes of address. The controlling rule here is: A restriction regulates the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered; it does not itself create the beneficial interests.","E":"Correct. A Form A restriction reflects the existence of a trust of land and restricts registration of dispositions by a sole proprietor where capital money arises, helping ensure the two-trustee mechanism relevant to overreaching."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules 2003, Form A restriction; Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A restriction regulates the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered; it does not itself create the beneficial interests.","common_trap":"Confusing a restriction with a notice or with an absolute prohibition on sale.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"218d6eb5edbb2b38"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0756","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — necessity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner divides a parcel and conveys an interior plot that has no access to any public highway except across the retained land. The conveyance says nothing about access, and without crossing the retained land the conveyed plot cannot practically be used at all.\n\nWhich doctrine may imply an easement?","options":{"A":"An easement of necessity, where the stringent requirement of necessity is satisfied.","B":"Prescription based solely on one day of use.","C":"A restrictive covenant automatically.","D":"Adverse possession of the retained land.","E":"Overreaching."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An easement of necessity may be implied on disposition where access or another right is truly necessary for use of the land, though courts distinguish necessity from mere convenience.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An easement of necessity may be implied on disposition where access or another right is truly necessary for use of the land, though courts distinguish necessity from mere convenience.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that prescription based solely on one day of use. The controlling rule here is: Necessity can support implication of an easement where the land would otherwise be inaccessible or unusable in the relevant legal sense.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a restrictive covenant automatically. The controlling rule here is: Necessity can support implication of an easement where the land would otherwise be inaccessible or unusable in the relevant legal sense.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that adverse possession of the retained land. The controlling rule here is: Necessity can support implication of an easement where the land would otherwise be inaccessible or unusable in the relevant legal sense.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that overreaching. The controlling rule here is: Necessity can support implication of an easement where the land would otherwise be inaccessible or unusable in the relevant legal sense."},"primary_legal_authority":"Easements of necessity; Nickerson v Barraclough [1981] Ch 426.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Necessity can support implication of an easement where the land would otherwise be inaccessible or unusable in the relevant legal sense.","common_trap":"Treating any convenient route as sufficient for the narrow necessity doctrine.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b0132646b3da94f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0757","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — replies to enquiries","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller's solicitor answers a buyer's specific pre-contract enquiry about a boundary dispute. The seller knows a serious ongoing dispute exists but provides a response that falsely states there has never been any dispute.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A seller may knowingly give false replies because caveat emptor eliminates misrepresentation law.","B":"A seller must answer enquiries accurately if choosing to answer them; a false statement may expose the seller to a misrepresentation claim.","C":"Pre-contract enquiries can never have legal consequences.","D":"The buyer's solicitor must ignore all written replies.","E":"A false reply automatically transfers the disputed land to the buyer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Although caveat emptor places substantial investigative responsibility on a buyer, a seller who answers enquiries must not make actionable misrepresentations. False factual replies can found contractual or misrepresentation remedies depending on the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a seller may knowingly give false replies because caveat emptor eliminates misrepresentation law. The controlling rule here is: Caveat emptor does not license a seller to respond dishonestly or inaccurately to specific enquiries.","B":"Correct. Although caveat emptor places substantial investigative responsibility on a buyer, a seller who answers enquiries must not make actionable misrepresentations. False factual replies can found contractual or misrepresentation remedies depending on the circumstances.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that pre-contract enquiries can never have legal consequences. The controlling rule here is: Caveat emptor does not license a seller to respond dishonestly or inaccurately to specific enquiries.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the buyer's solicitor must ignore all written replies. The controlling rule here is: Caveat emptor does not license a seller to respond dishonestly or inaccurately to specific enquiries.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a false reply automatically transfers the disputed land to the buyer. The controlling rule here is: Caveat emptor does not license a seller to respond dishonestly or inaccurately to specific enquiries."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967; conveyancing pre-contract enquiry principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Caveat emptor does not license a seller to respond dishonestly or inaccurately to specific enquiries.","common_trap":"Treating the buyer's due-diligence burden as immunity for false representations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26c3ca41347cc4fe"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0758","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Leasehold transactions — service charges","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer of a leasehold flat wants to understand expected building expenditure and whether substantial works are anticipated.\n\nWhich pre-contract investigation is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 search alone provides the building's future service-charge budget.","B":"The buyer should ignore the lease because service charges are never contractual.","C":"Reviewing management information, service-charge accounts, budgets and information about proposed major works supplied through the leasehold enquiries process.","D":"Only the Land Registry title reveals all anticipated management expenditure.","E":"Service charges become irrelevant on assignment."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Leasehold due diligence commonly includes the lease, management pack/LPE information, service-charge statements, budgets, reserve funds, arrears and anticipated major works, because these can materially affect the buyer's obligations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that an OS1 search alone provides the building's future service-charge budget. The controlling rule here is: Leasehold investigation extends beyond title to the financial and management regime governing occupation.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the buyer should ignore the lease because service charges are never contractual. The controlling rule here is: Leasehold investigation extends beyond title to the financial and management regime governing occupation.","C":"Correct. Leasehold due diligence commonly includes the lease, management pack/LPE information, service-charge statements, budgets, reserve funds, arrears and anticipated major works, because these can materially affect the buyer's obligations.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that only the Land Registry title reveals all anticipated management expenditure. The controlling rule here is: Leasehold investigation extends beyond title to the financial and management regime governing occupation.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that service charges become irrelevant on assignment. The controlling rule here is: Leasehold investigation extends beyond title to the financial and management regime governing occupation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Leasehold conveyancing practice; LPE1 and lease/service-charge documentation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Leasehold investigation extends beyond title to the financial and management regime governing occupation.","common_trap":"Assuming registered title searches reveal the operational financial information held by the landlord or managing agent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"90891e7e19b1656e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0759","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Non-fatal offences — assault occasioning ABH","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally strikes V without lawful excuse. The unlawful assault causes an injury amounting to actual bodily harm. D intended the assault but did not intend or foresee actual bodily harm.\n\nWhich is the best general mens rea position for an offence under section 47?","options":{"A":"D must always intend serious bodily harm.","B":"Section 47 is strict liability and requires no mens rea at all.","C":"D must intend the precise injury actually suffered.","D":"It is sufficient that D had the mens rea for the underlying assault or battery; D need not separately intend or foresee the actual bodily harm.","E":"Recklessness as to causing death is required."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"For section 47, the prosecution must establish an assault or battery and resulting actual bodily harm. The defendant need not intend or foresee the ABH itself; mens rea for the underlying assault or battery is sufficient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that d must always intend serious bodily harm. The controlling rule here is: The injury elevates the offence where it is occasioned by an intentional or reckless assault/battery, without a separate mens rea for ABH.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that section 47 is strict liability and requires no mens rea at all. The controlling rule here is: The injury elevates the offence where it is occasioned by an intentional or reckless assault/battery, without a separate mens rea for ABH.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that d must intend the precise injury actually suffered. The controlling rule here is: The injury elevates the offence where it is occasioned by an intentional or reckless assault/battery, without a separate mens rea for ABH.","D":"Correct. For section 47, the prosecution must establish an assault or battery and resulting actual bodily harm. The defendant need not intend or foresee the ABH itself; mens rea for the underlying assault or battery is sufficient.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that recklessness as to causing death is required. The controlling rule here is: The injury elevates the offence where it is occasioned by an intentional or reckless assault/battery, without a separate mens rea for ABH."},"primary_legal_authority":"Offences Against the Person Act 1861, s 47; R v Savage; DPP v Parmenter [1992] 1 AC 699.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The injury elevates the offence where it is occasioned by an intentional or reckless assault/battery, without a separate mens rea for ABH.","common_trap":"Importing the injury-level mens rea associated with more serious offences into section 47.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b65835da5fdb4df1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0760","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — prosecution disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In criminal proceedings, the prosecution possesses unused material that might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence case.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The prosecution may withhold all unused material merely because it does not intend to rely on it.","B":"Only material proving the defendant innocent beyond doubt must be disclosed.","C":"Disclosure duties begin only after conviction.","D":"The defence has no entitlement to any information outside the prosecution's trial bundle.","E":"The prosecution's statutory disclosure regime requires qualifying unused material to be disclosed, subject to the legislation and public-interest procedures."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act establishes prosecution disclosure duties concerning unused material meeting the statutory disclosure test, alongside continuing review and procedures for sensitive material.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the prosecution may withhold all unused material merely because it does not intend to rely on it. The controlling rule here is: Non-reliance by the prosecution does not itself remove material from the disclosure regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that only material proving the defendant innocent beyond doubt must be disclosed. The controlling rule here is: Non-reliance by the prosecution does not itself remove material from the disclosure regime.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that disclosure duties begin only after conviction. The controlling rule here is: Non-reliance by the prosecution does not itself remove material from the disclosure regime.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the defence has no entitlement to any information outside the prosecution's trial bundle. The controlling rule here is: Non-reliance by the prosecution does not itself remove material from the disclosure regime.","E":"Correct. The Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act establishes prosecution disclosure duties concerning unused material meeting the statutory disclosure test, alongside continuing review and procedures for sensitive material."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996, especially s 3; Criminal Procedure Rules and disclosure guidance.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Non-reliance by the prosecution does not itself remove material from the disclosure regime.","common_trap":"Confusing the prosecution's evidential case with its wider statutory duties concerning relevant unused material.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"df897b931fa6fd04"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0761","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — alteration of articles","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company wants to amend its articles to introduce a new governance provision. No entrenched provision or other special complication applies.\n\nWhat shareholder approval is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"A special resolution of the members.","B":"An ordinary resolution of the directors.","C":"Unanimous consent of all creditors.","D":"A court order in every case.","E":"No formal approval if the board supports the amendment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A company may generally amend its articles by special resolution, subject to the Companies Act and any valid entrenchment or other applicable restriction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A company may generally amend its articles by special resolution, subject to the Companies Act and any valid entrenchment or other applicable restriction.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an ordinary resolution of the directors. The controlling point for these facts is: Amending the constitutional articles ordinarily requires the statutory special-resolution threshold.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that unanimous consent of all creditors. The controlling point for these facts is: Amending the constitutional articles ordinarily requires the statutory special-resolution threshold.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a court order in every case. The controlling point for these facts is: Amending the constitutional articles ordinarily requires the statutory special-resolution threshold.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no formal approval if the board supports the amendment. The controlling point for these facts is: Amending the constitutional articles ordinarily requires the statutory special-resolution threshold."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 21 and 283.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Amending the constitutional articles ordinarily requires the statutory special-resolution threshold.","common_trap":"Confusing ordinary management decisions with alteration of the company's constitution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8bd5ede9033b37d3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0762","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — dividends and directors' liability","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Directors approve a dividend when the company's relevant accounts do not support sufficient distributable profits. A shareholder receives the distribution knowing the facts that make it unlawful.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Shareholders can never be required to repay an unlawful distribution.","B":"The distribution may be unlawful, and statutory rules can require a member who knew or had reasonable grounds for believing it was unlawful to repay it; directors may also face consequences for authorising an improper distribution.","C":"A dividend is lawful whenever directors honestly believe cash is available.","D":"Only the company's bank can challenge a dividend.","E":"An unlawful dividend automatically becomes a director's salary."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The capital-maintenance regime restricts distributions to profits available for the purpose. Section 847 addresses recovery from members who knew or had reasonable grounds to believe a distribution contravened the statutory rules, alongside potential director liability under general duties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that shareholders can never be required to repay an unlawful distribution. The controlling point for these facts is: Knowledge can matter to a recipient's repayment obligation, while directors must comply with the statutory distribution regime and their duties.","B":"Correct. The capital-maintenance regime restricts distributions to profits available for the purpose. Section 847 addresses recovery from members who knew or had reasonable grounds to believe a distribution contravened the statutory rules, alongside potential director liability under general duties.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a dividend is lawful whenever directors honestly believe cash is available. The controlling point for these facts is: Knowledge can matter to a recipient's repayment obligation, while directors must comply with the statutory distribution regime and their duties.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the company's bank can challenge a dividend. The controlling point for these facts is: Knowledge can matter to a recipient's repayment obligation, while directors must comply with the statutory distribution regime and their duties.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an unlawful dividend automatically becomes a director's salary. The controlling point for these facts is: Knowledge can matter to a recipient's repayment obligation, while directors must comply with the statutory distribution regime and their duties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 830 and 847; directors' duties.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowledge can matter to a recipient's repayment obligation, while directors must comply with the statutory distribution regime and their duties.","common_trap":"Assuming payment and receipt conclusively validate an otherwise unlawful distribution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b727324833eb582b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0763","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Business taxation — trading losses","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole trader makes a genuine trading loss. The adviser is considering whether tax legislation permits the loss to be relieved against other income or profits, subject to statutory conditions and restrictions.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition?","options":{"A":"A trading loss can never affect income-tax liability.","B":"Every trading loss must automatically be repaid in cash by HMRC.","C":"Trading losses may qualify for statutory loss relief, but the available route depends on the taxpayer's circumstances, timing and applicable conditions rather than the loss simply disappearing for tax purposes.","D":"Trading losses are treated as dividends.","E":"A sole trader can use only corporation-tax loss rules."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Income-tax legislation contains specific reliefs for trading losses, including routes that may set qualifying losses against income or carry them to other periods, subject to detailed statutory requirements and restrictions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a trading loss can never affect income-tax liability. The controlling point for these facts is: Tax treatment of a trading loss depends on the applicable statutory relief rather than an assumption that losses are irrelevant or automatically refunded.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every trading loss must automatically be repaid in cash by HMRC. The controlling point for these facts is: Tax treatment of a trading loss depends on the applicable statutory relief rather than an assumption that losses are irrelevant or automatically refunded.","C":"Correct. Income-tax legislation contains specific reliefs for trading losses, including routes that may set qualifying losses against income or carry them to other periods, subject to detailed statutory requirements and restrictions.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trading losses are treated as dividends. The controlling point for these facts is: Tax treatment of a trading loss depends on the applicable statutory relief rather than an assumption that losses are irrelevant or automatically refunded.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a sole trader can use only corporation-tax loss rules. The controlling point for these facts is: Tax treatment of a trading loss depends on the applicable statutory relief rather than an assumption that losses are irrelevant or automatically refunded."},"primary_legal_authority":"Income Tax Act 2007, Part 4; trading-loss relief principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Tax treatment of a trading loss depends on the applicable statutory relief rather than an assumption that losses are irrelevant or automatically refunded.","common_trap":"Confusing income-tax treatment of an unincorporated trader with corporation-tax rules or an automatic cash reimbursement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9b877563751c931b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0764","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — liquidation expenses and preferential debts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In a corporate liquidation there are insufficient free assets to pay all claims. The liquidator is determining the statutory order in which liquidation expenses, preferential claims and ordinary unsecured claims are addressed.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"All unsecured claims rank before the costs of administering the liquidation.","B":"Shareholders rank ahead of creditors for return of capital.","C":"Preferential status is created merely by a creditor demanding priority.","D":"The Insolvency Act establishes a statutory distribution hierarchy; ordinary unsecured creditors do not simply rank ahead of liquidation expenses and preferential debts.","E":"The liquidator may choose the order solely according to commercial preference."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Insolvency distributions follow statutory priorities. Proper liquidation expenses and categories of preferential debt receive priority according to the statutory scheme before ordinary unsecured distributions, subject to secured interests and other statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that all unsecured claims rank before the costs of administering the liquidation. The controlling point for these facts is: The office-holder cannot replace the statutory priority scheme with a discretionary ranking of creditors.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that shareholders rank ahead of creditors for return of capital. The controlling point for these facts is: The office-holder cannot replace the statutory priority scheme with a discretionary ranking of creditors.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that preferential status is created merely by a creditor demanding priority. The controlling point for these facts is: The office-holder cannot replace the statutory priority scheme with a discretionary ranking of creditors.","D":"Correct. Insolvency distributions follow statutory priorities. Proper liquidation expenses and categories of preferential debt receive priority according to the statutory scheme before ordinary unsecured distributions, subject to secured interests and other statutory rules.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the liquidator may choose the order solely according to commercial preference. The controlling point for these facts is: The office-holder cannot replace the statutory priority scheme with a discretionary ranking of creditors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 107, 115 and Sch 6; Insolvency Rules 2016.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The office-holder cannot replace the statutory priority scheme with a discretionary ranking of creditors.","common_trap":"Treating pari passu sharing among ordinary unsecured creditors as though it displaced prior statutory categories.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1ff79bab54f948e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0765","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"ADR — unreasonable refusal to mediate","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During litigation, one party unreasonably refuses a serious invitation to mediate. The case proceeds to judgment and the refusing party wins on the merits.\n\nWhat may the court do when dealing with costs?","options":{"A":"A successful party can never face an adverse costs consequence for refusing ADR.","B":"Refusal to mediate automatically determines liability on the substantive claim.","C":"Mediation is compulsory in every civil case solely because an opponent requests it.","D":"Costs rules prohibit the court from considering parties' conduct.","E":"The court may take unreasonable ADR conduct into account and depart from the usual costs outcome where appropriate."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The court's costs discretion can reflect unreasonable refusal or failure to engage with ADR. Success on the merits does not create an absolute entitlement immune from conduct-based costs consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a successful party can never face an adverse costs consequence for refusing ADR. The controlling point for these facts is: ADR conduct can be relevant to costs even though it does not itself decide the underlying legal merits.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that refusal to mediate automatically determines liability on the substantive claim. The controlling point for these facts is: ADR conduct can be relevant to costs even though it does not itself decide the underlying legal merits.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that mediation is compulsory in every civil case solely because an opponent requests it. The controlling point for these facts is: ADR conduct can be relevant to costs even though it does not itself decide the underlying legal merits.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that costs rules prohibit the court from considering parties' conduct. The controlling point for these facts is: ADR conduct can be relevant to costs even though it does not itself decide the underlying legal merits.","E":"Correct. The court's costs discretion can reflect unreasonable refusal or failure to engage with ADR. Success on the merits does not create an absolute entitlement immune from conduct-based costs consequences."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 44.2; Halsey v Milton Keynes General NHS Trust [2004] EWCA Civ 576; Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil CBC [2023] EWCA Civ 1416.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"ADR conduct can be relevant to costs even though it does not itself decide the underlying legal merits.","common_trap":"Assuming the general rule that costs follow the event eliminates the court's broader costs discretion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"242310cde1975e50"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0766","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim payments","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant has obtained judgment on liability for damages to be assessed later and needs part of the likely damages before final assessment.\n\nWhich procedural application is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An application for an interim payment under CPR Part 25, subject to the relevant conditions and amount limits.","B":"A Part 36 offer by the court.","C":"A charging order against the claimant.","D":"An application to strike out the judgment.","E":"A request for default judgment on liability already determined."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where specified conditions are satisfied, including where the claimant has obtained judgment for damages to be assessed, while limiting the amount to a reasonable proportion of the likely final judgment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where specified conditions are satisfied, including where the claimant has obtained judgment for damages to be assessed, while limiting the amount to a reasonable proportion of the likely final judgment.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer by the court. The controlling point for these facts is: Interim payment can provide an advance on likely damages after the procedural threshold is met.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a charging order against the claimant. The controlling point for these facts is: Interim payment can provide an advance on likely damages after the procedural threshold is met.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an application to strike out the judgment. The controlling point for these facts is: Interim payment can provide an advance on likely damages after the procedural threshold is met.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a request for default judgment on liability already determined. The controlling point for these facts is: Interim payment can provide an advance on likely damages after the procedural threshold is met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 25.20–25.22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Interim payment can provide an advance on likely damages after the procedural threshold is met.","common_trap":"Confusing interim financial relief within the claim with settlement or post-judgment enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a9f12be138f2564d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0767","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — third-party debt orders","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment creditor knows that a bank owes money to the judgment debtor in an account. The creditor wants an enforcement mechanism capable of requiring the bank to pay the judgment creditor instead.\n\nWhich mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A charging order over land.","B":"A third-party debt order.","C":"An interim injunction against an unrelated witness.","D":"A Part 36 offer.","E":"Specific disclosure."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A third-party debt order can attach a debt owed by a third party, commonly a bank, to the judgment debtor and ultimately require payment to the judgment creditor, subject to CPR Part 72.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a charging order over land. The controlling point for these facts is: The procedure targets a debt due from a third party to the judgment debtor rather than creating security over the debtor's land.","B":"Correct. A third-party debt order can attach a debt owed by a third party, commonly a bank, to the judgment debtor and ultimately require payment to the judgment creditor, subject to CPR Part 72.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an interim injunction against an unrelated witness. The controlling point for these facts is: The procedure targets a debt due from a third party to the judgment debtor rather than creating security over the debtor's land.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer. The controlling point for these facts is: The procedure targets a debt due from a third party to the judgment debtor rather than creating security over the debtor's land.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that specific disclosure. The controlling point for these facts is: The procedure targets a debt due from a third party to the judgment debtor rather than creating security over the debtor's land."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 72.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The procedure targets a debt due from a third party to the judgment debtor rather than creating security over the debtor's land.","common_trap":"Confusing attachment of a third-party debt with a charging order over an asset.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"84fdf1717aae5f05"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0768","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Legal Services — civil legal aid","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A prospective client asks whether civil legal aid is available for a matter. The solicitor knows that the client's finances alone do not determine eligibility.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every person below a single income figure automatically receives civil legal aid for every civil dispute.","B":"Civil legal aid is available only for criminal prosecutions.","C":"Eligibility depends on the matter falling within the statutory scope and on applicable means and merits requirements, subject to exceptions and the legal-aid scheme.","D":"A solicitor may award legal aid without applying the statutory scheme.","E":"Merits are never relevant to civil legal-aid eligibility."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Civil legal aid under LASPO depends on whether the service is within statutory scope and, where applicable, financial eligibility and merits criteria, with specific exceptions and gateways under the scheme.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every person below a single income figure automatically receives civil legal aid for every civil dispute. The controlling point for these facts is: Legal-aid analysis requires both subject-matter scope and the relevant eligibility tests.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that civil legal aid is available only for criminal prosecutions. The controlling point for these facts is: Legal-aid analysis requires both subject-matter scope and the relevant eligibility tests.","C":"Correct. Civil legal aid under LASPO depends on whether the service is within statutory scope and, where applicable, financial eligibility and merits criteria, with specific exceptions and gateways under the scheme.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a solicitor may award legal aid without applying the statutory scheme. The controlling point for these facts is: Legal-aid analysis requires both subject-matter scope and the relevant eligibility tests.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that merits are never relevant to civil legal-aid eligibility. The controlling point for these facts is: Legal-aid analysis requires both subject-matter scope and the relevant eligibility tests."},"primary_legal_authority":"Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012; Civil Legal Aid regulations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Legal-aid analysis requires both subject-matter scope and the relevant eligibility tests.","common_trap":"Reducing legal-aid eligibility to a single financial threshold while ignoring statutory scope and merits.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f66ee97effacb806"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0769","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Financial services — regulated activities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor proposes, as a separate business activity, to advise clients routinely on the merits of buying specified investments. No relevant exemption or exclusion has yet been identified.\n\nWhich regulatory issue should be considered before the activity is undertaken?","options":{"A":"Solicitors are automatically exempt from all financial-services regulation.","B":"Only banks can carry on regulated activities.","C":"FCA regulation is irrelevant whenever legal advice is also provided.","D":"Whether the activity is a regulated activity requiring FCA authorisation or falling within a lawful exemption or exclusion, including the professional-firms regime where applicable.","E":"Client consent alone authorises any regulated investment activity."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Specified investment activities can fall within the Financial Services and Markets Act regulatory perimeter. Solicitors must consider whether authorisation, an exemption or the professional-firms arrangements permit the proposed activity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that solicitors are automatically exempt from all financial-services regulation. The controlling point for these facts is: Professional status does not itself remove activities from the statutory financial-services perimeter.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only banks can carry on regulated activities. The controlling point for these facts is: Professional status does not itself remove activities from the statutory financial-services perimeter.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that fCA regulation is irrelevant whenever legal advice is also provided. The controlling point for these facts is: Professional status does not itself remove activities from the statutory financial-services perimeter.","D":"Correct. Specified investment activities can fall within the Financial Services and Markets Act regulatory perimeter. Solicitors must consider whether authorisation, an exemption or the professional-firms arrangements permit the proposed activity.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that client consent alone authorises any regulated investment activity. The controlling point for these facts is: Professional status does not itself remove activities from the statutory financial-services perimeter."},"primary_legal_authority":"Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, ss 19 and 22; Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001; SRA Financial Services rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional status does not itself remove activities from the statutory financial-services perimeter.","common_trap":"Assuming SRA regulation is a blanket substitute for FCA authorisation requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b3a5c60fe72e8de4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0770","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — unilateral offers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company publicly promises £1,000 to anyone who performs a specified act on stated conditions. A person, knowing of the offer, fully performs the act in accordance with those conditions.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"No contract can arise because acceptance must always be communicated before performance.","B":"Public offers are never legally capable of acceptance.","C":"The performer must sign a deed before beginning the act.","D":"Performance can amount only to consideration, never acceptance.","E":"A unilateral contract may arise through performance of the stipulated act without a separate communicated promise to perform."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A unilateral offer invites acceptance by performance. Where the offer is sufficiently certain and intended to be binding, a person who knows of it can ordinarily accept by completing the stipulated conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no contract can arise because acceptance must always be communicated before performance. The controlling point for these facts is: In a unilateral contract, performance can constitute acceptance as well as consideration without prior notification of a promise to perform.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that public offers are never legally capable of acceptance. The controlling point for these facts is: In a unilateral contract, performance can constitute acceptance as well as consideration without prior notification of a promise to perform.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the performer must sign a deed before beginning the act. The controlling point for these facts is: In a unilateral contract, performance can constitute acceptance as well as consideration without prior notification of a promise to perform.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that performance can amount only to consideration, never acceptance. The controlling point for these facts is: In a unilateral contract, performance can constitute acceptance as well as consideration without prior notification of a promise to perform.","E":"Correct. A unilateral offer invites acceptance by performance. Where the offer is sufficiently certain and intended to be binding, a person who knows of it can ordinarily accept by completing the stipulated conditions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball Co [1893] 1 QB 256.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"In a unilateral contract, performance can constitute acceptance as well as consideration without prior notification of a promise to perform.","common_trap":"Applying bilateral-contract communication rules mechanically to an offer expressly inviting acceptance by conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"31614dfe83c6f601"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0771","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — innocent misrepresentation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller makes a false material statement that induces a contract. The seller proves reasonable grounds for believing the statement true, so liability under section 2(1) of the Misrepresentation Act is not established. No fraud is shown.\n\nWhich remedy may nevertheless be relevant for an actionable innocent misrepresentation?","options":{"A":"Rescission, subject to the usual bars, and potentially damages in lieu of rescission under section 2(2) where its conditions are met.","B":"Punitive damages are automatically awarded.","C":"The contract is automatically void from the outset without election.","D":"No remedy can ever exist if section 2(1) is unavailable.","E":"Specific performance must be ordered for the representor."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An actionable non-fraudulent misrepresentation can support rescission, subject to bars such as affirmation and third-party rights. Section 2(2) gives the court a statutory discretion in qualifying cases to award damages in lieu of rescission.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An actionable non-fraudulent misrepresentation can support rescission, subject to bars such as affirmation and third-party rights. Section 2(2) gives the court a statutory discretion in qualifying cases to award damages in lieu of rescission.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that punitive damages are automatically awarded. The controlling point for these facts is: Failure to establish section 2(1) damages does not necessarily eliminate rescission or the separate section 2(2) discretion.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the contract is automatically void from the outset without election. The controlling point for these facts is: Failure to establish section 2(1) damages does not necessarily eliminate rescission or the separate section 2(2) discretion.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no remedy can ever exist if section 2(1) is unavailable. The controlling point for these facts is: Failure to establish section 2(1) damages does not necessarily eliminate rescission or the separate section 2(2) discretion.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that specific performance must be ordered for the representor. The controlling point for these facts is: Failure to establish section 2(1) damages does not necessarily eliminate rescission or the separate section 2(2) discretion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967, ss 2(1)–2(2); rescission principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Failure to establish section 2(1) damages does not necessarily eliminate rescission or the separate section 2(2) discretion.","common_trap":"Treating the different remedial routes under misrepresentation law as identical.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"db0a62244042920e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0772","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer Rights Act — digital content","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer pays a trader for downloadable digital content. The content is defective and does not meet the statutory quality standard.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Digital content is outside the Consumer Rights Act entirely.","B":"The Consumer Rights Act contains specific statutory rights and remedies for digital content supplied by a trader to a consumer.","C":"Only physical goods can be subject to statutory quality rights.","D":"The consumer's only possible remedy is criminal prosecution of the trader.","E":"Digital content is treated exclusively as land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Part 1 Chapter 3 of the Consumer Rights Act establishes standards for digital content, including satisfactory quality, fitness for particular purpose and description, together with a specific remedies regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that digital content is outside the Consumer Rights Act entirely. The controlling point for these facts is: Paid digital content is expressly regulated rather than being left outside consumer-contract protection.","B":"Correct. Part 1 Chapter 3 of the Consumer Rights Act establishes standards for digital content, including satisfactory quality, fitness for particular purpose and description, together with a specific remedies regime.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only physical goods can be subject to statutory quality rights. The controlling point for these facts is: Paid digital content is expressly regulated rather than being left outside consumer-contract protection.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the consumer's only possible remedy is criminal prosecution of the trader. The controlling point for these facts is: Paid digital content is expressly regulated rather than being left outside consumer-contract protection.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that digital content is treated exclusively as land. The controlling point for these facts is: Paid digital content is expressly regulated rather than being left outside consumer-contract protection."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, ss 33–47.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Paid digital content is expressly regulated rather than being left outside consumer-contract protection.","common_trap":"Assuming consumer quality protections apply only to tangible goods.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"484133b34db82e2a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0773","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — rescuers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently creates a dangerous situation. A professional rescuer responds and suffers a foreseeable physical injury while carrying out a reasonable rescue.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Professional rescuers can never recover because danger is part of their work.","B":"Every rescue is a novus actus interveniens.","C":"A rescuer can in principle recover for foreseeable physical injury caused by the defendant's negligence; reasonable rescue does not automatically break causation or amount to volenti.","D":"A rescuer necessarily consents to all negligent risks.","E":"Liability exists only if the rescuer is related to the original victim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Negligence law recognises that rescue is a foreseeable response to danger. A reasonable rescuer is not ordinarily treated as breaking the chain of causation or voluntarily accepting the defendant's negligence merely by attempting the rescue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that professional rescuers can never recover because danger is part of their work. The controlling point for these facts is: Reasonable rescue can remain within the scope of foreseeable consequences of the defendant's wrongdoing.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every rescue is a novus actus interveniens. The controlling point for these facts is: Reasonable rescue can remain within the scope of foreseeable consequences of the defendant's wrongdoing.","C":"Correct. Negligence law recognises that rescue is a foreseeable response to danger. A reasonable rescuer is not ordinarily treated as breaking the chain of causation or voluntarily accepting the defendant's negligence merely by attempting the rescue.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a rescuer necessarily consents to all negligent risks. The controlling point for these facts is: Reasonable rescue can remain within the scope of foreseeable consequences of the defendant's wrongdoing.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that liability exists only if the rescuer is related to the original victim. The controlling point for these facts is: Reasonable rescue can remain within the scope of foreseeable consequences of the defendant's wrongdoing."},"primary_legal_authority":"Haynes v Harwood [1935] 1 KB 146; Baker v T E Hopkins & Son Ltd [1959] 1 WLR 966.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Reasonable rescue can remain within the scope of foreseeable consequences of the defendant's wrongdoing.","common_trap":"Treating occupational exposure to danger as blanket consent to negligent injury.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6d4894c36dbd95f1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0774","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Trespass to land — continuing trespass","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant wrongfully leaves a substantial object on the claimant's land and, after being asked to remove it, continues to leave it there.\n\nWhich is the best general characterisation?","options":{"A":"Trespass can occur only at the instant a person first enters land.","B":"Leaving an object on another's land can never be trespass.","C":"The claimant automatically becomes owner of the object.","D":"The continued presence can amount to a continuing trespass, potentially giving rise to remedies while the wrongful state continues.","E":"A continuing trespass is exclusively a criminal offence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Wrongfully placing and leaving an object on another's land can constitute a continuing trespass, with the cause continuing while the wrongful interference remains.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trespass can occur only at the instant a person first enters land. The controlling point for these facts is: Trespass protects possession against direct unjustified interference, which can persist over time.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that leaving an object on another's land can never be trespass. The controlling point for these facts is: Trespass protects possession against direct unjustified interference, which can persist over time.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant automatically becomes owner of the object. The controlling point for these facts is: Trespass protects possession against direct unjustified interference, which can persist over time.","D":"Correct. Wrongfully placing and leaving an object on another's land can constitute a continuing trespass, with the cause continuing while the wrongful interference remains.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a continuing trespass is exclusively a criminal offence. The controlling point for these facts is: Trespass protects possession against direct unjustified interference, which can persist over time."},"primary_legal_authority":"Holmes v Wilson (1839) 10 Ad & E 503; trespass-to-land principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trespass protects possession against direct unjustified interference, which can persist over time.","common_trap":"Assuming trespass is necessarily a momentary event incapable of continuing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"abedbadcccaef6cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0775","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — employee versus independent contractor","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business engages a genuinely independent contractor to perform specialist work. The contractor negligently injures a third party. No non-delegable duty or other exceptional basis of liability applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every person who pays another to work is automatically vicariously liable.","B":"Independent-contractor status is irrelevant to vicarious liability.","C":"The injured person can sue only the business and never the contractor.","D":"A written contract describing someone as independent is always conclusive regardless of the true relationship.","E":"A person is not ordinarily vicariously liable merely for the torts of a genuinely independent contractor, though exceptions and direct duties must be considered."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Vicarious liability ordinarily requires a relationship akin to employment and a sufficient connection between that relationship and the tort. A genuinely independent contractor generally falls outside that doctrine, subject to separate direct or non-delegable duties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every person who pays another to work is automatically vicariously liable. The controlling point for these facts is: Independent contracting does not ordinarily create vicarious liability merely because work benefits the defendant.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that independent-contractor status is irrelevant to vicarious liability. The controlling point for these facts is: Independent contracting does not ordinarily create vicarious liability merely because work benefits the defendant.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the injured person can sue only the business and never the contractor. The controlling point for these facts is: Independent contracting does not ordinarily create vicarious liability merely because work benefits the defendant.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a written contract describing someone as independent is always conclusive regardless of the true relationship. The controlling point for these facts is: Independent contracting does not ordinarily create vicarious liability merely because work benefits the defendant.","E":"Correct. Vicarious liability ordinarily requires a relationship akin to employment and a sufficient connection between that relationship and the tort. A genuinely independent contractor generally falls outside that doctrine, subject to separate direct or non-delegable duties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barclays Bank plc v Various Claimants [2020] UKSC 13; vicarious-liability principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Independent contracting does not ordinarily create vicarious liability merely because work benefits the defendant.","common_trap":"Assuming all outsourced work carries the same vicarious-liability consequences as employment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"06d811317d6818dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0776","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Constitutional law — royal prerogative","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister relies on a prerogative power in an area where Parliament has enacted a statutory scheme that is inconsistent with using the prerogative to bypass statutory controls.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The executive cannot ordinarily use prerogative power to frustrate or circumvent a statutory scheme governing the same subject matter.","B":"Prerogative powers always override Acts of Parliament.","C":"A minister can suspend any statute by executive decision.","D":"Statutes and prerogative powers have identical constitutional status, so the minister may choose freely.","E":"Courts have no role in determining the legal limits of prerogative power."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where statute occupies the field or confers powers subject to statutory conditions, the executive cannot use prerogative powers to evade Parliament's scheme. Parliamentary legislation is constitutionally superior to inconsistent prerogative action.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where statute occupies the field or confers powers subject to statutory conditions, the executive cannot use prerogative powers to evade Parliament's scheme. Parliamentary legislation is constitutionally superior to inconsistent prerogative action.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that prerogative powers always override Acts of Parliament. The controlling point for these facts is: Executive residual powers operate within limits set by statute.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a minister can suspend any statute by executive decision. The controlling point for these facts is: Executive residual powers operate within limits set by statute.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that statutes and prerogative powers have identical constitutional status, so the minister may choose freely. The controlling point for these facts is: Executive residual powers operate within limits set by statute.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that courts have no role in determining the legal limits of prerogative power. The controlling point for these facts is: Executive residual powers operate within limits set by statute."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd [1920] AC 508; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the EU [2017] UKSC 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executive residual powers operate within limits set by statute.","common_trap":"Treating prerogative authority as capable of overriding or neutralising Parliament's legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b887b44b9f35847d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0777","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"EU law — assimilated law interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is considering a domestic rule that originated in retained EU law and continued after the end of 2023 as assimilated law. The issue concerns the current domestic status of the rule rather than pre-Brexit EU obligations.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"All pre-Brexit EU principles continue with unlimited supremacy over every later Act of Parliament.","B":"Apply the current domestic statutory framework governing assimilated law, including amendments made by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023, rather than assuming the former general supremacy of EU law continues unchanged.","C":"Assimilated law ceased to exist automatically at the end of 2023.","D":"Every assimilated rule is now a treaty obligation enforceable directly against Parliament.","E":"UK courts must make a preliminary reference to the CJEU whenever assimilated law is disputed."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The 2023 Act changed the terminology and constitutional treatment of retained EU law from the end of 2023, including changes to supremacy and general principles. Current questions require application of the amended domestic framework to the particular rule and time period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that all pre-Brexit EU principles continue with unlimited supremacy over every later Act of Parliament. The controlling point for these facts is: Post-Brexit EU-derived rules must be analysed under the legislation now governing assimilated law, not by mechanically importing the former EU supremacy model.","B":"Correct. The 2023 Act changed the terminology and constitutional treatment of retained EU law from the end of 2023, including changes to supremacy and general principles. Current questions require application of the amended domestic framework to the particular rule and time period.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that assimilated law ceased to exist automatically at the end of 2023. The controlling point for these facts is: Post-Brexit EU-derived rules must be analysed under the legislation now governing assimilated law, not by mechanically importing the former EU supremacy model.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every assimilated rule is now a treaty obligation enforceable directly against Parliament. The controlling point for these facts is: Post-Brexit EU-derived rules must be analysed under the legislation now governing assimilated law, not by mechanically importing the former EU supremacy model.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that uK courts must make a preliminary reference to the CJEU whenever assimilated law is disputed. The controlling point for these facts is: Post-Brexit EU-derived rules must be analysed under the legislation now governing assimilated law, not by mechanically importing the former EU supremacy model."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, as amended by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-Brexit EU-derived rules must be analysed under the legislation now governing assimilated law, not by mechanically importing the former EU supremacy model.","common_trap":"Assuming either total disappearance of EU-derived domestic rules or unchanged pre-exit supremacy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bb8d1c48748d79c9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0778","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — ejusdem generis","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute refers to 'cars, vans, lorries and other vehicles' in a provision regulating motor traffic. A court considers whether the general words should take meaning from the specific items preceding them.\n\nWhich interpretive principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Res judicata.","B":"Volenti non fit injuria.","C":"The ejusdem generis principle.","D":"The postal rule.","E":"Overreaching."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Ejusdem generis is a linguistic canon under which general words following specific examples may be construed as limited to the same class or genus, where the statutory context supports that reading.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that res judicata. The controlling point for these facts is: The canon helps infer the scope of general language from a preceding list of specific items.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that volenti non fit injuria. The controlling point for these facts is: The canon helps infer the scope of general language from a preceding list of specific items.","C":"Correct. Ejusdem generis is a linguistic canon under which general words following specific examples may be construed as limited to the same class or genus, where the statutory context supports that reading.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the postal rule. The controlling point for these facts is: The canon helps infer the scope of general language from a preceding list of specific items.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that overreaching. The controlling point for these facts is: The canon helps infer the scope of general language from a preceding list of specific items."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory interpretation principles; ejusdem generis.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The canon helps infer the scope of general language from a preceding list of specific items.","common_trap":"Confusing a statutory-construction canon with unrelated doctrines from procedure, tort, contract or land law.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3d3def213c117cbf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0779","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — privileged wills","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A serving member of the armed forces is on actual military service and makes an informal testamentary disposition in circumstances falling within the statutory privileged-wills regime.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"All members of the armed forces can ignore will formalities at all times.","B":"Privileged wills exist only for minors who own land.","C":"Section 9 formalities can never be relaxed in any circumstances.","D":"The Wills Act provides a special exception under which qualifying soldiers on actual military service and certain mariners or seamen at sea may make privileged wills without ordinary section 9 formalities.","E":"A privileged will must always be executed by two solicitors."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 11 preserves a narrow privileged-will regime for qualifying soldiers in actual military service and mariners or seamen at sea, allowing testamentary dispositions without ordinary formalities in circumstances within the provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that all members of the armed forces can ignore will formalities at all times. The controlling point for these facts is: The exception is status- and circumstance-specific, not a general military exemption from will formalities.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that privileged wills exist only for minors who own land. The controlling point for these facts is: The exception is status- and circumstance-specific, not a general military exemption from will formalities.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that section 9 formalities can never be relaxed in any circumstances. The controlling point for these facts is: The exception is status- and circumstance-specific, not a general military exemption from will formalities.","D":"Correct. Section 11 preserves a narrow privileged-will regime for qualifying soldiers in actual military service and mariners or seamen at sea, allowing testamentary dispositions without ordinary formalities in circumstances within the provision.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a privileged will must always be executed by two solicitors. The controlling point for these facts is: The exception is status- and circumstance-specific, not a general military exemption from will formalities."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 11.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The exception is status- and circumstance-specific, not a general military exemption from will formalities.","common_trap":"Extending the privileged-wills exception to every service member regardless of actual service circumstances.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1c9f09a7a2c8893b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0780","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — insolvent estates","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased's estate is insolvent: the assets are insufficient to pay all debts and liabilities. The personal representatives are preparing to distribute the estate.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"Specific legatees must always be paid before creditors.","B":"The personal representatives may choose whichever creditor they personally prefer.","C":"Insolvency rules do not apply after death.","D":"All debts are automatically extinguished on death.","E":"The personal representatives must follow the statutory insolvency administration and priority rules rather than paying beneficiaries or selected ordinary creditors at will."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An insolvent estate must be administered according to the statutory order applicable to insolvent estates, protecting creditor priorities before any beneficiary distribution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that specific legatees must always be paid before creditors. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal representatives administering an insolvent estate must respect the prescribed order of payment.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the personal representatives may choose whichever creditor they personally prefer. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal representatives administering an insolvent estate must respect the prescribed order of payment.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that insolvency rules do not apply after death. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal representatives administering an insolvent estate must respect the prescribed order of payment.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that all debts are automatically extinguished on death. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal representatives administering an insolvent estate must respect the prescribed order of payment.","E":"Correct. An insolvent estate must be administered according to the statutory order applicable to insolvent estates, protecting creditor priorities before any beneficiary distribution."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Insolvent Estates of Deceased Persons Order 1986; Administration of Estates Act 1925.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Personal representatives administering an insolvent estate must respect the prescribed order of payment.","common_trap":"Treating estate administration as discretionary once assets are insufficient to satisfy all liabilities.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"494c607fd7ceff81"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0781","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — business relief","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased owned qualifying business property. The executors ask whether the taxable value may be reduced by a statutory relief, assuming all ownership and qualification conditions are satisfied.\n\nWhich relief is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Business relief under the Inheritance Tax Act 1984.","B":"Principal private residence relief under capital gains tax.","C":"Roll-over relief for replacement business assets only.","D":"Gift Aid.","E":"VAT input-tax recovery."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Business relief can reduce the value transferred for IHT purposes in relation to qualifying business property, at the statutory rate applicable to the category and subject to ownership and exclusion rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Business relief can reduce the value transferred for IHT purposes in relation to qualifying business property, at the statutory rate applicable to the category and subject to ownership and exclusion rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that principal private residence relief under capital gains tax. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying business property can attract a specific IHT valuation relief.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that roll-over relief for replacement business assets only. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying business property can attract a specific IHT valuation relief.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that gift Aid. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying business property can attract a specific IHT valuation relief.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that vAT input-tax recovery. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying business property can attract a specific IHT valuation relief."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, ss 103–114.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying business property can attract a specific IHT valuation relief.","common_trap":"Confusing inheritance-tax business relief with reliefs belonging to CGT, income tax or VAT.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"11c6c7f2443b04c7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0782","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — business money and client account","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client pays a solicitor an amount that is immediately and properly due to the firm for billed legal fees. No part is held for the client's future costs or disbursements.\n\nWhich is the best general classification?","options":{"A":"Every payment made by a client is client money indefinitely.","B":"Money properly due to the firm is business money rather than client money and should be dealt with through the firm's business account arrangements.","C":"Billed fees must remain in client account until the matter closes.","D":"Business money can never be paid electronically.","E":"The money belongs beneficially to the client even after becoming properly due to the firm."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules distinguish client money from money belonging to the authorised body. Amounts properly due to the firm for its own fees are business money, although transfers from client account for costs are subject to specific requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every payment made by a client is client money indefinitely. The controlling point for these facts is: Ownership and purpose determine classification; a client's payment can become the firm's money when properly due.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules distinguish client money from money belonging to the authorised body. Amounts properly due to the firm for its own fees are business money, although transfers from client account for costs are subject to specific requirements.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that billed fees must remain in client account until the matter closes. The controlling point for these facts is: Ownership and purpose determine classification; a client's payment can become the firm's money when properly due.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that business money can never be paid electronically. The controlling point for these facts is: Ownership and purpose determine classification; a client's payment can become the firm's money when properly due.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the money belongs beneficially to the client even after becoming properly due to the firm. The controlling point for these facts is: Ownership and purpose determine classification; a client's payment can become the firm's money when properly due."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 2 and 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ownership and purpose determine classification; a client's payment can become the firm's money when properly due.","common_trap":"Assuming the identity of the payer, rather than beneficial ownership and purpose, determines whether money is client money.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e57159f8415b4ecc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0783","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — advancement of capital","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary has a vested or presumptive share of trust capital but is not yet entitled to receive it outright. Trustees consider using part of that capital to assist the beneficiary with a significant educational opportunity.\n\nWhich statutory power may be relevant, subject to the trust instrument and statutory conditions?","options":{"A":"The power to revoke the trust unilaterally.","B":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier regardless of the beneficiary's age and other beneficiaries.","C":"The statutory power of advancement under section 32 of the Trustee Act 1925.","D":"The statutory maintenance power is the only possible use of trust property.","E":"The trustees must distribute the entire trust fund whenever education is involved."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 32 provides trustees with a statutory power, subject to its conditions and the trust terms, to apply capital for the advancement or benefit of a beneficiary with the requisite interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the power to revoke the trust unilaterally. The controlling point for these facts is: Advancement permits qualifying capital to be applied for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit before the ordinary time for outright receipt.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the rule in Saunders v Vautier regardless of the beneficiary's age and other beneficiaries. The controlling point for these facts is: Advancement permits qualifying capital to be applied for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit before the ordinary time for outright receipt.","C":"Correct. Section 32 provides trustees with a statutory power, subject to its conditions and the trust terms, to apply capital for the advancement or benefit of a beneficiary with the requisite interest.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the statutory maintenance power is the only possible use of trust property. The controlling point for these facts is: Advancement permits qualifying capital to be applied for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit before the ordinary time for outright receipt.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the trustees must distribute the entire trust fund whenever education is involved. The controlling point for these facts is: Advancement permits qualifying capital to be applied for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit before the ordinary time for outright receipt."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 32, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Advancement permits qualifying capital to be applied for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit before the ordinary time for outright receipt.","common_trap":"Confusing the use of capital under section 32 with maintenance from income under section 31.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dcd98cea75761321"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0784","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Breach of trust — limitation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary discovers that a trustee fraudulently converted trust property to the trustee's own use many years ago. The trustee argues that the ordinary six-year limitation period automatically bars the beneficiary's claim.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every breach-of-trust claim is automatically barred six years after the transaction.","B":"Limitation never applies to any trust claim.","C":"Fraud is irrelevant to limitation.","D":"Statutory limitation contains important exceptions for claims against trustees involving fraud or fraudulent breach of trust and recovery of trust property or proceeds retained or converted by the trustee.","E":"Only criminal proceedings can be brought after six years."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 21 of the Limitation Act contains specific rules for trust claims, including no limitation period for certain actions involving trustee fraud/fraudulent breach or recovery of trust property or proceeds in the trustee's possession or previously received and converted.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every breach-of-trust claim is automatically barred six years after the transaction. The controlling point for these facts is: Trust limitation cannot be analysed by applying a single six-year rule without checking statutory exceptions.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that limitation never applies to any trust claim. The controlling point for these facts is: Trust limitation cannot be analysed by applying a single six-year rule without checking statutory exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that fraud is irrelevant to limitation. The controlling point for these facts is: Trust limitation cannot be analysed by applying a single six-year rule without checking statutory exceptions.","D":"Correct. Section 21 of the Limitation Act contains specific rules for trust claims, including no limitation period for certain actions involving trustee fraud/fraudulent breach or recovery of trust property or proceeds in the trustee's possession or previously received and converted.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only criminal proceedings can be brought after six years. The controlling point for these facts is: Trust limitation cannot be analysed by applying a single six-year rule without checking statutory exceptions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trust limitation cannot be analysed by applying a single six-year rule without checking statutory exceptions.","common_trap":"Assuming all trust claims share one ordinary limitation period regardless of fraud or proprietary recovery.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dd68cf7bec6ef9fe"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0785","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — TLATA sale applications","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Co-owners hold a family property on trust of land. They disagree about whether it should be sold. One co-owner applies to court for an order concerning the exercise of the trustees' functions.\n\nWhich statute gives the court the central power to determine such an application?","options":{"A":"The Sale of Goods Act 1979.","B":"The Defamation Act 2013.","C":"The Companies Act 2006.","D":"The Fraud Act 2006.","E":"The Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, particularly sections 14 and 15."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 14 TLATA permits trustees or persons with an interest in property subject to a trust of land to apply for orders concerning trustees' functions, while section 15 specifies factors relevant to the court's determination.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the Sale of Goods Act 1979. The controlling point for these facts is: Disputes about sale of co-owned trust land are centrally governed by TLATA rather than the old automatic trust-for-sale model.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the Defamation Act 2013. The controlling point for these facts is: Disputes about sale of co-owned trust land are centrally governed by TLATA rather than the old automatic trust-for-sale model.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the Companies Act 2006. The controlling point for these facts is: Disputes about sale of co-owned trust land are centrally governed by TLATA rather than the old automatic trust-for-sale model.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the Fraud Act 2006. The controlling point for these facts is: Disputes about sale of co-owned trust land are centrally governed by TLATA rather than the old automatic trust-for-sale model.","E":"Correct. Section 14 TLATA permits trustees or persons with an interest in property subject to a trust of land to apply for orders concerning trustees' functions, while section 15 specifies factors relevant to the court's determination."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, ss 14–15.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disputes about sale of co-owned trust land are centrally governed by TLATA rather than the old automatic trust-for-sale model.","common_trap":"Looking for a general property power without applying the statutory trust-of-land regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"38d056a5c9b9b8d2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0786","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — bad character: gateways","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution wants to adduce evidence of a defendant's previous misconduct as evidence of bad character. The defendant objects.\n\nWhat framework governs admissibility?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The Criminal Justice Act 2003 gateways govern admissibility of defendant bad-character evidence, and the court must identify an applicable gateway rather than admitting it simply because it seems relevant.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Bad-character evidence is governed by a specific statutory admissibility regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. Bad-character evidence is governed by a specific statutory admissibility regime.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, ss 98–101","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Bad-character evidence is governed by a specific statutory admissibility regime.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"915b6258064d6066"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0787","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — rent review","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease contains an open-market rent-review clause. The review date arrives and the parties disagree about the new rent.\n\nWhich document is the starting point for determining the review mechanism, assumptions, disregards and dispute procedure?","options":{"A":"The tenant's company articles.","B":"The lease itself, particularly the rent-review clause.","C":"The seller's old residential property questionnaire.","D":"The criminal procedure rules.","E":"The grant of probate."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Commercial rent review is primarily contractual. The lease defines the review date, valuation assumptions and disregards, notice requirements and any expert or arbitrator mechanism, subject to applicable law.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the tenant's company articles. The controlling point for these facts is: The parties' agreed lease machinery governs how the reviewed rent is determined.","B":"Correct. Commercial rent review is primarily contractual. The lease defines the review date, valuation assumptions and disregards, notice requirements and any expert or arbitrator mechanism, subject to applicable law.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the seller's old residential property questionnaire. The controlling point for these facts is: The parties' agreed lease machinery governs how the reviewed rent is determined.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the criminal procedure rules. The controlling point for these facts is: The parties' agreed lease machinery governs how the reviewed rent is determined.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the grant of probate. The controlling point for these facts is: The parties' agreed lease machinery governs how the reviewed rent is determined."},"primary_legal_authority":"Commercial lease drafting and rent-review principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The parties' agreed lease machinery governs how the reviewed rent is determined.","common_trap":"Assuming there is a universal statutory formula that replaces the negotiated lease clause.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a1a654a8620f0f17"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0788","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — hearsay: unavailable witness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A prosecution witness with important first-hand evidence has died before trial. The prosecution seeks to tender the witness's earlier statement for its truth.\n\nWhich issue is central?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The statutory hearsay regime includes specified routes for admitting first-hand hearsay where the maker is unavailable for a recognised reason such as death, subject to safeguards.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Death of the maker can engage a statutory hearsay exception but does not make every statement automatically admissible.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Correct. Death of the maker can engage a statutory hearsay exception but does not make every statement automatically admissible.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, ss 114 and 116","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Death of the maker can engage a statutory hearsay exception but does not make every statement automatically admissible.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"57c81081ac3fbc66"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0789","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — property belonging to another","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D takes money from a charity collection box intending permanently to keep it. D argues that the money cannot 'belong to another' because individual donors no longer own the exact coins.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Property belongs to another only if the original purchaser still owns it.","B":"Money can never be stolen once placed in a collection box.","C":"Theft requires the victim to identify the serial number of each coin.","D":"Property belongs to another for theft if another person has possession or control of it, or any proprietary right or interest, so the absence of ownership by the original donors does not prevent the element being satisfied.","E":"Only land can belong to another for Theft Act purposes."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 5 defines property as belonging to any person having possession or control of it or any proprietary right or interest. Theft therefore does not depend solely on identifying the original owner.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that property belongs to another only if the original purchaser still owns it. The controlling point for these facts is: The statutory concept of belonging to another is broader than strict ownership.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that money can never be stolen once placed in a collection box. The controlling point for these facts is: The statutory concept of belonging to another is broader than strict ownership.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that theft requires the victim to identify the serial number of each coin. The controlling point for these facts is: The statutory concept of belonging to another is broader than strict ownership.","D":"Correct. Section 5 defines property as belonging to any person having possession or control of it or any proprietary right or interest. Theft therefore does not depend solely on identifying the original owner.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only land can belong to another for Theft Act purposes. The controlling point for these facts is: The statutory concept of belonging to another is broader than strict ownership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory concept of belonging to another is broader than strict ownership.","common_trap":"Equating 'belonging to another' exclusively with original legal title.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7da18aa4cd4abcd8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0790","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — submission of no case to answer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At the close of the prosecution case at a criminal trial, the defence argues that the prosecution evidence is legally insufficient for a properly directed tribunal to convict.\n\nWhich procedural step is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A Part 36 offer.","B":"A civil summary-judgment application.","C":"A third-party debt order.","D":"A statutory demand.","E":"A submission of no case to answer, applying the Galbraith principles."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A defendant may submit that there is no case to answer after the prosecution evidence. Galbraith addresses cases where there is no evidence on an essential element or where the evidence is so weak that a properly directed jury could not properly convict, while recognising the jury's role where evidence depends on assessment of reliability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer. The controlling point for these facts is: The submission tests whether the prosecution evidence is legally sufficient to require an answer from the defence.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a civil summary-judgment application. The controlling point for these facts is: The submission tests whether the prosecution evidence is legally sufficient to require an answer from the defence.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a third-party debt order. The controlling point for these facts is: The submission tests whether the prosecution evidence is legally sufficient to require an answer from the defence.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a statutory demand. The controlling point for these facts is: The submission tests whether the prosecution evidence is legally sufficient to require an answer from the defence.","E":"Correct. A defendant may submit that there is no case to answer after the prosecution evidence. Galbraith addresses cases where there is no evidence on an essential element or where the evidence is so weak that a properly directed jury could not properly convict, while recognising the jury's role where evidence depends on assessment of reliability."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Galbraith [1981] 1 WLR 1039; Criminal Procedure Rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The submission tests whether the prosecution evidence is legally sufficient to require an answer from the defence.","common_trap":"Confusing a criminal trial sufficiency application with civil procedure or enforcement mechanisms.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f5e3581123dd7262"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0791","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — class rights","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company has ordinary and preference shares. It proposes to vary rights attached to the preference class. The articles contain no bespoke variation procedure.\n\nWhich statutory issue is central?","options":{"A":"The variation must comply with the Companies Act procedure for class rights, including the requisite consent or class resolution and minority challenge rights.","B":"A board resolution can always vary class rights unilaterally.","C":"Class rights can never be varied.","D":"Only ordinary shareholders vote on a variation of preference rights.","E":"Variation automatically requires liquidation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where articles do not provide another compliant route, the Companies Act regulates variation of class rights, including consent thresholds and a statutory mechanism by which a qualifying minority may apply to court to cancel a variation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where articles do not provide another compliant route, the Companies Act regulates variation of class rights, including consent thresholds and a statutory mechanism by which a qualifying minority may apply to court to cancel a variation.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a board resolution can always vary class rights unilaterally. The controlling point for these facts is: Rights attached to a share class cannot ordinarily be altered merely through ordinary board management.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that class rights can never be varied. The controlling point for these facts is: Rights attached to a share class cannot ordinarily be altered merely through ordinary board management.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only ordinary shareholders vote on a variation of preference rights. The controlling point for these facts is: Rights attached to a share class cannot ordinarily be altered merely through ordinary board management.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that variation automatically requires liquidation. The controlling point for these facts is: Rights attached to a share class cannot ordinarily be altered merely through ordinary board management."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 630–634.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Rights attached to a share class cannot ordinarily be altered merely through ordinary board management.","common_trap":"Ignoring the separate statutory protection given to holders of the affected class.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ad4818775dd339fd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0792","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — dividends in specie","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solvent company with adequate distributable profits proposes to satisfy a declared dividend by transferring an asset rather than cash. Its articles permit distributions in specie.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every dividend must legally be paid in cash.","B":"A distribution in specie may be possible if authorised and the statutory distribution and valuation requirements are satisfied.","C":"A non-cash dividend is outside the statutory distribution regime.","D":"Directors may distribute any asset regardless of distributable profits.","E":"An in-specie dividend automatically transfers all company liabilities with the asset."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Company distributions can be made otherwise than in cash where corporate authority permits, but capital-maintenance rules remain relevant and the amount of the distribution must be determined under the statutory framework.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every dividend must legally be paid in cash. The controlling point for these facts is: Changing the form of a dividend does not remove it from the statutory rules governing distributions.","B":"Correct. Company distributions can be made otherwise than in cash where corporate authority permits, but capital-maintenance rules remain relevant and the amount of the distribution must be determined under the statutory framework.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a non-cash dividend is outside the statutory distribution regime. The controlling point for these facts is: Changing the form of a dividend does not remove it from the statutory rules governing distributions.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that directors may distribute any asset regardless of distributable profits. The controlling point for these facts is: Changing the form of a dividend does not remove it from the statutory rules governing distributions.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an in-specie dividend automatically transfers all company liabilities with the asset. The controlling point for these facts is: Changing the form of a dividend does not remove it from the statutory rules governing distributions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 23, including ss 829–846.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Changing the form of a dividend does not remove it from the statutory rules governing distributions.","common_trap":"Assuming an asset transfer escapes capital-maintenance requirements merely because no cash is paid.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fc07845b851b2b98"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0793","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — expulsion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three partners have no express partnership agreement authorising expulsion. Two become dissatisfied with the third and vote to expel that partner while continuing the business.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A simple majority always has a statutory power to expel any partner.","B":"The partner is automatically expelled once the others give oral notice.","C":"A majority has no implied power to expel a partner unless an express agreement confers such a power.","D":"Only a court can ever end a partnership relationship.","E":"Expulsion requires only that the majority honestly dislike the partner."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Partnership Act states that no majority of partners can expel a partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by express agreement between the partners.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a simple majority always has a statutory power to expel any partner. The controlling point for these facts is: There is no default majority-expulsion power in an ordinary partnership.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the partner is automatically expelled once the others give oral notice. The controlling point for these facts is: There is no default majority-expulsion power in an ordinary partnership.","C":"Correct. The Partnership Act states that no majority of partners can expel a partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by express agreement between the partners.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only a court can ever end a partnership relationship. The controlling point for these facts is: There is no default majority-expulsion power in an ordinary partnership.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that expulsion requires only that the majority honestly dislike the partner. The controlling point for these facts is: There is no default majority-expulsion power in an ordinary partnership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"There is no default majority-expulsion power in an ordinary partnership.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary majority decision-making extends to removing a partner from the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"99ac74e0563e56a4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0794","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — statutory demands","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company owes a creditor a presently due, undisputed debt exceeding the statutory minimum. The creditor is considering a statutory demand as part of establishing inability to pay debts for a winding-up petition.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition?","options":{"A":"A statutory demand itself immediately liquidates the company.","B":"Any disputed debt must be paid merely because a demand is served.","C":"A creditor needs a final Supreme Court judgment before serving a statutory demand.","D":"Failure to satisfy a qualifying statutory demand within the statutory period can provide evidence of inability to pay debts, subject to the current statutory requirements and any genuine dispute or other ground to resist winding up.","E":"The company has no opportunity to respond to a demand."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Insolvency Act provides statutory tests for inability to pay debts, including failure to comply with a qualifying written demand for a debt above the prescribed threshold. A genuinely disputed debt or other substantial reason may make winding-up proceedings inappropriate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a statutory demand itself immediately liquidates the company. The controlling point for these facts is: A statutory demand is evidential/procedural machinery; it is not itself a winding-up order.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any disputed debt must be paid merely because a demand is served. The controlling point for these facts is: A statutory demand is evidential/procedural machinery; it is not itself a winding-up order.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a creditor needs a final Supreme Court judgment before serving a statutory demand. The controlling point for these facts is: A statutory demand is evidential/procedural machinery; it is not itself a winding-up order.","D":"Correct. The Insolvency Act provides statutory tests for inability to pay debts, including failure to comply with a qualifying written demand for a debt above the prescribed threshold. A genuinely disputed debt or other substantial reason may make winding-up proceedings inappropriate.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the company has no opportunity to respond to a demand. The controlling point for these facts is: A statutory demand is evidential/procedural machinery; it is not itself a winding-up order."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 122–123.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A statutory demand is evidential/procedural machinery; it is not itself a winding-up order.","common_trap":"Treating service of the demand as conclusive liquidation or ignoring a genuine substantial dispute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"20162d7f2cede4eb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0795","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Tort — causation: material contribution","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant suffers an indivisible injury produced by multiple cumulative exposures, and medical evidence cannot identify which exposure alone was sufficient, but the defendant's breach materially contributed to the injury.\n\nWhich causation principle may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Material contribution to an indivisible injury can satisfy factual causation in appropriate circumstances even where the claimant cannot isolate a single sufficient exposure.","B":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The law recognises material-contribution analysis for certain cumulative causal processes.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The law recognises material-contribution analysis for certain cumulative causal processes.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bonnington Castings Ltd v Wardlaw [1956] AC 613","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The law recognises material-contribution analysis for certain cumulative causal processes.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bf70c86cbc6cd59c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0796","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Admissions — judgment on admission","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant formally admits the whole monetary claim under CPR Part 14 but does not pay the admitted amount. The claimant wants judgment without trying the admitted issue.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The claimant may seek judgment on the admission in accordance with Part 14.","B":"The admission has no procedural consequence unless repeated orally at trial.","C":"The claimant must prove the admitted issue at a full trial in every case.","D":"An admission automatically strikes out the claimant's claim.","E":"Only Part 36 can produce judgment without trial."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 14 provides procedures for admissions and enables judgment to be entered on an admission in appropriate circumstances, avoiding unnecessary adjudication of an issue no longer contested.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 14 provides procedures for admissions and enables judgment to be entered on an admission in appropriate circumstances, avoiding unnecessary adjudication of an issue no longer contested.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the admission has no procedural consequence unless repeated orally at trial. The controlling point for these facts is: A formal admission can remove the need to try the admitted liability and support judgment under the procedural rules.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant must prove the admitted issue at a full trial in every case. The controlling point for these facts is: A formal admission can remove the need to try the admitted liability and support judgment under the procedural rules.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an admission automatically strikes out the claimant's claim. The controlling point for these facts is: A formal admission can remove the need to try the admitted liability and support judgment under the procedural rules.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only Part 36 can produce judgment without trial. The controlling point for these facts is: A formal admission can remove the need to try the admitted liability and support judgment under the procedural rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 14.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A formal admission can remove the need to try the admitted liability and support judgment under the procedural rules.","common_trap":"Assuming every pleaded issue must be tried even after an effective formal admission.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4aa62d94d4897757"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0797","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — writ or warrant of control","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment creditor has an unpaid money judgment and wants enforcement officers to take control of the debtor's goods and sell them, subject to exemptions and procedural rules.\n\nWhich enforcement method is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A freezing injunction as the final method of selling goods.","B":"A writ or warrant of control, depending on the court and circumstances.","C":"A Part 36 offer.","D":"Specific performance of the original contract only.","E":"A declaration of incompatibility."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Taking control of goods is a statutory enforcement method for money judgments. The procedural route includes writs or warrants of control depending on the court and judgment, subject to the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act and CPR Part 83.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a freezing injunction as the final method of selling goods. The controlling point for these facts is: Control of goods is an execution remedy directed at the debtor's eligible goods.","B":"Correct. Taking control of goods is a statutory enforcement method for money judgments. The procedural route includes writs or warrants of control depending on the court and judgment, subject to the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act and CPR Part 83.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer. The controlling point for these facts is: Control of goods is an execution remedy directed at the debtor's eligible goods.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that specific performance of the original contract only. The controlling point for these facts is: Control of goods is an execution remedy directed at the debtor's eligible goods.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a declaration of incompatibility. The controlling point for these facts is: Control of goods is an execution remedy directed at the debtor's eligible goods."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007, Part 3; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 83.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Control of goods is an execution remedy directed at the debtor's eligible goods.","common_trap":"Confusing preservation by injunction with execution and sale to satisfy a judgment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"453e20f7783c664f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0798","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — referral fees and independence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A third-party introducer offers a solicitor a substantial payment arrangement on condition that the solicitor recommends a particular litigation strategy to referred clients, regardless of whether it suits each client's interests.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"The introducer may dictate legal strategy because it generated the client.","B":"Any referral payment overrides professional duties once disclosed.","C":"The solicitor must not allow a referral or financial arrangement to compromise independence or the duty to act in each client's best interests, and must comply with applicable transparency and statutory restrictions.","D":"Independence is irrelevant outside advocacy.","E":"A client's interests may be subordinated to the referrer's commercial preferences."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Referral and fee arrangements do not displace SRA duties of independence, integrity and acting in the client's best interests. Additional statutory restrictions can apply to referral fees in particular categories of work.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the introducer may dictate legal strategy because it generated the client. The controlling point for these facts is: Commercial introductions cannot dictate professional advice contrary to the individual client's interests.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any referral payment overrides professional duties once disclosed. The controlling point for these facts is: Commercial introductions cannot dictate professional advice contrary to the individual client's interests.","C":"Correct. Referral and fee arrangements do not displace SRA duties of independence, integrity and acting in the client's best interests. Additional statutory restrictions can apply to referral fees in particular categories of work.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that independence is irrelevant outside advocacy. The controlling point for these facts is: Commercial introductions cannot dictate professional advice contrary to the individual client's interests.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a client's interests may be subordinated to the referrer's commercial preferences. The controlling point for these facts is: Commercial introductions cannot dictate professional advice contrary to the individual client's interests."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles and Code of Conduct; Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012, ss 56–60 where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Commercial introductions cannot dictate professional advice contrary to the individual client's interests.","common_trap":"Treating disclosure of a financial arrangement as permission to surrender independent judgment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8246043bef67d315"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0799","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — publicity","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm plans an advertisement stating that it has a '100% guaranteed success rate' although it has lost several recent cases.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Advertising is outside SRA regulation.","B":"A solicitor may publish any claim if it attracts clients.","C":"Only statements made in court must be accurate.","D":"The firm must ensure publicity is accurate and not misleading; the proposed claim should not be published.","E":"The claim is acceptable if printed in small type."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires publicity concerning a solicitor's practice to be accurate and not misleading, including information about charges and services where relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that advertising is outside SRA regulation. The controlling point for these facts is: Marketing does not create an exception to duties of honesty and accurate communication.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a solicitor may publish any claim if it attracts clients. The controlling point for these facts is: Marketing does not create an exception to duties of honesty and accurate communication.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only statements made in court must be accurate. The controlling point for these facts is: Marketing does not create an exception to duties of honesty and accurate communication.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires publicity concerning a solicitor's practice to be accurate and not misleading, including information about charges and services where relevant.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claim is acceptable if printed in small type. The controlling point for these facts is: Marketing does not create an exception to duties of honesty and accurate communication."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 8.9; SRA Principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Marketing does not create an exception to duties of honesty and accurate communication.","common_trap":"Assuming promotional context permits objectively false or misleading claims.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9c8440bc2239c1b7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0800","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — invitation to treat","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shop displays an ordinary product on a shelf with a price label. A customer takes it to the till and insists that the display itself created a contract that the shop cannot refuse.\n\nWhich is the ordinary contractual analysis?","options":{"A":"Every shelf display is necessarily a binding unilateral offer.","B":"The customer accepts the shop's offer merely by entering the premises.","C":"Price labels have no legal relevance whatsoever.","D":"A contract is formed only when the customer consumes the product.","E":"The shelf display is generally an invitation to treat, with the customer making an offer to buy that the retailer may accept at the till."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Ordinary displays of goods in self-service shops are generally invitations to treat rather than offers; the customer's presentation of goods is ordinarily the offer, capable of acceptance by the retailer.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every shelf display is necessarily a binding unilateral offer. The controlling point for these facts is: The invitation-to-treat analysis preserves the retailer's ability to decide whether to accept the proposed purchase.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the customer accepts the shop's offer merely by entering the premises. The controlling point for these facts is: The invitation-to-treat analysis preserves the retailer's ability to decide whether to accept the proposed purchase.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that price labels have no legal relevance whatsoever. The controlling point for these facts is: The invitation-to-treat analysis preserves the retailer's ability to decide whether to accept the proposed purchase.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a contract is formed only when the customer consumes the product. The controlling point for these facts is: The invitation-to-treat analysis preserves the retailer's ability to decide whether to accept the proposed purchase.","E":"Correct. Ordinary displays of goods in self-service shops are generally invitations to treat rather than offers; the customer's presentation of goods is ordinarily the offer, capable of acceptance by the retailer."},"primary_legal_authority":"Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v Boots Cash Chemists [1953] 1 QB 401.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The invitation-to-treat analysis preserves the retailer's ability to decide whether to accept the proposed purchase.","common_trap":"Treating an invitation inviting customers to make offers as itself a binding offer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b804510221dea192"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0801","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — entire agreement clauses","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sophisticated commercial contract contains an entire-agreement clause. One party later relies on a pre-contract representation and the other argues that the clause automatically excludes all liability for misrepresentation.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An entire-agreement clause does not necessarily exclude misrepresentation liability; its wording and any exclusion must be construed and may be subject to statutory controls such as section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act 1967.","B":"Every entire-agreement clause automatically bars all misrepresentation claims.","C":"An entire-agreement clause is legally ineffective in every contract.","D":"Misrepresentation can be excluded orally after litigation begins.","E":"Section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act applies only to wills."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An entire-agreement clause commonly defines the contractual documents, but excluding liability for pre-contract misrepresentation requires appropriate wording. Terms excluding or restricting misrepresentation liability are subject to the statutory reasonableness regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An entire-agreement clause commonly defines the contractual documents, but excluding liability for pre-contract misrepresentation requires appropriate wording. Terms excluding or restricting misrepresentation liability are subject to the statutory reasonableness regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every entire-agreement clause automatically bars all misrepresentation claims. The controlling point for these facts is: Contractual integration and exclusion of misrepresentation liability are related but distinct questions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an entire-agreement clause is legally ineffective in every contract. The controlling point for these facts is: Contractual integration and exclusion of misrepresentation liability are related but distinct questions.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that misrepresentation can be excluded orally after litigation begins. The controlling point for these facts is: Contractual integration and exclusion of misrepresentation liability are related but distinct questions.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act applies only to wills. The controlling point for these facts is: Contractual integration and exclusion of misrepresentation liability are related but distinct questions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 3; contractual interpretation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contractual integration and exclusion of misrepresentation liability are related but distinct questions.","common_trap":"Assuming the label 'entire agreement' itself conclusively excludes statutory and common-law misrepresentation remedies.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7be1498ced78232d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0802","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Breach — anticipatory repudiation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor unequivocally tells the customer, weeks before the contractual performance date, that it will not perform. The customer treats the contract as terminated immediately.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"A breach cannot have legal effect before the contractual due date.","B":"An unequivocal anticipatory repudiation can entitle the innocent party to accept the repudiation, terminate future performance obligations and claim damages without waiting for the due date.","C":"The innocent party must always keep the contract alive until performance falls due.","D":"Anticipatory repudiation automatically rescinds the contract ab initio.","E":"Only non-payment can constitute anticipatory breach."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where one party clearly renounces future performance or disables itself from performing, the innocent party may accept that anticipatory repudiation and sue without awaiting the performance date, subject to the ordinary law of termination and damages.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a breach cannot have legal effect before the contractual due date. The controlling point for these facts is: Repudiatory intent communicated before performance falls due can accelerate the innocent party's remedial choice.","B":"Correct. Where one party clearly renounces future performance or disables itself from performing, the innocent party may accept that anticipatory repudiation and sue without awaiting the performance date, subject to the ordinary law of termination and damages.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the innocent party must always keep the contract alive until performance falls due. The controlling point for these facts is: Repudiatory intent communicated before performance falls due can accelerate the innocent party's remedial choice.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that anticipatory repudiation automatically rescinds the contract ab initio. The controlling point for these facts is: Repudiatory intent communicated before performance falls due can accelerate the innocent party's remedial choice.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only non-payment can constitute anticipatory breach. The controlling point for these facts is: Repudiatory intent communicated before performance falls due can accelerate the innocent party's remedial choice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hochster v De la Tour (1853) 2 E & B 678.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Repudiatory intent communicated before performance falls due can accelerate the innocent party's remedial choice.","common_trap":"Confusing termination for anticipatory breach with retrospective avoidance of the contract from inception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4492cb0db9cfe366"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0803","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — multiple sufficient causes","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two independent fires, each negligently started and each sufficient on its own to destroy the claimant's property, combine and destroy it. The ordinary 'but for' test cannot sensibly identify either fire as individually necessary.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition?","options":{"A":"The claimant must necessarily lose because neither fire is a but-for cause.","B":"Causation is irrelevant in negligence.","C":"The law can adapt causation analysis where multiple independently sufficient causes operate; strict mechanical use of the but-for test is not invariably decisive.","D":"Both defendants are automatically criminally liable.","E":"The claimant can recover only if one defendant admits sole causation."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Common-law causation recognises exceptional situations in which multiple sufficient causes make a simple but-for formulation inadequate. The court asks whether the defendant's wrongful conduct was legally causative under the applicable doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant must necessarily lose because neither fire is a but-for cause. The controlling point for these facts is: The but-for test is foundational but not an inflexible answer to every multiple-cause problem.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that causation is irrelevant in negligence. The controlling point for these facts is: The but-for test is foundational but not an inflexible answer to every multiple-cause problem.","C":"Correct. Common-law causation recognises exceptional situations in which multiple sufficient causes make a simple but-for formulation inadequate. The court asks whether the defendant's wrongful conduct was legally causative under the applicable doctrine.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that both defendants are automatically criminally liable. The controlling point for these facts is: The but-for test is foundational but not an inflexible answer to every multiple-cause problem.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant can recover only if one defendant admits sole causation. The controlling point for these facts is: The but-for test is foundational but not an inflexible answer to every multiple-cause problem."},"primary_legal_authority":"Performance Cars Ltd v Abraham [1962] 1 QB 33; causation principles, with multiple-cause authorities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The but-for test is foundational but not an inflexible answer to every multiple-cause problem.","common_trap":"Treating a useful causation test as though it were a universal statutory formula incapable of qualification.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f190fe5f9e589010"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0804","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance — remedies","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A continuing private nuisance substantially interferes with the claimant's use of land. Liability is established.\n\nWhich is the best general position about remedies?","options":{"A":"Damages are never available for nuisance.","B":"An injunction must be granted in every nuisance case regardless of proportionality or circumstances.","C":"Only criminal punishment is available.","D":"Depending on the circumstances, the court may award damages and may grant an injunction; injunctive relief is discretionary rather than automatic.","E":"The claimant automatically acquires the defendant's land."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Private nuisance can attract damages and equitable injunctive relief. Following modern authority, the grant and terms of an injunction are matters of judicial discretion; damages may sometimes be awarded instead.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that damages are never available for nuisance. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing nuisance does not mechanically determine a single remedy.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an injunction must be granted in every nuisance case regardless of proportionality or circumstances. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing nuisance does not mechanically determine a single remedy.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only criminal punishment is available. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing nuisance does not mechanically determine a single remedy.","D":"Correct. Private nuisance can attract damages and equitable injunctive relief. Following modern authority, the grant and terms of an injunction are matters of judicial discretion; damages may sometimes be awarded instead.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant automatically acquires the defendant's land. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing nuisance does not mechanically determine a single remedy."},"primary_legal_authority":"Coventry v Lawrence [2014] UKSC 13; Senior Courts Act 1981, s 50.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Establishing nuisance does not mechanically determine a single remedy.","common_trap":"Assuming equitable relief follows automatically from proof of the tort.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"66af8a4027e49e54"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0805","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — honest opinion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A columnist publishes a defamatory value judgment, clearly indicates the basis of the opinion, and honestly holds it. The relevant statutory conditions are otherwise satisfied.\n\nWhich defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Absolute privilege for every newspaper column.","B":"Truth, which requires the statement to be treated as pure fact.","C":"Volenti.","D":"Contributory negligence.","E":"The statutory defence of honest opinion."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 3 provides a defence where the statement is one of opinion, indicates the basis of that opinion, and an honest person could have held it on the basis of existing facts or privileged material, subject to the statutory defeat mechanism concerning the defendant's actual belief.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that absolute privilege for every newspaper column. The controlling point for these facts is: Honest opinion protects qualifying evaluative statements without requiring them to be proved factually true as opinions.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that truth, which requires the statement to be treated as pure fact. The controlling point for these facts is: Honest opinion protects qualifying evaluative statements without requiring them to be proved factually true as opinions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that volenti. The controlling point for these facts is: Honest opinion protects qualifying evaluative statements without requiring them to be proved factually true as opinions.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that contributory negligence. The controlling point for these facts is: Honest opinion protects qualifying evaluative statements without requiring them to be proved factually true as opinions.","E":"Correct. Section 3 provides a defence where the statement is one of opinion, indicates the basis of that opinion, and an honest person could have held it on the basis of existing facts or privileged material, subject to the statutory defeat mechanism concerning the defendant's actual belief."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Honest opinion protects qualifying evaluative statements without requiring them to be proved factually true as opinions.","common_trap":"Confusing an opinion defence with the separate truth defence for substantially true imputations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dd524130fa3b7b6d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0806","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Civil courts — appeals","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party loses a civil case and wants to appeal merely because it would prefer a different factual conclusion. No procedural irregularity is identified.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An appeal is not simply a retrial on demand; appellate intervention ordinarily requires an error of law or principle, a decision that is wrong, or a serious procedural or other irregularity within the applicable appellate test.","B":"Every losing party is entitled to a complete rehearing as of right.","C":"Appeal courts never review factual findings.","D":"Permission to appeal is never required in civil proceedings.","E":"An appeal automatically suspends every order."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under CPR Part 52, appeals are generally reviews rather than rehearings unless the court considers a rehearing appropriate, and an appeal is allowed where the lower decision was wrong or unjust because of serious procedural or other irregularity. Permission requirements also apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under CPR Part 52, appeals are generally reviews rather than rehearings unless the court considers a rehearing appropriate, and an appeal is allowed where the lower decision was wrong or unjust because of serious procedural or other irregularity. Permission requirements also apply.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every losing party is entitled to a complete rehearing as of right. The controlling point for these facts is: Civil appeals correct qualifying error; they are not automatic opportunities to rerun a case.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that appeal courts never review factual findings. The controlling point for these facts is: Civil appeals correct qualifying error; they are not automatic opportunities to rerun a case.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that permission to appeal is never required in civil proceedings. The controlling point for these facts is: Civil appeals correct qualifying error; they are not automatic opportunities to rerun a case.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an appeal automatically suspends every order. The controlling point for these facts is: Civil appeals correct qualifying error; they are not automatic opportunities to rerun a case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 52, especially rr 52.6 and 52.21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil appeals correct qualifying error; they are not automatic opportunities to rerun a case.","common_trap":"Treating dissatisfaction with the result as itself a ground of appeal.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"80eba863daf09362"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0807","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — remedies and discretion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant establishes that a public authority acted unlawfully and asks the Administrative Court for a quashing order.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Proof of unlawfulness always compels a quashing order.","B":"Judicial-review remedies are discretionary, and the court must apply the statutory and common-law rules governing whether and what relief should be granted.","C":"The court can award only damages in judicial review.","D":"A quashing order is issued by the defendant authority itself.","E":"Judicial-review remedies cannot affect public decisions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Even where unlawfulness is established, public-law relief is governed by remedial discretion and statutory provisions, including rules concerning outcome, timing and the modern forms and effects of quashing orders.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that proof of unlawfulness always compels a quashing order. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing a ground of review and obtaining a particular remedy are distinct stages.","B":"Correct. Even where unlawfulness is established, public-law relief is governed by remedial discretion and statutory provisions, including rules concerning outcome, timing and the modern forms and effects of quashing orders.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the court can award only damages in judicial review. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing a ground of review and obtaining a particular remedy are distinct stages.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a quashing order is issued by the defendant authority itself. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing a ground of review and obtaining a particular remedy are distinct stages.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that judicial-review remedies cannot affect public decisions. The controlling point for these facts is: Establishing a ground of review and obtaining a particular remedy are distinct stages."},"primary_legal_authority":"Senior Courts Act 1981, ss 31 and 31A; Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Establishing a ground of review and obtaining a particular remedy are distinct stages.","common_trap":"Assuming substantive unlawfulness creates an automatic entitlement to the claimant's preferred remedy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8e2bbd74c0c51342"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0808","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — victim status","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person who is not personally affected by an alleged Convention violation wants to bring Human Rights Act proceedings solely as a concerned member of the public.\n\nWhich standing requirement is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Any member of the public has automatic standing under section 7.","B":"Only public authorities can be HRA claimants.","C":"Proceedings under section 7 ordinarily require the claimant to be a 'victim' of the unlawful act within the Convention meaning.","D":"Victim status means the claimant must have suffered financial loss.","E":"Section 7 applies only to criminal defendants."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 7 restricts proceedings against a public authority under the HRA to persons who are victims of the unlawful act, using the meaning in Article 34 of the Convention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any member of the public has automatic standing under section 7. The controlling point for these facts is: HRA standing is narrower than a general public-interest action and requires Convention victim status.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only public authorities can be HRA claimants. The controlling point for these facts is: HRA standing is narrower than a general public-interest action and requires Convention victim status.","C":"Correct. Section 7 restricts proceedings against a public authority under the HRA to persons who are victims of the unlawful act, using the meaning in Article 34 of the Convention.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that victim status means the claimant must have suffered financial loss. The controlling point for these facts is: HRA standing is narrower than a general public-interest action and requires Convention victim status.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that section 7 applies only to criminal defendants. The controlling point for these facts is: HRA standing is narrower than a general public-interest action and requires Convention victim status."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 7; European Convention on Human Rights, Art 34.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA standing is narrower than a general public-interest action and requires Convention victim status.","common_trap":"Importing judicial-review sufficient-interest standing wholesale into section 7 HRA proceedings.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c570e593a903e7e6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0809","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — testamentary capacity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An elderly testator has a diagnosed mental disorder but, at the time of giving instructions and executing the will, understands the nature and effect of making a will, the broad extent of the property, and the claims of those who might expect consideration, with no disorder poisoning the dispositions.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any mental-health diagnosis automatically invalidates a will.","B":"Capacity requires perfect memory of every asset and beneficiary.","C":"Only the Court of Protection can decide whether a person may make a will.","D":"A diagnosis alone does not negate testamentary capacity; the Banks v Goodfellow criteria focus on the testator's decision-making capacity at the relevant time.","E":"A person over 80 is presumed incapable."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The classic testamentary-capacity test asks whether the testator understands the nature and effects of the act, the extent of the property, relevant claims, and is not affected by a disorder that distorts the dispositions. Diagnosis is evidence, not an automatic legal conclusion.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any mental-health diagnosis automatically invalidates a will. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal test concerns functional testamentary understanding at the relevant time.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that capacity requires perfect memory of every asset and beneficiary. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal test concerns functional testamentary understanding at the relevant time.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the Court of Protection can decide whether a person may make a will. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal test concerns functional testamentary understanding at the relevant time.","D":"Correct. The classic testamentary-capacity test asks whether the testator understands the nature and effects of the act, the extent of the property, relevant claims, and is not affected by a disorder that distorts the dispositions. Diagnosis is evidence, not an automatic legal conclusion.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a person over 80 is presumed incapable. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal test concerns functional testamentary understanding at the relevant time."},"primary_legal_authority":"Banks v Goodfellow (1870) LR 5 QB 549.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The legal test concerns functional testamentary understanding at the relevant time.","common_trap":"Equating medical diagnosis or age with automatic legal incapacity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"00ac9eaf9f085ff9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0810","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — undue influence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary persistently pressures a vulnerable testator. After the testator's death, the will is challenged on the basis that the testator's free will was overborne.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition for testamentary undue influence?","options":{"A":"Undue influence is automatically presumed whenever a child benefits under a parent's will.","B":"Any request for a legacy invalidates the will.","C":"The burden always rests on the beneficiary to prove absence of influence merely because the gift is large.","D":"Testamentary undue influence is identical to the presumed undue-influence rules for lifetime transactions.","E":"The challenger must establish coercion that overpowered the testator's volition; mere persuasion, appeals to affection or opportunity to influence are not enough."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Probate undue influence requires coercion: pressure that overbears the testator's free volition so that the will expresses another's wishes rather than the testator's. Suspicion or persuasion alone is insufficient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that undue influence is automatically presumed whenever a child benefits under a parent's will. The controlling point for these facts is: The probate doctrine focuses on coercion, and does not generally use the same presumptions as inter vivos undue influence.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any request for a legacy invalidates the will. The controlling point for these facts is: The probate doctrine focuses on coercion, and does not generally use the same presumptions as inter vivos undue influence.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the burden always rests on the beneficiary to prove absence of influence merely because the gift is large. The controlling point for these facts is: The probate doctrine focuses on coercion, and does not generally use the same presumptions as inter vivos undue influence.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that testamentary undue influence is identical to the presumed undue-influence rules for lifetime transactions. The controlling point for these facts is: The probate doctrine focuses on coercion, and does not generally use the same presumptions as inter vivos undue influence.","E":"Correct. Probate undue influence requires coercion: pressure that overbears the testator's free volition so that the will expresses another's wishes rather than the testator's. Suspicion or persuasion alone is insufficient."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wingrove v Wingrove (1885) 11 PD 81; Edwards v Edwards [2007] WTLR 1387.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The probate doctrine focuses on coercion, and does not generally use the same presumptions as inter vivos undue influence.","common_trap":"Assuming a close relationship or an unexpectedly large gift automatically reverses the burden and proves coercion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9fb1d9d130a4984a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0811","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — devastavit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A personal representative negligently distributes estate assets to residuary beneficiaries before paying a known enforceable debt, leaving insufficient assets to satisfy the creditor.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The personal representative may incur personal liability for maladministration or devastavit by improperly dissipating estate assets.","B":"A personal representative can never be personally liable when acting in that capacity.","C":"Beneficiaries always bear all administration mistakes regardless of the representative's conduct.","D":"Known creditors lose their debts once any beneficiary is paid.","E":"Devastavit is a criminal offence only."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Personal representatives must collect, preserve and properly administer estate assets and satisfy liabilities before distributing the residue. Improper distribution can expose them personally for loss caused by breach of duty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Personal representatives must collect, preserve and properly administer estate assets and satisfy liabilities before distributing the residue. Improper distribution can expose them personally for loss caused by breach of duty.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a personal representative can never be personally liable when acting in that capacity. The controlling point for these facts is: The representative's office carries duties to creditors and beneficiaries; premature distribution can create personal exposure.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that beneficiaries always bear all administration mistakes regardless of the representative's conduct. The controlling point for these facts is: The representative's office carries duties to creditors and beneficiaries; premature distribution can create personal exposure.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that known creditors lose their debts once any beneficiary is paid. The controlling point for these facts is: The representative's office carries duties to creditors and beneficiaries; premature distribution can create personal exposure.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that devastavit is a criminal offence only. The controlling point for these facts is: The representative's office carries duties to creditors and beneficiaries; premature distribution can create personal exposure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 25; personal-representative duties and devastavit principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The representative's office carries duties to creditors and beneficiaries; premature distribution can create personal exposure.","common_trap":"Assuming representative capacity itself immunises negligent or improper administration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a773c7b6563d3a76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0812","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — reconciliation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm maintains client accounts but has not compared its client-bank balances with its client ledger totals for several months.\n\nWhich is the best general requirement?","options":{"A":"Reconciliation is required only once each financial year.","B":"The firm must complete client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks and promptly investigate and resolve differences.","C":"Bank statements replace the need for reconciliation.","D":"Only the firm's external accountant may reconcile client money.","E":"Differences can remain unexplained if individually small."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The SRA Accounts Rules require client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks, comparing bank statements and cash-book balances with client-ledger totals and identifying and correcting discrepancies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that reconciliation is required only once each financial year. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","B":"Correct. The SRA Accounts Rules require client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks, comparing bank statements and cash-book balances with client-ledger totals and identifying and correcting discrepancies.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that bank statements replace the need for reconciliation. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the firm's external accountant may reconcile client money. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that differences can remain unexplained if individually small. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 8.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","common_trap":"Treating annual accounting or possession of bank statements as a substitute for frequent reconciliation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3a2ca45923c3b3fe"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0813","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — appointment of new trustees","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole individual trustee wishes to retire from an ongoing private trust, but retirement would leave no trustee. The trust instrument contains no bespoke machinery.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A trustee can always resign unilaterally by sending beneficiaries an email.","B":"Every retirement automatically terminates the trust.","C":"Statutory appointment and retirement provisions must be considered so that the trust is left with a legally sufficient trusteeship; a trustee cannot simply walk away and leave the trust without a trustee.","D":"Only the settlor can ever appoint a replacement trustee.","E":"A trust becomes ownerless property whenever a trustee retires."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act contains mechanisms for appointment and discharge of trustees. Retirement without replacement is constrained because the trust must remain capable of administration and statutory conditions governing discharge must be met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a trustee can always resign unilaterally by sending beneficiaries an email. The controlling point for these facts is: Trusteeship cannot ordinarily be abandoned informally where that would leave the trust without proper legal holders and administrators.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every retirement automatically terminates the trust. The controlling point for these facts is: Trusteeship cannot ordinarily be abandoned informally where that would leave the trust without proper legal holders and administrators.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act contains mechanisms for appointment and discharge of trustees. Retirement without replacement is constrained because the trust must remain capable of administration and statutory conditions governing discharge must be met.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the settlor can ever appoint a replacement trustee. The controlling point for these facts is: Trusteeship cannot ordinarily be abandoned informally where that would leave the trust without proper legal holders and administrators.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a trust becomes ownerless property whenever a trustee retires. The controlling point for these facts is: Trusteeship cannot ordinarily be abandoned informally where that would leave the trust without proper legal holders and administrators."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, ss 36 and 39.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trusteeship cannot ordinarily be abandoned informally where that would leave the trust without proper legal holders and administrators.","common_trap":"Confusing a wish to retire with an unrestricted unilateral power to extinguish fiduciary office.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"13a4e96638a6ce18"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0814","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Tracing — mixed bank account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee wrongfully pays £20,000 of trust money into the trustee's personal bank account, which already contains £10,000 of the trustee's own money, and later makes withdrawals.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Mixing automatically destroys every proprietary claim.","B":"The entire account automatically belongs to the trustee because the account is in the trustee's name.","C":"Beneficiaries may trace only physical banknotes.","D":"Equitable tracing rules can identify the beneficiary's value in a mixed fund and apply presumptions designed not to let a wrongdoing trustee profit from uncertainty created by the mixing.","E":"Tracing is available only after a criminal conviction."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Equity permits tracing through mixed funds and applies established presumptions to dealings by a trustee or fiduciary who has mixed trust money with personal money. The precise proprietary remedy depends on subsequent transactions and remaining assets.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that mixing automatically destroys every proprietary claim. The controlling point for these facts is: Wrongful mixing does not by itself defeat equitable identification of trust value.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the entire account automatically belongs to the trustee because the account is in the trustee's name. The controlling point for these facts is: Wrongful mixing does not by itself defeat equitable identification of trust value.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that beneficiaries may trace only physical banknotes. The controlling point for these facts is: Wrongful mixing does not by itself defeat equitable identification of trust value.","D":"Correct. Equity permits tracing through mixed funds and applies established presumptions to dealings by a trustee or fiduciary who has mixed trust money with personal money. The precise proprietary remedy depends on subsequent transactions and remaining assets.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that tracing is available only after a criminal conviction. The controlling point for these facts is: Wrongful mixing does not by itself defeat equitable identification of trust value."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Hallett's Estate (1880) 13 Ch D 696; Re Oatway [1903] 2 Ch 356; Foskett v McKeown [2001] 1 AC 102.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wrongful mixing does not by itself defeat equitable identification of trust value.","common_trap":"Assuming fungibility or the account's legal name necessarily eliminates the beneficiary's proprietary response.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a1497ff4c74952da"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0815","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — notices","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser is investigating a registered title. A third party claims the benefit of an equitable restrictive covenant capable of protection by notice.\n\nWhat is the principal function of entering a notice on the register?","options":{"A":"To transfer legal ownership of the freehold to the third party.","B":"To create the underlying covenant even if none exists.","C":"To guarantee that the interest is substantively valid.","D":"To prevent every future dealing with the title.","E":"To protect the priority of the third-party interest against a later registrable disposition for value, subject to the Land Registration Act."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A notice records the burden of an interest affecting a registered estate and can protect its priority for the purposes of section 29. Entry does not itself validate or create the underlying interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to transfer legal ownership of the freehold to the third party. The controlling point for these facts is: Registration machinery protects priority; it does not manufacture substantive rights.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to create the underlying covenant even if none exists. The controlling point for these facts is: Registration machinery protects priority; it does not manufacture substantive rights.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to guarantee that the interest is substantively valid. The controlling point for these facts is: Registration machinery protects priority; it does not manufacture substantive rights.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to prevent every future dealing with the title. The controlling point for these facts is: Registration machinery protects priority; it does not manufacture substantive rights.","E":"Correct. A notice records the burden of an interest affecting a registered estate and can protect its priority for the purposes of section 29. Entry does not itself validate or create the underlying interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 29 and 32.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Registration machinery protects priority; it does not manufacture substantive rights.","common_trap":"Confusing protection of an existing interest with creation or conclusive validation of that interest.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1fa91bdd1d960bb4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0816","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — possession","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A legal mortgage is in default. The mortgagee considers possession of the mortgaged property. The property is a dwelling house and court proceedings are contemplated.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A legal mortgagee has a right to possession subject to statutory and equitable controls, and the court has statutory powers in qualifying dwelling-house cases to adjourn, stay or suspend possession where payment can be made within a reasonable period.","B":"A mortgagee can never seek possession before the contractual term ends.","C":"Default automatically transfers beneficial ownership to the mortgagee.","D":"Courts have no power concerning possession of mortgaged homes.","E":"Possession extinguishes the mortgage debt automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The mortgagee's traditional right to possession is subject to important statutory controls. Section 36 of the Administration of Justice Act 1970 gives the court protective powers in proceedings concerning qualifying dwelling houses where the mortgagor can address sums due within a reasonable period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The mortgagee's traditional right to possession is subject to important statutory controls. Section 36 of the Administration of Justice Act 1970 gives the court protective powers in proceedings concerning qualifying dwelling houses where the mortgagor can address sums due within a reasonable period.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a mortgagee can never seek possession before the contractual term ends. The controlling point for these facts is: Mortgage possession rights are substantial but not procedurally or statutorily absolute.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that default automatically transfers beneficial ownership to the mortgagee. The controlling point for these facts is: Mortgage possession rights are substantial but not procedurally or statutorily absolute.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that courts have no power concerning possession of mortgaged homes. The controlling point for these facts is: Mortgage possession rights are substantial but not procedurally or statutorily absolute.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that possession extinguishes the mortgage debt automatically. The controlling point for these facts is: Mortgage possession rights are substantial but not procedurally or statutorily absolute."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Justice Act 1970, s 36; Administration of Justice Act 1973, s 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Mortgage possession rights are substantial but not procedurally or statutorily absolute.","common_trap":"Treating the mortgagee's proprietary right as unaffected by statutory judicial protection for homes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"137240a6159bb778"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0817","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — completion statements","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Shortly before completion, a buyer's solicitor calculates the balance needed from the buyer after taking account of the deposit, purchase price, apportionments and other agreed completion items.\n\nWhich document ordinarily communicates this calculation to the buyer?","options":{"A":"A statutory demand.","B":"A completion statement.","C":"A witness statement.","D":"A memorandum of association.","E":"A defence and counterclaim."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A conveyancing completion statement sets out the financial calculation for completion, showing the price and relevant credits, debits, apportionments, costs and funds required from or due to the client.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a statutory demand. The controlling point for these facts is: The completion statement translates the contractual and transaction figures into the cash balance needed to complete.","B":"Correct. A conveyancing completion statement sets out the financial calculation for completion, showing the price and relevant credits, debits, apportionments, costs and funds required from or due to the client.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a witness statement. The controlling point for these facts is: The completion statement translates the contractual and transaction figures into the cash balance needed to complete.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a memorandum of association. The controlling point for these facts is: The completion statement translates the contractual and transaction figures into the cash balance needed to complete.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a defence and counterclaim. The controlling point for these facts is: The completion statement translates the contractual and transaction figures into the cash balance needed to complete."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; completion accounting procedures.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The completion statement translates the contractual and transaction figures into the cash balance needed to complete.","common_trap":"Confusing a conveyancing accounting document with litigation or corporate documents.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b5fdc5e805106b7a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0818","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Leasehold — licence to assign","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease contains a qualified covenant against assignment without landlord consent. The statutory regime applies. The tenant makes a proper written application with sufficient information.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The landlord may ignore the application indefinitely.","B":"A qualified covenant gives the landlord an absolute right to refuse for any reason.","C":"The landlord is subject to statutory duties to deal with the application within a reasonable time and not unreasonably withhold consent, subject to the lease and legislation.","D":"Consent is automatically deemed granted the moment the tenant applies.","E":"The tenant may always assign without regard to the covenant."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 19(1) of the 1927 Act converts certain qualified alienation covenants, while the 1988 Act imposes duties concerning written applications, including giving consent except where reasonable to withhold it and responding within a reasonable time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the landlord may ignore the application indefinitely. The controlling point for these facts is: A qualified alienation covenant is constrained by statutory reasonableness and procedural duties.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a qualified covenant gives the landlord an absolute right to refuse for any reason. The controlling point for these facts is: A qualified alienation covenant is constrained by statutory reasonableness and procedural duties.","C":"Correct. Section 19(1) of the 1927 Act converts certain qualified alienation covenants, while the 1988 Act imposes duties concerning written applications, including giving consent except where reasonable to withhold it and responding within a reasonable time.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that consent is automatically deemed granted the moment the tenant applies. The controlling point for these facts is: A qualified alienation covenant is constrained by statutory reasonableness and procedural duties.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the tenant may always assign without regard to the covenant. The controlling point for these facts is: A qualified alienation covenant is constrained by statutory reasonableness and procedural duties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19(1); Landlord and Tenant Act 1988.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualified alienation covenant is constrained by statutory reasonableness and procedural duties.","common_trap":"Treating a qualified covenant as an absolute prohibition or assuming silence automatically grants consent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b6df234fbd4d7090"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0819","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — identification evidence: Turnbull direction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A prosecution case depends substantially on disputed visual identification by an eyewitness made in difficult conditions.\n\nWhat special trial safeguard is relevant?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"The court should apply the Turnbull approach, warning the jury of the special need for caution and examining the quality of the identification evidence."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Disputed identification evidence attracts specific judicial safeguards because honest witnesses can be mistaken.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. Disputed identification evidence attracts specific judicial safeguards because honest witnesses can be mistaken."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Turnbull [1977] QB 224","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disputed identification evidence attracts specific judicial safeguards because honest witnesses can be mistaken.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8ce7193449c22da6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0820","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — totality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is being sentenced at the same hearing for several offences. Individual sentences that would be proportionate viewed separately would produce an excessive aggregate punishment if simply added consecutively.\n\nWhich sentencing principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The rule against hearsay.","B":"The postal rule.","C":"Overreaching.","D":"Res ipsa loquitur.","E":"The totality principle, requiring the overall sentence to be just and proportionate to the offending as a whole."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"When sentencing multiple offences, the court must consider the totality of the sentence so that the overall punishment is just and proportionate, deciding appropriately between concurrent and consecutive terms and adjusting the aggregate where necessary.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the rule against hearsay. The controlling point for these facts is: Sentencing is concerned with the proportionality of the overall package, not merely isolated arithmetic for each count.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the postal rule. The controlling point for these facts is: Sentencing is concerned with the proportionality of the overall package, not merely isolated arithmetic for each count.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that overreaching. The controlling point for these facts is: Sentencing is concerned with the proportionality of the overall package, not merely isolated arithmetic for each count.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that res ipsa loquitur. The controlling point for these facts is: Sentencing is concerned with the proportionality of the overall package, not merely isolated arithmetic for each count.","E":"Correct. When sentencing multiple offences, the court must consider the totality of the sentence so that the overall punishment is just and proportionate, deciding appropriately between concurrent and consecutive terms and adjusting the aggregate where necessary."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Act 2020; Sentencing Council, Totality guideline.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Sentencing is concerned with the proportionality of the overall package, not merely isolated arithmetic for each count.","common_trap":"Adding individual sentences mechanically without reviewing the resulting overall punishment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3202808fa226fa16"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0821","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — shareholder meetings: quorum","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company's articles adopt the statutory model position and contain no bespoke quorum rule. Two qualifying members are present at a general meeting.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The meeting will ordinarily satisfy the statutory default quorum of two qualifying persons, subject to the special rule for a single-member company.","B":"A general meeting always requires every shareholder to attend.","C":"One director alone is necessarily the quorum even if not a member.","D":"A quorum can never be prescribed by statute or articles.","E":"At least five members must attend every private-company meeting."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act provides a default quorum of two qualifying persons for a company meeting, while a single-member company has a specific one-person rule; articles may make relevant provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act provides a default quorum of two qualifying persons for a company meeting, while a single-member company has a specific one-person rule; articles may make relevant provision.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a general meeting always requires every shareholder to attend. The controlling point for these facts is: General-meeting validity requires the applicable quorum, which is not the same as unanimity of shareholders.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that one director alone is necessarily the quorum even if not a member. The controlling point for these facts is: General-meeting validity requires the applicable quorum, which is not the same as unanimity of shareholders.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a quorum can never be prescribed by statute or articles. The controlling point for these facts is: General-meeting validity requires the applicable quorum, which is not the same as unanimity of shareholders.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that at least five members must attend every private-company meeting. The controlling point for these facts is: General-meeting validity requires the applicable quorum, which is not the same as unanimity of shareholders."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 318.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"General-meeting validity requires the applicable quorum, which is not the same as unanimity of shareholders.","common_trap":"Confusing the quorum needed to transact business with the voting threshold needed to pass a resolution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e85579092634dd61"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0822","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — removal of director","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Members of a company want to remove a director before expiry of the director's term. The articles do not eliminate the statutory removal mechanism.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A director can be removed only by special resolution.","B":"The company may remove the director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, subject to special notice and the director's statutory rights to representations and to be heard.","C":"A written resolution is always the required method for removal.","D":"Only the board may remove a director.","E":"A director cannot be removed before the end of a fixed term."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 168 permits removal by ordinary resolution notwithstanding contrary agreement, while sections 169 and 312 provide procedural protections including special notice and representations. The statutory removal resolution cannot be passed as a private-company written resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a director can be removed only by special resolution. The controlling point for these facts is: Removal uses a specific statutory procedure rather than an ordinary written-resolution shortcut.","B":"Correct. Section 168 permits removal by ordinary resolution notwithstanding contrary agreement, while sections 169 and 312 provide procedural protections including special notice and representations. The statutory removal resolution cannot be passed as a private-company written resolution.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a written resolution is always the required method for removal. The controlling point for these facts is: Removal uses a specific statutory procedure rather than an ordinary written-resolution shortcut.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the board may remove a director. The controlling point for these facts is: Removal uses a specific statutory procedure rather than an ordinary written-resolution shortcut.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a director cannot be removed before the end of a fixed term. The controlling point for these facts is: Removal uses a specific statutory procedure rather than an ordinary written-resolution shortcut."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168–169, 288(2)(a) and 312.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Removal uses a specific statutory procedure rather than an ordinary written-resolution shortcut.","common_trap":"Focusing only on the voting percentage and overlooking special notice, meeting procedure and the director's protections.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"190efe9b48d8f25e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0823","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — remuneration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An ordinary partnership has no agreement about remuneration. One partner spends substantially more time managing the firm and demands a salary in addition to the partner's share of profits.\n\nWhich default rule ordinarily applies?","options":{"A":"The hardest-working partner automatically receives a market salary.","B":"Every partner is legally an employee of the firm.","C":"A partner is not entitled to remuneration for acting in the partnership business unless the partners have agreed otherwise, subject to statutory exceptions such as winding up after death.","D":"Partners must be paid according to hours worked.","E":"Only the partner contributing the most capital may receive remuneration."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 24 supplies the default that a partner is not entitled to remuneration for acting in the partnership business, absent agreement, with a statutory qualification concerning surviving partners winding up after a death.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the hardest-working partner automatically receives a market salary. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit entitlement and remuneration for working in the business are distinct matters under the default rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every partner is legally an employee of the firm. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit entitlement and remuneration for working in the business are distinct matters under the default rules.","C":"Correct. Section 24 supplies the default that a partner is not entitled to remuneration for acting in the partnership business, absent agreement, with a statutory qualification concerning surviving partners winding up after a death.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that partners must be paid according to hours worked. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit entitlement and remuneration for working in the business are distinct matters under the default rules.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the partner contributing the most capital may receive remuneration. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit entitlement and remuneration for working in the business are distinct matters under the default rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 24(6).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Profit entitlement and remuneration for working in the business are distinct matters under the default rules.","common_trap":"Assuming greater effort automatically creates an employment-style salary entitlement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"abc402af1581a4dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0824","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — floating charge avoidance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company grants a floating charge shortly before entering insolvency to secure an existing unsecured debt. The charge holder is not connected with the company and gives no new money or other fresh value at the time.\n\nWhich statutory issue is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Every floating charge created before insolvency is automatically valid.","B":"Section 245 applies only to fixed charges.","C":"An existing unsecured debt always counts as fresh money advanced at the time of the charge.","D":"The floating charge may be invalid under section 245 to the extent prescribed if created within the relevant time without qualifying new consideration.","E":"A liquidator has no power to examine pre-insolvency security."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 245 can invalidate a floating charge created within the relevant period except to the extent of qualifying new money, goods, services, discharge of debt or other value supplied at or after creation, plus permitted interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every floating charge created before insolvency is automatically valid. The controlling point for these facts is: Late security for old debt is specifically vulnerable under the statutory floating-charge rule.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that section 245 applies only to fixed charges. The controlling point for these facts is: Late security for old debt is specifically vulnerable under the statutory floating-charge rule.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an existing unsecured debt always counts as fresh money advanced at the time of the charge. The controlling point for these facts is: Late security for old debt is specifically vulnerable under the statutory floating-charge rule.","D":"Correct. Section 245 can invalidate a floating charge created within the relevant period except to the extent of qualifying new money, goods, services, discharge of debt or other value supplied at or after creation, plus permitted interest.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a liquidator has no power to examine pre-insolvency security. The controlling point for these facts is: Late security for old debt is specifically vulnerable under the statutory floating-charge rule."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 245.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Late security for old debt is specifically vulnerable under the statutory floating-charge rule.","common_trap":"Treating security for pre-existing indebtedness as though it necessarily constituted fresh statutory value.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"93d80c88260eb517"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0825","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Case management — allocation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After statements of case and directions questionnaires, the court must decide the appropriate procedural track for a defended civil claim.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The claimant has an absolute right to choose the track.","B":"Every claim above the small-claims limit must use the same track regardless of complexity.","C":"The defendant alone determines allocation.","D":"Track allocation is fixed solely by the amount pleaded and the court cannot consider other factors.","E":"The court allocates using the CPR criteria, including value, remedy, complexity, parties and other relevant circumstances; value alone is not invariably decisive."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 26 governs allocation. Although financial value is important and statutory/CPR thresholds matter, the court considers the nature of the remedy, complexity, number of parties, evidence, importance and parties' views and circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant has an absolute right to choose the track. The controlling point for these facts is: Allocation is a judicial case-management decision applying multiple prescribed factors.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every claim above the small-claims limit must use the same track regardless of complexity. The controlling point for these facts is: Allocation is a judicial case-management decision applying multiple prescribed factors.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defendant alone determines allocation. The controlling point for these facts is: Allocation is a judicial case-management decision applying multiple prescribed factors.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that track allocation is fixed solely by the amount pleaded and the court cannot consider other factors. The controlling point for these facts is: Allocation is a judicial case-management decision applying multiple prescribed factors.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 26 governs allocation. Although financial value is important and statutory/CPR thresholds matter, the court considers the nature of the remedy, complexity, number of parties, evidence, importance and parties' views and circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 26.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Allocation is a judicial case-management decision applying multiple prescribed factors.","common_trap":"Reducing the allocation exercise to a single monetary figure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9c4b9171699ae5d9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0826","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — witness statements","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A witness statement is prepared for trial in civil proceedings. The witness can read English and approves the statement.\n\nWhich formal requirement is ordinarily essential?","options":{"A":"The witness statement should be verified by the prescribed statement of truth.","B":"The witness must swear every civil witness statement before a High Court judge.","C":"A witness statement must never be signed by the witness.","D":"Statements of truth are used only for expert reports.","E":"No verification is required if a solicitor drafted the statement."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 32 and the relevant Practice Direction require witness statements intended for use in proceedings to be verified by a statement of truth in the prescribed form.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 32 and the relevant Practice Direction require witness statements intended for use in proceedings to be verified by a statement of truth in the prescribed form.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the witness must swear every civil witness statement before a High Court judge. The controlling point for these facts is: The statement of truth formally confirms the witness's belief in the truth of the facts stated.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a witness statement must never be signed by the witness. The controlling point for these facts is: The statement of truth formally confirms the witness's belief in the truth of the facts stated.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that statements of truth are used only for expert reports. The controlling point for these facts is: The statement of truth formally confirms the witness's belief in the truth of the facts stated.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no verification is required if a solicitor drafted the statement. The controlling point for these facts is: The statement of truth formally confirms the witness's belief in the truth of the facts stated."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 32.8; Practice Direction 32; CPR Part 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statement of truth formally confirms the witness's belief in the truth of the facts stated.","common_trap":"Assuming solicitor involvement replaces the witness's own formal verification.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bc6506f788e4f15b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0827","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — security for costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A corporate claimant appears unable to pay the defendant's costs if ordered to do so. The defendant seeks protection while the litigation continues.\n\nWhich procedural remedy may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Default judgment against the claimant automatically.","B":"An application for security for costs under CPR Part 25, if a specified ground and the court's discretionary requirements are satisfied.","C":"A charging order before any judgment.","D":"A Part 36 offer made by the court.","E":"Automatic dismissal whenever a claimant has limited resources."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR Part 25 permits a defendant to seek security for costs where specified conditions are met, including certain cases involving companies unable to pay an adverse costs order, subject to the court's discretion and access-to-justice considerations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that default judgment against the claimant automatically. The controlling point for these facts is: Security protects against the risk of an unrecoverable future costs order without itself deciding the merits.","B":"Correct. CPR Part 25 permits a defendant to seek security for costs where specified conditions are met, including certain cases involving companies unable to pay an adverse costs order, subject to the court's discretion and access-to-justice considerations.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a charging order before any judgment. The controlling point for these facts is: Security protects against the risk of an unrecoverable future costs order without itself deciding the merits.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer made by the court. The controlling point for these facts is: Security protects against the risk of an unrecoverable future costs order without itself deciding the merits.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that automatic dismissal whenever a claimant has limited resources. The controlling point for these facts is: Security protects against the risk of an unrecoverable future costs order without itself deciding the merits."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 25.26–25.27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Security protects against the risk of an unrecoverable future costs order without itself deciding the merits.","common_trap":"Treating financial weakness as automatic defeat rather than a ground feeding a discretionary security application.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"36701c9a4c22a308"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0828","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — reporting serious breaches","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor becomes aware of credible facts indicating a serious breach of regulatory arrangements by another regulated person. The matter is sufficiently serious to engage the SRA reporting obligation.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The solicitor must never report another regulated person.","B":"Reporting is optional whenever the individuals work at different firms.","C":"The solicitor must make the required prompt report to the SRA, subject to applicable legal and professional restrictions such as privilege.","D":"Only clients may report regulatory breaches.","E":"A report may be postponed indefinitely until civil proceedings end."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires prompt reporting to the SRA of facts or matters reasonably believed capable of amounting to a serious breach, while separate rules govern cooperation and legally protected information.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor must never report another regulated person. The controlling point for these facts is: Regulatory reporting duties can arise independently of whether the conduct occurred within the solicitor's own firm.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that reporting is optional whenever the individuals work at different firms. The controlling point for these facts is: Regulatory reporting duties can arise independently of whether the conduct occurred within the solicitor's own firm.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires prompt reporting to the SRA of facts or matters reasonably believed capable of amounting to a serious breach, while separate rules govern cooperation and legally protected information.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only clients may report regulatory breaches. The controlling point for these facts is: Regulatory reporting duties can arise independently of whether the conduct occurred within the solicitor's own firm.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a report may be postponed indefinitely until civil proceedings end. The controlling point for these facts is: Regulatory reporting duties can arise independently of whether the conduct occurred within the solicitor's own firm."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 7.7–7.8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regulatory reporting duties can arise independently of whether the conduct occurred within the solicitor's own firm.","common_trap":"Assuming professional collegiality or unrelated litigation removes an express regulatory reporting obligation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"45a2a9be175cf3d8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0829","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client care — authority to act","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client authorises a solicitor to negotiate settlement but expressly states that no settlement may be concluded without the client's further approval. The opponent makes an attractive offer.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may accept any offer considered objectively reasonable regardless of instructions.","B":"A retainer always gives a solicitor unlimited authority to compromise litigation.","C":"The opponent's offer itself expands the solicitor's authority.","D":"The solicitor may advise on the offer but must obtain the client's authority before binding the client to settlement.","E":"The solicitor should conceal the offer and decide alone."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A solicitor must act on the client's instructions and within the authority conferred by the retainer. Strategic advice is the solicitor's role, but the client retains decision-making authority over settlement unless valid authority has been granted.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor may accept any offer considered objectively reasonable regardless of instructions. The controlling point for these facts is: A solicitor cannot exceed an express limitation on authority merely because the proposed outcome seems advantageous.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a retainer always gives a solicitor unlimited authority to compromise litigation. The controlling point for these facts is: A solicitor cannot exceed an express limitation on authority merely because the proposed outcome seems advantageous.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the opponent's offer itself expands the solicitor's authority. The controlling point for these facts is: A solicitor cannot exceed an express limitation on authority merely because the proposed outcome seems advantageous.","D":"Correct. A solicitor must act on the client's instructions and within the authority conferred by the retainer. Strategic advice is the solicitor's role, but the client retains decision-making authority over settlement unless valid authority has been granted.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor should conceal the offer and decide alone. The controlling point for these facts is: A solicitor cannot exceed an express limitation on authority merely because the proposed outcome seems advantageous."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, including paras 3.1 and 3.3; law of authority.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor cannot exceed an express limitation on authority merely because the proposed outcome seems advantageous.","common_trap":"Confusing professional advice and conduct of litigation with ownership of the client's substantive settlement decision.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3326869135aba62a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0830","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — part-payment of debt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A debtor owes an undisputed £10,000 immediately due. The creditor simply promises to accept £7,000 paid on the same date by the same method in full satisfaction, with no additional consideration or other legally relevant factor.\n\nWhich traditional consideration rule is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Any part-payment automatically discharges the whole debt.","B":"A creditor's oral promise can never have any legal relevance.","C":"Payment of money cannot constitute consideration in any contract.","D":"The debt is discharged only if the debtor becomes insolvent.","E":"Part-payment of an undisputed debt ordinarily does not, without fresh consideration or another recognised basis, discharge the creditor's claim to the balance."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The rule in Pinnel's Case, affirmed in Foakes v Beer, is that payment of a lesser sum on the due date is not ordinarily good consideration for a promise to forgo the balance, though exceptions and doctrines such as promissory estoppel may matter on different facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any part-payment automatically discharges the whole debt. The controlling point for these facts is: A bare reduction in the amount of an undisputed due debt ordinarily needs some additional legal basis to make the creditor's promise binding.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a creditor's oral promise can never have any legal relevance. The controlling point for these facts is: A bare reduction in the amount of an undisputed due debt ordinarily needs some additional legal basis to make the creditor's promise binding.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that payment of money cannot constitute consideration in any contract. The controlling point for these facts is: A bare reduction in the amount of an undisputed due debt ordinarily needs some additional legal basis to make the creditor's promise binding.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the debt is discharged only if the debtor becomes insolvent. The controlling point for these facts is: A bare reduction in the amount of an undisputed due debt ordinarily needs some additional legal basis to make the creditor's promise binding.","E":"Correct. The rule in Pinnel's Case, affirmed in Foakes v Beer, is that payment of a lesser sum on the due date is not ordinarily good consideration for a promise to forgo the balance, though exceptions and doctrines such as promissory estoppel may matter on different facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Pinnel's Case (1602) 5 Co Rep 117a; Foakes v Beer (1884) 9 App Cas 605.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A bare reduction in the amount of an undisputed due debt ordinarily needs some additional legal basis to make the creditor's promise binding.","common_trap":"Assuming the creditor's unsupported promise alone necessarily extinguishes the balance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e25832be345807c3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0831","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Third-party rights — variation and rescission","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract expressly gives an identified third party an enforceable benefit under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. The third party has communicated assent to the term. The original parties later seek to remove the benefit without the third party's consent, and the contract contains no contrary variation provision.\n\nWhich issue is central?","options":{"A":"The Act can restrict the contracting parties' ability to rescind or vary the third party's right after the statutory protection conditions, including assent, are met.","B":"Third-party rights can always be removed freely because the third party supplied no consideration.","C":"The third party automatically becomes a contracting party for all purposes.","D":"The 1999 Act prohibits every variation of a contract containing a third-party term.","E":"Assent is irrelevant under the statutory variation regime."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 2 limits rescission or variation of a third-party right in specified circumstances, including where the third party has assented, subject to contractual provisions and the court's statutory powers.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 2 limits rescission or variation of a third-party right in specified circumstances, including where the third party has assented, subject to contractual provisions and the court's statutory powers.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that third-party rights can always be removed freely because the third party supplied no consideration. The controlling point for these facts is: An enforceable third-party right can acquire protection against unilateral removal by the original parties.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the third party automatically becomes a contracting party for all purposes. The controlling point for these facts is: An enforceable third-party right can acquire protection against unilateral removal by the original parties.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the 1999 Act prohibits every variation of a contract containing a third-party term. The controlling point for these facts is: An enforceable third-party right can acquire protection against unilateral removal by the original parties.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that assent is irrelevant under the statutory variation regime. The controlling point for these facts is: An enforceable third-party right can acquire protection against unilateral removal by the original parties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, ss 1–2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An enforceable third-party right can acquire protection against unilateral removal by the original parties.","common_trap":"Focusing only on creation of the right under section 1 and overlooking the separate statutory protection against later variation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0b81046ccf71d8d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0832","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — loss of bargain","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier repudiates a profitable contract. The customer validly terminates and can prove the net profit it would have earned had the contract been performed.\n\nWhat is the ordinary compensatory aim of contractual damages?","options":{"A":"To punish every contract breaker regardless of loss.","B":"So far as money can do it, to place the innocent party in the position it would have occupied had the contract been performed, subject to causation, remoteness, mitigation and proof.","C":"To put the claimant in a better position than full performance would have produced.","D":"To confiscate all profits earned by the defendant in every case.","E":"To compensate only physical injury."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The orthodox expectation measure seeks to protect the claimant's performance interest by placing it, so far as damages can, in the position it would have occupied if the contract had been performed, subject to limiting doctrines.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to punish every contract breaker regardless of loss. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract damages are principally compensatory and commonly protect the expectation or bargain.","B":"Correct. The orthodox expectation measure seeks to protect the claimant's performance interest by placing it, so far as damages can, in the position it would have occupied if the contract had been performed, subject to limiting doctrines.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to put the claimant in a better position than full performance would have produced. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract damages are principally compensatory and commonly protect the expectation or bargain.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to confiscate all profits earned by the defendant in every case. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract damages are principally compensatory and commonly protect the expectation or bargain.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that to compensate only physical injury. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract damages are principally compensatory and commonly protect the expectation or bargain."},"primary_legal_authority":"Robinson v Harman (1848) 1 Ex 850; contractual damages principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract damages are principally compensatory and commonly protect the expectation or bargain.","common_trap":"Treating ordinary damages as punitive or as a route to overcompensation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d90351add0f65aa1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0833","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Psychiatric injury — secondary victims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant who was not personally endangered witnesses the immediate aftermath of a negligently caused accident involving a close relative and develops a recognised psychiatric illness.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Foreseeability of any physical injury to anyone is by itself sufficient.","B":"A recognised psychiatric illness is never compensable for a secondary victim.","C":"As a secondary victim, the claimant must satisfy the special control mechanisms governing psychiatric injury, including the relevant relationship, proximity and perception requirements.","D":"Secondary victims are treated identically to primary victims.","E":"Distress alone automatically establishes actionable psychiatric injury."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Secondary-victim claims are subject to restrictive control mechanisms developed in cases such as Alcock, including close ties of love and affection, proximity in time and space, direct perception and the relevant shock requirements, alongside foreseeability of psychiatric injury.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that foreseeability of any physical injury to anyone is by itself sufficient. The controlling point for these facts is: The law distinguishes secondary witnesses from those directly involved in the danger.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a recognised psychiatric illness is never compensable for a secondary victim. The controlling point for these facts is: The law distinguishes secondary witnesses from those directly involved in the danger.","C":"Correct. Secondary-victim claims are subject to restrictive control mechanisms developed in cases such as Alcock, including close ties of love and affection, proximity in time and space, direct perception and the relevant shock requirements, alongside foreseeability of psychiatric injury.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that secondary victims are treated identically to primary victims. The controlling point for these facts is: The law distinguishes secondary witnesses from those directly involved in the danger.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that distress alone automatically establishes actionable psychiatric injury. The controlling point for these facts is: The law distinguishes secondary witnesses from those directly involved in the danger."},"primary_legal_authority":"Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire Police [1992] 1 AC 310; Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust [2024] UKSC 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The law distinguishes secondary witnesses from those directly involved in the danger.","common_trap":"Applying the more permissive primary-victim approach or treating ordinary grief and distress as sufficient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d94721c1f5f044a2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0834","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — contributory negligence and children","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A child claimant is injured partly because of the defendant's negligence. The defendant alleges contributory negligence by the child.\n\nWhich standard is most appropriate when assessing the child's own care?","options":{"A":"A child can never be contributorily negligent.","B":"The adult reasonable-person standard applies without adjustment in every case.","C":"The defendant chooses the standard.","D":"The court takes account of the standard reasonably expected of a child of the claimant's age and circumstances rather than mechanically applying an adult standard.","E":"Any careless act by a child eliminates the claim entirely."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Contributory negligence can apply to children, but the assessment reflects the care reasonably expected from a child of the relevant age and circumstances. Any reduction must also be just and equitable under the statute.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a child can never be contributorily negligent. The controlling point for these facts is: The claimant's age informs the objective assessment of contributory fault.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the adult reasonable-person standard applies without adjustment in every case. The controlling point for these facts is: The claimant's age informs the objective assessment of contributory fault.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defendant chooses the standard. The controlling point for these facts is: The claimant's age informs the objective assessment of contributory fault.","D":"Correct. Contributory negligence can apply to children, but the assessment reflects the care reasonably expected from a child of the relevant age and circumstances. Any reduction must also be just and equitable under the statute.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any careless act by a child eliminates the claim entirely. The controlling point for these facts is: The claimant's age informs the objective assessment of contributory fault."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, s 1; Gough v Thorne [1966] 1 WLR 1387.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The claimant's age informs the objective assessment of contributory fault.","common_trap":"Assuming either complete immunity for children or an unmodified adult standard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b1c441b8478d1f32"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0835","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — warnings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier knows of a non-obvious danger to lawful visitors and places a warning sign nearby. A visitor is injured and the occupier argues that the sign automatically eliminates liability.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any warning sign automatically excludes liability.","B":"Warnings are legally irrelevant under the 1957 Act.","C":"An occupier must eliminate every risk rather than warn.","D":"A warning works only if the visitor signs it.","E":"A warning discharges the occupier's duty only if, in all the circumstances, it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 2(4)(a) provides that a warning does not, by itself, absolve an occupier unless in all the circumstances it was sufficient to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any warning sign automatically excludes liability. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal question is the adequacy of the warning in context, not merely its existence.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that warnings are legally irrelevant under the 1957 Act. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal question is the adequacy of the warning in context, not merely its existence.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an occupier must eliminate every risk rather than warn. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal question is the adequacy of the warning in context, not merely its existence.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a warning works only if the visitor signs it. The controlling point for these facts is: The legal question is the adequacy of the warning in context, not merely its existence.","E":"Correct. Section 2(4)(a) provides that a warning does not, by itself, absolve an occupier unless in all the circumstances it was sufficient to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(4)(a).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The legal question is the adequacy of the warning in context, not merely its existence.","common_trap":"Treating a warning as an automatic exclusion clause regardless of visibility, specificity or the danger.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e1958582565a709"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0836","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — bias","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A decision-maker has a relationship with one party that creates an appearance of partiality, although actual bias cannot be proved.\n\nWhich test is ordinarily applied to apparent bias?","options":{"A":"Whether the fair-minded and informed observer, having considered the facts, would conclude that there was a real possibility of bias.","B":"Whether the claimant personally suspects bias, however unreasonable the suspicion.","C":"Whether actual corruption is proved beyond reasonable doubt.","D":"Whether the decision-maker says subjectively that they felt impartial.","E":"Whether the decision was financially disadvantageous to the claimant."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The modern apparent-bias test asks whether a fair-minded and informed observer, having considered the facts, would conclude that there was a real possibility that the tribunal was biased.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The modern apparent-bias test asks whether a fair-minded and informed observer, having considered the facts, would conclude that there was a real possibility that the tribunal was biased.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that whether the claimant personally suspects bias, however unreasonable the suspicion. The controlling point for these facts is: Public-law fairness protects both actual impartiality and objectively justified confidence in impartial decision-making.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that whether actual corruption is proved beyond reasonable doubt. The controlling point for these facts is: Public-law fairness protects both actual impartiality and objectively justified confidence in impartial decision-making.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that whether the decision-maker says subjectively that they felt impartial. The controlling point for these facts is: Public-law fairness protects both actual impartiality and objectively justified confidence in impartial decision-making.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that whether the decision was financially disadvantageous to the claimant. The controlling point for these facts is: Public-law fairness protects both actual impartiality and objectively justified confidence in impartial decision-making."},"primary_legal_authority":"Porter v Magill [2001] UKHL 67; common-law procedural fairness.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public-law fairness protects both actual impartiality and objectively justified confidence in impartial decision-making.","common_trap":"Using the claimant's subjective suspicion or requiring proof of actual bias.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"43fac085f386fa0b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0837","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — damages","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant establishes that a public authority unlawfully violated a Convention right and asks for damages under the Human Rights Act.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every HRA breach automatically produces punitive damages.","B":"Damages are not automatic; the court may award them where necessary to afford just satisfaction, applying section 8 and relevant Convention principles.","C":"Damages are never available under the HRA.","D":"The claimant receives a fixed statutory sum for each breach.","E":"Only Parliament may award compensation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 8 permits relief within the court's powers and allows damages only where, taking account of all circumstances including other relief, an award is necessary to afford just satisfaction. Convention principles inform the assessment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every HRA breach automatically produces punitive damages. The controlling point for these facts is: A proven breach does not create an automatic common-law-style damages entitlement.","B":"Correct. Section 8 permits relief within the court's powers and allows damages only where, taking account of all circumstances including other relief, an award is necessary to afford just satisfaction. Convention principles inform the assessment.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that damages are never available under the HRA. The controlling point for these facts is: A proven breach does not create an automatic common-law-style damages entitlement.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant receives a fixed statutory sum for each breach. The controlling point for these facts is: A proven breach does not create an automatic common-law-style damages entitlement.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only Parliament may award compensation. The controlling point for these facts is: A proven breach does not create an automatic common-law-style damages entitlement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A proven breach does not create an automatic common-law-style damages entitlement.","common_trap":"Assuming every rights violation necessarily translates into a monetary award.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a8b5de4988cbcc5f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0838","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — parliamentary control","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An enabling Act authorises a minister to make regulations subject to the negative resolution procedure.\n\nWhich is the best general description?","options":{"A":"The instrument cannot take effect until approved by a referendum.","B":"Negative procedure means courts cannot review the instrument.","C":"The instrument can generally become or remain law without an affirmative vote but is subject to annulment in accordance with the prescribed parliamentary procedure.","D":"The minister may legislate outside the enabling Act.","E":"Every negative instrument requires prior approval by both Houses."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the negative procedure, a statutory instrument is generally made and can take effect without an affirmative parliamentary vote, but may be annulled if the prescribed parliamentary motion is carried within the applicable period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the instrument cannot take effect until approved by a referendum. The controlling point for these facts is: Negative resolution is a form of legislative scrutiny distinct from affirmative approval.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that negative procedure means courts cannot review the instrument. The controlling point for these facts is: Negative resolution is a form of legislative scrutiny distinct from affirmative approval.","C":"Correct. Under the negative procedure, a statutory instrument is generally made and can take effect without an affirmative parliamentary vote, but may be annulled if the prescribed parliamentary motion is carried within the applicable period.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the minister may legislate outside the enabling Act. The controlling point for these facts is: Negative resolution is a form of legislative scrutiny distinct from affirmative approval.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every negative instrument requires prior approval by both Houses. The controlling point for these facts is: Negative resolution is a form of legislative scrutiny distinct from affirmative approval."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory Instruments Act 1946; relevant enabling legislation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Negative resolution is a form of legislative scrutiny distinct from affirmative approval.","common_trap":"Confusing negative and affirmative procedures or treating parliamentary procedure as eliminating judicial ultra vires review.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a1da78c9a9ca0aab"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0839","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — rectification","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professionally drafted will contains a clerical error that causes the text to fail to carry out the testator's instructions. The statutory time and evidential requirements can be satisfied.\n\nWhich remedy may be available?","options":{"A":"The executors may rewrite the will themselves after death.","B":"Rectification is never available for wills.","C":"The beneficiary may alter the original document without court involvement.","D":"The court may rectify the will under section 20 of the Administration of Justice Act 1982 where the statutory grounds are established.","E":"The will is automatically revoked by the drafting error."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 20 empowers the court to rectify a will that fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a clerical error or failure to understand instructions, subject to statutory requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the executors may rewrite the will themselves after death. The controlling point for these facts is: Rectification corrects qualifying drafting failures so the will reflects the testator's intentions.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that rectification is never available for wills. The controlling point for these facts is: Rectification corrects qualifying drafting failures so the will reflects the testator's intentions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the beneficiary may alter the original document without court involvement. The controlling point for these facts is: Rectification corrects qualifying drafting failures so the will reflects the testator's intentions.","D":"Correct. Section 20 empowers the court to rectify a will that fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a clerical error or failure to understand instructions, subject to statutory requirements.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the will is automatically revoked by the drafting error. The controlling point for these facts is: Rectification corrects qualifying drafting failures so the will reflects the testator's intentions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Justice Act 1982, s 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Rectification corrects qualifying drafting failures so the will reflects the testator's intentions.","common_trap":"Confusing judicial rectification with unilateral post-death rewriting by executors or beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8f98b4ae798aa779"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0840","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — disclaimer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary under a will does not want to accept a legacy and has not yet accepted any benefit from it.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A beneficiary must accept every testamentary gift.","B":"Disclaimer allows the beneficiary to nominate a substitute recipient as of right.","C":"A disclaimer is the same as an assignment after acceptance.","D":"The executor becomes beneficial owner of every disclaimed gift.","E":"The beneficiary may generally disclaim the gift, with the property then devolving according to the will and applicable succession rules rather than being redirected by the disclaiming beneficiary."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A beneficiary can generally disclaim a testamentary gift before acceptance. A disclaimer rejects the entitlement rather than transferring it, so the destination of the property follows the will and succession rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a beneficiary must accept every testamentary gift. The controlling point for these facts is: Disclaimer is a refusal of the gift, not a power to redesign the deceased's dispositive scheme.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that disclaimer allows the beneficiary to nominate a substitute recipient as of right. The controlling point for these facts is: Disclaimer is a refusal of the gift, not a power to redesign the deceased's dispositive scheme.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a disclaimer is the same as an assignment after acceptance. The controlling point for these facts is: Disclaimer is a refusal of the gift, not a power to redesign the deceased's dispositive scheme.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the executor becomes beneficial owner of every disclaimed gift. The controlling point for these facts is: Disclaimer is a refusal of the gift, not a power to redesign the deceased's dispositive scheme.","E":"Correct. A beneficiary can generally disclaim a testamentary gift before acceptance. A disclaimer rejects the entitlement rather than transferring it, so the destination of the property follows the will and succession rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Succession and disclaimer principles; Administration of Estates practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disclaimer is a refusal of the gift, not a power to redesign the deceased's dispositive scheme.","common_trap":"Treating disclaimer as though it were a beneficiary-controlled transfer to a chosen third party.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"258d69b56ba66543"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0841","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — normal expenditure out of income","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes regular lifetime gifts from surplus income. After the gifts, the individual can maintain the usual standard of living from remaining income.\n\nWhich IHT exemption may be relevant if the statutory conditions are proved?","options":{"A":"The normal-expenditure-out-of-income exemption.","B":"The spouse exemption regardless of recipient.","C":"Business relief automatically.","D":"The annual exemption only, because no other lifetime exemption exists.","E":"Agricultural relief regardless of the property gifted."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 21 can exempt transfers forming part of the transferor's normal expenditure, made out of income, where after allowing for them the transferor retains sufficient income to maintain the usual standard of living.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 21 can exempt transfers forming part of the transferor's normal expenditure, made out of income, where after allowing for them the transferor retains sufficient income to maintain the usual standard of living.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the spouse exemption regardless of recipient. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular gifting from genuine surplus income can qualify separately from the annual exemption if the statutory pattern and affordability tests are met.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that business relief automatically. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular gifting from genuine surplus income can qualify separately from the annual exemption if the statutory pattern and affordability tests are met.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the annual exemption only, because no other lifetime exemption exists. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular gifting from genuine surplus income can qualify separately from the annual exemption if the statutory pattern and affordability tests are met.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that agricultural relief regardless of the property gifted. The controlling point for these facts is: Regular gifting from genuine surplus income can qualify separately from the annual exemption if the statutory pattern and affordability tests are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regular gifting from genuine surplus income can qualify separately from the annual exemption if the statutory pattern and affordability tests are met.","common_trap":"Assuming all lifetime gifting must rely exclusively on the annual exemption.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"652ff407597b0271"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0842","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — residual balances","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After a matter ends, a small amount of client money remains because repeated reasonable attempts to locate the former client have failed.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The firm may automatically transfer any unclaimed client balance to profit on file closure.","B":"The firm must deal with the residual balance under the SRA Accounts Rules and applicable SRA guidance rather than simply treating it as the firm's money.","C":"Residual client money ceases to be client money after 30 days.","D":"The fee earner may personally keep the balance.","E":"No records of attempts to return the money are needed."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it. Where the rightful owner cannot be located, firms must follow the Accounts Rules and SRA requirements/guidance governing residual balances, including appropriate records and, where relevant, prescribed charitable disposal arrangements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the firm may automatically transfer any unclaimed client balance to profit on file closure. The controlling point for these facts is: Difficulty locating the owner does not convert client money into business money.","B":"Correct. Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it. Where the rightful owner cannot be located, firms must follow the Accounts Rules and SRA requirements/guidance governing residual balances, including appropriate records and, where relevant, prescribed charitable disposal arrangements.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that residual client money ceases to be client money after 30 days. The controlling point for these facts is: Difficulty locating the owner does not convert client money into business money.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the fee earner may personally keep the balance. The controlling point for these facts is: Difficulty locating the owner does not convert client money into business money.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no records of attempts to return the money are needed. The controlling point for these facts is: Difficulty locating the owner does not convert client money into business money."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, especially r 2.5; SRA guidance on residual client balances.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Difficulty locating the owner does not convert client money into business money.","common_trap":"Assuming passage of time or file closure transfers beneficial ownership to the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"43d322d4fe2fc399"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0843","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — duty to consider investment advice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees with a substantial investment portfolio propose a major change in investments. No statutory exception makes advice unnecessary.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Trustees must always invest without professional advice.","B":"Advice is relevant only after an investment loses money.","C":"When exercising the statutory investment power, trustees must obtain and consider proper advice unless they reasonably conclude that it is unnecessary or inappropriate in the circumstances.","D":"Beneficiaries alone select all investments.","E":"Trustees may ignore the statutory investment criteria if the trust is large."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act requires trustees, when exercising investment powers, to have regard to the standard investment criteria and ordinarily obtain and consider proper advice, subject to the statutory exception.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trustees must always invest without professional advice. The controlling point for these facts is: Investment decision-making is a continuing fiduciary process involving suitability, diversification and appropriate advice.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that advice is relevant only after an investment loses money. The controlling point for these facts is: Investment decision-making is a continuing fiduciary process involving suitability, diversification and appropriate advice.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act requires trustees, when exercising investment powers, to have regard to the standard investment criteria and ordinarily obtain and consider proper advice, subject to the statutory exception.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that beneficiaries alone select all investments. The controlling point for these facts is: Investment decision-making is a continuing fiduciary process involving suitability, diversification and appropriate advice.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trustees may ignore the statutory investment criteria if the trust is large. The controlling point for these facts is: Investment decision-making is a continuing fiduciary process involving suitability, diversification and appropriate advice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 3–5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Investment decision-making is a continuing fiduciary process involving suitability, diversification and appropriate advice.","common_trap":"Treating the general investment power as an unrestricted personal discretion detached from statutory process.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"02d236ceb21b98d9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0844","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Breach of trust — bona fide purchaser","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee wrongfully transfers trust property to a purchaser who gives value, acts in good faith and has no notice of the beneficiary's equitable interest.\n\nWhich equitable principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Every recipient of trust property is automatically a constructive trustee.","B":"Equitable interests always bind every purchaser regardless of notice and registration rules.","C":"Giving value is irrelevant in equity.","D":"A bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice can take free of the prior equitable interest, defeating proprietary enforcement against that asset in the purchaser's hands.","E":"The beneficiary automatically becomes legal owner of the purchaser's other assets."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Equity's bona fide purchaser defence protects a purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice of the prior equitable interest. Other personal or proprietary remedies may remain against trustees or other recipients depending on the facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every recipient of trust property is automatically a constructive trustee. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence marks a limit on the persistence of equitable proprietary rights against innocent purchasers.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that equitable interests always bind every purchaser regardless of notice and registration rules. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence marks a limit on the persistence of equitable proprietary rights against innocent purchasers.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that giving value is irrelevant in equity. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence marks a limit on the persistence of equitable proprietary rights against innocent purchasers.","D":"Correct. Equity's bona fide purchaser defence protects a purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice of the prior equitable interest. Other personal or proprietary remedies may remain against trustees or other recipients depending on the facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the beneficiary automatically becomes legal owner of the purchaser's other assets. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence marks a limit on the persistence of equitable proprietary rights against innocent purchasers."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bona fide purchaser doctrine; Pilcher v Rawlins (1872) LR 7 Ch App 259.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defence marks a limit on the persistence of equitable proprietary rights against innocent purchasers.","common_trap":"Assuming trust property can always be recovered from every downstream recipient regardless of purchaser status.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"559a0d8cf88140db"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0845","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — forfeiture relief","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landlord validly forfeits a lease for breach. The tenant promptly seeks to preserve the lease and is willing to remedy the breach and meet appropriate conditions.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A forfeited lease can never be restored.","B":"Relief from forfeiture is available only to freeholders.","C":"The tenant automatically acquires the freehold after forfeiture.","D":"Courts have no equitable or statutory jurisdiction concerning forfeiture.","E":"The tenant may be able to seek relief from forfeiture; forfeiture does not necessarily make termination irreversible in every case."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Courts possess equitable and statutory jurisdiction to grant relief from forfeiture in appropriate circumstances, commonly on terms designed to remedy the breach and compensate the landlord, with specific statutory rules applying to different breaches.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a forfeited lease can never be restored. The controlling point for these facts is: Forfeiture is a security-like remedy and may be relieved against where the legal conditions and discretion permit.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that relief from forfeiture is available only to freeholders. The controlling point for these facts is: Forfeiture is a security-like remedy and may be relieved against where the legal conditions and discretion permit.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the tenant automatically acquires the freehold after forfeiture. The controlling point for these facts is: Forfeiture is a security-like remedy and may be relieved against where the legal conditions and discretion permit.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that courts have no equitable or statutory jurisdiction concerning forfeiture. The controlling point for these facts is: Forfeiture is a security-like remedy and may be relieved against where the legal conditions and discretion permit.","E":"Correct. Courts possess equitable and statutory jurisdiction to grant relief from forfeiture in appropriate circumstances, commonly on terms designed to remedy the breach and compensate the landlord, with specific statutory rules applying to different breaches."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 146(2); equitable relief from forfeiture.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Forfeiture is a security-like remedy and may be relieved against where the legal conditions and discretion permit.","common_trap":"Assuming a valid act of forfeiture necessarily forecloses all remedial jurisdiction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7a22d318cfa6f693"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0846","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Covenants — benefit of restrictive covenant","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A successor to the original covenantee seeks to enforce a restrictive covenant against a successor to the covenantor. The covenant genuinely benefits identifiable land retained by the original covenantee.\n\nWhich issue is central to enforcement by the successor claimant?","options":{"A":"Whether the benefit of the covenant has passed to the claimant with the benefited land by annexation, assignment or an applicable scheme, alongside the requirements for the burden to bind the defendant.","B":"The claimant must always have been an original party to the covenant.","C":"The benefit of a restrictive covenant can never pass with land.","D":"Only positive covenants can run in equity.","E":"Ownership of benefited land is always irrelevant."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For a successor claimant to enforce, the benefit of a restrictive covenant must have passed through a recognised route such as annexation, assignment or building scheme, while separate requirements govern whether the burden binds the defendant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For a successor claimant to enforce, the benefit of a restrictive covenant must have passed through a recognised route such as annexation, assignment or building scheme, while separate requirements govern whether the burden binds the defendant.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant must always have been an original party to the covenant. The controlling point for these facts is: Passing of benefit and passing of burden are distinct elements in successor enforcement.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the benefit of a restrictive covenant can never pass with land. The controlling point for these facts is: Passing of benefit and passing of burden are distinct elements in successor enforcement.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only positive covenants can run in equity. The controlling point for these facts is: Passing of benefit and passing of burden are distinct elements in successor enforcement.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that ownership of benefited land is always irrelevant. The controlling point for these facts is: Passing of benefit and passing of burden are distinct elements in successor enforcement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 78; Federated Homes Ltd v Mill Lodge Properties Ltd [1980] 1 WLR 594.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Passing of benefit and passing of burden are distinct elements in successor enforcement.","common_trap":"Focusing only on the defendant's burden while assuming any later owner can enforce regardless of entitlement to the benefit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"304681fc11ff051f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0847","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — bankruptcy search","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual buyer is financing a purchase with a mortgage. Shortly before completion, the lender's solicitor wants to check whether the buyer is subject to relevant bankruptcy entries.\n\nWhich pre-completion search is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 search against the seller is the only search ever required.","B":"A bankruptcy search against the borrower, commonly using the Land Charges Department procedure required by the lender.","C":"A local authority search reveals all personal bankruptcy entries.","D":"An environmental search substitutes for insolvency checks.","E":"No lender needs insolvency information about an individual borrower."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Mortgage lenders ordinarily require a bankruptcy search against individual borrowers immediately before completion because bankruptcy can affect the borrower's capacity, title and the lender's security. The search is distinct from title priority searches.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an OS1 search against the seller is the only search ever required. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal insolvency checks and registered-title priority searches perform different functions in mortgage conveyancing.","B":"Correct. Mortgage lenders ordinarily require a bankruptcy search against individual borrowers immediately before completion because bankruptcy can affect the borrower's capacity, title and the lender's security. The search is distinct from title priority searches.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a local authority search reveals all personal bankruptcy entries. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal insolvency checks and registered-title priority searches perform different functions in mortgage conveyancing.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an environmental search substitutes for insolvency checks. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal insolvency checks and registered-title priority searches perform different functions in mortgage conveyancing.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no lender needs insolvency information about an individual borrower. The controlling point for these facts is: Personal insolvency checks and registered-title priority searches perform different functions in mortgage conveyancing."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Charges Act 1972 and conveyancing/lender requirements; Land Charges Department bankruptcy searches.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Personal insolvency checks and registered-title priority searches perform different functions in mortgage conveyancing.","common_trap":"Assuming an OS1 title search also performs the personal bankruptcy search against the borrower.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d83772217eaab561"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0848","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial property — VAT option to tax","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller of commercial property has validly opted to tax the property for VAT purposes. No exemption or transfer-of-going-concern treatment applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An option to tax converts the property into residential land.","B":"VAT can never apply to commercial-property transactions.","C":"The option to tax can cause an otherwise exempt supply of the relevant property to become taxable for VAT, subject to the statutory regime.","D":"The buyer's SDLT liability automatically disappears whenever VAT is charged.","E":"An option to tax is simply a Land Registry restriction."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the VAT legislation, many land transactions are exempt, but an effective option to tax can make supplies of the opted property taxable, subject to detailed exceptions and anti-avoidance rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an option to tax converts the property into residential land. The controlling point for these facts is: VAT treatment of commercial land can be altered by a valid option to tax and must be investigated transactionally.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that vAT can never apply to commercial-property transactions. The controlling point for these facts is: VAT treatment of commercial land can be altered by a valid option to tax and must be investigated transactionally.","C":"Correct. Under the VAT legislation, many land transactions are exempt, but an effective option to tax can make supplies of the opted property taxable, subject to detailed exceptions and anti-avoidance rules.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the buyer's SDLT liability automatically disappears whenever VAT is charged. The controlling point for these facts is: VAT treatment of commercial land can be altered by a valid option to tax and must be investigated transactionally.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an option to tax is simply a Land Registry restriction. The controlling point for these facts is: VAT treatment of commercial land can be altered by a valid option to tax and must be investigated transactionally."},"primary_legal_authority":"Value Added Tax Act 1994, Sch 10; HMRC option-to-tax regime.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"VAT treatment of commercial land can be altered by a valid option to tax and must be investigated transactionally.","common_trap":"Confusing VAT treatment with land-registration machinery or assuming one transaction tax eliminates another.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9a3b4d27d4422ad2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0849","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Homicide — loss of control","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally kills V. D relies on loss of control and produces sufficient evidence to raise the statutory defence.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Loss of control is a complete acquittal defence to every homicide.","B":"The defence requires D's loss of control to be sudden.","C":"Any anger automatically reduces murder to manslaughter.","D":"If the statutory requirements are satisfied, loss of control is a partial defence to murder, reducing liability to manslaughter; once sufficiently raised, the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt.","E":"The defence applies to attempted murder in exactly the same statutory way."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Sections 54–55 replace provocation with a partial defence requiring loss of self-control, a qualifying trigger and the objective statutory comparison, subject to exclusions. The loss need not be sudden. Once sufficient evidence is adduced, the prosecution bears the burden of disproving the defence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that loss of control is a complete acquittal defence to every homicide. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence is carefully defined and, if successful, changes murder to manslaughter rather than producing acquittal.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defence requires D's loss of control to be sudden. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence is carefully defined and, if successful, changes murder to manslaughter rather than producing acquittal.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any anger automatically reduces murder to manslaughter. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence is carefully defined and, if successful, changes murder to manslaughter rather than producing acquittal.","D":"Correct. Sections 54–55 replace provocation with a partial defence requiring loss of self-control, a qualifying trigger and the objective statutory comparison, subject to exclusions. The loss need not be sudden. Once sufficient evidence is adduced, the prosecution bears the burden of disproving the defence.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defence applies to attempted murder in exactly the same statutory way. The controlling point for these facts is: The defence is carefully defined and, if successful, changes murder to manslaughter rather than producing acquittal."},"primary_legal_authority":"Coroners and Justice Act 2009, ss 54–56.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defence is carefully defined and, if successful, changes murder to manslaughter rather than producing acquittal.","common_trap":"Importing the abolished suddenness requirement or treating ordinary anger as a qualifying trigger by itself.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"35175da99de58f7d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0850","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal appeals — Magistrates' Court to Crown Court","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant convicted after a not-guilty plea in the Magistrates' Court wishes to challenge conviction and sentence through the ordinary appeal route to the Crown Court.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An appeal from magistrates after trial can only go directly to the Supreme Court.","B":"The Crown Court can review only points of law and never rehear evidence.","C":"A defendant has no appeal from the Magistrates' Court.","D":"Every such appeal requires permission from the trial magistrates.","E":"The defendant generally has a statutory right to appeal conviction and/or sentence to the Crown Court, where the appeal is ordinarily by way of rehearing."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A person convicted by a magistrates' court generally has a statutory appeal to the Crown Court against conviction and sentence in the circumstances prescribed by legislation. The Crown Court appeal is ordinarily a rehearing before a judge with magistrates.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an appeal from magistrates after trial can only go directly to the Supreme Court. The controlling point for these facts is: The ordinary Crown Court route differs from case stated or judicial review, which address different kinds of challenge.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the Crown Court can review only points of law and never rehear evidence. The controlling point for these facts is: The ordinary Crown Court route differs from case stated or judicial review, which address different kinds of challenge.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a defendant has no appeal from the Magistrates' Court. The controlling point for these facts is: The ordinary Crown Court route differs from case stated or judicial review, which address different kinds of challenge.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every such appeal requires permission from the trial magistrates. The controlling point for these facts is: The ordinary Crown Court route differs from case stated or judicial review, which address different kinds of challenge.","E":"Correct. A person convicted by a magistrates' court generally has a statutory appeal to the Crown Court against conviction and sentence in the circumstances prescribed by legislation. The Crown Court appeal is ordinarily a rehearing before a judge with magistrates."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980, s 108; criminal appeal procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The ordinary Crown Court route differs from case stated or judicial review, which address different kinds of challenge.","common_trap":"Confusing an appeal by rehearing with appellate routes confined to legal error.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a8f4cf5bb4a51288"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0851","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — members' power to require general meeting","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Members of a company holding the statutory minimum voting rights want the directors to call a general meeting to consider resolutions. Their request satisfies the statutory content requirements.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Qualifying members can require the directors to call a general meeting under the Companies Act, and statutory timing rules then govern the directors' response.","B":"Only directors can ever initiate a general meeting.","C":"Members need unanimous shareholder support to require a meeting.","D":"The request automatically passes the proposed resolutions.","E":"A court order is always required before members may request a meeting."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act gives members meeting the statutory threshold power to require directors to call a general meeting, with detailed requirements governing the request, directors' duty to call and the timetable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act gives members meeting the statutory threshold power to require directors to call a general meeting, with detailed requirements governing the request, directors' duty to call and the timetable.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only directors can ever initiate a general meeting. The controlling point for these facts is: Minority shareholders meeting the statutory threshold can trigger the meeting machinery without controlling the board.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that members need unanimous shareholder support to require a meeting. The controlling point for these facts is: Minority shareholders meeting the statutory threshold can trigger the meeting machinery without controlling the board.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the request automatically passes the proposed resolutions. The controlling point for these facts is: Minority shareholders meeting the statutory threshold can trigger the meeting machinery without controlling the board.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a court order is always required before members may request a meeting. The controlling point for these facts is: Minority shareholders meeting the statutory threshold can trigger the meeting machinery without controlling the board."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 303–305.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Minority shareholders meeting the statutory threshold can trigger the meeting machinery without controlling the board.","common_trap":"Confusing the power to require a meeting with the separate power to pass the resolutions proposed at that meeting.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"663253c441aea679"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0852","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — pre-emption disapplication","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes a cash allotment of equity securities but wants to issue them without first offering them pro rata to existing shareholders. The statutory pre-emption regime applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Directors may ignore statutory pre-emption whenever they consider the issue commercially useful.","B":"The company must identify a valid statutory route to disapply or exclude the pre-emption rights, normally involving the required shareholder authority or resolution under the Companies Act.","C":"Pre-emption rights apply only after shares have already been allotted.","D":"Cash allotments are never subject to statutory pre-emption.","E":"An ordinary board minute automatically amends the Companies Act."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act gives existing holders statutory pre-emption rights on relevant cash allotments and provides mechanisms by which those rights may be disapplied or excluded in prescribed circumstances and with the required member authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that directors may ignore statutory pre-emption whenever they consider the issue commercially useful. The controlling point for these facts is: Statutory pre-emption cannot simply be bypassed by directors; the applicable disapplication machinery must be used.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act gives existing holders statutory pre-emption rights on relevant cash allotments and provides mechanisms by which those rights may be disapplied or excluded in prescribed circumstances and with the required member authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that pre-emption rights apply only after shares have already been allotted. The controlling point for these facts is: Statutory pre-emption cannot simply be bypassed by directors; the applicable disapplication machinery must be used.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that cash allotments are never subject to statutory pre-emption. The controlling point for these facts is: Statutory pre-emption cannot simply be bypassed by directors; the applicable disapplication machinery must be used.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an ordinary board minute automatically amends the Companies Act. The controlling point for these facts is: Statutory pre-emption cannot simply be bypassed by directors; the applicable disapplication machinery must be used."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 561–577.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory pre-emption cannot simply be bypassed by directors; the applicable disapplication machinery must be used.","common_trap":"Assuming directors' allotment authority is identical to authority to disapply pre-emption rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0e2b67baebf8e91e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0853","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — dissolution by illegality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A partnership's only business becomes unlawful because new legislation prohibits the activity.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The partnership must continue until every partner signs a deed.","B":"Illegality affects only future profits and never the partnership's existence.","C":"The partnership is dissolved where an event makes it unlawful for the firm's business to be carried on or for the partners to carry it on in partnership.","D":"Only bankruptcy can dissolve a partnership automatically.","E":"The partners may lawfully continue because the business was legal when formed."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Partnership Act provides for dissolution where an event occurs that makes it unlawful for the business of the firm to be carried on or for the members to carry it on in partnership.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the partnership must continue until every partner signs a deed. The controlling point for these facts is: A partnership cannot continue its business relationship on a basis that has become unlawful.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that illegality affects only future profits and never the partnership's existence. The controlling point for these facts is: A partnership cannot continue its business relationship on a basis that has become unlawful.","C":"Correct. The Partnership Act provides for dissolution where an event occurs that makes it unlawful for the business of the firm to be carried on or for the members to carry it on in partnership.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only bankruptcy can dissolve a partnership automatically. The controlling point for these facts is: A partnership cannot continue its business relationship on a basis that has become unlawful.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the partners may lawfully continue because the business was legal when formed. The controlling point for these facts is: A partnership cannot continue its business relationship on a basis that has become unlawful."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 34.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A partnership cannot continue its business relationship on a basis that has become unlawful.","common_trap":"Assuming the legality of the business at formation immunises the partnership from later legal change.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8867b7fa2b614eaf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0854","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — set-off","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company enters liquidation owing £80,000 to a supplier, while the supplier separately owes the company £30,000 under mutual pre-liquidation dealings. The relevant insolvency set-off conditions are met.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The two debts must always be enforced separately in full.","B":"Set-off is unavailable in insolvency.","C":"The supplier automatically receives priority for the full £80,000.","D":"Insolvency set-off requires the mutual dealings to be accounted for so that only the balance is provable or payable, subject to the statutory rules.","E":"The liquidator may disregard the company's £30,000 claim as a gift."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Insolvency set-off operates mandatorily where the statutory conditions of mutuality are met, requiring an account of mutual credits, debts and dealings and leaving the balance due from one side to the other.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the two debts must always be enforced separately in full. The controlling point for these facts is: The insolvency regime nets qualifying mutual obligations rather than treating them as wholly independent claims.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that set-off is unavailable in insolvency. The controlling point for these facts is: The insolvency regime nets qualifying mutual obligations rather than treating them as wholly independent claims.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the supplier automatically receives priority for the full £80,000. The controlling point for these facts is: The insolvency regime nets qualifying mutual obligations rather than treating them as wholly independent claims.","D":"Correct. Insolvency set-off operates mandatorily where the statutory conditions of mutuality are met, requiring an account of mutual credits, debts and dealings and leaving the balance due from one side to the other.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the liquidator may disregard the company's £30,000 claim as a gift. The controlling point for these facts is: The insolvency regime nets qualifying mutual obligations rather than treating them as wholly independent claims."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Rules 2016, r 14.25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The insolvency regime nets qualifying mutual obligations rather than treating them as wholly independent claims.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary separate enforcement continues unchanged after insolvency despite the mandatory set-off regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"025d7e0f13b1403c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0855","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — requests for further information","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defence contains a vague allegation central to the dispute. The claimant needs clarification before preparing evidence.\n\nWhich CPR mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A third-party debt order.","B":"A Part 36 offer.","C":"An application for probate.","D":"A writ of control.","E":"A request for further information or clarification under CPR Part 18, subject to proportionality and the court's powers."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 18 permits the court to order, and parties to request, clarification of matters in dispute or additional information where necessary and proportionate for resolving the proceedings.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a third-party debt order. The controlling point for these facts is: Part 18 is designed to clarify pleaded cases and obtain information about disputed matters, not to enforce judgments.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a Part 36 offer. The controlling point for these facts is: Part 18 is designed to clarify pleaded cases and obtain information about disputed matters, not to enforce judgments.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an application for probate. The controlling point for these facts is: Part 18 is designed to clarify pleaded cases and obtain information about disputed matters, not to enforce judgments.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a writ of control. The controlling point for these facts is: Part 18 is designed to clarify pleaded cases and obtain information about disputed matters, not to enforce judgments.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 18 permits the court to order, and parties to request, clarification of matters in dispute or additional information where necessary and proportionate for resolving the proceedings."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 18; Practice Direction 18.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Part 18 is designed to clarify pleaded cases and obtain information about disputed matters, not to enforce judgments.","common_trap":"Confusing clarification of the opponent's case with disclosure of documents or enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f7c61b191ba93e8a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0856","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — privilege and inadvertent inspection","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During disclosure, a privileged legal-advice email is accidentally provided for inspection. The receiving solicitor immediately realises from its contents that disclosure was probably inadvertent.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The receiving party may use the inadvertently disclosed privileged document only with the court's permission, and professional duties also require careful handling of the obvious mistake.","B":"Accidental disclosure always destroys privilege conclusively and permits unrestricted use.","C":"The receiving solicitor must publish the email to preserve it as evidence.","D":"Privilege never applies to written legal advice.","E":"The document automatically becomes the receiving party's property."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 31.20 provides that where a privileged document has been inadvertently permitted to be inspected, the inspecting party may use it or its contents only with the court's permission. Professional obligations can additionally constrain taking advantage of an obvious error.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 31.20 provides that where a privileged document has been inadvertently permitted to be inspected, the inspecting party may use it or its contents only with the court's permission. Professional obligations can additionally constrain taking advantage of an obvious error.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that accidental disclosure always destroys privilege conclusively and permits unrestricted use. The controlling point for these facts is: Inadvertent inspection does not automatically entitle the opponent to exploit privileged material.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the receiving solicitor must publish the email to preserve it as evidence. The controlling point for these facts is: Inadvertent inspection does not automatically entitle the opponent to exploit privileged material.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that privilege never applies to written legal advice. The controlling point for these facts is: Inadvertent inspection does not automatically entitle the opponent to exploit privileged material.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the document automatically becomes the receiving party's property. The controlling point for these facts is: Inadvertent inspection does not automatically entitle the opponent to exploit privileged material."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 31.20; professional conduct principles concerning inadvertently disclosed material.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Inadvertent inspection does not automatically entitle the opponent to exploit privileged material.","common_trap":"Assuming any accidental disclosure irrevocably waives privilege for all purposes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa881c60f3a7e11b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0857","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — qualified one-way costs shifting","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant brings a personal injury claim to which the ordinary QOCS regime applies. The claimant loses after trial. No exception or special enforcement circumstance is established.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The defendant can always enforce all costs against all of the claimant's assets.","B":"A costs order may be made against the claimant, but enforcement is restricted by the QOCS rules rather than ordinary costs enforcement applying without limitation.","C":"No costs order can ever be made against a personal-injury claimant.","D":"QOCS determines liability on the underlying tort claim.","E":"QOCS applies to every commercial contract dispute."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR Part 44 provides qualified one-way costs shifting for specified personal injury proceedings. The regime primarily restricts enforcement of costs orders against claimants, subject to the detailed rules and exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defendant can always enforce all costs against all of the claimant's assets. The controlling point for these facts is: QOCS is an enforcement protection, not a rule that prevents every adverse costs order from being made.","B":"Correct. CPR Part 44 provides qualified one-way costs shifting for specified personal injury proceedings. The regime primarily restricts enforcement of costs orders against claimants, subject to the detailed rules and exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no costs order can ever be made against a personal-injury claimant. The controlling point for these facts is: QOCS is an enforcement protection, not a rule that prevents every adverse costs order from being made.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that qOCS determines liability on the underlying tort claim. The controlling point for these facts is: QOCS is an enforcement protection, not a rule that prevents every adverse costs order from being made.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that qOCS applies to every commercial contract dispute. The controlling point for these facts is: QOCS is an enforcement protection, not a rule that prevents every adverse costs order from being made."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 44.13–44.17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"QOCS is an enforcement protection, not a rule that prevents every adverse costs order from being made.","common_trap":"Confusing the existence of a costs order with the separate limits on enforcing it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"265d713fba9df6e9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0858","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — client identification","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor receives instructions through an intermediary who says they speak for an elderly property owner. The solicitor has never communicated with the owner and has reason to doubt whether the owner actually authorised the transaction.\n\nWhich is the best professional response?","options":{"A":"The intermediary's assertion must be accepted without enquiry.","B":"A solicitor never needs to identify the actual client.","C":"The solicitor should establish who the client is and obtain satisfactory evidence of the client's instructions and authority before proceeding.","D":"Authority is irrelevant if the transaction appears profitable.","E":"The solicitor should sign documents in the owner's name to save time."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A solicitor must identify the client, understand who is giving instructions and be satisfied that instructions reflect the client's wishes and proper authority, alongside applicable AML and capacity considerations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the intermediary's assertion must be accepted without enquiry. The controlling point for these facts is: The retainer and authority to act cannot safely be inferred from an intermediary where circumstances create doubt.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a solicitor never needs to identify the actual client. The controlling point for these facts is: The retainer and authority to act cannot safely be inferred from an intermediary where circumstances create doubt.","C":"Correct. A solicitor must identify the client, understand who is giving instructions and be satisfied that instructions reflect the client's wishes and proper authority, alongside applicable AML and capacity considerations.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that authority is irrelevant if the transaction appears profitable. The controlling point for these facts is: The retainer and authority to act cannot safely be inferred from an intermediary where circumstances create doubt.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor should sign documents in the owner's name to save time. The controlling point for these facts is: The retainer and authority to act cannot safely be inferred from an intermediary where circumstances create doubt."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 3.1–3.3; Money Laundering Regulations 2017 where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The retainer and authority to act cannot safely be inferred from an intermediary where circumstances create doubt.","common_trap":"Treating convenience or apparent commercial benefit as a substitute for verifying the client's identity and authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e9c2eeee44ba2ff0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0859","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — vulnerable clients","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client has difficulty understanding complex written advice because of a communication disability but has capacity to make the relevant decisions.\n\nWhich is the best professional approach?","options":{"A":"The solicitor should automatically treat the client as lacking capacity.","B":"The solicitor should take instructions from a relative instead without authority.","C":"The disability permits the solicitor to make substantive decisions for the client.","D":"The solicitor should make reasonable adjustments and communicate in a way the client can understand, while continuing to take instructions from the client.","E":"The solicitor should terminate the retainer solely because communication takes longer."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Solicitors must provide competent service, take account of clients' attributes, needs and circumstances, and comply with equality duties. Communication difficulty does not itself establish lack of decision-making capacity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor should automatically treat the client as lacking capacity. The controlling point for these facts is: Accessible communication supports the client's own autonomous instructions rather than replacing them.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor should take instructions from a relative instead without authority. The controlling point for these facts is: Accessible communication supports the client's own autonomous instructions rather than replacing them.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the disability permits the solicitor to make substantive decisions for the client. The controlling point for these facts is: Accessible communication supports the client's own autonomous instructions rather than replacing them.","D":"Correct. Solicitors must provide competent service, take account of clients' attributes, needs and circumstances, and comply with equality duties. Communication difficulty does not itself establish lack of decision-making capacity.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the solicitor should terminate the retainer solely because communication takes longer. The controlling point for these facts is: Accessible communication supports the client's own autonomous instructions rather than replacing them."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 3.2–3.4; Equality Act 2010; Mental Capacity Act 2005 principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Accessible communication supports the client's own autonomous instructions rather than replacing them.","common_trap":"Equating disability or communication needs with legal incapacity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"691bceee41682b7a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0860","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — acceptance by conduct","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier sends contractual terms offering to provide services if the recipient begins using a specified platform. The recipient knows the terms and starts using the platform in the manner contemplated.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Acceptance is valid only if expressed orally.","B":"Conduct can never amount to acceptance.","C":"A signed deed is required for every service contract.","D":"The recipient's subjective uncommunicated intention is always decisive.","E":"Acceptance may be inferred from conduct where the conduct objectively manifests assent to the offer."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Contractual acceptance can be communicated by conduct where, objectively assessed, the offeree's actions correspond to the mode of acceptance contemplated by the offer and demonstrate assent.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that acceptance is valid only if expressed orally. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract formation looks objectively at words and conduct, not solely at formal signatures or undisclosed intention.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that conduct can never amount to acceptance. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract formation looks objectively at words and conduct, not solely at formal signatures or undisclosed intention.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a signed deed is required for every service contract. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract formation looks objectively at words and conduct, not solely at formal signatures or undisclosed intention.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the recipient's subjective uncommunicated intention is always decisive. The controlling point for these facts is: Contract formation looks objectively at words and conduct, not solely at formal signatures or undisclosed intention.","E":"Correct. Contractual acceptance can be communicated by conduct where, objectively assessed, the offeree's actions correspond to the mode of acceptance contemplated by the offer and demonstrate assent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Brogden v Metropolitan Railway Co (1877) 2 App Cas 666; contract-formation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract formation looks objectively at words and conduct, not solely at formal signatures or undisclosed intention.","common_trap":"Assuming every contract requires express verbal or written acceptance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"afefc5df29f619fc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0861","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — implied terms in fact","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A detailed commercial contract is silent on a point. One party asks the court to imply a term merely because the proposed term would make the bargain fairer.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A term is not implied in fact merely because it seems fair; implication requires the stringent necessity/obviousness criteria and consistency with the express contract.","B":"Courts may rewrite every contract to reach a fair result.","C":"Any commercially useful term must be implied.","D":"Terms in fact are implied whenever one party later regrets the bargain.","E":"An implied term can contradict an express term."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Terms implied in fact must satisfy the strict common-law test: they must be necessary for business efficacy or so obvious as to go without saying, be capable of clear expression and not contradict express terms. Reasonableness alone is insufficient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Terms implied in fact must satisfy the strict common-law test: they must be necessary for business efficacy or so obvious as to go without saying, be capable of clear expression and not contradict express terms. Reasonableness alone is insufficient.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that courts may rewrite every contract to reach a fair result. The controlling point for these facts is: Implication identifies what the contract must objectively be taken to include; it is not a general fairness jurisdiction.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any commercially useful term must be implied. The controlling point for these facts is: Implication identifies what the contract must objectively be taken to include; it is not a general fairness jurisdiction.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that terms in fact are implied whenever one party later regrets the bargain. The controlling point for these facts is: Implication identifies what the contract must objectively be taken to include; it is not a general fairness jurisdiction.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an implied term can contradict an express term. The controlling point for these facts is: Implication identifies what the contract must objectively be taken to include; it is not a general fairness jurisdiction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Marks and Spencer plc v BNP Paribas Securities Services Trust Co (Jersey) Ltd [2015] UKSC 72.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Implication identifies what the contract must objectively be taken to include; it is not a general fairness jurisdiction.","common_trap":"Substituting a broad reasonableness test for the stringent test of contractual necessity or obviousness.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"04a8aca7b72da530"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0862","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remedies — reliance loss","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant cannot reliably prove the profit it would have earned from a breached contract but can prove reasonable expenditure incurred in reliance on the contract. Recovery would not place the claimant in a better position than performance.\n\nWhich measure may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Reliance expenditure can never be recovered in contract.","B":"Reliance damages may compensate wasted expenditure caused by the breach, subject to ordinary limits and the defendant's ability to show the expenditure would have been wasted even if the contract were performed.","C":"The claimant automatically receives every expense ever incurred.","D":"Reliance damages are punitive damages.","E":"The claimant must elect criminal restitution."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Contract damages can protect the reliance interest by compensating expenditure reasonably incurred in reliance on performance, particularly where expectation loss is difficult to prove, while avoiding recovery for expenditure that performance would not have recouped.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that reliance expenditure can never be recovered in contract. The controlling point for these facts is: Reliance is an alternative compensatory measure, not a route to escape the rule against overcompensation.","B":"Correct. Contract damages can protect the reliance interest by compensating expenditure reasonably incurred in reliance on performance, particularly where expectation loss is difficult to prove, while avoiding recovery for expenditure that performance would not have recouped.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant automatically receives every expense ever incurred. The controlling point for these facts is: Reliance is an alternative compensatory measure, not a route to escape the rule against overcompensation.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that reliance damages are punitive damages. The controlling point for these facts is: Reliance is an alternative compensatory measure, not a route to escape the rule against overcompensation.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the claimant must elect criminal restitution. The controlling point for these facts is: Reliance is an alternative compensatory measure, not a route to escape the rule against overcompensation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Anglia Television Ltd v Reed [1972] 1 QB 60; C & P Haulage v Middleton [1983] 1 WLR 1461.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Reliance is an alternative compensatory measure, not a route to escape the rule against overcompensation.","common_trap":"Assuming uncertainty over profit means the claimant either recovers nothing or automatically recovers all expenditure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"27bfedf332bf5701"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0863","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — pure omissions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A passer-by sees a stranger in danger but did not create the danger, has assumed no responsibility, exercises no control over the source of danger and has no special relationship with the stranger.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every person owes a universal duty to rescue strangers.","B":"Failure to help is automatically negligence whenever harm is foreseeable.","C":"English negligence law generally imposes no duty merely to rescue or prevent harm caused by a third party or natural event, absent a recognised basis for a positive duty.","D":"A duty arises solely because the passer-by is physically nearby.","E":"Omissions are governed by strict liability."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The common law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit or prevent harm. Positive duties arise only within recognised categories such as assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control or certain relationships.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every person owes a universal duty to rescue strangers. The controlling point for these facts is: Foreseeability alone does not normally create a duty to act where the complaint is a pure omission.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that failure to help is automatically negligence whenever harm is foreseeable. The controlling point for these facts is: Foreseeability alone does not normally create a duty to act where the complaint is a pure omission.","C":"Correct. The common law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit or prevent harm. Positive duties arise only within recognised categories such as assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control or certain relationships.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a duty arises solely because the passer-by is physically nearby. The controlling point for these facts is: Foreseeability alone does not normally create a duty to act where the complaint is a pure omission.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that omissions are governed by strict liability. The controlling point for these facts is: Foreseeability alone does not normally create a duty to act where the complaint is a pure omission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Stovin v Wise [1996] AC 923; Michael v Chief Constable of South Wales Police [2015] UKSC 2; Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police [2018] UKSC 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Foreseeability alone does not normally create a duty to act where the complaint is a pure omission.","common_trap":"Applying the ordinary positive-act duty analysis without identifying a recognised basis for an affirmative duty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e43c3cb544d4443d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0864","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Nuisance — prescription","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A factory has interfered with neighbouring land in substantially the same actionable manner continuously for more than 20 years. The interference was actionable throughout that period and the neighbour took no legal action.\n\nWhich doctrine may potentially provide the factory with a defence?","options":{"A":"Coming to the nuisance is always a complete defence from day one.","B":"Prescription arises after six months.","C":"Any planning permission automatically authorises private nuisance.","D":"A right to continue the nuisance may in appropriate circumstances be acquired by prescription after 20 years of actionable nuisance as of right.","E":"A nuisance can never acquire prescriptive protection."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant may acquire by prescription a right to commit what would otherwise be a private nuisance after at least 20 years of the relevant actionable interference as of right. Time runs from when the activity becomes actionable as a nuisance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that coming to the nuisance is always a complete defence from day one. The controlling point for these facts is: Prescription is exceptional and depends on the duration of actionable interference, not merely the age of the defendant's activity.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that prescription arises after six months. The controlling point for these facts is: Prescription is exceptional and depends on the duration of actionable interference, not merely the age of the defendant's activity.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any planning permission automatically authorises private nuisance. The controlling point for these facts is: Prescription is exceptional and depends on the duration of actionable interference, not merely the age of the defendant's activity.","D":"Correct. A defendant may acquire by prescription a right to commit what would otherwise be a private nuisance after at least 20 years of the relevant actionable interference as of right. Time runs from when the activity becomes actionable as a nuisance.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a nuisance can never acquire prescriptive protection. The controlling point for these facts is: Prescription is exceptional and depends on the duration of actionable interference, not merely the age of the defendant's activity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sturges v Bridgman (1879) 11 Ch D 852; Coventry v Lawrence [2014] UKSC 13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Prescription is exceptional and depends on the duration of actionable interference, not merely the age of the defendant's activity.","common_trap":"Counting time from commencement of an activity before it constituted an actionable interference.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec77c13732eafc91"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0865","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — serious harm","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company trading for profit alleges that a publication damaged its reputation but produces no evidence of actual or likely serious financial loss.\n\nWhich statutory threshold is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"A trading company automatically establishes serious harm whenever words are defamatory.","B":"Companies cannot sue in defamation.","C":"Financial consequences are legally irrelevant to corporate serious harm.","D":"The company need prove only nominal loss of £1.","E":"A body trading for profit suffers 'serious harm' for Defamation Act purposes only if the publication has caused or is likely to cause it serious financial loss."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 1 requires serious harm to reputation and specifically provides that harm to the reputation of a body trading for profit is not serious harm unless it has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a trading company automatically establishes serious harm whenever words are defamatory. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit-making bodies face an express financial-loss component within the statutory serious-harm threshold.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that companies cannot sue in defamation. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit-making bodies face an express financial-loss component within the statutory serious-harm threshold.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that financial consequences are legally irrelevant to corporate serious harm. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit-making bodies face an express financial-loss component within the statutory serious-harm threshold.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the company need prove only nominal loss of £1. The controlling point for these facts is: Profit-making bodies face an express financial-loss component within the statutory serious-harm threshold.","E":"Correct. Section 1 requires serious harm to reputation and specifically provides that harm to the reputation of a body trading for profit is not serious harm unless it has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 1; Lachaux v Independent Print Ltd [2019] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Profit-making bodies face an express financial-loss component within the statutory serious-harm threshold.","common_trap":"Applying the individual claimant test without the additional statutory requirement for bodies trading for profit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d28267ab4c1ed3d6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0866","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — ouster clauses","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An Act states that a tribunal's decisions are 'final' and seeks to restrict judicial review. A claimant alleges that the tribunal made a fundamental error of law.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The effect of an ouster clause is a question of statutory interpretation, and courts traditionally require clear language before concluding that Parliament has excluded supervisory review, subject to the wording and modern statutory context.","B":"Any use of the word 'final' automatically excludes all judicial review.","C":"Ouster clauses are always legally void.","D":"A tribunal can determine conclusively the limits of its own jurisdiction regardless of statute.","E":"Judicial review is available only if the tribunal consents."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Courts construe provisions restricting judicial review in light of the rule of law and the statutory text. Anisminic established a strong approach to purported finality clauses, while later cases and legislation require close attention to Parliament's actual wording.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Courts construe provisions restricting judicial review in light of the rule of law and the statutory text. Anisminic established a strong approach to purported finality clauses, while later cases and legislation require close attention to Parliament's actual wording.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any use of the word 'final' automatically excludes all judicial review. The controlling point for these facts is: Restrictions on supervisory jurisdiction are not inferred casually; their legal effect turns on proper construction of the enacted provision.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that ouster clauses are always legally void. The controlling point for these facts is: Restrictions on supervisory jurisdiction are not inferred casually; their legal effect turns on proper construction of the enacted provision.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a tribunal can determine conclusively the limits of its own jurisdiction regardless of statute. The controlling point for these facts is: Restrictions on supervisory jurisdiction are not inferred casually; their legal effect turns on proper construction of the enacted provision.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that judicial review is available only if the tribunal consents. The controlling point for these facts is: Restrictions on supervisory jurisdiction are not inferred casually; their legal effect turns on proper construction of the enacted provision."},"primary_legal_authority":"Anisminic Ltd v Foreign Compensation Commission [1969] 2 AC 147; R (Privacy International) v Investigatory Powers Tribunal [2019] UKSC 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictions on supervisory jurisdiction are not inferred casually; their legal effect turns on proper construction of the enacted provision.","common_trap":"Treating either judicial review or legislative restriction as absolute without interpreting the particular statute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f6ab6b336f625c6d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0867","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — public authorities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company is contracted by a local authority to perform a function. A claimant argues that the company is a public authority for the purposes of the particular act complained of.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any private company contracting with government becomes a public authority for every purpose.","B":"A private body can be a functional public authority for particular acts if the nature of the function is public, but the analysis is fact-sensitive and does not make all of the body's acts public.","C":"Private bodies can never fall within section 6.","D":"Receipt of public money is the sole test.","E":"A functional public authority is immune from Convention rights."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 6 includes persons certain of whose functions are functions of a public nature, while excluding acts whose nature is private. Case law assesses factors surrounding the function rather than applying a single funding or contractual test.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that any private company contracting with government becomes a public authority for every purpose. The controlling point for these facts is: Hybrid bodies may be subject to HRA duties for public functions without becoming public authorities in everything they do.","B":"Correct. Section 6 includes persons certain of whose functions are functions of a public nature, while excluding acts whose nature is private. Case law assesses factors surrounding the function rather than applying a single funding or contractual test.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that private bodies can never fall within section 6. The controlling point for these facts is: Hybrid bodies may be subject to HRA duties for public functions without becoming public authorities in everything they do.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that receipt of public money is the sole test. The controlling point for these facts is: Hybrid bodies may be subject to HRA duties for public functions without becoming public authorities in everything they do.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a functional public authority is immune from Convention rights. The controlling point for these facts is: Hybrid bodies may be subject to HRA duties for public functions without becoming public authorities in everything they do."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6; YL v Birmingham City Council [2007] UKHL 27; relevant statutory developments.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Hybrid bodies may be subject to HRA duties for public functions without becoming public authorities in everything they do.","common_trap":"Using organisational form or public funding as a conclusive substitute for analysis of the particular function.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"105c4ed3145accba"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0868","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — ratio and obiter","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment contains a legal proposition necessary to the court's decision and several broader observations not necessary to decide the case.\n\nWhich is the best general distinction?","options":{"A":"Every sentence in a judgment is equally binding.","B":"Only the final order can ever have precedential value.","C":"The necessary legal reasoning forms the ratio decidendi capable of binding later courts within the hierarchy; non-essential observations are obiter and ordinarily persuasive only.","D":"Obiter dicta always override the ratio.","E":"A ratio is simply the factual evidence accepted by the court."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The ratio decidendi is the legal principle necessary for the decision on the material facts. Obiter dicta are observations not necessary to the outcome and do not carry the same binding force, although they may be highly persuasive.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that every sentence in a judgment is equally binding. The controlling point for these facts is: Precedential force depends on the role a proposition played in deciding the case, not merely its presence in a judgment.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the final order can ever have precedential value. The controlling point for these facts is: Precedential force depends on the role a proposition played in deciding the case, not merely its presence in a judgment.","C":"Correct. The ratio decidendi is the legal principle necessary for the decision on the material facts. Obiter dicta are observations not necessary to the outcome and do not carry the same binding force, although they may be highly persuasive.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that obiter dicta always override the ratio. The controlling point for these facts is: Precedential force depends on the role a proposition played in deciding the case, not merely its presence in a judgment.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a ratio is simply the factual evidence accepted by the court. The controlling point for these facts is: Precedential force depends on the role a proposition played in deciding the case, not merely its presence in a judgment."},"primary_legal_authority":"Doctrine of precedent; ratio decidendi and obiter dicta.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Precedential force depends on the role a proposition played in deciding the case, not merely its presence in a judgment.","common_trap":"Treating all judicial language as binding authority without identifying the reasoning necessary to the result.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"152418d3a5ec87a2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0869","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — lapse and survivorship","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will leaves £20,000 to the testator's friend absolutely. The friend dies before the testator. No substitution clause, statutory anti-lapse rule or other saving provision applies.\n\nWhich is the ordinary result?","options":{"A":"The deceased beneficiary's executors always take the legacy.","B":"The gift automatically doubles for the residuary beneficiary.","C":"The testator is treated as having died intestate as to the entire estate.","D":"The gift lapses because the beneficiary did not survive the testator, and the property passes under the applicable residuary or succession provisions.","E":"The beneficiary is deemed to survive the testator indefinitely."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if its beneficiary predeceases the testator unless the will, section 33 or another rule saves the gift. The destination then depends on the structure of the will and succession rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the deceased beneficiary's executors always take the legacy. The controlling point for these facts is: Survival of the testator is ordinarily required for a beneficiary to take a simple testamentary gift.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the gift automatically doubles for the residuary beneficiary. The controlling point for these facts is: Survival of the testator is ordinarily required for a beneficiary to take a simple testamentary gift.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the testator is treated as having died intestate as to the entire estate. The controlling point for these facts is: Survival of the testator is ordinarily required for a beneficiary to take a simple testamentary gift.","D":"Correct. A testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if its beneficiary predeceases the testator unless the will, section 33 or another rule saves the gift. The destination then depends on the structure of the will and succession rules.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the beneficiary is deemed to survive the testator indefinitely. The controlling point for these facts is: Survival of the testator is ordinarily required for a beneficiary to take a simple testamentary gift."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837; lapse principles, subject to s 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Survival of the testator is ordinarily required for a beneficiary to take a simple testamentary gift.","common_trap":"Assuming every predeceased beneficiary's estate substitutes automatically.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3d067a4deddde2c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0870","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — appropriation and consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives propose to appropriate an estate asset towards a beneficiary's share. The statutory appropriation power applies, and the beneficiary is competent and absolutely entitled to that share.\n\nWhich procedural point is ordinarily important?","options":{"A":"Personal representatives may appropriate any asset to any beneficiary without regard to consent or value.","B":"Appropriation automatically changes the beneficiary's share.","C":"Only the court may ever appropriate estate assets.","D":"Appropriation is available only in insolvent estates.","E":"The personal representatives ordinarily need the relevant beneficiary's consent to an appropriation under the statutory power, subject to the legislation and any contrary testamentary provision."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 41 confers a power of appropriation subject to statutory conditions, including the consent requirements applicable to beneficiaries with relevant interests and proper valuation of the appropriated property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that personal representatives may appropriate any asset to any beneficiary without regard to consent or value. The controlling point for these facts is: Appropriation is an administrative method of satisfying an existing entitlement and is constrained by safeguards for affected beneficiaries.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that appropriation automatically changes the beneficiary's share. The controlling point for these facts is: Appropriation is an administrative method of satisfying an existing entitlement and is constrained by safeguards for affected beneficiaries.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the court may ever appropriate estate assets. The controlling point for these facts is: Appropriation is an administrative method of satisfying an existing entitlement and is constrained by safeguards for affected beneficiaries.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that appropriation is available only in insolvent estates. The controlling point for these facts is: Appropriation is an administrative method of satisfying an existing entitlement and is constrained by safeguards for affected beneficiaries.","E":"Correct. Section 41 confers a power of appropriation subject to statutory conditions, including the consent requirements applicable to beneficiaries with relevant interests and proper valuation of the appropriated property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 41.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appropriation is an administrative method of satisfying an existing entitlement and is constrained by safeguards for affected beneficiaries.","common_trap":"Treating the power as an unrestricted discretion to force particular assets on beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8796f4165ddc390a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0871","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — residence nil-rate band","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An estate includes a qualifying residence that is closely inherited by direct descendants. The personal representatives are considering whether an additional IHT threshold may apply alongside the ordinary nil-rate band, subject to the estate value and statutory conditions.\n\nWhich relief is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The residence nil-rate band.","B":"Business relief.","C":"The annual lifetime exemption.","D":"Hold-over relief for capital gains.","E":"VAT zero-rating."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The IHT legislation provides an additional residence nil-rate amount where a qualifying residential interest is closely inherited, subject to statutory conditions, tapering for larger estates and detailed downsizing provisions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The IHT legislation provides an additional residence nil-rate amount where a qualifying residential interest is closely inherited, subject to statutory conditions, tapering for larger estates and detailed downsizing provisions.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that business relief. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying transmission of a residence to direct descendants can engage an additional death-estate threshold.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the annual lifetime exemption. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying transmission of a residence to direct descendants can engage an additional death-estate threshold.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that hold-over relief for capital gains. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying transmission of a residence to direct descendants can engage an additional death-estate threshold.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that vAT zero-rating. The controlling point for these facts is: Qualifying transmission of a residence to direct descendants can engage an additional death-estate threshold."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, ss 8D–8M.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying transmission of a residence to direct descendants can engage an additional death-estate threshold.","common_trap":"Confusing a death-estate nil-rate provision with lifetime gifting or business-property relief.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"12e010e5a9b2100c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0872","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — client money from third parties","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser's parent sends money to the solicitor to fund part of the purchase price for the client. The money is received in connection with the client's transaction and is to be held for completion.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Money is client money only if physically paid by the client.","B":"The money can constitute client money even though it came from a third party, and the firm must apply the Accounts Rules and relevant source-of-funds/AML checks.","C":"Third-party funds automatically belong to the solicitor.","D":"The firm may ignore the identity and source of the payer.","E":"Money intended for completion must always be paid into the firm's business account."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The definition and treatment of client money depend on why and for whom money is held, not simply the identity of the remitter. Third-party funding also raises important authority, source-of-funds and AML considerations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that money is client money only if physically paid by the client. The controlling point for these facts is: Funds held in connection with a client's transaction can be client money even when remitted by someone else.","B":"Correct. The definition and treatment of client money depend on why and for whom money is held, not simply the identity of the remitter. Third-party funding also raises important authority, source-of-funds and AML considerations.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that third-party funds automatically belong to the solicitor. The controlling point for these facts is: Funds held in connection with a client's transaction can be client money even when remitted by someone else.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the firm may ignore the identity and source of the payer. The controlling point for these facts is: Funds held in connection with a client's transaction can be client money even when remitted by someone else.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that money intended for completion must always be paid into the firm's business account. The controlling point for these facts is: Funds held in connection with a client's transaction can be client money even when remitted by someone else."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 2–3; Money Laundering Regulations 2017.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Funds held in connection with a client's transaction can be client money even when remitted by someone else.","common_trap":"Using the payer's identity as the sole classification test and overlooking financial-crime checks.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fae5ce16e76f1648"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0873","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — maintenance: accumulation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trust income arises for a minor beneficiary. Trustees do not need all of it for the minor's maintenance, education or benefit during the year.\n\nWhich is the best general position under the statutory maintenance regime, subject to the trust instrument?","options":{"A":"Trustees must distribute all income immediately to the minor personally.","B":"Unused income automatically becomes the trustees' beneficial property.","C":"Income not applied may be accumulated and dealt with under the statutory rules governing accumulated income.","D":"Accumulation is always unlawful.","E":"Unused income must be paid to the settlor."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 31 governs application of income for minors and the treatment of unapplied income, including accumulation and later application or entitlement in accordance with the statutory scheme and trust terms.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trustees must distribute all income immediately to the minor personally. The controlling point for these facts is: The maintenance power includes machinery for income that is not presently needed for the minor's benefit.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that unused income automatically becomes the trustees' beneficial property. The controlling point for these facts is: The maintenance power includes machinery for income that is not presently needed for the minor's benefit.","C":"Correct. Section 31 governs application of income for minors and the treatment of unapplied income, including accumulation and later application or entitlement in accordance with the statutory scheme and trust terms.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that accumulation is always unlawful. The controlling point for these facts is: The maintenance power includes machinery for income that is not presently needed for the minor's benefit.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that unused income must be paid to the settlor. The controlling point for these facts is: The maintenance power includes machinery for income that is not presently needed for the minor's benefit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 31, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The maintenance power includes machinery for income that is not presently needed for the minor's benefit.","common_trap":"Assuming the power to apply income requires immediate distribution of every pound received.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0ef78eff8c02cc47"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0874","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — contribution between trustees","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two trustees are jointly responsible for the same breach of trust and compensate the trust. One trustee paid the whole amount and seeks a contribution from the other. No special indemnity factor applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Trustees can never claim contribution from one another.","B":"The beneficiary must always reimburse the trustees.","C":"The paying trustee automatically recovers the whole amount from the settlor.","D":"A trustee who has paid more than a fair share may in principle seek contribution from a co-trustee responsible for the same breach, subject to equitable allocation and any circumstances justifying a different result.","E":"Contribution is available only in criminal proceedings."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Co-trustees responsible for a common breach can be jointly and severally liable to the trust, while equitable principles permit contribution between them, with allocation capable of adjustment where responsibility or indemnity factors justify it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that trustees can never claim contribution from one another. The controlling point for these facts is: Liability to beneficiaries and the internal allocation of that liability between defaulting trustees are separate questions.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the beneficiary must always reimburse the trustees. The controlling point for these facts is: Liability to beneficiaries and the internal allocation of that liability between defaulting trustees are separate questions.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the paying trustee automatically recovers the whole amount from the settlor. The controlling point for these facts is: Liability to beneficiaries and the internal allocation of that liability between defaulting trustees are separate questions.","D":"Correct. Co-trustees responsible for a common breach can be jointly and severally liable to the trust, while equitable principles permit contribution between them, with allocation capable of adjustment where responsibility or indemnity factors justify it.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that contribution is available only in criminal proceedings. The controlling point for these facts is: Liability to beneficiaries and the internal allocation of that liability between defaulting trustees are separate questions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee contribution principles; Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978 where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Liability to beneficiaries and the internal allocation of that liability between defaulting trustees are separate questions.","common_trap":"Assuming joint responsibility means the trustee who happens to pay first must necessarily bear the entire loss personally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6722408cbfbb309a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0875","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — severance by mutual agreement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two beneficial joint tenants expressly agree between themselves that from now on they will hold their beneficial interests in equal shares as tenants in common. No question arises about legal title.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Beneficial joint tenancy can be severed only by a section 36 written notice.","B":"Severance automatically divides the legal title into shares.","C":"An oral or informal mutual agreement can never be relevant.","D":"Severance requires sale of the land.","E":"A mutual agreement demonstrating an immediate intention to treat their beneficial interests as shares can sever the beneficial joint tenancy."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Equity recognises severance by mutual agreement in addition to statutory written notice and other methods. The agreement must demonstrate a common intention to sever rather than merely future negotiations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that beneficial joint tenancy can be severed only by a section 36 written notice. The controlling point for these facts is: Section 36 notice is an important route but not the exclusive equitable method of severance.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that severance automatically divides the legal title into shares. The controlling point for these facts is: Section 36 notice is an important route but not the exclusive equitable method of severance.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that an oral or informal mutual agreement can never be relevant. The controlling point for these facts is: Section 36 notice is an important route but not the exclusive equitable method of severance.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that severance requires sale of the land. The controlling point for these facts is: Section 36 notice is an important route but not the exclusive equitable method of severance.","E":"Correct. Equity recognises severance by mutual agreement in addition to statutory written notice and other methods. The agreement must demonstrate a common intention to sever rather than merely future negotiations."},"primary_legal_authority":"Burgess v Rawnsley [1975] Ch 429; Law of Property Act 1925, s 36(2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 36 notice is an important route but not the exclusive equitable method of severance.","common_trap":"Assuming the statutory notice mechanism exhausts all recognised methods or affects the indivisible legal estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6d3817825c7d99d7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0876","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — Wheeldon v Burrows","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner sells part of land and retains the rest. Before the sale, the sold part enjoyed a continuous and apparent quasi-easement over the retained land that was necessary for reasonable enjoyment, and the conveyance does not exclude implication.\n\nWhich doctrine may imply an easement for the buyer?","options":{"A":"The rule in Wheeldon v Burrows.","B":"Adverse possession.","C":"Overreaching.","D":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier.","E":"Promissory estoppel."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"On a grant of part, Wheeldon v Burrows can imply into the conveyance quasi-easements that were continuous and apparent, used by the common owner for the benefit of the part granted, and necessary for its reasonable enjoyment, subject to the conveyance and modern statutory context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. On a grant of part, Wheeldon v Burrows can imply into the conveyance quasi-easements that were continuous and apparent, used by the common owner for the benefit of the part granted, and necessary for its reasonable enjoyment, subject to the conveyance and modern statutory context.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that adverse possession. The controlling point for these facts is: The rule converts qualifying pre-conveyance quasi-easements into easements on severance of ownership.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that overreaching. The controlling point for these facts is: The rule converts qualifying pre-conveyance quasi-easements into easements on severance of ownership.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the rule in Saunders v Vautier. The controlling point for these facts is: The rule converts qualifying pre-conveyance quasi-easements into easements on severance of ownership.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that promissory estoppel. The controlling point for these facts is: The rule converts qualifying pre-conveyance quasi-easements into easements on severance of ownership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wheeldon v Burrows (1879) 12 Ch D 31; Law of Property Act 1925, s 62 where separately relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The rule converts qualifying pre-conveyance quasi-easements into easements on severance of ownership.","common_trap":"Confusing implication on a conveyance with acquisition by long user or unrelated equitable doctrines.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a0270930718950e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0877","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — title guarantee covenants","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller transfers registered freehold land with full title guarantee. A buyer asks what this wording does.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that the property has no physical defects.","B":"Full title guarantee implies statutory covenants for title, including that the disponer has the right to dispose and will do what is reasonably necessary to give the title purportedly transferred, with broader freedom-from-encumbrances protection than limited title guarantee.","C":"It is merely descriptive wording with no legal effect.","D":"It guarantees planning permission for every future use.","E":"It transfers beneficial ownership of the seller's other property."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994 gives statutory effect to dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee, implying specified covenants for title. The guarantee concerns title rather than the property's physical condition.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that it guarantees that the property has no physical defects. The controlling point for these facts is: Title guarantee allocates legal assurances about the disponer's title and encumbrances; it is not a survey warranty.","B":"Correct. The Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994 gives statutory effect to dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee, implying specified covenants for title. The guarantee concerns title rather than the property's physical condition.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that it is merely descriptive wording with no legal effect. The controlling point for these facts is: Title guarantee allocates legal assurances about the disponer's title and encumbrances; it is not a survey warranty.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that it guarantees planning permission for every future use. The controlling point for these facts is: Title guarantee allocates legal assurances about the disponer's title and encumbrances; it is not a survey warranty.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that it transfers beneficial ownership of the seller's other property. The controlling point for these facts is: Title guarantee allocates legal assurances about the disponer's title and encumbrances; it is not a survey warranty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994, ss 1–3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Title guarantee allocates legal assurances about the disponer's title and encumbrances; it is not a survey warranty.","common_trap":"Confusing covenants for title with promises about physical condition or planning matters.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa6ce299bfcf664c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0878","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — security of tenure termination","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business tenant has a protected tenancy under Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. The contractual term is approaching expiry and the landlord wants to terminate the tenancy and oppose renewal.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The tenancy automatically ends on the contractual expiry date with no statutory procedure.","B":"A protected business tenant has an unconditional right to a new tenancy in every case.","C":"The landlord must use the statutory termination/renewal procedure and, if opposing a new tenancy, rely on one or more statutory grounds under section 30.","D":"The landlord can oppose renewal for any reason without identifying a statutory ground.","E":"Part II applies only to residential leases."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Protected business tenancies continue within the statutory regime beyond contractual expiry until terminated in accordance with the 1954 Act. A landlord opposing renewal must establish an available section 30 ground through the statutory process.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the tenancy automatically ends on the contractual expiry date with no statutory procedure. The controlling point for these facts is: Security of tenure changes both termination mechanics and the grounds on which renewal may be resisted.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a protected business tenant has an unconditional right to a new tenancy in every case. The controlling point for these facts is: Security of tenure changes both termination mechanics and the grounds on which renewal may be resisted.","C":"Correct. Protected business tenancies continue within the statutory regime beyond contractual expiry until terminated in accordance with the 1954 Act. A landlord opposing renewal must establish an available section 30 ground through the statutory process.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the landlord can oppose renewal for any reason without identifying a statutory ground. The controlling point for these facts is: Security of tenure changes both termination mechanics and the grounds on which renewal may be resisted.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that part II applies only to residential leases. The controlling point for these facts is: Security of tenure changes both termination mechanics and the grounds on which renewal may be resisted."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, ss 24–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Security of tenure changes both termination mechanics and the grounds on which renewal may be resisted.","common_trap":"Treating contractual expiry as sufficient to end a protected business tenancy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"95881afc523bc2ef"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0879","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Homicide — unlawful act manslaughter","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally commits a criminal assault that is objectively dangerous. The assault causes V's death, but D neither intended death nor grievous bodily harm.\n\nWhich offence may be established if all requirements are proved?","options":{"A":"Murder necessarily, because every intentional assault causing death is murder.","B":"No homicide offence unless D intended death.","C":"Theft.","D":"Unlawful act manslaughter.","E":"Fraud by false representation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires an intentional criminal act, objectively dangerous in the sense that a sober and reasonable person would recognise a risk of some physical harm, which causes death, together with the mens rea for the underlying offence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that murder necessarily, because every intentional assault causing death is murder. The controlling point for these facts is: Manslaughter can arise from a dangerous unlawful act without the malice aforethought required for murder.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that no homicide offence unless D intended death. The controlling point for these facts is: Manslaughter can arise from a dangerous unlawful act without the malice aforethought required for murder.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that theft. The controlling point for these facts is: Manslaughter can arise from a dangerous unlawful act without the malice aforethought required for murder.","D":"Correct. Unlawful act manslaughter requires an intentional criminal act, objectively dangerous in the sense that a sober and reasonable person would recognise a risk of some physical harm, which causes death, together with the mens rea for the underlying offence.","E":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that fraud by false representation. The controlling point for these facts is: Manslaughter can arise from a dangerous unlawful act without the malice aforethought required for murder."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Church [1966] 1 QB 59; DPP v Newbury and Jones [1977] AC 500.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Manslaughter can arise from a dangerous unlawful act without the malice aforethought required for murder.","common_trap":"Assuming homicide liability either requires murderous intent or cannot arise at all.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"848bae9e32cdebe2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0880","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — defence disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An accused is sent for Crown Court trial. The prosecution has complied with initial disclosure obligations. The statutory defence-statement regime applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A defence statement is prohibited because it would reveal the defence case.","B":"Only the prosecution has disclosure obligations of any kind.","C":"The defence must disclose every privileged communication with its solicitor.","D":"Failure to provide a required defence statement automatically proves guilt.","E":"The accused must provide a defence statement in accordance with the statutory Crown Court regime, identifying the nature of the defence and prescribed matters; failures can have evidential consequences."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The CPIA requires defence disclosure in Crown Court proceedings, including a defence statement setting out the nature of the defence, matters of disagreement and reasons, and specified particulars. Certain defaults may permit comment or inferences, but do not themselves establish guilt.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that a defence statement is prohibited because it would reveal the defence case. The controlling point for these facts is: Defence disclosure is structured and limited; it does not abolish privilege or reverse the prosecution's burden of proof.","B":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that only the prosecution has disclosure obligations of any kind. The controlling point for these facts is: Defence disclosure is structured and limited; it does not abolish privilege or reverse the prosecution's burden of proof.","C":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that the defence must disclose every privileged communication with its solicitor. The controlling point for these facts is: Defence disclosure is structured and limited; it does not abolish privilege or reverse the prosecution's burden of proof.","D":"Incorrect. This option would apply the proposition that failure to provide a required defence statement automatically proves guilt. The controlling point for these facts is: Defence disclosure is structured and limited; it does not abolish privilege or reverse the prosecution's burden of proof.","E":"Correct. The CPIA requires defence disclosure in Crown Court proceedings, including a defence statement setting out the nature of the defence, matters of disagreement and reasons, and specified particulars. Certain defaults may permit comment or inferences, but do not themselves establish guilt."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996, ss 5–6E; Criminal Procedure Rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defence disclosure is structured and limited; it does not abolish privilege or reverse the prosecution's burden of proof.","common_trap":"Treating defence disclosure as either nonexistent or as compulsory disclosure of privileged legal advice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f2ec437d64665bb5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0881","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — shareholder resolutions: circulation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Qualifying members of a private company want the company to circulate a proposed written resolution and accompanying statement to eligible members. The statutory threshold and formal requirements are satisfied.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The members can invoke the statutory procedure requiring circulation of the written resolution and accompanying statement, subject to the Companies Act rules on expenses and improper use.","B":"Only directors may propose a written resolution.","C":"A private company cannot use written resolutions.","D":"The resolution is passed merely because circulation is requested.","E":"The company must circulate any defamatory material regardless of statutory safeguards."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act permits qualifying members to require circulation of a written resolution and related statement, subject to statutory thresholds, timing, expenses and safeguards against abuse.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act permits qualifying members to require circulation of a written resolution and related statement, subject to statutory thresholds, timing, expenses and safeguards against abuse.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that only directors may propose a written resolution; that does not fit the governing rule here. Minority members can trigger circulation machinery without thereby determining the outcome of the vote.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a private company cannot use written resolutions; that does not fit the governing rule here. Minority members can trigger circulation machinery without thereby determining the outcome of the vote.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the resolution is passed merely because circulation is requested; that does not fit the governing rule here. Minority members can trigger circulation machinery without thereby determining the outcome of the vote.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the company must circulate any defamatory material regardless of statutory safeguards; that does not fit the governing rule here. Minority members can trigger circulation machinery without thereby determining the outcome of the vote."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 292–295.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Minority members can trigger circulation machinery without thereby determining the outcome of the vote.","common_trap":"Confusing a right to put a proposal before members with the voting threshold required to pass it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"260bb447f762c603"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0882","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' service contracts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company proposes to guarantee a director's employment under a service contract for a term exceeding two years without a provision allowing the company to terminate within that period without compensation.\n\nWhich corporate approval issue arises?","options":{"A":"Board approval alone is always sufficient.","B":"The guaranteed term requires approval by resolution of the members under the Companies Act before the company agrees to it.","C":"Any director service contract longer than six months is void.","D":"The contract requires a special resolution of creditors.","E":"Member approval is relevant only after the director leaves office."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 188 requires member approval for a director's service contract containing a guaranteed term exceeding two years, subject to the statutory definitions and exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that board approval alone is always sufficient; that does not fit the governing rule here. Long guaranteed director commitments receive a specific shareholder-approval safeguard.","B":"Correct. Section 188 requires member approval for a director's service contract containing a guaranteed term exceeding two years, subject to the statutory definitions and exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that any director service contract longer than six months is void; that does not fit the governing rule here. Long guaranteed director commitments receive a specific shareholder-approval safeguard.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the contract requires a special resolution of creditors; that does not fit the governing rule here. Long guaranteed director commitments receive a specific shareholder-approval safeguard.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that member approval is relevant only after the director leaves office; that does not fit the governing rule here. Long guaranteed director commitments receive a specific shareholder-approval safeguard."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 188–189.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Long guaranteed director commitments receive a specific shareholder-approval safeguard.","common_trap":"Assuming directors can approve their own long-term guaranteed service arrangements solely through board powers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"532291483a9f2a7d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0883","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Business taxation — trading losses","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole trader makes a genuine trading loss in a tax year and asks whether the loss must simply disappear because there is no trading profit for that year.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A trading loss can never obtain tax relief.","B":"Every trading loss is automatically refunded in cash by HMRC.","C":"Trading-loss legislation provides specified relief routes, potentially including set-off against income and/or carry-forward against future profits of the same trade, depending on the statutory conditions and elections.","D":"The trader may deduct the loss from any person's income.","E":"Loss relief applies only to companies."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Income-tax legislation provides defined mechanisms for relieving qualifying trade losses. The available route depends on matters such as the period, continuity of the trade, claims and statutory restrictions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a trading loss can never obtain tax relief; that does not fit the governing rule here. A tax loss is dealt with through prescribed relief provisions rather than simply ignored or freely allocated.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that every trading loss is automatically refunded in cash by HMRC; that does not fit the governing rule here. A tax loss is dealt with through prescribed relief provisions rather than simply ignored or freely allocated.","C":"Correct. Income-tax legislation provides defined mechanisms for relieving qualifying trade losses. The available route depends on matters such as the period, continuity of the trade, claims and statutory restrictions.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the trader may deduct the loss from any person's income; that does not fit the governing rule here. A tax loss is dealt with through prescribed relief provisions rather than simply ignored or freely allocated.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that loss relief applies only to companies; that does not fit the governing rule here. A tax loss is dealt with through prescribed relief provisions rather than simply ignored or freely allocated."},"primary_legal_authority":"Income Tax Act 2007, Part 4, including ss 64 and 83.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A tax loss is dealt with through prescribed relief provisions rather than simply ignored or freely allocated.","common_trap":"Assuming either that losses vanish or that taxpayers may use them without statutory limits.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c9a09a91cf2ce291"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0884","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — liquidation expenses and priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company is in liquidation. After realising assets, the liquidator must distribute the estate among claims of different statutory ranks.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition?","options":{"A":"All creditors always rank pari passu regardless of security or statutory preference.","B":"Shareholders are paid before creditors because they own the company.","C":"The liquidator may choose priorities according to personal fairness.","D":"Distribution follows the statutory insolvency priority regime; liquidation expenses and preferential or secured claims are treated according to their prescribed ranking rather than all creditors sharing equally.","E":"Unsecured creditors automatically outrank fixed-charge holders."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Insolvency Act and Rules establish an ordered distribution regime involving proprietary claims, secured claims, expenses, preferential claims, the prescribed part and unsecured claims as applicable, rather than universal equality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that all creditors always rank pari passu regardless of security or statutory preference; that does not fit the governing rule here. Pari passu operates within relevant classes; it does not erase valid security or statutory priority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that shareholders are paid before creditors because they own the company; that does not fit the governing rule here. Pari passu operates within relevant classes; it does not erase valid security or statutory priority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that the liquidator may choose priorities according to personal fairness; that does not fit the governing rule here. Pari passu operates within relevant classes; it does not erase valid security or statutory priority.","D":"Correct. The Insolvency Act and Rules establish an ordered distribution regime involving proprietary claims, secured claims, expenses, preferential claims, the prescribed part and unsecured claims as applicable, rather than universal equality.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that unsecured creditors automatically outrank fixed-charge holders; that does not fit the governing rule here. Pari passu operates within relevant classes; it does not erase valid security or statutory priority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986; Insolvency Rules 2016, including the statutory priority provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Pari passu operates within relevant classes; it does not erase valid security or statutory priority.","common_trap":"Applying equality of distribution across legally different ranks of claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"152fd3fbe88b62f9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0885","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"ADR — unreasonable refusal","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party wins at trial but had earlier unreasonably refused a serious invitation to mediate. The court is considering costs.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A successful party's conduct concerning ADR can never affect costs.","B":"Mediation is compulsory in every civil claim solely because an opponent proposes it.","C":"Refusing ADR automatically causes the substantive claim to fail.","D":"Costs may be adjusted only for misconduct occurring inside the courtroom.","E":"Unreasonable failure to engage in ADR can be relevant to costs and may justify a departure from the usual costs result, assessed in all the circumstances."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The CPR's overriding objective and costs discretion permit the court to consider parties' conduct, including unreasonable failure to engage in ADR. Modern authority also confirms courts may lawfully order or encourage ADR where proportionate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a successful party's conduct concerning ADR can never affect costs; that does not fit the governing rule here. Litigation success does not immunise unreasonable procedural conduct from costs consequences.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that mediation is compulsory in every civil claim solely because an opponent proposes it; that does not fit the governing rule here. Litigation success does not immunise unreasonable procedural conduct from costs consequences.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that refusing ADR automatically causes the substantive claim to fail; that does not fit the governing rule here. Litigation success does not immunise unreasonable procedural conduct from costs consequences.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that costs may be adjusted only for misconduct occurring inside the courtroom; that does not fit the governing rule here. Litigation success does not immunise unreasonable procedural conduct from costs consequences.","E":"Correct. The CPR's overriding objective and costs discretion permit the court to consider parties' conduct, including unreasonable failure to engage in ADR. Modern authority also confirms courts may lawfully order or encourage ADR where proportionate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Parts 1 and 44; Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil CBC [2023] EWCA Civ 1416.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Litigation success does not immunise unreasonable procedural conduct from costs consequences.","common_trap":"Assuming the merits result mechanically determines costs regardless of ADR conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dea9d158e17d846e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0886","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim remedies — interim payment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant has obtained judgment on liability for damages to be assessed, but final quantum will take months to determine. The claimant seeks part of the likely damages now.\n\nWhich procedural mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An application for an interim payment under CPR Part 25, subject to the specified conditions and amount restrictions.","B":"A statutory demand against the claimant.","C":"A default judgment on liability that has already been decided.","D":"A witness summons.","E":"An application to dissolve the defendant company."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where one of the prescribed conditions is satisfied, including where the claimant has obtained judgment for damages to be assessed, subject to limits designed to avoid overpayment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where one of the prescribed conditions is satisfied, including where the claimant has obtained judgment for damages to be assessed, subject to limits designed to avoid overpayment.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that a statutory demand against the claimant; that does not fit the governing rule here. An interim payment advances part of likely final recovery without finally determining quantum.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a default judgment on liability that has already been decided; that does not fit the governing rule here. An interim payment advances part of likely final recovery without finally determining quantum.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a witness summons; that does not fit the governing rule here. An interim payment advances part of likely final recovery without finally determining quantum.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that an application to dissolve the defendant company; that does not fit the governing rule here. An interim payment advances part of likely final recovery without finally determining quantum."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 25.20–25.25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An interim payment advances part of likely final recovery without finally determining quantum.","common_trap":"Confusing temporary monetary relief within existing litigation with enforcement or insolvency procedures.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"430c84e444d80100"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0887","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — attachment of earnings","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual judgment debtor is employed and has not paid a county court money judgment. The creditor wants periodic deductions from the debtor's wages.\n\nWhich enforcement method is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A charging order over the debtor's salary.","B":"An attachment of earnings order, if the statutory requirements are met.","C":"A Part 36 offer.","D":"An injunction requiring the employer to dismiss the debtor.","E":"A company administration order."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An attachment of earnings order can require an employer to make deductions from an employed judgment debtor's earnings and pay them towards a qualifying judgment debt, subject to statutory protections and procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a charging order over the debtor's salary; that does not fit the governing rule here. This enforcement method targets earnings through the employer rather than assets or bank balances.","B":"Correct. An attachment of earnings order can require an employer to make deductions from an employed judgment debtor's earnings and pay them towards a qualifying judgment debt, subject to statutory protections and procedure.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a Part 36 offer; that does not fit the governing rule here. This enforcement method targets earnings through the employer rather than assets or bank balances.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that an injunction requiring the employer to dismiss the debtor; that does not fit the governing rule here. This enforcement method targets earnings through the employer rather than assets or bank balances.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a company administration order; that does not fit the governing rule here. This enforcement method targets earnings through the employer rather than assets or bank balances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attachment of Earnings Act 1971; County Court procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"This enforcement method targets earnings through the employer rather than assets or bank balances.","common_trap":"Confusing wage deductions with security over land or other enforcement methods.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"aa29cef213d5ea8f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0888","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — complaints information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is opening a new matter for an individual client.\n\nWhich is the best general regulatory position concerning complaints?","options":{"A":"A firm need explain complaints procedures only after litigation begins.","B":"Solicitors may charge clients for considering complaints about their service.","C":"The client should receive required information about the firm's complaints handling and routes to external redress, and complaints must be handled promptly, fairly and free of charge.","D":"External redress must never be mentioned to clients.","E":"A complaint can be ignored if the solicitor believes it lacks merit."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"SRA rules require clients to be informed in writing at engagement about how to complain and, at appropriate stages, about the Legal Ombudsman and relevant time limits, with complaints handled promptly, fairly and free of charge.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a firm need explain complaints procedures only after litigation begins; that does not fit the governing rule here. Complaint handling is part of regulated client service and includes both advance information and proper response.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that solicitors may charge clients for considering complaints about their service; that does not fit the governing rule here. Complaint handling is part of regulated client service and includes both advance information and proper response.","C":"Correct. SRA rules require clients to be informed in writing at engagement about how to complain and, at appropriate stages, about the Legal Ombudsman and relevant time limits, with complaints handled promptly, fairly and free of charge.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that external redress must never be mentioned to clients; that does not fit the governing rule here. Complaint handling is part of regulated client service and includes both advance information and proper response.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a complaint can be ignored if the solicitor believes it lacks merit; that does not fit the governing rule here. Complaint handling is part of regulated client service and includes both advance information and proper response."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 8.2–8.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Complaint handling is part of regulated client service and includes both advance information and proper response.","common_trap":"Treating complaints information as optional or permitting the firm to deter complaints through fees.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c923179793de6ebb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0889","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Financial services — regulated activities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A law firm proposes to arrange investments for clients as a regular standalone service rather than merely doing work incidental to legal services. No exemption or exclusion has yet been established.\n\nWhich issue must be addressed before the firm proceeds?","options":{"A":"Solicitors are automatically exempt from all financial-services regulation.","B":"Any investment activity is unregulated if a client requests it.","C":"SRA authorisation automatically equals FCA authorisation for every activity.","D":"Whether the activity is a regulated activity requiring FCA authorisation or a valid statutory/exempt-professional-firm route.","E":"Financial-services legislation applies only to banks."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Financial Services and Markets Act generally prohibits carrying on regulated activities by way of business without authorisation or exemption. Solicitors may rely on limited professional-firm arrangements only where the statutory conditions are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that solicitors are automatically exempt from all financial-services regulation; that does not fit the governing rule here. Professional status does not provide an unlimited exemption from the general prohibition.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that any investment activity is unregulated if a client requests it; that does not fit the governing rule here. Professional status does not provide an unlimited exemption from the general prohibition.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that sRA authorisation automatically equals FCA authorisation for every activity; that does not fit the governing rule here. Professional status does not provide an unlimited exemption from the general prohibition.","D":"Correct. The Financial Services and Markets Act generally prohibits carrying on regulated activities by way of business without authorisation or exemption. Solicitors may rely on limited professional-firm arrangements only where the statutory conditions are met.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that financial-services legislation applies only to banks; that does not fit the governing rule here. Professional status does not provide an unlimited exemption from the general prohibition."},"primary_legal_authority":"Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, s 19 and Part XX; SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional status does not provide an unlimited exemption from the general prohibition.","common_trap":"Assuming legal-practice authorisation alone permits a standalone regulated investment business.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"218c74b7b901e5e4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0890","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — intention to create legal relations","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two close friends make an informal social promise over dinner, with no commercial setting, documentation or indication that either expects legal enforcement.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every promise between adults is automatically a contract.","B":"Social agreements can never be legally binding under any circumstances.","C":"Consideration alone conclusively proves intention.","D":"The parties' secret subjective thoughts are decisive.","E":"The court assesses objectively whether the parties intended legal relations; purely social arrangements commonly indicate no such intention, although the presumption can be displaced by the facts."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Intention to create legal relations is assessed objectively in context. Domestic and social arrangements commonly carry a presumption against legal enforceability, while evidence of seriousness, reliance or formality can rebut it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that every promise between adults is automatically a contract; that does not fit the governing rule here. Contract formation requires an objective intention that the arrangement carry legal consequences.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that social agreements can never be legally binding under any circumstances; that does not fit the governing rule here. Contract formation requires an objective intention that the arrangement carry legal consequences.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that consideration alone conclusively proves intention; that does not fit the governing rule here. Contract formation requires an objective intention that the arrangement carry legal consequences.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the parties' secret subjective thoughts are decisive; that does not fit the governing rule here. Contract formation requires an objective intention that the arrangement carry legal consequences.","E":"Correct. Intention to create legal relations is assessed objectively in context. Domestic and social arrangements commonly carry a presumption against legal enforceability, while evidence of seriousness, reliance or formality can rebut it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Balfour v Balfour [1919] 2 KB 571; Merritt v Merritt [1970] 1 WLR 1211.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract formation requires an objective intention that the arrangement carry legal consequences.","common_trap":"Treating social context as either irrelevant or an irrebuttable prohibition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee9d5996d4a0f104"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0891","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — rescission bars","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer discovers an actionable misrepresentation but, with full knowledge of the right to rescind, unequivocally elects to continue the contract.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Affirmation can bar rescission, although any separate damages claim must be analysed under its own rules.","B":"The buyer can always rescind indefinitely after affirming.","C":"Affirmation automatically eliminates every possible damages claim.","D":"Rescission is a criminal sanction and cannot be waived.","E":"Only lapse of time can bar rescission."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Rescission is an equitable remedy subject to bars. A representee who, after discovering the truth and the right to rescind, clearly elects to affirm the contract generally loses the right to rescind.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Rescission is an equitable remedy subject to bars. A representee who, after discovering the truth and the right to rescind, clearly elects to affirm the contract generally loses the right to rescind.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the buyer can always rescind indefinitely after affirming; that does not fit the governing rule here. A party cannot ordinarily both affirm the contract with knowledge and later avoid it by rescission.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that affirmation automatically eliminates every possible damages claim; that does not fit the governing rule here. A party cannot ordinarily both affirm the contract with knowledge and later avoid it by rescission.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that rescission is a criminal sanction and cannot be waived; that does not fit the governing rule here. A party cannot ordinarily both affirm the contract with knowledge and later avoid it by rescission.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that only lapse of time can bar rescission; that does not fit the governing rule here. A party cannot ordinarily both affirm the contract with knowledge and later avoid it by rescission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Long v Lloyd [1958] 1 WLR 753; Misrepresentation Act 1967.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A party cannot ordinarily both affirm the contract with knowledge and later avoid it by rescission.","common_trap":"Confusing loss of the rescission remedy with automatic extinction of all other remedies.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7bc7af6b0261330c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0892","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer contracts — digital content","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer pays for downloadable software supplied by a trader. The software is materially defective at the time of supply.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Digital content is wholly outside consumer legislation.","B":"The Consumer Rights Act implies statutory standards for digital content, including satisfactory quality, fitness for particular purpose where applicable and correspondence with description, with specific remedies for breach.","C":"The consumer's only possible remedy is damages for personal injury.","D":"Software must be perfect rather than of satisfactory quality.","E":"Statutory rights apply only to tangible goods."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Part 1 of the Consumer Rights Act contains a distinct regime for contracts to supply digital content, implying quality, fitness and description terms and providing remedies such as repair or replacement and price reduction in qualifying cases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that digital content is wholly outside consumer legislation; that does not fit the governing rule here. Digital content has its own statutory consumer regime rather than being excluded because it is intangible.","B":"Correct. Part 1 of the Consumer Rights Act contains a distinct regime for contracts to supply digital content, implying quality, fitness and description terms and providing remedies such as repair or replacement and price reduction in qualifying cases.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that the consumer's only possible remedy is damages for personal injury; that does not fit the governing rule here. Digital content has its own statutory consumer regime rather than being excluded because it is intangible.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that software must be perfect rather than of satisfactory quality; that does not fit the governing rule here. Digital content has its own statutory consumer regime rather than being excluded because it is intangible.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that statutory rights apply only to tangible goods; that does not fit the governing rule here. Digital content has its own statutory consumer regime rather than being excluded because it is intangible."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, ss 33–47.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Digital content has its own statutory consumer regime rather than being excluded because it is intangible.","common_trap":"Applying only the tangible-goods rules or assuming no statutory quality protection exists.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec6a2046d369f54f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0893","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — rescuers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D negligently creates a dangerous situation. A rescuer reasonably attempts to save a person endangered by D's negligence and is injured in the rescue.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A rescuer can never recover because intervention is always a novus actus.","B":"Rescuers automatically assume every risk as a matter of law.","C":"Injury to a reasonable rescuer can fall within the scope of D's duty because rescue is a foreseeable response to danger, subject to ordinary negligence principles.","D":"D owes a duty only to the original victim.","E":"Any rescue, however reckless, must produce full recovery."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The law generally treats reasonable rescue as a foreseeable consequence of negligently created danger rather than a break in causation. Liability remains subject to duty, breach, causation and any issue concerning the rescuer's own conduct.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a rescuer can never recover because intervention is always a novus actus; that does not fit the governing rule here. Danger may foreseeably invite rescue, so reasonable intervention does not ordinarily sever responsibility.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that rescuers automatically assume every risk as a matter of law; that does not fit the governing rule here. Danger may foreseeably invite rescue, so reasonable intervention does not ordinarily sever responsibility.","C":"Correct. The law generally treats reasonable rescue as a foreseeable consequence of negligently created danger rather than a break in causation. Liability remains subject to duty, breach, causation and any issue concerning the rescuer's own conduct.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that d owes a duty only to the original victim; that does not fit the governing rule here. Danger may foreseeably invite rescue, so reasonable intervention does not ordinarily sever responsibility.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that any rescue, however reckless, must produce full recovery; that does not fit the governing rule here. Danger may foreseeably invite rescue, so reasonable intervention does not ordinarily sever responsibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Haynes v Harwood [1935] 1 KB 146; Baker v T E Hopkins & Son Ltd [1959] 1 WLR 966.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Danger may foreseeably invite rescue, so reasonable intervention does not ordinarily sever responsibility.","common_trap":"Treating the rescuer's voluntary action as automatically breaking causation or constituting volenti.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a166d9fd8e19a44f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0894","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Trespass to land — continuing trespass","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D unlawfully leaves a structure on C's land. The structure remains there day after day despite C's demand for removal.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Trespass can occur only at the instant D first enters the land.","B":"Leaving an object on land can never be trespass.","C":"The claim automatically becomes nuisance and ceases to be trespass after 24 hours.","D":"The continued unlawful presence can constitute a continuing trespass, potentially giving rise to a fresh cause of action while it persists.","E":"C loses title to the land if the structure remains."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"An unlawful state of affairs maintained on another's land, such as leaving an object there, can amount to continuing trespass. This has consequences for remedies and limitation analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that trespass can occur only at the instant D first enters the land; that does not fit the governing rule here. Trespass protects possession against continuing as well as momentary direct interference.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that leaving an object on land can never be trespass; that does not fit the governing rule here. Trespass protects possession against continuing as well as momentary direct interference.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that the claim automatically becomes nuisance and ceases to be trespass after 24 hours; that does not fit the governing rule here. Trespass protects possession against continuing as well as momentary direct interference.","D":"Correct. An unlawful state of affairs maintained on another's land, such as leaving an object there, can amount to continuing trespass. This has consequences for remedies and limitation analysis.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that c loses title to the land if the structure remains; that does not fit the governing rule here. Trespass protects possession against continuing as well as momentary direct interference."},"primary_legal_authority":"Holmes v Wilson (1839) 10 Ad & E 503; continuing-trespass principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trespass protects possession against continuing as well as momentary direct interference.","common_trap":"Assuming trespass is necessarily a single completed event at first entry.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a2a73fd00d87c0cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0895","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — independent contractors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business hires a genuinely independent specialist contractor. The contractor negligently injures a third party while performing ordinary work, and no non-delegable duty or other exceptional basis applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every hirer is automatically vicariously liable for every contractor.","B":"Independent-contractor status is irrelevant to vicarious liability.","C":"The injured party can never sue the contractor personally.","D":"A written contract automatically eliminates all possible liability of the hirer.","E":"A person is not ordinarily vicariously liable merely for the torts of a genuine independent contractor, though direct or non-delegable liability may arise on different facts."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Vicarious liability ordinarily attaches to employment or relationships sufficiently akin to employment, not to genuinely independent businesses performing work on their own account. Separate direct duties can alter the outcome.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that every hirer is automatically vicariously liable for every contractor; that does not fit the governing rule here. The relationship between tortfeasor and defendant is a distinct requirement for vicarious liability.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that independent-contractor status is irrelevant to vicarious liability; that does not fit the governing rule here. The relationship between tortfeasor and defendant is a distinct requirement for vicarious liability.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that the injured party can never sue the contractor personally; that does not fit the governing rule here. The relationship between tortfeasor and defendant is a distinct requirement for vicarious liability.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a written contract automatically eliminates all possible liability of the hirer; that does not fit the governing rule here. The relationship between tortfeasor and defendant is a distinct requirement for vicarious liability.","E":"Correct. Vicarious liability ordinarily attaches to employment or relationships sufficiently akin to employment, not to genuinely independent businesses performing work on their own account. Separate direct duties can alter the outcome."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barclays Bank plc v Various Claimants [2020] UKSC 13; vicarious-liability principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The relationship between tortfeasor and defendant is a distinct requirement for vicarious liability.","common_trap":"Assuming engagement to perform work is by itself enough to make the hirer vicariously liable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a9a341c8a520c287"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0896","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Constitutional law — prerogative powers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister claims a prerogative power permits executive action that would frustrate rights Parliament has granted by statute.\n\nWhich is the best general constitutional position?","options":{"A":"Prerogative powers cannot lawfully be used to override or frustrate statutory law; statute prevails where the two conflict.","B":"Prerogative powers always override Acts of Parliament.","C":"Ministers may suspend statutes whenever politically convenient.","D":"Courts can never examine the legal limits of prerogative powers.","E":"A prerogative power becomes primary legislation when exercised."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The executive cannot use prerogative power to change domestic law or frustrate statutory rights and schemes. Parliamentary legislation prevails where statute occupies or constrains the field.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The executive cannot use prerogative power to change domestic law or frustrate statutory rights and schemes. Parliamentary legislation prevails where statute occupies or constrains the field.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that prerogative powers always override Acts of Parliament; that does not fit the governing rule here. Executive prerogative exists within legal limits set by common law and Parliament.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that ministers may suspend statutes whenever politically convenient; that does not fit the governing rule here. Executive prerogative exists within legal limits set by common law and Parliament.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that courts can never examine the legal limits of prerogative powers; that does not fit the governing rule here. Executive prerogative exists within legal limits set by common law and Parliament.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a prerogative power becomes primary legislation when exercised; that does not fit the governing rule here. Executive prerogative exists within legal limits set by common law and Parliament."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd [1920] AC 508; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the EU [2017] UKSC 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executive prerogative exists within legal limits set by common law and Parliament.","common_trap":"Treating prerogative as a source of authority superior to enacted statute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b7a8857d5b41ed77"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0897","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Assimilated EU law — interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court is dealing in 2027 with domestic law derived from pre-exit EU legislation. A lawyer assumes that every old EU interpretive principle continues unchanged merely because the rule originated in EU law.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"All EU-derived law automatically ceased to exist on 31 December 2023.","B":"The court must apply the post-Brexit domestic statutory framework governing assimilated law, including amendments made by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023, rather than assuming all former EU interpretive effects continue unchanged.","C":"Every pre-exit CJEU decision remains absolutely binding on every UK court forever.","D":"EU supremacy continues universally over all domestic legislation.","E":"Origin in EU law is now legally irrelevant in every respect."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The post-Brexit framework preserved and renamed much former retained EU law as assimilated law while altering features including supremacy and general principles. The precise effect depends on the relevant enactments, timing and statutory saving rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that all EU-derived law automatically ceased to exist on 31 December 2023; that does not fit the governing rule here. The source's EU origin does not justify applying the pre-exit legal framework without checking subsequent domestic legislation.","B":"Correct. The post-Brexit framework preserved and renamed much former retained EU law as assimilated law while altering features including supremacy and general principles. The precise effect depends on the relevant enactments, timing and statutory saving rules.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that every pre-exit CJEU decision remains absolutely binding on every UK court forever; that does not fit the governing rule here. The source's EU origin does not justify applying the pre-exit legal framework without checking subsequent domestic legislation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that eU supremacy continues universally over all domestic legislation; that does not fit the governing rule here. The source's EU origin does not justify applying the pre-exit legal framework without checking subsequent domestic legislation.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that origin in EU law is now legally irrelevant in every respect; that does not fit the governing rule here. The source's EU origin does not justify applying the pre-exit legal framework without checking subsequent domestic legislation."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, as amended; Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The source's EU origin does not justify applying the pre-exit legal framework without checking subsequent domestic legislation.","common_trap":"Assuming either complete extinction or complete unchanged continuation of the former retained-EU-law regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a21cb9c90086b1dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0898","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — ejusdem generis","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute refers to 'cars, vans, lorries and other vehicles'. A court must decide whether an item falls within 'other vehicles'.\n\nWhich interpretive principle may be relevant?","options":{"A":"The general words must always be ignored.","B":"Ejusdem generis requires the broadest imaginable meaning in every case.","C":"Under ejusdem generis, general words following a list may be construed by reference to the class indicated by the specific words, where the statutory context supports such a genus.","D":"The rule applies only to contracts and never statutes.","E":"The court must ask what the minister privately intended."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Ejusdem generis is a linguistic canon under which general words following specific examples can take colour from an identifiable class formed by those examples, always subject to statutory text, purpose and context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the general words must always be ignored; that does not fit the governing rule here. The canon helps infer the intended scope of general language from its surrounding specific terms.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that ejusdem generis requires the broadest imaginable meaning in every case; that does not fit the governing rule here. The canon helps infer the intended scope of general language from its surrounding specific terms.","C":"Correct. Ejusdem generis is a linguistic canon under which general words following specific examples can take colour from an identifiable class formed by those examples, always subject to statutory text, purpose and context.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the rule applies only to contracts and never statutes; that does not fit the governing rule here. The canon helps infer the intended scope of general language from its surrounding specific terms.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the court must ask what the minister privately intended; that does not fit the governing rule here. The canon helps infer the intended scope of general language from its surrounding specific terms."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory interpretation principles; ejusdem generis canon.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The canon helps infer the intended scope of general language from its surrounding specific terms.","common_trap":"Treating a contextual aid as either mandatory exclusion of general words or an inquiry into subjective ministerial intention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2fe8c854c0c31990"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0899","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — privileged wills: loss of privileged status","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person validly made a privileged will while a soldier in actual military service. Years later, after leaving service, the person survives but never executes an ordinary will.\n\nWhich point distinguishes a privileged will from an ordinary informally made document?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"A privileged will validly made under the statutory exception is not invalid merely because the maker later ceases the qualifying service; its validity depends on whether the statutory privilege applied when it was made."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The exception concerns capacity/formality at execution and is not simply a temporary document expiring automatically when service ends.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. The exception concerns capacity/formality at execution and is not simply a temporary document expiring automatically when service ends."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 11","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The exception concerns capacity/formality at execution and is not simply a temporary document expiring automatically when service ends.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c1a4f8c07945e9ed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0900","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — insolvent estates","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased's estate is insufficient to pay all debts and liabilities. The personal representatives are preparing to distribute the available assets.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Beneficiaries are always paid before creditors.","B":"The representatives may choose whichever creditors they personally consider deserving.","C":"All debts are ignored if the deceased left a valid will.","D":"Insolvency converts every specific legacy into secured debt.","E":"The personal representatives must administer the insolvent estate according to the statutory insolvency order and must not simply pay beneficiaries or preferred creditors at will."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An insolvent estate is administered under statutory rules that adapt insolvency priorities to deceased estates. Personal representatives must respect those priorities before any beneficiary can receive value.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that beneficiaries are always paid before creditors; that does not fit the governing rule here. The deceased's testamentary wishes operate subject to the estate's enforceable liabilities and insolvency regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the representatives may choose whichever creditors they personally consider deserving; that does not fit the governing rule here. The deceased's testamentary wishes operate subject to the estate's enforceable liabilities and insolvency regime.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that all debts are ignored if the deceased left a valid will; that does not fit the governing rule here. The deceased's testamentary wishes operate subject to the estate's enforceable liabilities and insolvency regime.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that insolvency converts every specific legacy into secured debt; that does not fit the governing rule here. The deceased's testamentary wishes operate subject to the estate's enforceable liabilities and insolvency regime.","E":"Correct. An insolvent estate is administered under statutory rules that adapt insolvency priorities to deceased estates. Personal representatives must respect those priorities before any beneficiary can receive value."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Insolvent Estates of Deceased Persons Order 1986; Administration of Estates Act 1925.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The deceased's testamentary wishes operate subject to the estate's enforceable liabilities and insolvency regime.","common_trap":"Assuming a will permits representatives to defeat creditor priority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ade3e8d016d26b24"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0901","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — business relief","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased owned qualifying unquoted trading-company shares for the required period. The estate claims inheritance-tax relief, and no statutory exclusion applies.\n\nWhich relief is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Business relief may reduce the taxable value of qualifying business property, potentially by 100% or 50% depending on the statutory category and rules applicable at the relevant date.","B":"The residence nil-rate band applies because all shares are residences.","C":"VAT input-tax credit eliminates IHT.","D":"Business assets can never receive IHT relief.","E":"The annual exemption automatically exempts the entire shareholding on death."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The IHT business-relief regime reduces the value transferred by qualifying business property, with the applicable rate and conditions depending on the property and legislation in force at the relevant time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The IHT business-relief regime reduces the value transferred by qualifying business property, with the applicable rate and conditions depending on the property and legislation in force at the relevant time.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the residence nil-rate band applies because all shares are residences; that does not fit the governing rule here. Qualifying trading-business interests receive a specific IHT relief distinct from general nil-rate bands and lifetime exemptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that vAT input-tax credit eliminates IHT; that does not fit the governing rule here. Qualifying trading-business interests receive a specific IHT relief distinct from general nil-rate bands and lifetime exemptions.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that business assets can never receive IHT relief; that does not fit the governing rule here. Qualifying trading-business interests receive a specific IHT relief distinct from general nil-rate bands and lifetime exemptions.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the annual exemption automatically exempts the entire shareholding on death; that does not fit the governing rule here. Qualifying trading-business interests receive a specific IHT relief distinct from general nil-rate bands and lifetime exemptions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, Part V, Chapter I, including ss 103–114.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying trading-business interests receive a specific IHT relief distinct from general nil-rate bands and lifetime exemptions.","common_trap":"Assuming all business property is either fully taxable or automatically exempt without applying the statutory conditions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f9541b381d10d9cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0902","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — correcting breaches","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm's cashier discovers that client money was mistakenly transferred to the business account without proper authority.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The firm should wait until the annual audit before correcting it.","B":"The breach should be corrected promptly on discovery, including restoring any improperly withdrawn client money and addressing records and reporting obligations as applicable.","C":"Once client money reaches the business account it becomes the firm's property.","D":"Only the client can correct an Accounts Rules breach.","E":"The firm may conceal the error if no client has complained."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require breaches to be remedied promptly upon discovery. Improperly withdrawn client money must be replaced, records corrected and any serious regulatory issue considered under applicable reporting duties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the firm should wait until the annual audit before correcting it; that does not fit the governing rule here. Prompt restoration protects client funds and regulatory records rather than allowing an identified breach to persist.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require breaches to be remedied promptly upon discovery. Improperly withdrawn client money must be replaced, records corrected and any serious regulatory issue considered under applicable reporting duties.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that once client money reaches the business account it becomes the firm's property; that does not fit the governing rule here. Prompt restoration protects client funds and regulatory records rather than allowing an identified breach to persist.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that only the client can correct an Accounts Rules breach; that does not fit the governing rule here. Prompt restoration protects client funds and regulatory records rather than allowing an identified breach to persist.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the firm may conceal the error if no client has complained; that does not fit the governing rule here. Prompt restoration protects client funds and regulatory records rather than allowing an identified breach to persist."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 6.1; SRA Code reporting obligations where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Prompt restoration protects client funds and regulatory records rather than allowing an identified breach to persist.","common_trap":"Treating an accounting mistake as self-curing or permitting delay because harm has not yet been reported.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d63e6004f6e96801"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0903","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — power of advancement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Capital is held on trust for a beneficiary with a future or contingent interest. Trustees consider using part of that capital now for the beneficiary's advancement or benefit.\n\nWhich statutory power is most directly relevant, subject to the trust instrument and current statutory conditions?","options":{"A":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier automatically, regardless of age or entitlement.","B":"The power to accumulate income under section 31 only.","C":"The statutory power of advancement under section 32 of the Trustee Act 1925.","D":"The rule against perpetuities as a spending power.","E":"A trustee's personal power to make gifts from trust property."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 32 provides trustees with a statutory power to apply capital for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit, subject to the trust terms, statutory limits and protections for other interests.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the rule in Saunders v Vautier automatically, regardless of age or entitlement; that does not fit the governing rule here. Advancement concerns authorised early application of trust capital for a beneficiary rather than personal generosity by trustees.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the power to accumulate income under section 31 only; that does not fit the governing rule here. Advancement concerns authorised early application of trust capital for a beneficiary rather than personal generosity by trustees.","C":"Correct. Section 32 provides trustees with a statutory power to apply capital for a beneficiary's advancement or benefit, subject to the trust terms, statutory limits and protections for other interests.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the rule against perpetuities as a spending power; that does not fit the governing rule here. Advancement concerns authorised early application of trust capital for a beneficiary rather than personal generosity by trustees.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a trustee's personal power to make gifts from trust property; that does not fit the governing rule here. Advancement concerns authorised early application of trust capital for a beneficiary rather than personal generosity by trustees."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 32, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Advancement concerns authorised early application of trust capital for a beneficiary rather than personal generosity by trustees.","common_trap":"Confusing the capital-advancement power with maintenance from income or beneficiary termination rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c4392409465c613f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0904","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trusts — limitation: trustee fraud exception","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary discovers that a trustee fraudulently concealed a breach of trust and argues that an ordinary limitation defence bars the claim.\n\nWhich rule requires particular attention?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"Statutory limitation protection is restricted for claims involving trustee fraud or fraudulent breach and for certain claims to recover trust property retained or converted by the trustee."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Trust claims have special limitation rules, including important exceptions for fraud and recovery of trust property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. Trust claims have special limitation rules, including important exceptions for fraud and recovery of trust property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 21","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trust claims have special limitation rules, including important exceptions for fraud and recovery of trust property.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e56650e329415566"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0905","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — TLATA occupation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Land is held on trust of land for two adult beneficiaries. One beneficiary is entitled to occupy under the trust's purposes, while practical circumstances make simultaneous occupation by both difficult.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every beneficiary has an absolute right to occupy regardless of the trust or circumstances.","B":"Trustees can never regulate occupation.","C":"Only the legal owners' personal preferences matter.","D":"TLATA applies only after the land is sold.","E":"Trustees' powers concerning occupation are governed by TLATA, including duties to consider relevant intentions and circumstances and powers to impose reasonable conditions when regulating occupation."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"TLATA sections 12 and 13 regulate beneficiaries' occupation rights and trustees' powers, including restrictions, exclusions and reasonable conditions, while requiring consideration of the trust's purposes, settlor intentions and beneficiaries' circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that every beneficiary has an absolute right to occupy regardless of the trust or circumstances; that does not fit the governing rule here. Beneficial occupation is governed by a statutory balancing framework rather than an unlimited possessory entitlement.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that trustees can never regulate occupation; that does not fit the governing rule here. Beneficial occupation is governed by a statutory balancing framework rather than an unlimited possessory entitlement.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that only the legal owners' personal preferences matter; that does not fit the governing rule here. Beneficial occupation is governed by a statutory balancing framework rather than an unlimited possessory entitlement.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that tLATA applies only after the land is sold; that does not fit the governing rule here. Beneficial occupation is governed by a statutory balancing framework rather than an unlimited possessory entitlement.","E":"Correct. TLATA sections 12 and 13 regulate beneficiaries' occupation rights and trustees' powers, including restrictions, exclusions and reasonable conditions, while requiring consideration of the trust's purposes, settlor intentions and beneficiaries' circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, ss 12–13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Beneficial occupation is governed by a statutory balancing framework rather than an unlimited possessory entitlement.","common_trap":"Treating an occupation entitlement as absolute or ignoring trustees' statutory regulatory powers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"548342a7e4df4f15"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0906","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — forfeiture for non-payment of rent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial tenant is in arrears of rent. The lease contains a forfeiture clause and the landlord wishes to forfeit solely for non-payment.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Forfeiture for non-payment of rent is subject to the lease and applicable law, but the section 146 notice ordinarily required for other breaches is not required for non-payment of rent.","B":"A section 146 notice is invariably required before forfeiture for unpaid rent.","C":"Rent arrears can never justify forfeiture.","D":"The landlord automatically acquires the tenant's business assets.","E":"Forfeiture is available without any contractual right of re-entry."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 146's notice requirement expressly does not apply to forfeiture for non-payment of rent. The landlord must nevertheless have an enforceable right of re-entry and comply with other relevant procedural and relief rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 146's notice requirement expressly does not apply to forfeiture for non-payment of rent. The landlord must nevertheless have an enforceable right of re-entry and comply with other relevant procedural and relief rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that a section 146 notice is invariably required before forfeiture for unpaid rent; that does not fit the governing rule here. Rent arrears are treated differently from many other covenant breaches for section 146 notice purposes.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that rent arrears can never justify forfeiture; that does not fit the governing rule here. Rent arrears are treated differently from many other covenant breaches for section 146 notice purposes.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the landlord automatically acquires the tenant's business assets; that does not fit the governing rule here. Rent arrears are treated differently from many other covenant breaches for section 146 notice purposes.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that forfeiture is available without any contractual right of re-entry; that does not fit the governing rule here. Rent arrears are treated differently from many other covenant breaches for section 146 notice purposes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 146; lease forfeiture principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Rent arrears are treated differently from many other covenant breaches for section 146 notice purposes.","common_trap":"Mechanically applying the section 146 notice requirement to the statutory rent exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8be96f86ef7b9d11"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0907","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — rent review","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A lease provides for an open-market rent review every five years and specifies assumptions and disregards for the hypothetical letting.\n\nWhat is the best approach when determining the reviewed rent?","options":{"A":"Ignore the lease wording and use any figure the landlord requests.","B":"Apply the contractual rent-review machinery, including the stated hypothetical assumptions and disregards, rather than valuing the actual occupation without reference to the clause.","C":"Rent review automatically means a fixed statutory 10% increase.","D":"The tenant's actual business profit is always the sole measure of open-market rent.","E":"A rent-review clause is unenforceable unless the court sets the rent in advance."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Open-market rent review is fundamentally contractual. Valuation must follow the hypothetical lease, valuation date, assumptions, disregards and dispute-resolution machinery specified in the clause.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that ignore the lease wording and use any figure the landlord requests; that does not fit the governing rule here. The reviewed rent is the product of the agreed valuation hypothesis, not an unconstrained assessment of fairness.","B":"Correct. Open-market rent review is fundamentally contractual. Valuation must follow the hypothetical lease, valuation date, assumptions, disregards and dispute-resolution machinery specified in the clause.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that rent review automatically means a fixed statutory 10% increase; that does not fit the governing rule here. The reviewed rent is the product of the agreed valuation hypothesis, not an unconstrained assessment of fairness.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the tenant's actual business profit is always the sole measure of open-market rent; that does not fit the governing rule here. The reviewed rent is the product of the agreed valuation hypothesis, not an unconstrained assessment of fairness.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a rent-review clause is unenforceable unless the court sets the rent in advance; that does not fit the governing rule here. The reviewed rent is the product of the agreed valuation hypothesis, not an unconstrained assessment of fairness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Commercial lease rent-review principles; contractual interpretation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The reviewed rent is the product of the agreed valuation hypothesis, not an unconstrained assessment of fairness.","common_trap":"Using real-world facts that the clause expressly requires the valuer to assume away or disregard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"445aa04698384c6d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0908","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — requisitions on title","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After investigating title and before completion, a buyer's solicitor identifies practical and title matters requiring clarification from the seller, including arrangements for discharge of a registered mortgage.\n\nWhich conveyancing step is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Serving a criminal witness summons.","B":"Applying for a winding-up order.","C":"Raising appropriate requisitions or completion enquiries with the seller's solicitor.","D":"Issuing a Part 36 offer.","E":"Registering the buyer before exchange."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Pre-completion requisitions and enquiries address matters needed to ensure effective completion, including execution, undertakings, mortgage discharge and other title/completion issues not already resolved.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that serving a criminal witness summons; that does not fit the governing rule here. Requisitions form part of the final conveyancing checks before completion and registration.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that applying for a winding-up order; that does not fit the governing rule here. Requisitions form part of the final conveyancing checks before completion and registration.","C":"Correct. Pre-completion requisitions and enquiries address matters needed to ensure effective completion, including execution, undertakings, mortgage discharge and other title/completion issues not already resolved.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that issuing a Part 36 offer; that does not fit the governing rule here. Requisitions form part of the final conveyancing checks before completion and registration.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that registering the buyer before exchange; that does not fit the governing rule here. Requisitions form part of the final conveyancing checks before completion and registration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; Law Society completion procedures and Standard Conditions where incorporated.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Requisitions form part of the final conveyancing checks before completion and registration.","common_trap":"Confusing transaction completion enquiries with litigation or insolvency procedures.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5522c32e030617a6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0909","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — property belonging to another","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D finds property that V has accidentally lost. D learns who V is and dishonestly decides to keep the property permanently.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Lost property immediately becomes ownerless for Theft Act purposes.","B":"Finding property gives D legal title automatically.","C":"Theft can apply only to property physically taken from V's hands.","D":"Lost property can still 'belong to another' because V retains a proprietary interest, so the Theft Act element may be satisfied if the other elements of theft are proved.","E":"Dishonesty is irrelevant once property is lost."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Property belongs to another for Theft Act purposes where another has possession or control or a proprietary right or interest. A loser ordinarily retains ownership, so later dishonest appropriation by a finder can amount to theft.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that lost property immediately becomes ownerless for Theft Act purposes; that does not fit the governing rule here. Physical loss of possession does not ordinarily extinguish the owner's proprietary interest.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that finding property gives D legal title automatically; that does not fit the governing rule here. Physical loss of possession does not ordinarily extinguish the owner's proprietary interest.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that theft can apply only to property physically taken from V's hands; that does not fit the governing rule here. Physical loss of possession does not ordinarily extinguish the owner's proprietary interest.","D":"Correct. Property belongs to another for Theft Act purposes where another has possession or control or a proprietary right or interest. A loser ordinarily retains ownership, so later dishonest appropriation by a finder can amount to theft.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that dishonesty is irrelevant once property is lost; that does not fit the governing rule here. Physical loss of possession does not ordinarily extinguish the owner's proprietary interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, ss 1, 3 and 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Physical loss of possession does not ordinarily extinguish the owner's proprietary interest.","common_trap":"Equating 'lost' with legally abandoned or ownerless property.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c936a5c738c049d1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0910","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Trial — no case to answer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At the close of the prosecution case in a criminal trial, the defence argues that the evidence is incapable of supporting a conviction.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The judge must always leave every charge to the fact-finder regardless of evidential sufficiency.","B":"The defence must prove innocence before making the submission.","C":"A no-case submission can be made only after the defence has called all its evidence.","D":"The test is whether the judge personally believes the defendant is innocent.","E":"The court considers whether there is no case to answer under the Galbraith approach, including whether there is no evidence on an essential element or prosecution evidence is such that a properly directed tribunal could not properly convict."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Galbraith requires the judge to stop a case where there is no evidence that the offence was committed by the defendant, or where prosecution evidence taken at its highest is such that a properly directed jury could not properly convict, subject to the distinction between evidential weakness and matters properly left to the jury.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the judge must always leave every charge to the fact-finder regardless of evidential sufficiency; that does not fit the governing rule here. The submission tests legal/evidential sufficiency of the prosecution case, not the judge's personal view of ultimate guilt.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the defence must prove innocence before making the submission; that does not fit the governing rule here. The submission tests legal/evidential sufficiency of the prosecution case, not the judge's personal view of ultimate guilt.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a no-case submission can be made only after the defence has called all its evidence; that does not fit the governing rule here. The submission tests legal/evidential sufficiency of the prosecution case, not the judge's personal view of ultimate guilt.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the test is whether the judge personally believes the defendant is innocent; that does not fit the governing rule here. The submission tests legal/evidential sufficiency of the prosecution case, not the judge's personal view of ultimate guilt.","E":"Correct. Galbraith requires the judge to stop a case where there is no evidence that the offence was committed by the defendant, or where prosecution evidence taken at its highest is such that a properly directed jury could not properly convict, subject to the distinction between evidential weakness and matters properly left to the jury."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Galbraith [1981] 1 WLR 1039.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The submission tests legal/evidential sufficiency of the prosecution case, not the judge's personal view of ultimate guilt.","common_trap":"Confusing the threshold sufficiency ruling with final fact-finding or shifting the burden to the defence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2798ccd2d3344cf2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0911","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation Tax — allowable deductions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trading company incurs expenditure wholly and exclusively for the purposes of its trade. The expenditure is revenue rather than capital and no specific statutory prohibition applies.\n\nHow should the expenditure ordinarily be treated when calculating trading profits for corporation tax?","options":{"A":"It is ordinarily deductible in calculating taxable trading profits.","B":"It is never deductible because companies may deduct only capital expenditure.","C":"It is deducted only from the company's share capital.","D":"It is automatically treated as a chargeable gain.","E":"It is deductible only if every shareholder approves it."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Corporation tax trading profits are calculated using income-tax trading principles as applied by CTA 2009. Revenue expenditure incurred wholly and exclusively for the trade is generally deductible unless a statutory rule disallows it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Corporation tax trading profits are calculated using income-tax trading principles as applied by CTA 2009. Revenue expenditure incurred wholly and exclusively for the trade is generally deductible unless a statutory rule disallows it.","B":"Incorrect. It is never deductible because companies may deduct only capital expenditure does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The tax computation distinguishes deductible trading expenditure from capital or specifically disallowed items.","C":"Incorrect. It is deducted only from the company's share capital does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The tax computation distinguishes deductible trading expenditure from capital or specifically disallowed items.","D":"Incorrect. It is automatically treated as a chargeable gain does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The tax computation distinguishes deductible trading expenditure from capital or specifically disallowed items.","E":"Incorrect. It is deductible only if every shareholder approves it does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The tax computation distinguishes deductible trading expenditure from capital or specifically disallowed items."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009, including ss 46 and 54.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The tax computation distinguishes deductible trading expenditure from capital or specifically disallowed items.","common_trap":"Assuming all expenditure is treated alike without classifying its nature and statutory deductibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"24d5d413cecdc228"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0912","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation Tax — chargeable gains","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company disposes of a capital asset for more than its allowable acquisition and disposal costs. No exemption or relief applies.\n\nWhich is the best general tax treatment?","options":{"A":"The company pays personal capital gains tax outside corporation tax.","B":"The company's chargeable gain is brought into its corporation tax computation rather than being charged to capital gains tax as if the company were an individual.","C":"Companies are never taxed on gains from asset disposals.","D":"The entire sale proceeds are automatically taxable profit without allowable cost.","E":"The gain is treated as a dividend to shareholders."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Companies are generally charged to corporation tax on chargeable gains. The gain is computed under the corporation-tax/chargeable-gains regime using allowable consideration and costs, subject to available reliefs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The company pays personal capital gains tax outside corporation tax does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. For companies, chargeable gains form part of the corporation-tax framework rather than the individual's CGT charge.","B":"Correct. Companies are generally charged to corporation tax on chargeable gains. The gain is computed under the corporation-tax/chargeable-gains regime using allowable consideration and costs, subject to available reliefs.","C":"Incorrect. Companies are never taxed on gains from asset disposals does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. For companies, chargeable gains form part of the corporation-tax framework rather than the individual's CGT charge.","D":"Incorrect. The entire sale proceeds are automatically taxable profit without allowable cost does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. For companies, chargeable gains form part of the corporation-tax framework rather than the individual's CGT charge.","E":"Incorrect. The gain is treated as a dividend to shareholders does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. For companies, chargeable gains form part of the corporation-tax framework rather than the individual's CGT charge."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009; Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992, including s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"For companies, chargeable gains form part of the corporation-tax framework rather than the individual's CGT charge.","common_trap":"Applying the individual's CGT charging mechanism directly to a company.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"15dbcb1d45d6e15d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0913","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation Tax — group relief","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two UK companies are members of the same qualifying corporate group. One has a current-period trading loss while the other has taxable profits. The statutory ownership and other conditions are satisfied.\n\nWhich relief may allow the loss to be used within the group?","options":{"A":"The loss must remain forever with the loss-making company.","B":"The profitable company automatically becomes a partnership.","C":"Corporation tax group relief may permit qualifying losses to be surrendered to another qualifying group company.","D":"Only individuals can transfer tax losses.","E":"The loss can be deducted from the shareholders' personal income."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The corporation-tax group-relief regime permits qualifying losses and other amounts to be surrendered between companies satisfying the statutory group relationship and claim requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The loss must remain forever with the loss-making company does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Group relief can match qualifying losses and profits within a sufficiently connected corporate group.","B":"Incorrect. The profitable company automatically becomes a partnership does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Group relief can match qualifying losses and profits within a sufficiently connected corporate group.","C":"Correct. The corporation-tax group-relief regime permits qualifying losses and other amounts to be surrendered between companies satisfying the statutory group relationship and claim requirements.","D":"Incorrect. Only individuals can transfer tax losses does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Group relief can match qualifying losses and profits within a sufficiently connected corporate group.","E":"Incorrect. The loss can be deducted from the shareholders' personal income does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Group relief can match qualifying losses and profits within a sufficiently connected corporate group."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2010, Part 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Group relief can match qualifying losses and profits within a sufficiently connected corporate group.","common_trap":"Assuming separate corporate personality prevents every statutory sharing of tax losses.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0426f977468fc859"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0914","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Business finance — debentures and security","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company borrows from a bank under a debenture containing a fixed charge over specified machinery and a floating charge over the company's circulating asset pool.\n\nWhich distinction is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A floating charge always gives the company no ability to deal with charged assets in the ordinary course.","B":"A fixed charge can exist only over land.","C":"Fixed and floating charges are legally identical labels.","D":"A fixed charge attaches to identified assets with the requisite control, whereas a floating charge can hover over a changing class of assets until crystallisation or another relevant event.","E":"A floating charge automatically transfers ownership of all assets to the bank."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The characterisation depends on substantive rights and control, not merely drafting labels. A floating charge typically covers a changing fund of assets the company may deal with in the ordinary course until crystallisation; effective fixed security requires the requisite degree of control.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A floating charge always gives the company no ability to deal with charged assets in the ordinary course does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Security classification affects control and insolvency priority and is determined substantively.","B":"Incorrect. A fixed charge can exist only over land does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Security classification affects control and insolvency priority and is determined substantively.","C":"Incorrect. Fixed and floating charges are legally identical labels does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Security classification affects control and insolvency priority and is determined substantively.","D":"Correct. The characterisation depends on substantive rights and control, not merely drafting labels. A floating charge typically covers a changing fund of assets the company may deal with in the ordinary course until crystallisation; effective fixed security requires the requisite degree of control.","E":"Incorrect. A floating charge automatically transfers ownership of all assets to the bank does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Security classification affects control and insolvency priority and is determined substantively."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Spectrum Plus Ltd [2005] UKHL 41; Companies Act 2006, Part 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Security classification affects control and insolvency priority and is determined substantively.","common_trap":"Treating the document's label as conclusive or assuming fixed security is limited to land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bf1b0e354cfe38b3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0915","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — obtaining information from judgment debtor","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment creditor has a money judgment but knows little about the debtor's employment, bank accounts or assets and wants information to choose an enforcement method.\n\nWhich procedure is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A Part 36 offer.","B":"A request for further information under Part 18 directed at the concluded merits dispute.","C":"A declaration of incompatibility.","D":"An application for probate.","E":"An order requiring the judgment debtor, or an officer of a corporate debtor, to attend court and provide information under CPR Part 71."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 71 provides a post-judgment procedure requiring a judgment debtor, or an officer of a corporate debtor, to attend court to provide information needed to enforce a judgment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A Part 36 offer does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Part 71 is an enforcement-information mechanism distinct from merits-stage clarification.","B":"Incorrect. A request for further information under Part 18 directed at the concluded merits dispute does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Part 71 is an enforcement-information mechanism distinct from merits-stage clarification.","C":"Incorrect. A declaration of incompatibility does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Part 71 is an enforcement-information mechanism distinct from merits-stage clarification.","D":"Incorrect. An application for probate does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Part 71 is an enforcement-information mechanism distinct from merits-stage clarification.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 71 provides a post-judgment procedure requiring a judgment debtor, or an officer of a corporate debtor, to attend court to provide information needed to enforce a judgment."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 71.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Part 71 is an enforcement-information mechanism distinct from merits-stage clarification.","common_trap":"Using pre-judgment case-definition procedures instead of the specific post-judgment information process.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5cbdfe4a07ed74c0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0916","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — costs budgeting","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A represented party in multi-track proceedings falls within the costs-management regime and is required to file a costs budget. The party fails to do so by the applicable deadline without obtaining relief.\n\nWhich consequence is specifically associated with failure to file a required budget?","options":{"A":"Subject to the rules and any relief, the party may be treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees.","B":"The claim is automatically won by the opponent.","C":"All future legal costs become criminal penalties.","D":"The court loses jurisdiction over the proceedings.","E":"The party automatically receives indemnity costs."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 3.14 imposes a specific sanction where a party fails to file a required budget: unless the court otherwise orders, the party is treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 3.14 imposes a specific sanction where a party fails to file a required budget: unless the court otherwise orders, the party is treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees.","B":"Incorrect. The claim is automatically won by the opponent does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Costs budgeting has its own procedural sanction, making timely compliance important.","C":"Incorrect. All future legal costs become criminal penalties does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Costs budgeting has its own procedural sanction, making timely compliance important.","D":"Incorrect. The court loses jurisdiction over the proceedings does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Costs budgeting has its own procedural sanction, making timely compliance important.","E":"Incorrect. The party automatically receives indemnity costs does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Costs budgeting has its own procedural sanction, making timely compliance important."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 3.12–3.18, especially r 3.14.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Costs budgeting has its own procedural sanction, making timely compliance important.","common_trap":"Assuming every procedural default produces strike-out rather than applying the sanction prescribed for that default.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e7decb47d322b434"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0917","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — hearsay in civil proceedings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant wants to rely at a civil trial on a written statement made by a person who will not be called as a witness. The statement is hearsay.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Hearsay is automatically inadmissible in every civil case.","B":"Hearsay is generally admissible in civil proceedings, subject to statutory notice/procedural requirements and the court's assessment of weight.","C":"Hearsay is admissible only if the opposing party agrees.","D":"Civil hearsay is treated as conclusive proof.","E":"The Civil Evidence Act applies only to criminal trials."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Civil Evidence Act abolished the general exclusion of hearsay in civil proceedings. CPR Part 33 regulates notice and related procedures, while reliability factors affect evidential weight.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Hearsay is automatically inadmissible in every civil case does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Civil law generally addresses hearsay through notice and weight rather than blanket exclusion.","B":"Correct. The Civil Evidence Act abolished the general exclusion of hearsay in civil proceedings. CPR Part 33 regulates notice and related procedures, while reliability factors affect evidential weight.","C":"Incorrect. Hearsay is admissible only if the opposing party agrees does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Civil law generally addresses hearsay through notice and weight rather than blanket exclusion.","D":"Incorrect. Civil hearsay is treated as conclusive proof does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Civil law generally addresses hearsay through notice and weight rather than blanket exclusion.","E":"Incorrect. The Civil Evidence Act applies only to criminal trials does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Civil law generally addresses hearsay through notice and weight rather than blanket exclusion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Evidence Act 1995; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil law generally addresses hearsay through notice and weight rather than blanket exclusion.","common_trap":"Importing the criminal hearsay exclusionary framework into ordinary civil proceedings.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fccf1ce6d900a107"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0918","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Funding legal services — conditional fee agreements","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor proposes a conditional fee agreement under which some or all fees depend on success. The type of proceedings permits such an agreement.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every conditional fee agreement is prohibited.","B":"A CFA automatically makes the opponent liable for every success fee.","C":"The arrangement must satisfy the statutory and regulatory requirements applicable to conditional fee agreements, including the rules governing success fees and client information.","D":"A solicitor may charge any success percentage without legal restriction.","E":"A CFA transfers ownership of the cause of action to the solicitor."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Conditional fee agreements are permitted within the statutory framework where applicable, but enforceability and charging depend on compliance with the Courts and Legal Services Act and related legislation and professional obligations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every conditional fee agreement is prohibited does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. A 'no win, no fee' structure is regulated rather than either universally prohibited or unrestricted.","B":"Incorrect. A CFA automatically makes the opponent liable for every success fee does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. A 'no win, no fee' structure is regulated rather than either universally prohibited or unrestricted.","C":"Correct. Conditional fee agreements are permitted within the statutory framework where applicable, but enforceability and charging depend on compliance with the Courts and Legal Services Act and related legislation and professional obligations.","D":"Incorrect. A solicitor may charge any success percentage without legal restriction does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. A 'no win, no fee' structure is regulated rather than either universally prohibited or unrestricted.","E":"Incorrect. A CFA transfers ownership of the cause of action to the solicitor does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. A 'no win, no fee' structure is regulated rather than either universally prohibited or unrestricted."},"primary_legal_authority":"Courts and Legal Services Act 1990, s 58; Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A 'no win, no fee' structure is regulated rather than either universally prohibited or unrestricted.","common_trap":"Assuming the funding label itself determines recoverability from the opponent or removes statutory controls.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b487c6785e459573"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0919","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Funding legal services — damages-based agreements","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client and representative consider an agreement under which the representative's payment will be determined by reference to the financial benefit obtained by the client if the case succeeds.\n\nWhich funding arrangement is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"A legal mortgage.","B":"A Part 36 offer.","C":"A general retainer that can never depend on outcome.","D":"A damages-based agreement, subject to the statutory and regulatory conditions governing such agreements.","E":"A statutory demand."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A damages-based agreement is an outcome-related funding arrangement under which remuneration is determined by reference to the financial benefit obtained, and its enforceability depends on compliance with the statutory regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A legal mortgage does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. DBAs are distinct from ordinary hourly retainers and from CFAs even though both may be outcome-related.","B":"Incorrect. A Part 36 offer does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. DBAs are distinct from ordinary hourly retainers and from CFAs even though both may be outcome-related.","C":"Incorrect. A general retainer that can never depend on outcome does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. DBAs are distinct from ordinary hourly retainers and from CFAs even though both may be outcome-related.","D":"Correct. A damages-based agreement is an outcome-related funding arrangement under which remuneration is determined by reference to the financial benefit obtained, and its enforceability depends on compliance with the statutory regime.","E":"Incorrect. A statutory demand does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. DBAs are distinct from ordinary hourly retainers and from CFAs even though both may be outcome-related."},"primary_legal_authority":"Courts and Legal Services Act 1990, s 58AA; Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"DBAs are distinct from ordinary hourly retainers and from CFAs even though both may be outcome-related.","common_trap":"Treating all outcome-related funding as the same contractual mechanism.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f05aa6df40926270"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0920","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — conditions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract expressly classifies a term as a condition. The classification is legally effective. One party commits a breach of that condition.\n\nWhich remedy consequence ordinarily follows?","options":{"A":"Termination is available only if the breach deprives the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit.","B":"Only nominal damages are available.","C":"The contract automatically becomes void ab initio.","D":"A condition can never be waived.","E":"The innocent party may elect to terminate for breach and claim damages, even if the consequences of that particular breach are not themselves very serious."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where a term is properly classified as a condition, any breach ordinarily gives the innocent party a right to terminate as well as claim damages, subject to election, affirmation and other applicable rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Termination is available only if the breach deprives the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Classification as a condition fixes the termination consequence without requiring the Hongkong Fir seriousness test for that breach.","B":"Incorrect. Only nominal damages are available does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Classification as a condition fixes the termination consequence without requiring the Hongkong Fir seriousness test for that breach.","C":"Incorrect. The contract automatically becomes void ab initio does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Classification as a condition fixes the termination consequence without requiring the Hongkong Fir seriousness test for that breach.","D":"Incorrect. A condition can never be waived does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Classification as a condition fixes the termination consequence without requiring the Hongkong Fir seriousness test for that breach.","E":"Correct. Where a term is properly classified as a condition, any breach ordinarily gives the innocent party a right to terminate as well as claim damages, subject to election, affirmation and other applicable rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bunge Corp v Tradax Export SA [1981] 1 WLR 711; condition/warranty principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Classification as a condition fixes the termination consequence without requiring the Hongkong Fir seriousness test for that breach.","common_trap":"Applying the innominate-term consequences test to a term validly classified as a condition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe6a36d10fcdad1c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0921","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — warranties","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractual term is properly classified as a warranty rather than a condition or innominate term. It is breached.\n\nWhich is the ordinary consequence?","options":{"A":"The innocent party may claim damages but breach of the warranty alone does not ordinarily confer a right to terminate the contract.","B":"Every breach of warranty automatically terminates the contract.","C":"A warranty is legally unenforceable.","D":"Only specific performance is available.","E":"No remedy exists for breach of a warranty."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A warranty is a contractual term whose breach sounds in damages but does not, by virtue of its classification alone, entitle the innocent party to terminate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A warranty is a contractual term whose breach sounds in damages but does not, by virtue of its classification alone, entitle the innocent party to terminate.","B":"Incorrect. Every breach of warranty automatically terminates the contract does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The classification distinguishes compensable breach from breach carrying an automatic termination right.","C":"Incorrect. A warranty is legally unenforceable does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The classification distinguishes compensable breach from breach carrying an automatic termination right.","D":"Incorrect. Only specific performance is available does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The classification distinguishes compensable breach from breach carrying an automatic termination right.","E":"Incorrect. No remedy exists for breach of a warranty does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The classification distinguishes compensable breach from breach carrying an automatic termination right."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bettini v Gye (1876) 1 QBD 183; classification of contractual terms.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The classification distinguishes compensable breach from breach carrying an automatic termination right.","common_trap":"Assuming every contractual term has the same remedial consequences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"77d3d24430111351"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0922","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — innominate terms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract contains an innominate term. A breach occurs, but its consequences are minor and do not substantially deprive the innocent party of the benefit of the contract.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every breach of an innominate term permits termination.","B":"Damages may be available, but termination is not ordinarily justified unless the consequences of breach are sufficiently serious.","C":"Innominate terms are unenforceable.","D":"No damages can be awarded unless termination is available.","E":"The court must retrospectively label the term a warranty."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For an innominate term, the right to terminate depends on the seriousness of the consequences of breach, classically whether the innocent party is deprived of substantially the whole benefit intended under the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every breach of an innominate term permits termination does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The consequences of the particular breach determine whether termination accompanies damages.","B":"Correct. For an innominate term, the right to terminate depends on the seriousness of the consequences of breach, classically whether the innocent party is deprived of substantially the whole benefit intended under the contract.","C":"Incorrect. Innominate terms are unenforceable does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The consequences of the particular breach determine whether termination accompanies damages.","D":"Incorrect. No damages can be awarded unless termination is available does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The consequences of the particular breach determine whether termination accompanies damages.","E":"Incorrect. The court must retrospectively label the term a warranty does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The consequences of the particular breach determine whether termination accompanies damages."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hongkong Fir Shipping Co Ltd v Kawasaki Kisen Kaisha Ltd [1962] 2 QB 26.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The consequences of the particular breach determine whether termination accompanies damages.","common_trap":"Treating an innominate term as if it carried the automatic termination rule of a condition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d81a7931a39f9cf2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0923","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Product liability — Consumer Protection Act","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer is injured because a product is not as safe as persons generally are entitled to expect. The producer exercised reasonable care in manufacturing but cannot establish a statutory defence.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always prove careless manufacturing.","B":"The Act applies only where the producer intended harm.","C":"The Consumer Protection Act can impose liability for damage caused by a defective product without requiring the claimant to prove negligence by the producer.","D":"Product liability under the Act is available only for breach of contract.","E":"Reasonable care is an automatic complete defence."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Part I of the Consumer Protection Act establishes a statutory product-liability regime focused on defect, causation and damage rather than proof of negligence, subject to specified defences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The claimant must always prove careless manufacturing does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The statutory claim is distinct from negligence and does not turn simply on whether the producer exercised reasonable care.","B":"Incorrect. The Act applies only where the producer intended harm does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The statutory claim is distinct from negligence and does not turn simply on whether the producer exercised reasonable care.","C":"Correct. Part I of the Consumer Protection Act establishes a statutory product-liability regime focused on defect, causation and damage rather than proof of negligence, subject to specified defences.","D":"Incorrect. Product liability under the Act is available only for breach of contract does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The statutory claim is distinct from negligence and does not turn simply on whether the producer exercised reasonable care.","E":"Incorrect. Reasonable care is an automatic complete defence does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The statutory claim is distinct from negligence and does not turn simply on whether the producer exercised reasonable care."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Protection Act 1987, ss 2–5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory claim is distinct from negligence and does not turn simply on whether the producer exercised reasonable care.","common_trap":"Importing the fault requirement from negligence into the statutory strict-liability regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"59bc7330c51464ac"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0924","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — trespassers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier knows of a dangerous feature on land, knows that trespassers regularly come near it, and the risk is one against which the occupier could reasonably be expected to offer some protection.\n\nWhich statutory regime is most directly relevant to the occupier's potential duty to the trespasser?","options":{"A":"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 only, because it covers all entrants identically.","B":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015.","C":"The Partnership Act 1890.","D":"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1984.","E":"The Defamation Act 2013."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The 1984 Act governs an occupier's potential duty to persons other than visitors. Section 1 identifies threshold conditions concerning awareness of danger, knowledge or grounds to believe the person may come into its vicinity, and reasonableness of offering protection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 only, because it covers all entrants identically does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Trespassers are assessed under the distinct statutory duty in the 1984 Act rather than the common duty to lawful visitors in the 1957 Act.","B":"Incorrect. The Consumer Rights Act 2015 does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Trespassers are assessed under the distinct statutory duty in the 1984 Act rather than the common duty to lawful visitors in the 1957 Act.","C":"Incorrect. The Partnership Act 1890 does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Trespassers are assessed under the distinct statutory duty in the 1984 Act rather than the common duty to lawful visitors in the 1957 Act.","D":"Correct. The 1984 Act governs an occupier's potential duty to persons other than visitors. Section 1 identifies threshold conditions concerning awareness of danger, knowledge or grounds to believe the person may come into its vicinity, and reasonableness of offering protection.","E":"Incorrect. The Defamation Act 2013 does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Trespassers are assessed under the distinct statutory duty in the 1984 Act rather than the common duty to lawful visitors in the 1957 Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trespassers are assessed under the distinct statutory duty in the 1984 Act rather than the common duty to lawful visitors in the 1957 Act.","common_trap":"Applying the lawful-visitor regime without identifying the entrant's status.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9e4c55ceade2c389"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0925","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — absolute privilege","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A witness makes a defamatory statement relevant to evidence given in court proceedings. The statement is within the scope of the judicial proceedings.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Privilege is only qualified and is defeated whenever malice is proved.","B":"Courtroom statements can never be defamatory but have no privilege.","C":"Absolute privilege applies only to newspapers.","D":"The witness is liable whenever the statement is false.","E":"Statements made in the course of judicial proceedings can attract absolute privilege, preventing a defamation claim even if made maliciously."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Public policy protects participants in judicial proceedings through absolute privilege for statements sufficiently connected with those proceedings, allowing evidence and advocacy without fear of defamation litigation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Privilege is only qualified and is defeated whenever malice is proved does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Absolute privilege differs from qualified privilege because malice does not defeat it.","B":"Incorrect. Courtroom statements can never be defamatory but have no privilege does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Absolute privilege differs from qualified privilege because malice does not defeat it.","C":"Incorrect. Absolute privilege applies only to newspapers does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Absolute privilege differs from qualified privilege because malice does not defeat it.","D":"Incorrect. The witness is liable whenever the statement is false does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Absolute privilege differs from qualified privilege because malice does not defeat it.","E":"Correct. Public policy protects participants in judicial proceedings through absolute privilege for statements sufficiently connected with those proceedings, allowing evidence and advocacy without fear of defamation litigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Judicial-proceedings absolute privilege; Defamation Act 1996, s 14.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absolute privilege differs from qualified privilege because malice does not defeat it.","common_trap":"Applying the malice limitation of qualified privilege to judicial absolute privilege.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a7f40a0efaed9323"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0926","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial function — independence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister privately directs a judge how to decide an individual pending case because the government dislikes one party.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Judicial independence and the separation of powers require adjudication to be free from executive direction in individual cases.","B":"Ministers may direct judicial outcomes whenever public money is involved.","C":"Judges are constitutionally subordinate to ministers when interpreting statutes.","D":"The rule of law concerns only criminal offences.","E":"Judicial independence prevents Parliament from legislating."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An independent judiciary determines cases according to law without improper executive interference. Constitutional arrangements and statute protect judicial independence while preserving Parliament's legislative authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An independent judiciary determines cases according to law without improper executive interference. Constitutional arrangements and statute protect judicial independence while preserving Parliament's legislative authority.","B":"Incorrect. Ministers may direct judicial outcomes whenever public money is involved does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The judicial function requires decisional independence from executive pressure in individual litigation.","C":"Incorrect. Judges are constitutionally subordinate to ministers when interpreting statutes does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The judicial function requires decisional independence from executive pressure in individual litigation.","D":"Incorrect. The rule of law concerns only criminal offences does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The judicial function requires decisional independence from executive pressure in individual litigation.","E":"Incorrect. Judicial independence prevents Parliament from legislating does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The judicial function requires decisional independence from executive pressure in individual litigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Constitutional Reform Act 2005, including s 3; separation-of-powers and rule-of-law principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The judicial function requires decisional independence from executive pressure in individual litigation.","common_trap":"Confusing legislative supremacy with executive authority to dictate adjudicative outcomes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d9f42d7626c1367d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0927","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Courts — jurisdiction","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A straightforward breach-of-contract claim for damages is to be commenced in England and Wales. No specialist tribunal or exclusive High Court jurisdiction applies.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition?","options":{"A":"Every contract claim must begin in the Supreme Court.","B":"Civil jurisdiction is allocated between the County Court and High Court under statutory and procedural rules, with claim type, value and complexity relevant to the appropriate court.","C":"The High Court has no contract jurisdiction.","D":"Parties can choose any criminal court for a civil claim.","E":"Only tribunals hear civil disputes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The civil court structure allocates first-instance work between the County Court and High Court, with legislation, CPR rules and practice directions guiding where proceedings should be started and managed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every contract claim must begin in the Supreme Court does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Court hierarchy and jurisdiction determine the proper forum before questions of track allocation within proceedings.","B":"Correct. The civil court structure allocates first-instance work between the County Court and High Court, with legislation, CPR rules and practice directions guiding where proceedings should be started and managed.","C":"Incorrect. The High Court has no contract jurisdiction does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Court hierarchy and jurisdiction determine the proper forum before questions of track allocation within proceedings.","D":"Incorrect. Parties can choose any criminal court for a civil claim does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Court hierarchy and jurisdiction determine the proper forum before questions of track allocation within proceedings.","E":"Incorrect. Only tribunals hear civil disputes does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Court hierarchy and jurisdiction determine the proper forum before questions of track allocation within proceedings."},"primary_legal_authority":"County Courts Act 1984; Senior Courts Act 1981; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 7 and relevant practice directions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Court hierarchy and jurisdiction determine the proper forum before questions of track allocation within proceedings.","common_trap":"Confusing court jurisdiction with either appellate hierarchy or case-management track.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d76d4534b77e9cda"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0928","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Rule of law — legality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority claims it may interfere with a person's legal rights merely because doing so would be administratively convenient, although it identifies no statutory, prerogative or common-law power.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Government bodies possess every power not expressly prohibited.","B":"Administrative convenience overrides statutory rights.","C":"Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action; executive convenience alone does not supply legal power.","D":"The rule of law applies only to courts.","E":"A public body can create legal authority retrospectively by asserting it."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The principle of legality requires public authorities to identify lawful power for action affecting legal rights. Executive bodies do not enjoy the private person's general freedom to act merely because no prohibition is identified.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Government bodies possess every power not expressly prohibited does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Government action must be grounded in law rather than convenience alone.","B":"Incorrect. Administrative convenience overrides statutory rights does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Government action must be grounded in law rather than convenience alone.","C":"Correct. The principle of legality requires public authorities to identify lawful power for action affecting legal rights. Executive bodies do not enjoy the private person's general freedom to act merely because no prohibition is identified.","D":"Incorrect. The rule of law applies only to courts does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Government action must be grounded in law rather than convenience alone.","E":"Incorrect. A public body can create legal authority retrospectively by asserting it does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Government action must be grounded in law rather than convenience alone."},"primary_legal_authority":"Entick v Carrington (1765) 19 St Tr 1029; rule-of-law principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Government action must be grounded in law rather than convenience alone.","common_trap":"Applying a private-law freedom principle to the exercise of public coercive power.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b3f7b1d01c7bd87d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0929","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — incorporation by reference","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A duly executed will refers to a separate written memorandum that already existed when the will was executed and describes it sufficiently clearly. The testator intended the memorandum to form part of the testamentary disposition.\n\nWhich doctrine may permit the memorandum's terms to take effect with the will?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"Lapse.","C":"Mutual wills automatically.","D":"Incorporation by reference, if the common-law requirements are satisfied.","E":"Intestacy."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A document not itself executed as a will may be incorporated if it existed when the will was executed, is referred to by the will, and is sufficiently identified, subject to the doctrine's requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Ademption does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Incorporation by reference is a narrow route by which an existing identified document can be treated as part of the testamentary instrument.","B":"Incorrect. Lapse does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Incorporation by reference is a narrow route by which an existing identified document can be treated as part of the testamentary instrument.","C":"Incorrect. Mutual wills automatically does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Incorporation by reference is a narrow route by which an existing identified document can be treated as part of the testamentary instrument.","D":"Correct. A document not itself executed as a will may be incorporated if it existed when the will was executed, is referred to by the will, and is sufficiently identified, subject to the doctrine's requirements.","E":"Incorrect. Intestacy does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Incorporation by reference is a narrow route by which an existing identified document can be treated as part of the testamentary instrument."},"primary_legal_authority":"Incorporation-by-reference principles; Wills Act 1837 formalities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Incorporation by reference is a narrow route by which an existing identified document can be treated as part of the testamentary instrument.","common_trap":"Assuming every external document mentioned after execution can alter a will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee204dee685853ae"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0930","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — assent: effect on legal estate","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives have completed the necessary administration work concerning a registered parcel of estate land and execute an effective assent of that land to the beneficiary entitled under the will.\n\nWhat is the principal legal function of the assent?","options":{"A":"It is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting the deceased's estate or interest in the beneficiary, subject to applicable registration requirements.","B":"It creates the beneficiary's entitlement under the will for the first time.","C":"It is a contract for sale requiring the beneficiary to pay market value.","D":"It automatically settles every outstanding estate liability.","E":"It operates only as a receipt and can never transfer an interest in land."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An assent is the mechanism by which personal representatives vest estate property in the person entitled to it; for registered land, registration requirements must then be observed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An assent is the mechanism by which personal representatives vest estate property in the person entitled to it; for registered land, registration requirements must then be observed.","B":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The beneficiary's underlying entitlement and the personal representatives' later vesting instrument are distinct concepts.","C":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The beneficiary's underlying entitlement and the personal representatives' later vesting instrument are distinct concepts.","D":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The beneficiary's underlying entitlement and the personal representatives' later vesting instrument are distinct concepts.","E":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The beneficiary's underlying entitlement and the personal representatives' later vesting instrument are distinct concepts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 36; Land Registration Rules","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The beneficiary's underlying entitlement and the personal representatives' later vesting instrument are distinct concepts.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6995108202e8e50d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0931","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — potentially exempt transfers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes an outright lifetime gift to another individual. The gift is not immediately chargeable under the relevant lifetime transfer regime and no exemption applies.\n\nWhich is the best general IHT characterisation?","options":{"A":"The gift can be a potentially exempt transfer and may become fully exempt if the donor survives the statutory period, subject to the IHT rules.","B":"Every lifetime gift to an individual is immediately taxed at the death rate.","C":"The gift is automatically exempt on the day it is made.","D":"Potentially exempt transfers apply only to companies.","E":"The recipient pays VAT instead of any IHT consequences."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An outright qualifying transfer by an individual to another individual is generally a potentially exempt transfer. Survival for the statutory period can render it exempt, while death within that period can bring it into the death-tax computation subject to the legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An outright qualifying transfer by an individual to another individual is generally a potentially exempt transfer. Survival for the statutory period can render it exempt, while death within that period can bring it into the death-tax computation subject to the legislation.","B":"Incorrect. Every lifetime gift to an individual is immediately taxed at the death rate does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. PET treatment defers the ultimate exemption question by reference to survival rather than imposing immediate lifetime IHT in the ordinary case.","C":"Incorrect. The gift is automatically exempt on the day it is made does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. PET treatment defers the ultimate exemption question by reference to survival rather than imposing immediate lifetime IHT in the ordinary case.","D":"Incorrect. Potentially exempt transfers apply only to companies does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. PET treatment defers the ultimate exemption question by reference to survival rather than imposing immediate lifetime IHT in the ordinary case.","E":"Incorrect. The recipient pays VAT instead of any IHT consequences does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. PET treatment defers the ultimate exemption question by reference to survival rather than imposing immediate lifetime IHT in the ordinary case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, ss 3A and 7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PET treatment defers the ultimate exemption question by reference to survival rather than imposing immediate lifetime IHT in the ordinary case.","common_trap":"Confusing a potentially exempt transfer with an immediately chargeable transfer or an immediate absolute exemption.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ab0bbbabef6af681"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0932","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — client account interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds a substantial amount of client money for a lengthy period in circumstances where interest is generated.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"All interest automatically belongs to the firm.","B":"The firm must account to the client or third party for a fair sum of interest in accordance with the SRA Accounts Rules and its interest policy, subject to any permitted written agreement.","C":"Interest can never be paid on client money.","D":"The client receives the Bank of England base rate automatically in every case.","E":"The firm may keep interest without considering fairness if the money is in a general client account."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require firms to account fairly for interest on client money and permit an alternative arrangement where agreed in writing with the client or third party. Firms should have a policy producing a fair outcome.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. All interest automatically belongs to the firm does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Interest treatment reflects beneficial ownership and fairness rather than an automatic entitlement of either side to a fixed statutory rate.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require firms to account fairly for interest on client money and permit an alternative arrangement where agreed in writing with the client or third party. Firms should have a policy producing a fair outcome.","C":"Incorrect. Interest can never be paid on client money does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Interest treatment reflects beneficial ownership and fairness rather than an automatic entitlement of either side to a fixed statutory rate.","D":"Incorrect. The client receives the Bank of England base rate automatically in every case does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Interest treatment reflects beneficial ownership and fairness rather than an automatic entitlement of either side to a fixed statutory rate.","E":"Incorrect. The firm may keep interest without considering fairness if the money is in a general client account does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Interest treatment reflects beneficial ownership and fairness rather than an automatic entitlement of either side to a fixed statutory rate."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Interest treatment reflects beneficial ownership and fairness rather than an automatic entitlement of either side to a fixed statutory rate.","common_trap":"Assuming either that the firm owns all interest or that one universal rate applies regardless of circumstances.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"07712b8d2b98857e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0933","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trusts — certainty of intention","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A property owner tells a friend, 'I hope you will use this money to help my children,' but transfers the money outright without language or circumstances imposing an obligation.\n\nWhich trust requirement is most directly in issue?","options":{"A":"Certainty of subject matter only.","B":"The beneficiary principle is irrelevant whenever children are mentioned.","C":"Certainty of intention: precatory words of hope or wish do not necessarily demonstrate an intention to impose binding trust obligations.","D":"Every moral wish automatically creates an express trust.","E":"A trust requires consideration from the beneficiaries."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An express trust requires a sufficiently certain intention to impose binding fiduciary obligations. Merely precatory language can indicate a gift accompanied by a moral wish rather than a trust, depending on the instrument as a whole.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Certainty of subject matter only does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The court asks whether the settlor objectively intended an enforceable obligation, not merely whether trust vocabulary was used.","B":"Incorrect. The beneficiary principle is irrelevant whenever children are mentioned does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The court asks whether the settlor objectively intended an enforceable obligation, not merely whether trust vocabulary was used.","C":"Correct. An express trust requires a sufficiently certain intention to impose binding fiduciary obligations. Merely precatory language can indicate a gift accompanied by a moral wish rather than a trust, depending on the instrument as a whole.","D":"Incorrect. Every moral wish automatically creates an express trust does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The court asks whether the settlor objectively intended an enforceable obligation, not merely whether trust vocabulary was used.","E":"Incorrect. A trust requires consideration from the beneficiaries does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The court asks whether the settlor objectively intended an enforceable obligation, not merely whether trust vocabulary was used."},"primary_legal_authority":"Lambe v Eames (1871) LR 6 Ch App 597; Re Adams and Kensington Vestry (1884) 27 Ch D 394.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The court asks whether the settlor objectively intended an enforceable obligation, not merely whether trust vocabulary was used.","common_trap":"Turning non-binding hopes into fiduciary duties without establishing objective trust intention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2af9c34abe7c2be2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0934","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trusts — certainty of subject matter","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a trust of 'most of my shares in X plc' without identifying how many shares or which proportion is to be held on trust.\n\nWhich is the principal validity problem?","options":{"A":"The trust necessarily fails because shares can never be trust property.","B":"The problem is solely lack of consideration.","C":"The trust is valid because 'most' always means exactly 51%.","D":"The trust may fail for uncertainty of subject matter because the quantum of property subject to the trust is not sufficiently defined.","E":"The trustees may choose any amount after the settlor's death."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The property or beneficial entitlement subject to an express trust must be sufficiently certain. A vague description of the amount to be held can prevent identification of the trust fund or beneficial shares.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The trust necessarily fails because shares can never be trust property does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Certainty must extend to what property is held and, where relevant, the extent of beneficial entitlements.","B":"Incorrect. The problem is solely lack of consideration does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Certainty must extend to what property is held and, where relevant, the extent of beneficial entitlements.","C":"Incorrect. The trust is valid because 'most' always means exactly 51% does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Certainty must extend to what property is held and, where relevant, the extent of beneficial entitlements.","D":"Correct. The property or beneficial entitlement subject to an express trust must be sufficiently certain. A vague description of the amount to be held can prevent identification of the trust fund or beneficial shares.","E":"Incorrect. The trustees may choose any amount after the settlor's death does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Certainty must extend to what property is held and, where relevant, the extent of beneficial entitlements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Palmer v Simmonds (1854) 2 Drew 221; certainty-of-subject-matter principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Certainty must extend to what property is held and, where relevant, the extent of beneficial entitlements.","common_trap":"Assuming fungibility of shares cures an undefined quantity rather than an identification issue.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bc41f43ebd72444d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0935","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — restrictions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Registered land is held by two trustees for beneficiaries. A Form A restriction is entered on the register.\n\nWhat is the restriction principally designed to do?","options":{"A":"It creates a new beneficial tenancy in common by itself.","B":"It prohibits every sale of the land.","C":"It guarantees the beneficiaries' shares are equal.","D":"It converts registered land into unregistered land.","E":"It regulates registration of dispositions by signalling that a sole proprietor generally cannot give a valid receipt for capital money where the beneficial ownership is held as tenants in common."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A Form A restriction reflects the limitation on a sole trustee's ability to overreach beneficial interests by receiving capital money alone. It controls registration of relevant dispositions without itself creating the underlying beneficial interests.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It creates a new beneficial tenancy in common by itself does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions regulate the circumstances in which dispositions may be registered; they do not themselves create the equitable ownership structure.","B":"Incorrect. It prohibits every sale of the land does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions regulate the circumstances in which dispositions may be registered; they do not themselves create the equitable ownership structure.","C":"Incorrect. It guarantees the beneficiaries' shares are equal does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions regulate the circumstances in which dispositions may be registered; they do not themselves create the equitable ownership structure.","D":"Incorrect. It converts registered land into unregistered land does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions regulate the circumstances in which dispositions may be registered; they do not themselves create the equitable ownership structure.","E":"Correct. A Form A restriction reflects the limitation on a sole trustee's ability to overreach beneficial interests by receiving capital money alone. It controls registration of relevant dispositions without itself creating the underlying beneficial interests."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002; Land Registration Rules 2003; Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictions regulate the circumstances in which dispositions may be registered; they do not themselves create the equitable ownership structure.","common_trap":"Confusing a register control mechanism with the substantive source of beneficial co-ownership.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e1ced3d91b4af1e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0936","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — equity of redemption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A mortgage deed provides that the borrower may redeem the property only during one short window 30 years after the loan is made, although the commercial loan could otherwise be repaid much earlier.\n\nWhich equitable principle is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"A mortgage must not contain a provision that improperly clogs or fetters the mortgagor's equity of redemption.","B":"The lender may always make redemption practically impossible.","C":"The borrower has no equitable right to redeem after granting a legal mortgage.","D":"Every postponement of redemption is automatically void regardless of context.","E":"The clause converts the mortgage into a lease."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Equity treats a mortgage as security and protects the mortgagor's right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. A term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters that right may be invalid, although postponement is assessed in context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Equity treats a mortgage as security and protects the mortgagor's right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. A term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters that right may be invalid, although postponement is assessed in context.","B":"Incorrect. The lender may always make redemption practically impossible does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The security cannot be structured so that the contractual bargain destroys the essential equitable right to get the property back on redemption.","C":"Incorrect. The borrower has no equitable right to redeem after granting a legal mortgage does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The security cannot be structured so that the contractual bargain destroys the essential equitable right to get the property back on redemption.","D":"Incorrect. Every postponement of redemption is automatically void regardless of context does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The security cannot be structured so that the contractual bargain destroys the essential equitable right to get the property back on redemption.","E":"Incorrect. The clause converts the mortgage into a lease does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. The security cannot be structured so that the contractual bargain destroys the essential equitable right to get the property back on redemption."},"primary_legal_authority":"Noakes & Co Ltd v Rice [1902] AC 24; Knightsbridge Estates Trust Ltd v Byrne [1940] AC 613.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The security cannot be structured so that the contractual bargain destroys the essential equitable right to get the property back on redemption.","common_trap":"Assuming either absolute lender freedom or that every delayed redemption term is necessarily invalid.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"008ae5be8fae3a83"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0937","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-contract searches — local authority search","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is purchasing residential property and wants information about matters such as local land charges and certain local authority records affecting the property.\n\nWhich search is principally relevant?","options":{"A":"A bankruptcy search against the seller only.","B":"A local authority search, comprising the relevant local land charges and local authority enquiries.","C":"A company search at Companies House.","D":"A criminal records check.","E":"An OS1 priority search alone."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Local authority searches provide specified information about local land charges and local-authority matters such as planning, building regulation and highway-related entries within the scope of the standard enquiries.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A bankruptcy search against the seller only does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Different conveyancing searches answer different risk questions; the local search focuses on public/local-authority matters affecting the property.","B":"Correct. Local authority searches provide specified information about local land charges and local-authority matters such as planning, building regulation and highway-related entries within the scope of the standard enquiries.","C":"Incorrect. A company search at Companies House does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Different conveyancing searches answer different risk questions; the local search focuses on public/local-authority matters affecting the property.","D":"Incorrect. A criminal records check does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Different conveyancing searches answer different risk questions; the local search focuses on public/local-authority matters affecting the property.","E":"Incorrect. An OS1 priority search alone does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Different conveyancing searches answer different risk questions; the local search focuses on public/local-authority matters affecting the property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Local Land Charges Act 1975; CON29/local-search conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Different conveyancing searches answer different risk questions; the local search focuses on public/local-authority matters affecting the property.","common_trap":"Assuming a title-priority or personal-insolvency search substitutes for local-authority due diligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5723f06a8b8e963d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0938","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Planning — change of use","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner proposes a material change in the use of a building. The proposed change is not protected by permitted development rights and does not fall within the same planning use class.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Planning permission is relevant only to construction of new buildings.","B":"Every change of occupier automatically requires planning permission.","C":"Planning permission may be required for a material change of use, subject to the statutory planning regime and the particular existing and proposed uses.","D":"A lease provision can itself grant public planning permission.","E":"Land Registry registration substitutes for planning permission."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Development includes material changes in the use of land or buildings. Whether permission is needed depends on materiality, use classes, permitted development rights and the relevant planning legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Planning permission is relevant only to construction of new buildings does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Planning control can apply to use changes even where no physical building works occur.","B":"Incorrect. Every change of occupier automatically requires planning permission does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Planning control can apply to use changes even where no physical building works occur.","C":"Correct. Development includes material changes in the use of land or buildings. Whether permission is needed depends on materiality, use classes, permitted development rights and the relevant planning legislation.","D":"Incorrect. A lease provision can itself grant public planning permission does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Planning control can apply to use changes even where no physical building works occur.","E":"Incorrect. Land Registry registration substitutes for planning permission does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Planning control can apply to use changes even where no physical building works occur."},"primary_legal_authority":"Town and Country Planning Act 1990, s 55; Town and Country Planning (Use Classes) Order 1987 and permitted-development regime.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Planning control can apply to use changes even where no physical building works occur.","common_trap":"Assuming planning law concerns only physical construction or that private contractual consent replaces public permission.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e14d03b5fb17b0d8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0939","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Criminal attempts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intends to commit an indictable offence and takes steps that go beyond mere preparation, but completion becomes impossible because an essential factual circumstance is absent.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Impossibility always prevents conviction for attempt.","B":"Attempt requires completion of every element of the full offence.","C":"Mere thoughts alone constitute an attempt.","D":"Factual impossibility does not necessarily prevent attempt liability if D has the required intent and has done an act more than merely preparatory.","E":"Attempt liability applies only to summary-only offences."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Criminal Attempts Act focuses on intent and conduct more than merely preparatory. Statutory impossibility rules mean a defendant can be guilty even though commission of the full offence was factually impossible on the circumstances as believed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Impossibility always prevents conviction for attempt does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Attempt liability addresses sufficiently advanced intentional criminal conduct even where completion cannot occur.","B":"Incorrect. Attempt requires completion of every element of the full offence does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Attempt liability addresses sufficiently advanced intentional criminal conduct even where completion cannot occur.","C":"Incorrect. Mere thoughts alone constitute an attempt does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Attempt liability addresses sufficiently advanced intentional criminal conduct even where completion cannot occur.","D":"Correct. The Criminal Attempts Act focuses on intent and conduct more than merely preparatory. Statutory impossibility rules mean a defendant can be guilty even though commission of the full offence was factually impossible on the circumstances as believed.","E":"Incorrect. Attempt liability applies only to summary-only offences does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Attempt liability addresses sufficiently advanced intentional criminal conduct even where completion cannot occur."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981, ss 1 and 4; R v Shivpuri [1987] AC 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Attempt liability addresses sufficiently advanced intentional criminal conduct even where completion cannot occur.","common_trap":"Reviving the old assumption that factual impossibility necessarily defeats attempt liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1df75478cba3b83a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0940","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal legal aid — interests of justice and means","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant in criminal proceedings asks whether public funding is automatically available simply because the allegation is criminal.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every criminal defendant automatically receives free representation regardless of circumstances.","B":"Criminal legal aid is determined solely by the prosecutor.","C":"Means are the only possible consideration and merits are irrelevant.","D":"Criminal legal aid is part of civil legal aid and has no separate rules.","E":"Criminal legal aid is governed by the applicable statutory scheme, including the interests-of-justice test and, where applicable, financial eligibility requirements; it is not automatic merely because proceedings are criminal."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Criminal legal aid depends on the statutory representation scheme. Eligibility commonly requires satisfaction of the interests-of-justice criteria and applicable means rules, with different arrangements at stages such as police-station advice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every criminal defendant automatically receives free representation regardless of circumstances does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Public funding in criminal proceedings is structured by statutory eligibility criteria rather than attaching automatically to every charge.","B":"Incorrect. Criminal legal aid is determined solely by the prosecutor does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Public funding in criminal proceedings is structured by statutory eligibility criteria rather than attaching automatically to every charge.","C":"Incorrect. Means are the only possible consideration and merits are irrelevant does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Public funding in criminal proceedings is structured by statutory eligibility criteria rather than attaching automatically to every charge.","D":"Incorrect. Criminal legal aid is part of civil legal aid and has no separate rules does not reflect the governing rule on these facts. Public funding in criminal proceedings is structured by statutory eligibility criteria rather than attaching automatically to every charge.","E":"Correct. Criminal legal aid depends on the statutory representation scheme. Eligibility commonly requires satisfaction of the interests-of-justice criteria and applicable means rules, with different arrangements at stages such as police-station advice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012; Criminal Legal Aid regulations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public funding in criminal proceedings is structured by statutory eligibility criteria rather than attaching automatically to every charge.","common_trap":"Assuming the criminal nature of proceedings alone guarantees publicly funded representation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"118d66058a056869"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0941","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Company decision-making — class rights","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company proposes to vary rights attached to a class of shares. Its articles do not prescribe a different procedure and the statutory default regime applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Variation requires the consent in writing of holders of at least three-quarters in nominal value of the issued shares of the class or a special resolution passed at a separate class meeting, subject to the statutory regime.","B":"An ordinary board resolution is always sufficient.","C":"The rights can never be varied once shares are issued.","D":"Unanimous consent of every company member is always required.","E":"A simple majority of all shareholders, regardless of class, automatically varies the rights."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act provides a statutory procedure for varying class rights where the company's constitution does not provide otherwise, including 75% class consent or a class special resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act provides a statutory procedure for varying class rights where the company's constitution does not provide otherwise, including 75% class consent or a class special resolution.","B":"Incorrect. An ordinary board resolution is always sufficient does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Class rights are protected through class-specific approval rather than ordinary company-wide decision-making.","C":"Incorrect. The rights can never be varied once shares are issued does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Class rights are protected through class-specific approval rather than ordinary company-wide decision-making.","D":"Incorrect. Unanimous consent of every company member is always required does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Class rights are protected through class-specific approval rather than ordinary company-wide decision-making.","E":"Incorrect. A simple majority of all shareholders, regardless of class, automatically varies the rights does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Class rights are protected through class-specific approval rather than ordinary company-wide decision-making."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 630–634.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Class rights are protected through class-specific approval rather than ordinary company-wide decision-making.","common_trap":"Confusing general shareholder voting with the special statutory protection given to a class.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"30fd4011dd3267dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0942","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — substantial property transactions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to acquire a substantial non-cash asset from one of its directors. No statutory exception applies.\n\nWhich approval issue should the solicitor identify?","options":{"A":"Only the director concerned needs to approve it.","B":"Member approval is generally required before the company enters into the arrangement, subject to the statutory thresholds and exceptions.","C":"No approval is needed because the transaction involves property rather than cash.","D":"Approval is required only after completion.","E":"The transaction is automatically void and incapable of ratification."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Companies Act provisions regulate substantial property transactions involving directors and connected persons, generally requiring member approval before the arrangement is entered into unless an exception applies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Only the director concerned needs to approve it does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The regime protects members against significant asset dealings between the company and directors.","B":"Correct. Companies Act provisions regulate substantial property transactions involving directors and connected persons, generally requiring member approval before the arrangement is entered into unless an exception applies.","C":"Incorrect. No approval is needed because the transaction involves property rather than cash does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The regime protects members against significant asset dealings between the company and directors.","D":"Incorrect. Approval is required only after completion does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The regime protects members against significant asset dealings between the company and directors.","E":"Incorrect. The transaction is automatically void and incapable of ratification does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The regime protects members against significant asset dealings between the company and directors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The regime protects members against significant asset dealings between the company and directors.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary board authority is sufficient for a regulated director transaction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6ef696ac7027d803"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0943","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — fiduciary duty to account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Without the consent of the other partners, a partner personally earns a secret commission from a transaction concerning the partnership business.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The commission belongs to the partner because it was paid directly to them.","B":"The firm can recover only if it proves negligence.","C":"The partner must account to the firm for the private benefit obtained from the partnership transaction.","D":"Partners owe no fiduciary obligations to one another.","E":"The commission becomes company share capital."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Partners must account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from transactions concerning the partnership or use of partnership property, name or business connection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The commission belongs to the partner because it was paid directly to them does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A partner cannot retain an unauthorised private profit generated through the partnership opportunity.","B":"Incorrect. The firm can recover only if it proves negligence does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A partner cannot retain an unauthorised private profit generated through the partnership opportunity.","C":"Correct. Partners must account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from transactions concerning the partnership or use of partnership property, name or business connection.","D":"Incorrect. Partners owe no fiduciary obligations to one another does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A partner cannot retain an unauthorised private profit generated through the partnership opportunity.","E":"Incorrect. The commission becomes company share capital does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A partner cannot retain an unauthorised private profit generated through the partnership opportunity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 29.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A partner cannot retain an unauthorised private profit generated through the partnership opportunity.","common_trap":"Treating receipt in the partner's own name as defeating the statutory duty to account.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f254caa80da59118"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0944","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — administration moratorium","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company enters administration. An unsecured creditor wants immediately to commence fresh proceedings and enforce against company property without consent or court permission.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Administration has no effect on creditor enforcement.","B":"Every creditor may seize company assets immediately.","C":"The moratorium permanently extinguishes all debts.","D":"The administration moratorium generally restricts commencement or continuation of proceedings and enforcement without the administrator's consent or the court's permission.","E":"Only shareholders are affected by administration."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Administration creates a statutory moratorium restricting specified proceedings and enforcement so that the administration can pursue its statutory purpose, subject to consent or permission mechanisms.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Administration has no effect on creditor enforcement does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The moratorium stays enforcement; it does not extinguish underlying creditor rights.","B":"Incorrect. Every creditor may seize company assets immediately does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The moratorium stays enforcement; it does not extinguish underlying creditor rights.","C":"Incorrect. The moratorium permanently extinguishes all debts does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The moratorium stays enforcement; it does not extinguish underlying creditor rights.","D":"Correct. Administration creates a statutory moratorium restricting specified proceedings and enforcement so that the administration can pursue its statutory purpose, subject to consent or permission mechanisms.","E":"Incorrect. Only shareholders are affected by administration does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The moratorium stays enforcement; it does not extinguish underlying creditor rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, Schedule B1, including paras 42–43.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The moratorium stays enforcement; it does not extinguish underlying creditor rights.","common_trap":"Confusing a temporary collective-procedure stay with discharge of the debt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"89c1137332bfa96b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0945","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Limitation — acknowledgment of debt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A debtor makes a signed written acknowledgment of a debt before a limitation defence has become conclusive. The creditor is considering the effect on limitation.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Acknowledgment can never affect limitation.","B":"Any oral remark automatically restarts every limitation period.","C":"Acknowledgment immediately satisfies the debt in full.","D":"Only a court judgment can affect time running.","E":"A qualifying written and signed acknowledgment can cause the right of action to be treated as accruing afresh for limitation purposes, subject to the statutory rules."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Limitation Act provides that qualifying acknowledgment or part payment can cause specified rights of action to accrue afresh, with acknowledgment required to be in writing and signed by the person making it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Acknowledgment can never affect limitation does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Limitation can be affected by statutorily defined post-accrual conduct.","B":"Incorrect. Any oral remark automatically restarts every limitation period does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Limitation can be affected by statutorily defined post-accrual conduct.","C":"Incorrect. Acknowledgment immediately satisfies the debt in full does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Limitation can be affected by statutorily defined post-accrual conduct.","D":"Incorrect. Only a court judgment can affect time running does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Limitation can be affected by statutorily defined post-accrual conduct.","E":"Correct. The Limitation Act provides that qualifying acknowledgment or part payment can cause specified rights of action to accrue afresh, with acknowledgment required to be in writing and signed by the person making it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, ss 29–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Limitation can be affected by statutorily defined post-accrual conduct.","common_trap":"Assuming limitation time is invariably immutable once it begins.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8b4614ad93ca4917"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0946","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Parties — adding or substituting parties","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After proceedings have begun, it becomes apparent that another person needs to be joined so that the court can resolve all matters in dispute. Limitation issues are not presently material.\n\nWhich CPR provision principally governs adding or substituting parties?","options":{"A":"CPR Part 19, under which the court may add a new party where the statutory procedural test is satisfied.","B":"CPR Part 36.","C":"CPR Part 71.","D":"The Insolvency Rules only.","E":"The criminal procedure rules."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 19 governs changes of parties and permits addition where, for example, it is desirable to resolve all matters in dispute or there is an issue involving the new and existing parties connected to the dispute.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 19 governs changes of parties and permits addition where, for example, it is desirable to resolve all matters in dispute or there is an issue involving the new and existing parties connected to the dispute.","B":"Incorrect. CPR Part 36 does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Joinder is governed by a specific procedural regime designed to permit effective resolution of connected disputes.","C":"Incorrect. CPR Part 71 does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Joinder is governed by a specific procedural regime designed to permit effective resolution of connected disputes.","D":"Incorrect. The Insolvency Rules only does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Joinder is governed by a specific procedural regime designed to permit effective resolution of connected disputes.","E":"Incorrect. The criminal procedure rules does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Joinder is governed by a specific procedural regime designed to permit effective resolution of connected disputes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 19.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Joinder is governed by a specific procedural regime designed to permit effective resolution of connected disputes.","common_trap":"Confusing party joinder with settlement offers or enforcement information.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8be9a09c90ff93c3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0947","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — burden of proof in civil claims","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant alleges breach of contract and seeks damages. No statutory reversal of the burden applies.\n\nWhich is the ordinary position?","options":{"A":"The defendant must disprove the claim beyond reasonable doubt.","B":"The claimant bears the legal burden of proving the essential elements of the claim on the balance of probabilities.","C":"The claimant must prove the claim beyond reasonable doubt.","D":"The court presumes liability whenever proceedings are issued.","E":"The burden is decided by whichever party has more documents."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"In ordinary civil litigation the party asserting the essential elements of a cause of action bears the legal burden, and facts are generally proved on the balance of probabilities.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The defendant must disprove the claim beyond reasonable doubt does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The civil standard asks whether the relevant fact is more likely than not.","B":"Correct. In ordinary civil litigation the party asserting the essential elements of a cause of action bears the legal burden, and facts are generally proved on the balance of probabilities.","C":"Incorrect. The claimant must prove the claim beyond reasonable doubt does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The civil standard asks whether the relevant fact is more likely than not.","D":"Incorrect. The court presumes liability whenever proceedings are issued does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The civil standard asks whether the relevant fact is more likely than not.","E":"Incorrect. The burden is decided by whichever party has more documents does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The civil standard asks whether the relevant fact is more likely than not."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re B (Children) [2008] UKHL 35; general civil evidence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The civil standard asks whether the relevant fact is more likely than not.","common_trap":"Importing the criminal standard or reversing the ordinary burden without legal basis.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a9232dfca24c0212"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0948","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Money laundering — source of funds and source of wealth","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A conveyancing client provides identification documents, but the proposed purchase is funded by an unexpectedly large payment from an opaque overseas structure. The transaction falls within the regulated sector.\n\nWhich is the best general response?","options":{"A":"A passport conclusively resolves every AML risk.","B":"The solicitor must accept any funds once the client's name is known.","C":"Identity verification alone is insufficient; the firm should conduct risk-based scrutiny, including source-of-funds and, where appropriate, source-of-wealth enquiries and enhanced due diligence.","D":"Source-of-funds enquiries are prohibited by confidentiality.","E":"Only cash transactions can create money-laundering risk."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Customer due diligence is risk-based. Higher-risk circumstances can require enhanced due diligence and closer examination of the source and destination of funds, and potentially source of wealth, rather than mere identity verification.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A passport conclusively resolves every AML risk does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Knowing who the client is does not by itself explain whether transaction funding is legitimate.","B":"Incorrect. The solicitor must accept any funds once the client's name is known does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Knowing who the client is does not by itself explain whether transaction funding is legitimate.","C":"Correct. Customer due diligence is risk-based. Higher-risk circumstances can require enhanced due diligence and closer examination of the source and destination of funds, and potentially source of wealth, rather than mere identity verification.","D":"Incorrect. Source-of-funds enquiries are prohibited by confidentiality does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Knowing who the client is does not by itself explain whether transaction funding is legitimate.","E":"Incorrect. Only cash transactions can create money-laundering risk does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Knowing who the client is does not by itself explain whether transaction funding is legitimate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Money Laundering Regulations 2017, including regs 28 and 33; Legal Sector Affinity Group guidance.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowing who the client is does not by itself explain whether transaction funding is legitimate.","common_trap":"Equating identity verification with completion of the entire AML risk assessment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"60a34c87f3c3bcc9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0949","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — undertakings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor gives a professional undertaking to discharge a mortgage from completion funds by a specified date.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An undertaking is merely aspirational and may be ignored.","B":"A solicitor may give any undertaking on behalf of a third party without authority.","C":"Undertakings bind only if supported by contractual consideration.","D":"The solicitor must perform the undertaking within the agreed timescale or, if none is stated, within a reasonable time, and should not give an undertaking they cannot control or fulfil.","E":"The undertaking ends automatically when the client changes solicitors."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"SRA conduct rules require solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed timescale or a reasonable time and to avoid commitments outside their ability or authority to fulfil.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An undertaking is merely aspirational and may be ignored does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Professional undertakings carry regulatory consequences independently of ordinary contractual analysis.","B":"Incorrect. A solicitor may give any undertaking on behalf of a third party without authority does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Professional undertakings carry regulatory consequences independently of ordinary contractual analysis.","C":"Incorrect. Undertakings bind only if supported by contractual consideration does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Professional undertakings carry regulatory consequences independently of ordinary contractual analysis.","D":"Correct. SRA conduct rules require solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed timescale or a reasonable time and to avoid commitments outside their ability or authority to fulfil.","E":"Incorrect. The undertaking ends automatically when the client changes solicitors does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Professional undertakings carry regulatory consequences independently of ordinary contractual analysis."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional undertakings carry regulatory consequences independently of ordinary contractual analysis.","common_trap":"Treating an undertaking as an informal promise with no professional force.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e1112624e5f3d589"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0950","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — promisor's existing contractual duty to a third party","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A promises B payment if B performs an obligation that B already owes under a separate contract with C. B performs that obligation.\n\nWhich is the best general position on consideration?","options":{"A":"It can never be consideration because B was already bound to someone.","B":"Only fresh physical work can constitute consideration.","C":"Consideration must move from C rather than B.","D":"A's promise is enforceable only by deed in every case.","E":"Performance of an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can constitute good consideration for A's promise."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Performance of an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can amount to consideration for a new promise because the promisor obtains the requested performance and the promisee incurs or performs the relevant legal obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It can never be consideration because B was already bound to someone does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The rule differs from some situations involving an existing duty owed to the same promisor.","B":"Incorrect. Only fresh physical work can constitute consideration does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The rule differs from some situations involving an existing duty owed to the same promisor.","C":"Incorrect. Consideration must move from C rather than B does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The rule differs from some situations involving an existing duty owed to the same promisor.","D":"Incorrect. A's promise is enforceable only by deed in every case does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The rule differs from some situations involving an existing duty owed to the same promisor.","E":"Correct. Performance of an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can amount to consideration for a new promise because the promisor obtains the requested performance and the promisee incurs or performs the relevant legal obligation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Shadwell v Shadwell (1860) 9 CB (NS) 159; Scotson v Pegg (1861) 6 H & N 295.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The rule differs from some situations involving an existing duty owed to the same promisor.","common_trap":"Confusing a duty owed to a third party with the existing-duty rule between the same contracting parties.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f2485047e635321"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0951","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Duress — economic duress requirements","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier threatens unlawfully to breach a critical contract unless the customer pays substantially more. The customer has no practical alternative, agrees under protest and promptly challenges the variation once the pressure ends.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Economic duress, requiring illegitimate pressure that causes the claimant to enter the transaction and leaves no reasonable practical alternative in the relevant sense.","B":"Undue influence automatically, because every commercial threat is relational influence.","C":"Frustration, because performance became more expensive.","D":"Mistake, because the customer knew exactly what was happening.","E":"Promissory estoppel as a cause of action for damages."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure is a significant cause of consent and the circumstances satisfy the demanding common-law test, with protest and practical alternatives relevant to the analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure is a significant cause of consent and the circumstances satisfy the demanding common-law test, with protest and practical alternatives relevant to the analysis.","B":"Incorrect. Undue influence automatically, because every commercial threat is relational influence does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The doctrine addresses coerced contractual consent caused by illegitimate commercial pressure.","C":"Incorrect. Frustration, because performance became more expensive does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The doctrine addresses coerced contractual consent caused by illegitimate commercial pressure.","D":"Incorrect. Mistake, because the customer knew exactly what was happening does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The doctrine addresses coerced contractual consent caused by illegitimate commercial pressure.","E":"Incorrect. Promissory estoppel as a cause of action for damages does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The doctrine addresses coerced contractual consent caused by illegitimate commercial pressure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Universe Tankships Inc v ITWF [1983] 1 AC 366; DSND Subsea Ltd v Petroleum Geo Services ASA [2000] BLR 530.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The doctrine addresses coerced contractual consent caused by illegitimate commercial pressure.","common_trap":"Treating hard bargaining alone as duress or using doctrines directed at different defects in consent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9311aaf1a314c1f4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0952","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Agency — undisclosed principal","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An authorised agent enters a simple commercial contract in the agent's own name without revealing that they act for a principal. The contract does not exclude intervention by an undisclosed principal and is not personal to the agent.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An undisclosed principal can never acquire rights or liabilities.","B":"Subject to exceptions, the undisclosed principal may intervene and enforce, and may be liable on, the contract made within the agent's actual authority.","C":"Only apparent authority matters because the third party did not know of the principal.","D":"The contract is automatically void for mistake.","E":"The agent is never personally liable."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Common-law agency permits an undisclosed principal in many cases to sue and be sued on a contract made by an authorised agent, subject to exceptions such as inconsistent contract terms or personal considerations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An undisclosed principal can never acquire rights or liabilities does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Actual authority can support intervention even though the third party was unaware of the principal.","B":"Correct. Common-law agency permits an undisclosed principal in many cases to sue and be sued on a contract made by an authorised agent, subject to exceptions such as inconsistent contract terms or personal considerations.","C":"Incorrect. Only apparent authority matters because the third party did not know of the principal does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Actual authority can support intervention even though the third party was unaware of the principal.","D":"Incorrect. The contract is automatically void for mistake does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Actual authority can support intervention even though the third party was unaware of the principal.","E":"Incorrect. The agent is never personally liable does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Actual authority can support intervention even though the third party was unaware of the principal."},"primary_legal_authority":"Siu Yin Kwan v Eastern Insurance Co Ltd [1994] 2 AC 199; undisclosed-principal doctrine.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Actual authority can support intervention even though the third party was unaware of the principal.","common_trap":"Assuming disclosure is an absolute prerequisite to principal liability or enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9877a7be7910be76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0953","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — psychiatric harm to secondary victims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person witnesses a sudden accident involving a close family member and suffers a recognised psychiatric illness. The witness was not personally endangered.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Foreseeability of any distress alone is sufficient.","B":"Psychiatric harm is never compensable without physical injury.","C":"As a secondary victim, the claimant must satisfy the special control mechanisms for psychiatric harm, including the required relationship, proximity and means of perception, as developed by authority.","D":"The claimant is automatically treated as a primary victim because a relative was endangered.","E":"Ordinary property-damage rules determine the claim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Secondary-victim claims for negligently caused psychiatric injury are subject to restrictive control mechanisms concerning close ties of love and affection, proximity in time and space, direct perception and the nature of the event, alongside recognised psychiatric illness.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Foreseeability of any distress alone is sufficient does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Secondary victims face additional limits beyond ordinary foreseeability.","B":"Incorrect. Psychiatric harm is never compensable without physical injury does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Secondary victims face additional limits beyond ordinary foreseeability.","C":"Correct. Secondary-victim claims for negligently caused psychiatric injury are subject to restrictive control mechanisms concerning close ties of love and affection, proximity in time and space, direct perception and the nature of the event, alongside recognised psychiatric illness.","D":"Incorrect. The claimant is automatically treated as a primary victim because a relative was endangered does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Secondary victims face additional limits beyond ordinary foreseeability.","E":"Incorrect. Ordinary property-damage rules determine the claim does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Secondary victims face additional limits beyond ordinary foreseeability."},"primary_legal_authority":"Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire Police [1992] 1 AC 310; Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust [2024] UKSC 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Secondary victims face additional limits beyond ordinary foreseeability.","common_trap":"Applying the primary-victim test merely because the claimant is closely related to the injured person.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f35ad7769a642267"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0954","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Employers' liability — safe system of work","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employer delegates workplace safety arrangements to a competent manager. The manager negligently establishes an unsafe system and an employee is injured.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Delegation to a competent manager automatically eliminates employer responsibility.","B":"An employer owes no duty concerning systems of work.","C":"Only vicarious liability can ever arise in workplace injury.","D":"The employer's personal duty to take reasonable care for a safe system of work is non-delegable in the sense that delegation of performance does not itself discharge the employer's duty.","E":"Employees assume all workplace risks by accepting employment."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"An employer owes personal duties concerning competent staff, adequate plant and a safe system and place of work. The employer may delegate performance but cannot escape the duty merely by delegating it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Delegation to a competent manager automatically eliminates employer responsibility does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Delegation of a safety task is not equivalent to delegation of legal responsibility for the employer's own duty.","B":"Incorrect. An employer owes no duty concerning systems of work does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Delegation of a safety task is not equivalent to delegation of legal responsibility for the employer's own duty.","C":"Incorrect. Only vicarious liability can ever arise in workplace injury does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Delegation of a safety task is not equivalent to delegation of legal responsibility for the employer's own duty.","D":"Correct. An employer owes personal duties concerning competent staff, adequate plant and a safe system and place of work. The employer may delegate performance but cannot escape the duty merely by delegating it.","E":"Incorrect. Employees assume all workplace risks by accepting employment does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Delegation of a safety task is not equivalent to delegation of legal responsibility for the employer's own duty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wilsons & Clyde Coal Co Ltd v English [1938] AC 57.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Delegation of a safety task is not equivalent to delegation of legal responsibility for the employer's own duty.","common_trap":"Confusing delegation of performance with discharge of a personal duty of care.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8f571318af6479dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0955","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance — standing","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person living in a house suffers substantial interference from neighbouring activity but is merely a licensee with no proprietary interest or exclusive possession in the property.\n\nWhich is the best general position for a private nuisance claim?","options":{"A":"Any visitor can sue in private nuisance for interference with the land.","B":"Only the freehold owner can ever sue.","C":"A licensee automatically acquires ownership when nuisance occurs.","D":"Standing depends solely on whether the defendant was negligent.","E":"Private nuisance ordinarily protects interests in land, so a claimant generally needs a sufficient proprietary interest rather than mere personal occupation as a licensee."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Private nuisance is a tort against land. Hunter confirms that a claimant ordinarily requires an interest in the affected land, although more than one person with qualifying interests may potentially sue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Any visitor can sue in private nuisance for interference with the land does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The cause of action protects proprietary enjoyment rather than personal discomfort detached from an interest in land.","B":"Incorrect. Only the freehold owner can ever sue does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The cause of action protects proprietary enjoyment rather than personal discomfort detached from an interest in land.","C":"Incorrect. A licensee automatically acquires ownership when nuisance occurs does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The cause of action protects proprietary enjoyment rather than personal discomfort detached from an interest in land.","D":"Incorrect. Standing depends solely on whether the defendant was negligent does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The cause of action protects proprietary enjoyment rather than personal discomfort detached from an interest in land.","E":"Correct. Private nuisance is a tort against land. Hunter confirms that a claimant ordinarily requires an interest in the affected land, although more than one person with qualifying interests may potentially sue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hunter v Canary Wharf Ltd [1997] AC 655.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The cause of action protects proprietary enjoyment rather than personal discomfort detached from an interest in land.","common_trap":"Treating residence alone as sufficient standing for a land-based tort.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a1570e37603d2ce"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0956","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — error of law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory tribunal interprets the legislation governing its powers incorrectly and makes a decision on that mistaken legal basis.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Illegality through error of law, subject to the statutory context and any effective restriction on review.","B":"Procedural fairness only, because courts cannot review legal interpretation.","C":"Irrationality is the exclusive ground for every legal error.","D":"No review is possible because tribunals conclusively determine all legal questions.","E":"Legitimate expectation automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Public decision-makers must correctly understand the law regulating their powers. Material error of law is ordinarily reviewable as illegality, subject to the statutory scheme and modern principles concerning jurisdiction and ouster.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Public decision-makers must correctly understand the law regulating their powers. Material error of law is ordinarily reviewable as illegality, subject to the statutory scheme and modern principles concerning jurisdiction and ouster.","B":"Incorrect. Procedural fairness only, because courts cannot review legal interpretation does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A decision-maker cannot ordinarily immunise a legally erroneous understanding of its statutory authority.","C":"Incorrect. Irrationality is the exclusive ground for every legal error does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A decision-maker cannot ordinarily immunise a legally erroneous understanding of its statutory authority.","D":"Incorrect. No review is possible because tribunals conclusively determine all legal questions does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A decision-maker cannot ordinarily immunise a legally erroneous understanding of its statutory authority.","E":"Incorrect. Legitimate expectation automatically does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A decision-maker cannot ordinarily immunise a legally erroneous understanding of its statutory authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Anisminic Ltd v Foreign Compensation Commission [1969] 2 AC 147; R v Hull University Visitor, ex p Page [1993] AC 682.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A decision-maker cannot ordinarily immunise a legally erroneous understanding of its statutory authority.","common_trap":"Forcing a legal-authority error into irrationality or procedural fairness rather than illegality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a5de01456e20b6f2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0957","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — Convention rights and private disputes","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two purely private parties litigate a common-law claim. One argues that the Human Rights Act simply creates a free-standing damages action against the other private party for every alleged Convention breach.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every private person is a public authority for every act.","B":"The HRA primarily binds public authorities, including courts; Convention rights can influence private-law adjudication through the court's duties, but the Act does not generally create a universal free-standing horizontal damages claim against private persons.","C":"Convention rights are irrelevant whenever both litigants are private.","D":"The HRA automatically replaces all common-law causes of action.","E":"Private parties can be sued under section 6 exactly as government departments in every circumstance."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 6 makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly with Convention rights, and courts themselves are public authorities. This can affect development and application of private law without turning every private actor into a core public authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every private person is a public authority for every act does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The HRA has important horizontal effects through judicial duties but does not generally impose identical direct obligations on all private persons.","B":"Correct. Section 6 makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly with Convention rights, and courts themselves are public authorities. This can affect development and application of private law without turning every private actor into a core public authority.","C":"Incorrect. Convention rights are irrelevant whenever both litigants are private does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The HRA has important horizontal effects through judicial duties but does not generally impose identical direct obligations on all private persons.","D":"Incorrect. The HRA automatically replaces all common-law causes of action does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The HRA has important horizontal effects through judicial duties but does not generally impose identical direct obligations on all private persons.","E":"Incorrect. Private parties can be sued under section 6 exactly as government departments in every circumstance does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The HRA has important horizontal effects through judicial duties but does not generally impose identical direct obligations on all private persons."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6; Campbell v MGN Ltd [2004] UKHL 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The HRA has important horizontal effects through judicial duties but does not generally impose identical direct obligations on all private persons.","common_trap":"Confusing the court's HRA duties with a universal direct cause of action against private defendants.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"20b8c3616f47d39a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0958","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — presumption against retrospectivity","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parliament enacts legislation affecting substantive rights but does not clearly state that it applies to events completed before commencement.\n\nWhich interpretive principle is relevant?","options":{"A":"Every statute automatically applies retrospectively.","B":"No statute can ever operate retrospectively even if Parliament expressly provides it.","C":"There is a common-law presumption against retrospective alteration of substantive rights unless Parliament has indicated the contrary with sufficient clarity.","D":"The presumption applies only to criminal procedure.","E":"Courts decide retrospectivity solely by asking what outcome seems fairest."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Courts generally presume that legislation is not intended to alter the legal consequences of completed past events or vested substantive rights unless the statutory language or necessary implication indicates retrospective operation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every statute automatically applies retrospectively does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The presumption protects legal certainty while remaining subject to Parliament's ability to legislate clearly for the past.","B":"Incorrect. No statute can ever operate retrospectively even if Parliament expressly provides it does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The presumption protects legal certainty while remaining subject to Parliament's ability to legislate clearly for the past.","C":"Correct. Courts generally presume that legislation is not intended to alter the legal consequences of completed past events or vested substantive rights unless the statutory language or necessary implication indicates retrospective operation.","D":"Incorrect. The presumption applies only to criminal procedure does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The presumption protects legal certainty while remaining subject to Parliament's ability to legislate clearly for the past.","E":"Incorrect. Courts decide retrospectivity solely by asking what outcome seems fairest does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The presumption protects legal certainty while remaining subject to Parliament's ability to legislate clearly for the past."},"primary_legal_authority":"Yew Bon Tew v Kenderaan Bas Mara [1983] 1 AC 553; statutory-interpretation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The presumption protects legal certainty while remaining subject to Parliament's ability to legislate clearly for the past.","common_trap":"Treating the presumption as either nonexistent or an absolute constitutional prohibition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f127a439af75c8d0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0959","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — revocation by later will","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator validly executes a new will containing an express clause revoking all earlier wills.\n\nWhich is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The earlier will remains operative because wills can be revoked only by physical destruction.","B":"Both wills always operate simultaneously in full.","C":"Revocation requires approval by the beneficiaries.","D":"The earlier wills are revoked by the later valid will, subject to any issue concerning the later will's validity or conditional operation.","E":"A later will cannot revoke an earlier will unless registered with the court."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A will may be revoked by a later duly executed will or codicil containing an express revocation, as well as through other statutory methods.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The earlier will remains operative because wills can be revoked only by physical destruction does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A valid later testamentary instrument can revoke prior instruments without physical destruction.","B":"Incorrect. Both wills always operate simultaneously in full does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A valid later testamentary instrument can revoke prior instruments without physical destruction.","C":"Incorrect. Revocation requires approval by the beneficiaries does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A valid later testamentary instrument can revoke prior instruments without physical destruction.","D":"Correct. A will may be revoked by a later duly executed will or codicil containing an express revocation, as well as through other statutory methods.","E":"Incorrect. A later will cannot revoke an earlier will unless registered with the court does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A valid later testamentary instrument can revoke prior instruments without physical destruction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A valid later testamentary instrument can revoke prior instruments without physical destruction.","common_trap":"Assuming destruction is the exclusive statutory mode of revocation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"72baf2052e3a591d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0960","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Probate — caveats","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person with a genuine interest disputes the validity of a will and wants to prevent a grant of representation from being issued while the dispute is investigated.\n\nWhich probate mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Registering a Form A restriction at HM Land Registry.","B":"Serving a Part 36 offer.","C":"Applying for an attachment of earnings order.","D":"Filing a company charge.","E":"Entering a caveat against the estate, subject to the probate rules and subsequent warning/appearance procedure."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A caveat can prevent a grant from issuing without notice while a probate dispute is addressed. The Non-Contentious Probate Rules govern its duration and the warning and appearance procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Registering a Form A restriction at HM Land Registry does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A caveat preserves the position in contentious probate before a grant is extracted.","B":"Incorrect. Serving a Part 36 offer does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A caveat preserves the position in contentious probate before a grant is extracted.","C":"Incorrect. Applying for an attachment of earnings order does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A caveat preserves the position in contentious probate before a grant is extracted.","D":"Incorrect. Filing a company charge does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. A caveat preserves the position in contentious probate before a grant is extracted.","E":"Correct. A caveat can prevent a grant from issuing without notice while a probate dispute is addressed. The Non-Contentious Probate Rules govern its duration and the warning and appearance procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1987, including rules governing caveats.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A caveat preserves the position in contentious probate before a grant is extracted.","common_trap":"Confusing probate protection with land-registration or civil-enforcement mechanisms.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"45ee81fbfc7f63a9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0961","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Intestacy — statutory trusts for issue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dies intestate leaving no spouse or civil partner but leaving two adult children. No other complication affects entitlement.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The issue take the residuary estate under the statutory intestacy trusts, subject to the statutory distribution rules.","B":"The Crown automatically takes the estate because there is no spouse.","C":"The estate passes entirely to the deceased's parents despite surviving children.","D":"Friends of the deceased share equally.","E":"The personal representatives become beneficial owners."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where an intestate leaves issue but no surviving spouse or civil partner, the residuary estate is held for the issue under the statutory trusts, incorporating the statutory per stirpes machinery where relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where an intestate leaves issue but no surviving spouse or civil partner, the residuary estate is held for the issue under the statutory trusts, incorporating the statutory per stirpes machinery where relevant.","B":"Incorrect. The Crown automatically takes the estate because there is no spouse does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Issue occupy a priority position in the intestacy hierarchy when no spouse or civil partner survives.","C":"Incorrect. The estate passes entirely to the deceased's parents despite surviving children does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Issue occupy a priority position in the intestacy hierarchy when no spouse or civil partner survives.","D":"Incorrect. Friends of the deceased share equally does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Issue occupy a priority position in the intestacy hierarchy when no spouse or civil partner survives.","E":"Incorrect. The personal representatives become beneficial owners does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Issue occupy a priority position in the intestacy hierarchy when no spouse or civil partner survives."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 46 and statutory trusts.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Issue occupy a priority position in the intestacy hierarchy when no spouse or civil partner survives.","common_trap":"Skipping surviving descendants in favour of more remote relatives or bona vacantia.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dde6285f7036010e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0962","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — mixed receipts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives one electronic payment comprising £8,000 client money for completion and £500 belonging immediately to the firm for billed costs.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The whole £8,500 permanently becomes client money.","B":"A mixed receipt must be allocated promptly and correctly between client and business money in accordance with the Accounts Rules, with the client-money element kept in the client account.","C":"The whole payment must permanently remain in the business account.","D":"Mixed receipts are prohibited and must be returned regardless of circumstances.","E":"The firm may leave the allocation unresolved indefinitely."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require firms to identify and appropriately allocate mixed payments so that client money is protected and money belonging to the firm is dealt with correctly, with prompt transfers where necessary.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The whole £8,500 permanently becomes client money does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Classification follows beneficial entitlement to the components of the receipt rather than treating the whole payment identically forever.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require firms to identify and appropriately allocate mixed payments so that client money is protected and money belonging to the firm is dealt with correctly, with prompt transfers where necessary.","C":"Incorrect. The whole payment must permanently remain in the business account does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Classification follows beneficial entitlement to the components of the receipt rather than treating the whole payment identically forever.","D":"Incorrect. Mixed receipts are prohibited and must be returned regardless of circumstances does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Classification follows beneficial entitlement to the components of the receipt rather than treating the whole payment identically forever.","E":"Incorrect. The firm may leave the allocation unresolved indefinitely does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Classification follows beneficial entitlement to the components of the receipt rather than treating the whole payment identically forever."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, including rr 2, 4 and 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Classification follows beneficial entitlement to the components of the receipt rather than treating the whole payment identically forever.","common_trap":"Assuming a mixed receipt loses the distinct character of its component amounts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e00425e9ebeaf456"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0963","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Express trusts — constitution by transfer of shares","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor intends to create a trust by transferring registered company shares to trustees but signs no stock transfer form and takes no other step to transfer legal title before death.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The trust is automatically constituted by intention alone regardless of the chosen transfer method.","B":"The trustees acquire legal title through oral acceptance.","C":"Unless an applicable exception applies, equity will not ordinarily perfect the settlor's incomplete transfer merely because there was an intention to create a trust.","D":"Consideration from beneficiaries is required to validate every trust.","E":"The court always treats an ineffective transfer as a self-declaration of trust."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where a settlor chooses to constitute a trust by transfer, the required transfer steps must ordinarily be completed; equity does not generally perfect an imperfect gift or recharacterise an unsuccessful transfer as a declaration of trust.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The trust is automatically constituted by intention alone regardless of the chosen transfer method does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Constitution depends on completing the chosen legal mechanism, subject to recognised exceptions.","B":"Incorrect. The trustees acquire legal title through oral acceptance does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Constitution depends on completing the chosen legal mechanism, subject to recognised exceptions.","C":"Correct. Where a settlor chooses to constitute a trust by transfer, the required transfer steps must ordinarily be completed; equity does not generally perfect an imperfect gift or recharacterise an unsuccessful transfer as a declaration of trust.","D":"Incorrect. Consideration from beneficiaries is required to validate every trust does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Constitution depends on completing the chosen legal mechanism, subject to recognised exceptions.","E":"Incorrect. The court always treats an ineffective transfer as a self-declaration of trust does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Constitution depends on completing the chosen legal mechanism, subject to recognised exceptions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Milroy v Lord (1862) 4 De GF & J 264.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Constitution depends on completing the chosen legal mechanism, subject to recognised exceptions.","common_trap":"Assuming benevolent intention alone allows equity to disregard incomplete transfer formalities.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f1c2404e30e395b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0964","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Equitable remedies — proprietary tracing and bona fide purchaser","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee misapplies trust property and transfers a traceable substitute to a purchaser who gives value, acts in good faith and has no notice of the beneficiary's equitable interest.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Equitable interests always bind every purchaser regardless of notice.","B":"The purchaser must return the asset even though equity's bona fide purchaser defence applies.","C":"Giving value is irrelevant in equity.","D":"The bona fide purchaser for value without notice takes free of the prior equitable proprietary interest, preventing proprietary recovery of that asset against the purchaser.","E":"The beneficiary automatically acquires the purchaser's unrelated assets."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Equity's bona fide purchaser defence protects a purchaser of the legal interest who gives value in good faith without notice, cutting off prior equitable proprietary interests in the acquired asset.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Equitable interests always bind every purchaser regardless of notice does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Tracing identifies substitutes but remains subject to defences protecting an innocent purchaser for value.","B":"Incorrect. The purchaser must return the asset even though equity's bona fide purchaser defence applies does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Tracing identifies substitutes but remains subject to defences protecting an innocent purchaser for value.","C":"Incorrect. Giving value is irrelevant in equity does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Tracing identifies substitutes but remains subject to defences protecting an innocent purchaser for value.","D":"Correct. Equity's bona fide purchaser defence protects a purchaser of the legal interest who gives value in good faith without notice, cutting off prior equitable proprietary interests in the acquired asset.","E":"Incorrect. The beneficiary automatically acquires the purchaser's unrelated assets does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Tracing identifies substitutes but remains subject to defences protecting an innocent purchaser for value."},"primary_legal_authority":"Pilcher v Rawlins (1872) LR 7 Ch App 259; equitable tracing principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Tracing identifies substitutes but remains subject to defences protecting an innocent purchaser for value.","common_trap":"Assuming tracing creates an indefeasible proprietary claim against every recipient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2f3a142142157c8c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0965","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Unregistered land — legal interests and notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser of an unregistered legal estate for money or money's worth encounters a pre-existing equitable interest that is not registrable as a land charge. The purchaser had neither actual nor constructive notice.\n\nWhich traditional doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The Land Registration Act 2002 priority rule applies identically because the title is unregistered.","B":"Every equitable interest automatically binds all purchasers.","C":"Overreaching occurs whenever one purchaser pays one legal owner.","D":"A Form A restriction determines priority.","E":"The bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice doctrine may allow the purchaser to take free of the equitable interest."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"In unregistered land, equitable interests outside the land-charges regime can be governed by the doctrine of notice, under which a bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice may take free.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The Land Registration Act 2002 priority rule applies identically because the title is unregistered does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Priority in unregistered conveyancing requires identifying whether an interest is legal, registrable as a land charge, or governed by notice.","B":"Incorrect. Every equitable interest automatically binds all purchasers does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Priority in unregistered conveyancing requires identifying whether an interest is legal, registrable as a land charge, or governed by notice.","C":"Incorrect. Overreaching occurs whenever one purchaser pays one legal owner does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Priority in unregistered conveyancing requires identifying whether an interest is legal, registrable as a land charge, or governed by notice.","D":"Incorrect. A Form A restriction determines priority does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Priority in unregistered conveyancing requires identifying whether an interest is legal, registrable as a land charge, or governed by notice.","E":"Correct. In unregistered land, equitable interests outside the land-charges regime can be governed by the doctrine of notice, under which a bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice may take free."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 199; doctrine of notice, subject to Land Charges Act 1972.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Priority in unregistered conveyancing requires identifying whether an interest is legal, registrable as a land charge, or governed by notice.","common_trap":"Applying registered-land priority machinery to an unregistered title.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8c5a540b7d86ae5a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0966","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Freehold covenants — passing benefit at law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner seeks to enforce the benefit of a restrictive covenant against a successor to the original covenantor. The claimant's land is capable of benefiting from the covenant.\n\nWhich is the best general proposition about the benefit at law?","options":{"A":"The benefit may pass to a successor where the covenant touches and concerns the benefited land and the legal requirements for annexation or assignment are satisfied.","B":"The benefit of a covenant can never pass at law.","C":"Only the burden, never the benefit, can pass.","D":"Every personal promise automatically binds and benefits all future landowners.","E":"The claimant must own the burdened land rather than benefited land."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The benefit of a covenant can run with the benefited land at law where the relevant requirements are satisfied, including that it touches and concerns the land and that the benefit is properly annexed or assigned.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The benefit of a covenant can run with the benefited land at law where the relevant requirements are satisfied, including that it touches and concerns the land and that the benefit is properly annexed or assigned.","B":"Incorrect. The benefit of a covenant can never pass at law does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Benefit and burden must be analysed separately; the benefit has recognised routes for transmission.","C":"Incorrect. Only the burden, never the benefit, can pass does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Benefit and burden must be analysed separately; the benefit has recognised routes for transmission.","D":"Incorrect. Every personal promise automatically binds and benefits all future landowners does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Benefit and burden must be analysed separately; the benefit has recognised routes for transmission.","E":"Incorrect. The claimant must own the burdened land rather than benefited land does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Benefit and burden must be analysed separately; the benefit has recognised routes for transmission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 78; P&A Swift Investments v Combined English Stores Group plc [1989] AC 632.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Benefit and burden must be analysed separately; the benefit has recognised routes for transmission.","common_trap":"Assuming the restrictive-covenant rule concerning equitable burden also determines passage of benefit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a487868e3a55b330"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0967","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — environmental searches","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is acquiring land previously used for industrial purposes and wants information about potential contamination, landfill and environmental risks that may not appear from title alone.\n\nWhich due-diligence step is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 priority search only.","B":"An appropriate environmental search and, where indicated, further environmental investigation.","C":"A bankruptcy search against the buyer only.","D":"A company name reservation.","E":"A probate caveat."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Environmental searches are commonly used to identify potential contamination, landfill, flooding and related environmental matters, with results potentially requiring specialist investigation or contractual protection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An OS1 priority search only does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Title investigation alone does not necessarily reveal physical or regulatory environmental risk.","B":"Correct. Environmental searches are commonly used to identify potential contamination, landfill, flooding and related environmental matters, with results potentially requiring specialist investigation or contractual protection.","C":"Incorrect. A bankruptcy search against the buyer only does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Title investigation alone does not necessarily reveal physical or regulatory environmental risk.","D":"Incorrect. A company name reservation does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Title investigation alone does not necessarily reveal physical or regulatory environmental risk.","E":"Incorrect. A probate caveat does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Title investigation alone does not necessarily reveal physical or regulatory environmental risk."},"primary_legal_authority":"Environmental Protection Act 1990, Part IIA; conveyancing search practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Title investigation alone does not necessarily reveal physical or regulatory environmental risk.","common_trap":"Using a registration-priority search as a substitute for environmental due diligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"67715984259748dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0968","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — drafting special conditions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller agrees that completion will occur with the property vacant and that a particular chattel is excluded from the sale despite appearing in marketing photographs.\n\nWhich is the best drafting approach?","options":{"A":"Rely solely on the marketing photographs.","B":"Leave the matters unwritten because conveyancing contracts cannot contain special conditions.","C":"Ensure the sale contract and incorporated conditions clearly record the agreed vacant-possession obligation and treatment of the chattel, using appropriate special conditions or contractual particulars.","D":"Record them only after completion.","E":"Assume the Land Registry title automatically resolves both points."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 2026 FLK expressly includes drafting the sale contract. Transaction-specific agreements should be captured clearly in the contractual documents rather than left to informal pre-contract material.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Rely solely on the marketing photographs does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Contract drafting should accurately allocate agreed completion obligations and identify what is or is not included in the transaction.","B":"Incorrect. Leave the matters unwritten because conveyancing contracts cannot contain special conditions does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Contract drafting should accurately allocate agreed completion obligations and identify what is or is not included in the transaction.","C":"Correct. The 2026 FLK expressly includes drafting the sale contract. Transaction-specific agreements should be captured clearly in the contractual documents rather than left to informal pre-contract material.","D":"Incorrect. Record them only after completion does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Contract drafting should accurately allocate agreed completion obligations and identify what is or is not included in the transaction.","E":"Incorrect. Assume the Land Registry title automatically resolves both points does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. Contract drafting should accurately allocate agreed completion obligations and identify what is or is not included in the transaction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, s 2; Standard Conditions of Sale where incorporated; SRA SQE1 FLK applicable from 1 September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract drafting should accurately allocate agreed completion obligations and identify what is or is not included in the transaction.","common_trap":"Assuming marketing material or registered title substitutes for precise contractual drafting.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b2c3b84b86404212"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0969","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Defences — duress","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D commits an offence after receiving a threat of death or serious injury directed at D or a person for whom D reasonably regards themselves as responsible. The threat is sufficiently immediate, a sober person of reasonable firmness sharing D's relevant characteristics might have responded similarly, and D had no safe avenue of escape.\n\nWhich defence is most directly relevant, assuming the offence is one to which the defence is legally available?","options":{"A":"Self-defence, because all threats are treated as attacks already underway.","B":"Intoxication.","C":"Automatism.","D":"Duress by threats.","E":"Consent."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Duress by threats can excuse an offence where sufficiently grave threats cause the defendant to act and the objective and subjective requirements are met, subject to exclusions including murder and attempted murder.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Self-defence, because all threats are treated as attacks already underway does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The defence addresses coercion by threats rather than use of reasonable force in defence.","B":"Incorrect. Intoxication does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The defence addresses coercion by threats rather than use of reasonable force in defence.","C":"Incorrect. Automatism does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The defence addresses coercion by threats rather than use of reasonable force in defence.","D":"Correct. Duress by threats can excuse an offence where sufficiently grave threats cause the defendant to act and the objective and subjective requirements are met, subject to exclusions including murder and attempted murder.","E":"Incorrect. Consent does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The defence addresses coercion by threats rather than use of reasonable force in defence."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Hasan [2005] UKHL 22; R v Howe [1987] AC 417.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defence addresses coercion by threats rather than use of reasonable force in defence.","common_trap":"Confusing threatened coercion with self-defence or ignoring offences for which duress is unavailable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"06e1ba4c65dcd108"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0970","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police powers — arrest without warrant","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A constable has reasonable grounds to suspect a person is committing an offence and is considering arrest without warrant.\n\nWhich additional statutory requirement is central under PACE?","options":{"A":"Reasonable suspicion alone always makes arrest mandatory.","B":"Arrest is lawful only for indictable-only offences.","C":"The suspect must first confess.","D":"A magistrate must approve every arrest in advance.","E":"The constable must also have reasonable grounds for believing that arrest is necessary for one or more statutory reasons."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE section 24 requires both the relevant suspicion and reasonable grounds for believing arrest is necessary for a statutory reason, such as enabling prompt and effective investigation or preventing injury or disappearance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Reasonable suspicion alone always makes arrest mandatory does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The necessity test is a distinct safeguard in modern arrest law.","B":"Incorrect. Arrest is lawful only for indictable-only offences does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The necessity test is a distinct safeguard in modern arrest law.","C":"Incorrect. The suspect must first confess does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The necessity test is a distinct safeguard in modern arrest law.","D":"Incorrect. A magistrate must approve every arrest in advance does not fit these facts or the governing doctrine. The necessity test is a distinct safeguard in modern arrest law.","E":"Correct. PACE section 24 requires both the relevant suspicion and reasonable grounds for believing arrest is necessary for a statutory reason, such as enabling prompt and effective investigation or preventing injury or disappearance."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 24; PACE Code G.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The necessity test is a distinct safeguard in modern arrest law.","common_trap":"Stopping at reasonable suspicion without applying the separate statutory necessity requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"52d3281b414b21b1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0971","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"LLPs — separate legal personality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three consultants want limited liability while retaining an internally flexible partnership-style management structure. They are considering incorporating an LLP.\n\nWhich statement best describes an LLP once validly incorporated?","options":{"A":"It is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members, while its internal arrangements may be governed by agreement between the members.","B":"It has no legal personality distinct from its members.","C":"It is legally identical to an ordinary partnership for liability purposes.","D":"It can exist only if one member is a public company.","E":"Its members necessarily own each LLP asset directly as co-owners."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An LLP is a body corporate with separate legal personality. Its members can structure their internal rights and duties through an LLP agreement, subject to the statutory regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An LLP is a body corporate with separate legal personality. Its members can structure their internal rights and duties through an LLP agreement, subject to the statutory regime.","B":"Incorrect. It has no legal personality distinct from its members does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Separate personality is a central distinction between an LLP and a traditional partnership.","C":"Incorrect. It is legally identical to an ordinary partnership for liability purposes does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Separate personality is a central distinction between an LLP and a traditional partnership.","D":"Incorrect. It can exist only if one member is a public company does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Separate personality is a central distinction between an LLP and a traditional partnership.","E":"Incorrect. Its members necessarily own each LLP asset directly as co-owners does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Separate personality is a central distinction between an LLP and a traditional partnership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000, ss 1 and 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Separate personality is a central distinction between an LLP and a traditional partnership.","common_trap":"Treating the partnership-style flexibility of an LLP as eliminating its corporate personality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0eac3297f4d4ceb0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0972","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"LLPs — designated members","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An LLP has appointed designated members. A filing deadline for the LLP's statutory accounts is approaching.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Only external accountants can bear responsibility for LLP filings.","B":"Designated members have particular statutory responsibilities for compliance matters such as accounts and filings, in addition to being members of the LLP.","C":"Designated members are automatically employees rather than members.","D":"An LLP has no statutory filing obligations.","E":"Every filing must instead be made personally by all ordinary members acting unanimously."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The LLP legislation assigns designated members particular compliance functions, including responsibilities connected with statutory accounts, returns and notifications.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Only external accountants can bear responsibility for LLP filings does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Designation allocates specific statutory compliance responsibilities within the LLP structure.","B":"Correct. The LLP legislation assigns designated members particular compliance functions, including responsibilities connected with statutory accounts, returns and notifications.","C":"Incorrect. Designated members are automatically employees rather than members does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Designation allocates specific statutory compliance responsibilities within the LLP structure.","D":"Incorrect. An LLP has no statutory filing obligations does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Designation allocates specific statutory compliance responsibilities within the LLP structure.","E":"Incorrect. Every filing must instead be made personally by all ordinary members acting unanimously does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Designation allocates specific statutory compliance responsibilities within the LLP structure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000, including s 8; Companies Act provisions applied to LLPs by regulations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Designation allocates specific statutory compliance responsibilities within the LLP structure.","common_trap":"Assuming the LLP's separate personality removes the need for individuals to perform statutory compliance functions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b4ef5c3a9fcdc74e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0973","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"VAT — output and input tax","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A VAT-registered trading business makes standard-rated taxable supplies and incurs VAT on qualifying business purchases.\n\nWhich is the best general description of its ordinary VAT accounting?","options":{"A":"It pays VAT only on its annual accounting profit.","B":"Input VAT can never be recovered by a VAT-registered business.","C":"It accounts for output VAT on taxable supplies and may deduct recoverable input VAT on qualifying business purchases, paying or reclaiming the resulting net amount subject to the VAT rules.","D":"Output VAT belongs beneficially to the business as untaxed profit.","E":"VAT is calculated only when the business is sold."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The VAT system ordinarily requires a registered taxable person to account for output tax on taxable supplies while allowing deduction of qualifying input tax, subject to statutory restrictions and evidence requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It pays VAT only on its annual accounting profit does not fit the governing rule on these facts. VAT accounting is transaction-based and ordinarily operates through the difference between output and recoverable input tax.","B":"Incorrect. Input VAT can never be recovered by a VAT-registered business does not fit the governing rule on these facts. VAT accounting is transaction-based and ordinarily operates through the difference between output and recoverable input tax.","C":"Correct. The VAT system ordinarily requires a registered taxable person to account for output tax on taxable supplies while allowing deduction of qualifying input tax, subject to statutory restrictions and evidence requirements.","D":"Incorrect. Output VAT belongs beneficially to the business as untaxed profit does not fit the governing rule on these facts. VAT accounting is transaction-based and ordinarily operates through the difference between output and recoverable input tax.","E":"Incorrect. VAT is calculated only when the business is sold does not fit the governing rule on these facts. VAT accounting is transaction-based and ordinarily operates through the difference between output and recoverable input tax."},"primary_legal_authority":"Value Added Tax Act 1994.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"VAT accounting is transaction-based and ordinarily operates through the difference between output and recoverable input tax.","common_trap":"Confusing VAT with a tax on accounting profit or assuming all input tax is either automatically recoverable or never recoverable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"73ff0c006c33b89e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0974","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Personal insolvency — bankruptcy restrictions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual sole trader is made bankrupt. The trader wants to obtain substantial credit without telling the lender about the bankruptcy.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Bankruptcy has no effect on the debtor's ability to obtain credit.","B":"The debtor is automatically prohibited from entering every contract.","C":"Bankruptcy converts the sole trader into a limited company.","D":"Bankruptcy imposes statutory restrictions, including restrictions on obtaining credit above the prescribed amount without disclosing bankrupt status while the restriction applies.","E":"Only company directors can be subject to insolvency restrictions."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"An undischarged bankrupt is subject to statutory restrictions, including a disclosure requirement when obtaining credit above the statutory threshold, alongside other consequences of bankruptcy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Bankruptcy has no effect on the debtor's ability to obtain credit does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Personal bankruptcy affects the debtor's legal and financial position without eliminating contractual capacity altogether.","B":"Incorrect. The debtor is automatically prohibited from entering every contract does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Personal bankruptcy affects the debtor's legal and financial position without eliminating contractual capacity altogether.","C":"Incorrect. Bankruptcy converts the sole trader into a limited company does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Personal bankruptcy affects the debtor's legal and financial position without eliminating contractual capacity altogether.","D":"Correct. An undischarged bankrupt is subject to statutory restrictions, including a disclosure requirement when obtaining credit above the statutory threshold, alongside other consequences of bankruptcy.","E":"Incorrect. Only company directors can be subject to insolvency restrictions does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Personal bankruptcy affects the debtor's legal and financial position without eliminating contractual capacity altogether."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, including s 360.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Personal bankruptcy affects the debtor's legal and financial position without eliminating contractual capacity altogether.","common_trap":"Assuming bankruptcy either has no consequences or creates a total incapacity to contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b204a0a122330711"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0975","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Service — deemed service of claim form","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant serves a claim form within England and Wales by a method permitted by CPR Part 6. A deadline depends on the date of deemed service.\n\nWhich is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use the recipient's subjective recollection of first reading the document in every case.","B":"All methods of service are deemed effective at midnight on posting.","C":"Deemed service applies only to witness statements.","D":"The parties may ignore CPR service rules whenever limitation is close.","E":"Apply the specific deemed-service rule in CPR Part 6 for a claim form rather than assuming the document was served whenever the recipient actually happened to read it."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The CPR contains specific rules governing when a claim form is deemed served. Procedural deadlines must be calculated from the legally relevant service date rather than informal assumptions about actual reading.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Use the recipient's subjective recollection of first reading the document in every case does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Service and deemed service are technical procedural concepts that can determine later response deadlines.","B":"Incorrect. All methods of service are deemed effective at midnight on posting does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Service and deemed service are technical procedural concepts that can determine later response deadlines.","C":"Incorrect. Deemed service applies only to witness statements does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Service and deemed service are technical procedural concepts that can determine later response deadlines.","D":"Incorrect. The parties may ignore CPR service rules whenever limitation is close does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Service and deemed service are technical procedural concepts that can determine later response deadlines.","E":"Correct. The CPR contains specific rules governing when a claim form is deemed served. Procedural deadlines must be calculated from the legally relevant service date rather than informal assumptions about actual reading."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 6, especially rr 6.14 and 6.26 as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Service and deemed service are technical procedural concepts that can determine later response deadlines.","common_trap":"Equating actual awareness with the procedural date of service without applying Part 6.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"03d253360b6c1987"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0976","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — counterclaims","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant says the claimant owes the defendant damages arising from the same commercial relationship and wants affirmative relief rather than merely defeating the claim.\n\nWhich pleading mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A counterclaim made in accordance with CPR Part 20.","B":"A witness summons.","C":"A costs budget.","D":"An acknowledgment of service only.","E":"A Part 71 information order."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 20 governs counterclaims and other additional claims. A defendant seeking its own remedy against the claimant can ordinarily advance that claim within the existing proceedings through the prescribed procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 20 governs counterclaims and other additional claims. A defendant seeking its own remedy against the claimant can ordinarily advance that claim within the existing proceedings through the prescribed procedure.","B":"Incorrect. A witness summons does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A counterclaim seeks affirmative relief and is distinct from a defence that merely resists liability.","C":"Incorrect. A costs budget does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A counterclaim seeks affirmative relief and is distinct from a defence that merely resists liability.","D":"Incorrect. An acknowledgment of service only does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A counterclaim seeks affirmative relief and is distinct from a defence that merely resists liability.","E":"Incorrect. A Part 71 information order does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A counterclaim seeks affirmative relief and is distinct from a defence that merely resists liability."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A counterclaim seeks affirmative relief and is distinct from a defence that merely resists liability.","common_trap":"Treating a substantive cross-claim as if it were only an evidential or administrative step.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"39d44f9ceb0b2297"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0977","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — litigation privilege","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After litigation is reasonably in prospect, a solicitor obtains a confidential expert report predominantly for the purpose of obtaining advice and evidence for that litigation.\n\nWhich privilege is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Legal advice privilege only because litigation privilege never covers third-party communications.","B":"Litigation privilege, provided the dominant-purpose and other requirements are satisfied.","C":"Without-prejudice privilege, even though there are no settlement negotiations.","D":"Parliamentary privilege.","E":"No privilege can ever attach to an expert report."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Litigation privilege can protect confidential communications between client or lawyer and third parties where litigation is in reasonable contemplation and the dominant purpose is conducting that litigation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Legal advice privilege only because litigation privilege never covers third-party communications does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unlike legal advice privilege, litigation privilege can extend to qualifying third-party material prepared for litigation.","B":"Correct. Litigation privilege can protect confidential communications between client or lawyer and third parties where litigation is in reasonable contemplation and the dominant purpose is conducting that litigation.","C":"Incorrect. Without-prejudice privilege, even though there are no settlement negotiations does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unlike legal advice privilege, litigation privilege can extend to qualifying third-party material prepared for litigation.","D":"Incorrect. Parliamentary privilege does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unlike legal advice privilege, litigation privilege can extend to qualifying third-party material prepared for litigation.","E":"Incorrect. No privilege can ever attach to an expert report does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unlike legal advice privilege, litigation privilege can extend to qualifying third-party material prepared for litigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Three Rivers DC v Bank of England (No 6) [2004] UKHL 48; WH Holding Ltd v E20 Stadium LLP [2018] EWCA Civ 2652.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unlike legal advice privilege, litigation privilege can extend to qualifying third-party material prepared for litigation.","common_trap":"Assuming only direct lawyer-client communications can ever attract privilege.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"07a57e740bf58941"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0978","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Civil legal aid — scope and eligibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client asks whether being unable to afford private representation automatically entitles them to civil legal aid for any type of civil dispute.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Yes. Financial hardship alone guarantees legal aid for every civil dispute.","B":"Civil legal aid is available only in criminal proceedings.","C":"No. Civil legal aid depends on the matter falling within statutory scope and on the applicable financial and merits criteria, subject to any relevant exceptions.","D":"The SRA personally decides every legal-aid application.","E":"A solicitor can convert any excluded matter into an in-scope matter by agreeing to act without charge."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"LASPO defines the categories of civil legal services within scope and provides for financial eligibility and merits criteria, with specific exceptional-funding provisions in appropriate cases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Yes. Financial hardship alone guarantees legal aid for every civil dispute does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Legal-aid entitlement is determined by statutory scope and eligibility rather than inability to pay alone.","B":"Incorrect. Civil legal aid is available only in criminal proceedings does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Legal-aid entitlement is determined by statutory scope and eligibility rather than inability to pay alone.","C":"Correct. LASPO defines the categories of civil legal services within scope and provides for financial eligibility and merits criteria, with specific exceptional-funding provisions in appropriate cases.","D":"Incorrect. The SRA personally decides every legal-aid application does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Legal-aid entitlement is determined by statutory scope and eligibility rather than inability to pay alone.","E":"Incorrect. A solicitor can convert any excluded matter into an in-scope matter by agreeing to act without charge does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Legal-aid entitlement is determined by statutory scope and eligibility rather than inability to pay alone."},"primary_legal_authority":"Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012, Part 1 and Schedule 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Legal-aid entitlement is determined by statutory scope and eligibility rather than inability to pay alone.","common_trap":"Treating means as the only legal-aid criterion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b9e18360bd3fdd54"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0979","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Financial services — exempt professional firms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An SRA-authorised firm carries on a financial activity that would otherwise be regulated, but says it can rely on the professional-firms exemption.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every solicitor is automatically exempt from every FCA rule.","B":"The exemption applies whenever the activity is profitable.","C":"The firm can rely on the exemption even if the activity is a separate standalone financial-services business outside its permitted scope.","D":"The firm must satisfy the statutory and regulatory conditions for the exemption; professional status does not create an unrestricted exemption from FSMA.","E":"SRA authorisation and FCA authorisation are legally identical."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Part XX of FSMA provides a limited route for members of designated professional bodies to carry on certain regulated activities without separate FCA authorisation, but only within defined conditions and professional rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every solicitor is automatically exempt from every FCA rule does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The professional-firms regime is conditional and limited, not a blanket exemption.","B":"Incorrect. The exemption applies whenever the activity is profitable does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The professional-firms regime is conditional and limited, not a blanket exemption.","C":"Incorrect. The firm can rely on the exemption even if the activity is a separate standalone financial-services business outside its permitted scope does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The professional-firms regime is conditional and limited, not a blanket exemption.","D":"Correct. Part XX of FSMA provides a limited route for members of designated professional bodies to carry on certain regulated activities without separate FCA authorisation, but only within defined conditions and professional rules.","E":"Incorrect. SRA authorisation and FCA authorisation are legally identical does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The professional-firms regime is conditional and limited, not a blanket exemption."},"primary_legal_authority":"Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, Part XX; SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The professional-firms regime is conditional and limited, not a blanket exemption.","common_trap":"Treating solicitor status as sufficient without analysing the activity and statutory conditions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4dc485b51c4cf3b4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0980","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Assignment — notice to debtor","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A creditor validly assigns the whole of a legal debt absolutely in writing to an assignee. The assignee wants the assignment to operate as a statutory legal assignment against the debtor.\n\nWhich additional step is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"The debtor must provide fresh consideration.","B":"The assignment must always be executed as a deed by the debtor.","C":"The original contract must be rescinded.","D":"The assignee must obtain a court judgment before notice can be given.","E":"Express notice in writing of the assignment must be given to the debtor."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 136 permits an absolute written assignment of a qualifying legal chose in action to operate at law when express written notice is given to the debtor or other person from whom the assignor would have been entitled to claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The debtor must provide fresh consideration does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Notice is a key requirement distinguishing a statutory legal assignment from an assignment effective only in equity.","B":"Incorrect. The assignment must always be executed as a deed by the debtor does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Notice is a key requirement distinguishing a statutory legal assignment from an assignment effective only in equity.","C":"Incorrect. The original contract must be rescinded does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Notice is a key requirement distinguishing a statutory legal assignment from an assignment effective only in equity.","D":"Incorrect. The assignee must obtain a court judgment before notice can be given does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Notice is a key requirement distinguishing a statutory legal assignment from an assignment effective only in equity.","E":"Correct. Section 136 permits an absolute written assignment of a qualifying legal chose in action to operate at law when express written notice is given to the debtor or other person from whom the assignor would have been entitled to claim."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 136.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Notice is a key requirement distinguishing a statutory legal assignment from an assignment effective only in equity.","common_trap":"Assuming the debtor's consent or consideration is required instead of notice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"39f6b5c53d32de71"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0981","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Illegality — contractual claims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks to enforce a contractual claim closely connected with unlawful conduct. The defendant invokes illegality.\n\nWhich is the modern general approach?","options":{"A":"The court applies the policy-based illegality analysis, considering the purpose of the prohibition, other relevant public policies and whether denying the claim would be proportionate.","B":"Every contract associated in any way with illegality is automatically void under a single inflexible rule.","C":"Illegality is irrelevant in private law.","D":"The claimant succeeds whenever the written contract appears valid on its face.","E":"The court considers only whether the claimant can prove the claim without mentioning the illegality."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The modern approach rejects a purely mechanical reliance test and evaluates the policies underlying the prohibition, countervailing policies and proportionality in determining the effect of illegality on the claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The modern approach rejects a purely mechanical reliance test and evaluates the policies underlying the prohibition, countervailing policies and proportionality in determining the effect of illegality on the claim.","B":"Incorrect. Every contract associated in any way with illegality is automatically void under a single inflexible rule does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Illegality is a policy-sensitive doctrine rather than a universal automatic bar.","C":"Incorrect. Illegality is irrelevant in private law does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Illegality is a policy-sensitive doctrine rather than a universal automatic bar.","D":"Incorrect. The claimant succeeds whenever the written contract appears valid on its face does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Illegality is a policy-sensitive doctrine rather than a universal automatic bar.","E":"Incorrect. The court considers only whether the claimant can prove the claim without mentioning the illegality does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Illegality is a policy-sensitive doctrine rather than a universal automatic bar."},"primary_legal_authority":"Patel v Mirza [2016] UKSC 42.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Illegality is a policy-sensitive doctrine rather than a universal automatic bar.","common_trap":"Applying the superseded reliance test as the exclusive rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5b5c458aae40885a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0982","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Restitution — total failure of basis","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer pays in advance for a performance that the seller never provides at all. No contractual allocation of the risk prevents restitution.\n\nWhich restitutionary principle may support recovery of the payment?","options":{"A":"Promissory estoppel as an independent damages claim.","B":"Restitution for failure of basis, where the basis on which the payment was made has wholly failed in the relevant sense.","C":"Specific performance of an impossible obligation automatically.","D":"Defamation.","E":"Vicarious liability."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Money transferred on a basis that fails can in appropriate circumstances be recovered in unjust enrichment. The inquiry focuses on whether the condition or basis for the transfer failed as required by the doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Promissory estoppel as an independent damages claim does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restitution can reverse an enrichment where the legal basis for retaining the payment has failed.","B":"Correct. Money transferred on a basis that fails can in appropriate circumstances be recovered in unjust enrichment. The inquiry focuses on whether the condition or basis for the transfer failed as required by the doctrine.","C":"Incorrect. Specific performance of an impossible obligation automatically does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restitution can reverse an enrichment where the legal basis for retaining the payment has failed.","D":"Incorrect. Defamation does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restitution can reverse an enrichment where the legal basis for retaining the payment has failed.","E":"Incorrect. Vicarious liability does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restitution can reverse an enrichment where the legal basis for retaining the payment has failed."},"primary_legal_authority":"Fibrosa Spolka Akcyjna v Fairbairn Lawson Combe Barbour Ltd [1943] AC 32; unjust-enrichment principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restitution can reverse an enrichment where the legal basis for retaining the payment has failed.","common_trap":"Confusing a restitutionary response with doctrines governing tort or equitable reliance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"486023c89cc78518"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0983","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Contribution between wrongdoers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two defendants are each liable in respect of the same damage suffered by a claimant. One defendant pays substantially more than its fair share.\n\nWhich statutory mechanism may permit adjustment between them?","options":{"A":"The paying defendant can never seek contribution.","B":"Contribution is available only between contracting parties.","C":"A contribution claim under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978, with the amount assessed as just and equitable having regard to responsibility for the damage.","D":"The claimant must repay the excess to the paying defendant before any contribution claim exists.","E":"Each liable defendant must always bear exactly 50% regardless of responsibility."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 1978 Act permits a person liable for damage to recover contribution from another person liable in respect of the same damage, with contribution fixed at a just and equitable amount reflecting responsibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The paying defendant can never seek contribution does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Contribution reallocates liability between wrongdoers without reducing the claimant's substantive entitlement.","B":"Incorrect. Contribution is available only between contracting parties does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Contribution reallocates liability between wrongdoers without reducing the claimant's substantive entitlement.","C":"Correct. The 1978 Act permits a person liable for damage to recover contribution from another person liable in respect of the same damage, with contribution fixed at a just and equitable amount reflecting responsibility.","D":"Incorrect. The claimant must repay the excess to the paying defendant before any contribution claim exists does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Contribution reallocates liability between wrongdoers without reducing the claimant's substantive entitlement.","E":"Incorrect. Each liable defendant must always bear exactly 50% regardless of responsibility does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Contribution reallocates liability between wrongdoers without reducing the claimant's substantive entitlement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978, ss 1–2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contribution reallocates liability between wrongdoers without reducing the claimant's substantive entitlement.","common_trap":"Assuming joint responsibility necessarily means equal shares or no inter-defendant adjustment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a093c1191ae639c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0984","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — honest opinion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A columnist clearly presents a statement as opinion, indicates the basis of that opinion, and an honest person could have held it on the basis of existing facts.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Truth only, because opinions can never receive a distinct defence.","B":"Absolute privilege merely because the statement appeared in a newspaper.","C":"Consent automatically.","D":"The defence of honest opinion, subject to the statutory conditions and potential defeat if the defendant did not genuinely hold the opinion.","E":"Contributory negligence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Defamation Act 2013 provides a defence where the statement was opinion, indicated its basis, and an honest person could have held the opinion on the basis of qualifying facts; the defence can be defeated in specified circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Truth only, because opinions can never receive a distinct defence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The law distinguishes protected opinion from assertions of fact requiring other defences.","B":"Incorrect. Absolute privilege merely because the statement appeared in a newspaper does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The law distinguishes protected opinion from assertions of fact requiring other defences.","C":"Incorrect. Consent automatically does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The law distinguishes protected opinion from assertions of fact requiring other defences.","D":"Correct. The Defamation Act 2013 provides a defence where the statement was opinion, indicated its basis, and an honest person could have held the opinion on the basis of qualifying facts; the defence can be defeated in specified circumstances.","E":"Incorrect. Contributory negligence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The law distinguishes protected opinion from assertions of fact requiring other defences."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The law distinguishes protected opinion from assertions of fact requiring other defences.","common_trap":"Treating opinion as though only the truth defence could protect it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e3f597322a53678f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0985","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — exclusion to visitors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business occupier displays a notice seeking to exclude liability to lawful visitors for personal injury caused by its negligence.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"An occupier may always exclude all negligence liability by any visible sign.","B":"The Occupiers' Liability Act makes exclusion impossible for every type of loss.","C":"A notice is effective only if signed by every visitor.","D":"UCTA applies only to sale-of-goods contracts.","E":"Any attempted exclusion is subject to statutory controls, including UCTA, and a business cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Although an occupier may in some circumstances restrict the duty owed to visitors, exclusion of business liability for negligence is controlled by UCTA, which prohibits exclusion for death or personal injury caused by negligence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An occupier may always exclude all negligence liability by any visible sign does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Occupiers' liability and exclusion clauses must be read together with statutory controls on negligence exclusions.","B":"Incorrect. The Occupiers' Liability Act makes exclusion impossible for every type of loss does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Occupiers' liability and exclusion clauses must be read together with statutory controls on negligence exclusions.","C":"Incorrect. A notice is effective only if signed by every visitor does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Occupiers' liability and exclusion clauses must be read together with statutory controls on negligence exclusions.","D":"Incorrect. UCTA applies only to sale-of-goods contracts does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Occupiers' liability and exclusion clauses must be read together with statutory controls on negligence exclusions.","E":"Correct. Although an occupier may in some circumstances restrict the duty owed to visitors, exclusion of business liability for negligence is controlled by UCTA, which prohibits exclusion for death or personal injury caused by negligence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(1); Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Occupiers' liability and exclusion clauses must be read together with statutory controls on negligence exclusions.","common_trap":"Assuming a warning or disclaimer automatically defeats personal-injury liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"694fb42d4aa3b0b4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0986","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"ECHR — role of Strasbourg decisions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK court is deciding a Convention-rights issue under the Human Rights Act and there is relevant European Court of Human Rights jurisprudence.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The UK court must take relevant Strasbourg judgments and decisions into account, but they are not formally binding domestic precedents in the same way as a decision of a higher UK court.","B":"Every Strasbourg judgment automatically overrides an Act of Parliament.","C":"UK courts must ignore Strasbourg case law.","D":"Strasbourg decisions are binding only if both parties consent.","E":"A Strasbourg judgment automatically repeals inconsistent domestic legislation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 2 of the HRA requires domestic courts determining Convention questions to take specified Strasbourg jurisprudence into account so far as relevant. The statutory wording does not make it a domestic doctrine of binding precedent.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 2 of the HRA requires domestic courts determining Convention questions to take specified Strasbourg jurisprudence into account so far as relevant. The statutory wording does not make it a domestic doctrine of binding precedent.","B":"Incorrect. Every Strasbourg judgment automatically overrides an Act of Parliament does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strasbourg jurisprudence is legally relevant without operating as ordinary vertical precedent within the UK court hierarchy.","C":"Incorrect. UK courts must ignore Strasbourg case law does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strasbourg jurisprudence is legally relevant without operating as ordinary vertical precedent within the UK court hierarchy.","D":"Incorrect. Strasbourg decisions are binding only if both parties consent does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strasbourg jurisprudence is legally relevant without operating as ordinary vertical precedent within the UK court hierarchy.","E":"Incorrect. A Strasbourg judgment automatically repeals inconsistent domestic legislation does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strasbourg jurisprudence is legally relevant without operating as ordinary vertical precedent within the UK court hierarchy."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Strasbourg jurisprudence is legally relevant without operating as ordinary vertical precedent within the UK court hierarchy.","common_trap":"Equating the duty to take account with automatic supremacy or binding precedent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"99c2b1476c443b51"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0987","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — ultra vires","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister makes regulations under an enabling Act, but a provision in the regulations goes beyond the power Parliament conferred.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Delegated legislation can never be reviewed by courts.","B":"The provision may be challenged by judicial review as substantively ultra vires.","C":"Any ministerial regulation has the same unlimited authority as an Act of Parliament.","D":"The regulation is valid whenever the minister acted in good faith.","E":"Only Parliament itself can identify whether delegated legislation exceeded the enabling power."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Delegated legislation must remain within the scope of the authority conferred by the parent Act. Courts can review whether the delegate exceeded that authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Delegated legislation can never be reviewed by courts does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Parliamentary delegation creates bounded legal power rather than an unrestricted legislative competence.","B":"Correct. Delegated legislation must remain within the scope of the authority conferred by the parent Act. Courts can review whether the delegate exceeded that authority.","C":"Incorrect. Any ministerial regulation has the same unlimited authority as an Act of Parliament does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Parliamentary delegation creates bounded legal power rather than an unrestricted legislative competence.","D":"Incorrect. The regulation is valid whenever the minister acted in good faith does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Parliamentary delegation creates bounded legal power rather than an unrestricted legislative competence.","E":"Incorrect. Only Parliament itself can identify whether delegated legislation exceeded the enabling power does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Parliamentary delegation creates bounded legal power rather than an unrestricted legislative competence."},"primary_legal_authority":"R (Public Law Project) v Lord Chancellor [2016] UKSC 39; ultra vires principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Parliamentary delegation creates bounded legal power rather than an unrestricted legislative competence.","common_trap":"Confusing delegated legislation with primary legislation for purposes of judicial review.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9b9d009e710662e1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0988","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — per incuriam","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An appellate court considers whether an earlier decision of the same court should exceptionally not be followed because it was given in ignorance of a binding statute or authority.\n\nWhich doctrine is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"The literal rule of statutory interpretation.","B":"Res judicata between strangers.","C":"The per incuriam exception within the limited circumstances in which the Court of Appeal may depart from its own previous decisions.","D":"Parliamentary privilege.","E":"Promissory estoppel."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the Young v Bristol Aeroplane framework, the Court of Appeal ordinarily follows its own decisions but recognises narrow exceptions, including a decision given per incuriam.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The literal rule of statutory interpretation does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Per incuriam is an exceptional precedent doctrine, not a general licence to disregard inconvenient authority.","B":"Incorrect. Res judicata between strangers does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Per incuriam is an exceptional precedent doctrine, not a general licence to disregard inconvenient authority.","C":"Correct. Under the Young v Bristol Aeroplane framework, the Court of Appeal ordinarily follows its own decisions but recognises narrow exceptions, including a decision given per incuriam.","D":"Incorrect. Parliamentary privilege does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Per incuriam is an exceptional precedent doctrine, not a general licence to disregard inconvenient authority.","E":"Incorrect. Promissory estoppel does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Per incuriam is an exceptional precedent doctrine, not a general licence to disregard inconvenient authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Young v Bristol Aeroplane Co Ltd [1944] KB 718.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Per incuriam is an exceptional precedent doctrine, not a general licence to disregard inconvenient authority.","common_trap":"Treating disagreement with an earlier case as sufficient to avoid horizontal precedent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4601ee62744c1b29"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0989","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — rectification","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professionally drafted will fails to carry out the testator's instructions because the drafter accidentally omitted a clause. The statutory conditions for rectification are otherwise met.\n\nWhich remedy may the court consider?","options":{"A":"Ademption of every gift in the will.","B":"Automatic intestacy without examining the will.","C":"Revocation by marriage retrospectively.","D":"Rectification of the will where it fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a clerical error or failure to understand instructions.","E":"A Part 36 order."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Administration of Justice Act permits rectification where a will fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of clerical error or failure to understand instructions, subject to the statutory procedure and evidence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Ademption of every gift in the will does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Rectification corrects specified drafting failures; it is distinct from construing ambiguous language.","B":"Incorrect. Automatic intestacy without examining the will does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Rectification corrects specified drafting failures; it is distinct from construing ambiguous language.","C":"Incorrect. Revocation by marriage retrospectively does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Rectification corrects specified drafting failures; it is distinct from construing ambiguous language.","D":"Correct. The Administration of Justice Act permits rectification where a will fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of clerical error or failure to understand instructions, subject to the statutory procedure and evidence.","E":"Incorrect. A Part 36 order does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Rectification corrects specified drafting failures; it is distinct from construing ambiguous language."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Justice Act 1982, s 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Rectification corrects specified drafting failures; it is distinct from construing ambiguous language.","common_trap":"Confusing correction of a drafting error with revocation or lapse doctrines.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"34859586b66045b1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0990","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — residence nil-rate amount","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased leaves a qualifying residence to direct descendants and the estate is considering whether an additional inheritance-tax threshold may apply alongside the ordinary nil-rate band.\n\nWhich relief is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Business relief solely because the asset is a house.","B":"The VAT annual accounting scheme.","C":"Group relief.","D":"A capital-gains rollover relief available only to companies.","E":"The residence nil-rate amount, subject to the statutory conditions, estate-size taper and applicable rules at the date of death."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The IHT regime provides an additional residence nil-rate amount where a qualifying residential interest is closely inherited, subject to statutory limits, tapering and downsizing provisions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Business relief solely because the asset is a house does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The relief is specifically connected with qualifying residences passing to direct descendants.","B":"Incorrect. The VAT annual accounting scheme does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The relief is specifically connected with qualifying residences passing to direct descendants.","C":"Incorrect. Group relief does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The relief is specifically connected with qualifying residences passing to direct descendants.","D":"Incorrect. A capital-gains rollover relief available only to companies does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The relief is specifically connected with qualifying residences passing to direct descendants.","E":"Correct. The IHT regime provides an additional residence nil-rate amount where a qualifying residential interest is closely inherited, subject to statutory limits, tapering and downsizing provisions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, ss 8D–8M.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The relief is specifically connected with qualifying residences passing to direct descendants.","common_trap":"Confusing a death-estate IHT threshold with business or corporate tax reliefs.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2797bc1bb93881d8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0991","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — survivorship","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two people die in circumstances where it is impossible to determine who died first. Their order of death affects devolution of property.\n\nWhich statutory presumption is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"For property purposes, the younger is presumed to have survived the elder where the order of death is uncertain, subject to applicable statutory context.","B":"The elder is always presumed to survive.","C":"Both are legally treated as having died exactly simultaneously for every purpose.","D":"The personal representatives may choose the order.","E":"The rule applies only where both left no wills."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 184 provides a statutory commorientes presumption that, where order of death is uncertain, deaths are presumed to have occurred in order of seniority, so the younger is deemed to survive the elder for relevant property purposes.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 184 provides a statutory commorientes presumption that, where order of death is uncertain, deaths are presumed to have occurred in order of seniority, so the younger is deemed to survive the elder for relevant property purposes.","B":"Incorrect. The elder is always presumed to survive does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The rule supplies a legal ordering where evidence cannot establish chronology.","C":"Incorrect. Both are legally treated as having died exactly simultaneously for every purpose does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The rule supplies a legal ordering where evidence cannot establish chronology.","D":"Incorrect. The personal representatives may choose the order does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The rule supplies a legal ordering where evidence cannot establish chronology.","E":"Incorrect. The rule applies only where both left no wills does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The rule supplies a legal ordering where evidence cannot establish chronology."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 184, subject to specific statutory modifications such as IHT rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The rule supplies a legal ordering where evidence cannot establish chronology.","common_trap":"Assuming uncertainty leaves representatives free to select the economically preferable sequence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bfaf80b42689bf4e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0992","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Variation of trusts — beneficiaries unable to consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Adult beneficiaries want to vary a trust, but the variation also affects contingent interests of minors who cannot consent.\n\nWhich statutory mechanism may permit the court to approve the arrangement on behalf of those unable to consent?","options":{"A":"Saunders v Vautier alone, because minors can consent through adult beneficiaries.","B":"The Variation of Trusts Act 1958, where the statutory requirements and benefit test are satisfied.","C":"The rule against perpetuities automatically.","D":"A trustee may extinguish minors' interests unilaterally.","E":"The Companies Act 2006."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The 1958 Act enables the court to approve qualifying arrangements varying or revoking trusts on behalf of specified persons unable to consent, provided the statutory requirements, including benefit where required, are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Saunders v Vautier alone, because minors can consent through adult beneficiaries does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The statute fills the gap where unanimous competent beneficiary consent under Saunders v Vautier is unavailable.","B":"Correct. The 1958 Act enables the court to approve qualifying arrangements varying or revoking trusts on behalf of specified persons unable to consent, provided the statutory requirements, including benefit where required, are met.","C":"Incorrect. The rule against perpetuities automatically does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The statute fills the gap where unanimous competent beneficiary consent under Saunders v Vautier is unavailable.","D":"Incorrect. A trustee may extinguish minors' interests unilaterally does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The statute fills the gap where unanimous competent beneficiary consent under Saunders v Vautier is unavailable.","E":"Incorrect. The Companies Act 2006 does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The statute fills the gap where unanimous competent beneficiary consent under Saunders v Vautier is unavailable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Variation of Trusts Act 1958, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statute fills the gap where unanimous competent beneficiary consent under Saunders v Vautier is unavailable.","common_trap":"Assuming adult beneficiaries can bind minors without court approval.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e11f2e86dda7cbd3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0993","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Charitable trusts — cy-près","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A charitable trust's original purpose can no longer be carried out in the manner contemplated, but the property remains dedicated to charity.\n\nWhich doctrine may permit the property to be redirected to an appropriate charitable purpose?","options":{"A":"The rule in Strong v Bird.","B":"Proprietary estoppel.","C":"The cy-près doctrine, subject to the statutory charitable-trust regime.","D":"Ademption.","E":"The doctrine of frustration of contracts."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Cy-près permits charitable property, in circumstances recognised by charity law, to be applied to purposes as near as practicable to the original charitable purposes rather than allowing the charitable dedication simply to fail.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule in Strong v Bird does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Charitable trusts receive a special mechanism for adapting purposes when original application becomes impossible or unsuitable.","B":"Incorrect. Proprietary estoppel does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Charitable trusts receive a special mechanism for adapting purposes when original application becomes impossible or unsuitable.","C":"Correct. Cy-près permits charitable property, in circumstances recognised by charity law, to be applied to purposes as near as practicable to the original charitable purposes rather than allowing the charitable dedication simply to fail.","D":"Incorrect. Ademption does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Charitable trusts receive a special mechanism for adapting purposes when original application becomes impossible or unsuitable.","E":"Incorrect. The doctrine of frustration of contracts does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Charitable trusts receive a special mechanism for adapting purposes when original application becomes impossible or unsuitable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Charities Act 2011, provisions governing cy-près occasions and schemes.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Charitable trusts receive a special mechanism for adapting purposes when original application becomes impossible or unsuitable.","common_trap":"Applying private-trust or contract doctrines to alteration of charitable purposes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"39928a02eec9533f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0994","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — Wheeldon v Burrows","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner sells part of land while retaining the rest. Before the sale, the sold part visibly and continuously enjoyed a quasi-easement over the retained part that was necessary for its reasonable enjoyment. The transfer is silent.\n\nWhich doctrine may imply an easement in favour of the sold land?","options":{"A":"The doctrine of adverse possession automatically.","B":"The rule in Tulk v Moxhay.","C":"The bona fide purchaser defence.","D":"The rule in Wheeldon v Burrows, subject to its requirements and the effect of the conveyance.","E":"A Form A restriction."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Wheeldon v Burrows rule can convert qualifying continuous and apparent quasi-easements used by the common owner for the benefit of the part sold into easements on disposition, subject to contrary intention and statutory context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The doctrine of adverse possession automatically does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The doctrine concerns implied grant on severance of commonly owned land.","B":"Incorrect. The rule in Tulk v Moxhay does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The doctrine concerns implied grant on severance of commonly owned land.","C":"Incorrect. The bona fide purchaser defence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The doctrine concerns implied grant on severance of commonly owned land.","D":"Correct. The Wheeldon v Burrows rule can convert qualifying continuous and apparent quasi-easements used by the common owner for the benefit of the part sold into easements on disposition, subject to contrary intention and statutory context.","E":"Incorrect. A Form A restriction does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The doctrine concerns implied grant on severance of commonly owned land."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wheeldon v Burrows (1879) 12 Ch D 31; Law of Property Act 1925, s 62 may also require separate consideration.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The doctrine concerns implied grant on severance of commonly owned land.","common_trap":"Confusing implied easements with restrictive covenants or adverse possession.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"78071d779e747deb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0995","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Adverse possession — unregistered land","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A squatter has been in factual possession of unregistered land with the necessary intention to possess, without the paper owner's consent, for the statutory limitation period.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The registered-land ten-year application procedure is the only possible adverse-possession route for all land.","B":"Adverse possession can never affect unregistered title.","C":"The squatter must have paid market rent throughout.","D":"Possession is irrelevant unless a written transfer was signed.","E":"The paper owner's action to recover the land may become statute-barred and title may be extinguished under the limitation regime, subject to the statutory requirements."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Adverse possession of unregistered land remains closely linked to limitation: after the relevant limitation period, the paper owner's right of action can be barred and title extinguished, provided the possession requirements are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The registered-land ten-year application procedure is the only possible adverse-possession route for all land does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The unregistered-land regime differs materially from the LRA 2002 application procedure for registered land.","B":"Incorrect. Adverse possession can never affect unregistered title does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The unregistered-land regime differs materially from the LRA 2002 application procedure for registered land.","C":"Incorrect. The squatter must have paid market rent throughout does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The unregistered-land regime differs materially from the LRA 2002 application procedure for registered land.","D":"Incorrect. Possession is irrelevant unless a written transfer was signed does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The unregistered-land regime differs materially from the LRA 2002 application procedure for registered land.","E":"Correct. Adverse possession of unregistered land remains closely linked to limitation: after the relevant limitation period, the paper owner's right of action can be barred and title extinguished, provided the possession requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, ss 15 and 17; JA Pye (Oxford) Ltd v Graham [2002] UKHL 30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The unregistered-land regime differs materially from the LRA 2002 application procedure for registered land.","common_trap":"Applying the registered-title statutory scheme indiscriminately to unregistered land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"79380ad659cb35fa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0996","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — mortgagee's duty on sale","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A mortgagee validly exercises its power of sale. The mortgagor alleges that the mortgagee sold quickly at a substantial undervalue without proper marketing.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The mortgagee must take reasonable care to obtain the true market value at the time of sale, although it need not postpone a proper sale merely to await a potentially better market.","B":"The mortgagee owes no duty concerning sale price.","C":"The mortgagee must always obtain the highest price that could ever arise in the future.","D":"Any sale below the mortgagor's preferred figure is automatically void.","E":"The mortgagee acts as the mortgagor's trustee in every respect."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A mortgagee exercising a power of sale must take reasonable precautions to obtain the true market value at the date of sale. The duty concerns proper conduct of the sale, not an obligation to delay indefinitely for market improvement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A mortgagee exercising a power of sale must take reasonable precautions to obtain the true market value at the date of sale. The duty concerns proper conduct of the sale, not an obligation to delay indefinitely for market improvement.","B":"Incorrect. The mortgagee owes no duty concerning sale price does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The mortgagee may realise its security but must act with reasonable care as to sale value.","C":"Incorrect. The mortgagee must always obtain the highest price that could ever arise in the future does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The mortgagee may realise its security but must act with reasonable care as to sale value.","D":"Incorrect. Any sale below the mortgagor's preferred figure is automatically void does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The mortgagee may realise its security but must act with reasonable care as to sale value.","E":"Incorrect. The mortgagee acts as the mortgagor's trustee in every respect does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The mortgagee may realise its security but must act with reasonable care as to sale value."},"primary_legal_authority":"Cuckmere Brick Co Ltd v Mutual Finance Ltd [1971] Ch 949.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The mortgagee may realise its security but must act with reasonable care as to sale value.","common_trap":"Equating the duty with either no price obligation or a duty to maximise value regardless of timing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e9255af8dcadd1a0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0997","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-contract searches — drainage and water","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A residential buyer wants information about whether the property is connected to mains water and sewerage and whether public sewers may affect the site.\n\nWhich search is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 priority search.","B":"A drainage and water search.","C":"A bankruptcy search.","D":"A Companies House search.","E":"A probate search."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A drainage and water search provides transaction-specific information concerning water and sewerage connections and related infrastructure relevant to the property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An OS1 priority search does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Conveyancing searches have distinct purposes; drainage information is not supplied by a title-priority search.","B":"Correct. A drainage and water search provides transaction-specific information concerning water and sewerage connections and related infrastructure relevant to the property.","C":"Incorrect. A bankruptcy search does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Conveyancing searches have distinct purposes; drainage information is not supplied by a title-priority search.","D":"Incorrect. A Companies House search does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Conveyancing searches have distinct purposes; drainage information is not supplied by a title-priority search.","E":"Incorrect. A probate search does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Conveyancing searches have distinct purposes; drainage information is not supplied by a title-priority search."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; standard CON29DW drainage and water enquiries.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Conveyancing searches have distinct purposes; drainage information is not supplied by a title-priority search.","common_trap":"Using a registration or personal-insolvency search for physical utility information.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ce922611241315e2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0998","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial property — VAT option to tax","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller of commercial property has made an effective option to tax that applies to the property. No disapplication or special transaction treatment applies.\n\nWhich is the best general consequence for the sale?","options":{"A":"An option to tax converts VAT into SDLT.","B":"The option always exempts the sale from VAT.","C":"The option can make a supply of the property subject to VAT that might otherwise be exempt, subject to the detailed VAT land rules.","D":"Only the buyer can make an option to tax on behalf of the seller.","E":"The option changes legal title without a transfer."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Commercial land supplies are often exempt, but an effective option to tax can cause relevant supplies by the person who opted to become taxable, subject to statutory exceptions and disapplication rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. An option to tax converts VAT into SDLT does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The option affects the VAT treatment of supplies of the opted land; it does not itself transfer title or replace other taxes.","B":"Incorrect. The option always exempts the sale from VAT does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The option affects the VAT treatment of supplies of the opted land; it does not itself transfer title or replace other taxes.","C":"Correct. Commercial land supplies are often exempt, but an effective option to tax can cause relevant supplies by the person who opted to become taxable, subject to statutory exceptions and disapplication rules.","D":"Incorrect. Only the buyer can make an option to tax on behalf of the seller does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The option affects the VAT treatment of supplies of the opted land; it does not itself transfer title or replace other taxes.","E":"Incorrect. The option changes legal title without a transfer does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The option affects the VAT treatment of supplies of the opted land; it does not itself transfer title or replace other taxes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Value Added Tax Act 1994, Schedule 10.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The option affects the VAT treatment of supplies of the opted land; it does not itself transfer title or replace other taxes.","common_trap":"Reversing the effect of the option or treating it as a conveyancing instrument.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d4966fc1283efd45"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0999","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Non-fatal offences — consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally causes another adult a minor injury during a properly conducted contact sport within the rules and ordinary expectations of that sport.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Consent can never be relevant to any offence against the person.","B":"Consent always legalises any level of deliberately inflicted injury.","C":"Sport automatically prevents criminal liability even for conduct wholly outside what participants consented to.","D":"Consent can operate within recognised categories such as properly conducted sport, subject to the scope of consent and public-policy limits.","E":"Consent is relevant only to property offences."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"English criminal law recognises circumstances in which consent to injury is effective, including properly conducted sports, while imposing limits based on the nature of the conduct, the scope of consent and public policy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Consent can never be relevant to any offence against the person does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Sport is a recognised context for consent, but the defence is not unlimited.","B":"Incorrect. Consent always legalises any level of deliberately inflicted injury does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Sport is a recognised context for consent, but the defence is not unlimited.","C":"Incorrect. Sport automatically prevents criminal liability even for conduct wholly outside what participants consented to does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Sport is a recognised context for consent, but the defence is not unlimited.","D":"Correct. English criminal law recognises circumstances in which consent to injury is effective, including properly conducted sports, while imposing limits based on the nature of the conduct, the scope of consent and public policy.","E":"Incorrect. Consent is relevant only to property offences does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Sport is a recognised context for consent, but the defence is not unlimited."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Brown [1994] 1 AC 212; R v Barnes [2004] EWCA Crim 3246.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Sport is a recognised context for consent, but the defence is not unlimited.","common_trap":"Treating consent as either categorically unavailable or universally effective regardless of the conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bdd2988ef8d70cb6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1000","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Youth courts — allocation and venue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A child is charged with an offence and appears before the criminal courts. No exceptional circumstance presently requires trial in the Crown Court.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every child must be tried in the Crown Court.","B":"Children are tried in the ordinary adult magistrates' court under exactly the same arrangements in every case.","C":"The defendant may choose any court regardless of statute.","D":"Youth courts deal only with civil safeguarding proceedings.","E":"Children are generally dealt with in the youth court, subject to statutory exceptions governing matters such as grave crimes and cases linked with adults."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The statutory scheme generally directs criminal proceedings against children to the youth court, a specialist form of magistrates' court, while providing defined exceptions in which Crown Court proceedings may occur.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every child must be tried in the Crown Court does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Venue for child defendants is governed by specialist statutory rules rather than adult allocation principles alone.","B":"Incorrect. Children are tried in the ordinary adult magistrates' court under exactly the same arrangements in every case does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Venue for child defendants is governed by specialist statutory rules rather than adult allocation principles alone.","C":"Incorrect. The defendant may choose any court regardless of statute does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Venue for child defendants is governed by specialist statutory rules rather than adult allocation principles alone.","D":"Incorrect. Youth courts deal only with civil safeguarding proceedings does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Venue for child defendants is governed by specialist statutory rules rather than adult allocation principles alone.","E":"Correct. The statutory scheme generally directs criminal proceedings against children to the youth court, a specialist form of magistrates' court, while providing defined exceptions in which Crown Court proceedings may occur."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; Children and Young Persons Act 1933; Crime and Disorder Act 1998.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Venue for child defendants is governed by specialist statutory rules rather than adult allocation principles alone.","common_trap":"Applying ordinary adult trial allocation without considering the youth-justice regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0fb43ee66fe0e44d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1001","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — derivative claims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minority shareholder alleges that a director breached duties owed to the company, causing loss to the company. The shareholder wants to pursue the company's cause of action.\n\nWhich procedure is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A statutory derivative claim brought on behalf of the company, subject to the permission procedure and statutory criteria.","B":"A personal debt claim for the whole of the company's loss.","C":"An unfair-dismissal claim.","D":"A winding-up petition that automatically awards damages to the shareholder.","E":"A claim requiring no court permission because every shareholder owns the company's causes of action."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act permits a member to bring a derivative claim in respect of a cause of action vested in the company arising from specified director wrongdoing, subject to a staged court-permission process.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act permits a member to bring a derivative claim in respect of a cause of action vested in the company arising from specified director wrongdoing, subject to a staged court-permission process.","B":"Incorrect. A personal debt claim for the whole of the company's loss does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The remedy addresses wrongs to the company while controlling shareholder interference with corporate litigation.","C":"Incorrect. An unfair-dismissal claim does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The remedy addresses wrongs to the company while controlling shareholder interference with corporate litigation.","D":"Incorrect. A winding-up petition that automatically awards damages to the shareholder does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The remedy addresses wrongs to the company while controlling shareholder interference with corporate litigation.","E":"Incorrect. A claim requiring no court permission because every shareholder owns the company's causes of action does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The remedy addresses wrongs to the company while controlling shareholder interference with corporate litigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 260–264.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The remedy addresses wrongs to the company while controlling shareholder interference with corporate litigation.","common_trap":"Confusing company loss with a shareholder's personal cause of action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"08eb8c45a43bde7b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1002","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — unfair prejudice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A majority shareholder uses control of a small company to divert business opportunities to another company they own, materially harming the minority shareholder's interests.\n\nWhich statutory remedy is most directly relevant to the minority shareholder?","options":{"A":"A derivative claim is the only possible shareholder remedy in all circumstances.","B":"A petition alleging that the company's affairs are being conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests.","C":"Automatic dissolution without a court application.","D":"A criminal prosecution under the Partnership Act.","E":"A Part 36 offer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 994 allows a member to petition where company affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers, commonly including share-purchase orders.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A derivative claim is the only possible shareholder remedy in all circumstances does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against unfairly harmful conduct of company affairs and is distinct from pursuing the company's own cause of action.","B":"Correct. Section 994 allows a member to petition where company affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers, commonly including share-purchase orders.","C":"Incorrect. Automatic dissolution without a court application does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against unfairly harmful conduct of company affairs and is distinct from pursuing the company's own cause of action.","D":"Incorrect. A criminal prosecution under the Partnership Act does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against unfairly harmful conduct of company affairs and is distinct from pursuing the company's own cause of action.","E":"Incorrect. A Part 36 offer does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against unfairly harmful conduct of company affairs and is distinct from pursuing the company's own cause of action."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 994–996.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unfair prejudice protects members against unfairly harmful conduct of company affairs and is distinct from pursuing the company's own cause of action.","common_trap":"Assuming every complaint involving director or majority misconduct must be framed only as a derivative claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f50209a627d05615"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1003","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — transaction at an undervalue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Shortly before insolvency, a company transfers a valuable asset to a connected person for a price substantially below its value. The statutory timing and insolvency conditions are satisfied.\n\nWhich avoidance provision is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A preference only, because undervalue is irrelevant.","B":"Wrongful trading automatically.","C":"A transaction at an undervalue under the Insolvency Act 1986.","D":"A floating-charge registration application.","E":"A shareholder written resolution."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Insolvency Act permits challenge to qualifying gifts and transactions for significantly less than value entered into during the relevant period, subject to statutory conditions and defences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A preference only, because undervalue is irrelevant does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The focus is depletion of the insolvent estate through inadequate consideration.","B":"Incorrect. Wrongful trading automatically does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The focus is depletion of the insolvent estate through inadequate consideration.","C":"Correct. The Insolvency Act permits challenge to qualifying gifts and transactions for significantly less than value entered into during the relevant period, subject to statutory conditions and defences.","D":"Incorrect. A floating-charge registration application does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The focus is depletion of the insolvent estate through inadequate consideration.","E":"Incorrect. A shareholder written resolution does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The focus is depletion of the insolvent estate through inadequate consideration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 238.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The focus is depletion of the insolvent estate through inadequate consideration.","common_trap":"Confusing an undervalue transaction with a preference, which focuses on improving a creditor or guarantor's position because of a desire to prefer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6f185a08caa52e74"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1004","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Appeals — permission and grounds","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party wants to appeal a civil judgment simply because it would prefer a different factual conclusion.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every losing party has an automatic unrestricted rehearing.","B":"An appeal is allowed whenever the appellant disagrees with a factual finding.","C":"Permission can never be required in civil appeals.","D":"An appeal ordinarily requires permission and is generally allowed only where the lower court was wrong or the decision was unjust because of a serious procedural or other irregularity; it is not simply a full retrial by right.","E":"The appeal court must hear all witnesses again in every case."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"CPR Part 52 treats an appeal principally as a review unless otherwise ordered. Permission is generally required and the appeal court applies the specified 'wrong' or serious-irregularity standards.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every losing party has an automatic unrestricted rehearing does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Appeal is a controlled review mechanism, not an automatic second trial.","B":"Incorrect. An appeal is allowed whenever the appellant disagrees with a factual finding does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Appeal is a controlled review mechanism, not an automatic second trial.","C":"Incorrect. Permission can never be required in civil appeals does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Appeal is a controlled review mechanism, not an automatic second trial.","D":"Correct. CPR Part 52 treats an appeal principally as a review unless otherwise ordered. Permission is generally required and the appeal court applies the specified 'wrong' or serious-irregularity standards.","E":"Incorrect. The appeal court must hear all witnesses again in every case does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Appeal is a controlled review mechanism, not an automatic second trial."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 52, especially rr 52.6 and 52.21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appeal is a controlled review mechanism, not an automatic second trial.","common_trap":"Equating dissatisfaction with appealable error.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"59a2527fe3bca5a5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1005","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Settlement — Tomlin orders","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parties settle civil proceedings on detailed confidential terms that include obligations going beyond the relief the court could conveniently order in the proceedings. They want the claim stayed while retaining a mechanism to enforce the settlement.\n\nWhich mechanism is commonly appropriate?","options":{"A":"A default judgment against both parties.","B":"A criminal restraining order.","C":"A statutory demand.","D":"A costs budget.","E":"A Tomlin order, with the proceedings stayed on agreed terms contained in a schedule and liberty to apply for enforcement."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A Tomlin order stays proceedings on agreed terms, commonly placing the substantive settlement in a schedule and allowing a party to apply to enforce the compromise without commencing an entirely new claim where appropriate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A default judgment against both parties does not fit the governing rule on these facts. It is especially useful for settlement terms extending beyond the ordinary form of a court order.","B":"Incorrect. A criminal restraining order does not fit the governing rule on these facts. It is especially useful for settlement terms extending beyond the ordinary form of a court order.","C":"Incorrect. A statutory demand does not fit the governing rule on these facts. It is especially useful for settlement terms extending beyond the ordinary form of a court order.","D":"Incorrect. A costs budget does not fit the governing rule on these facts. It is especially useful for settlement terms extending beyond the ordinary form of a court order.","E":"Correct. A Tomlin order stays proceedings on agreed terms, commonly placing the substantive settlement in a schedule and allowing a party to apply to enforce the compromise without commencing an entirely new claim where appropriate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tomlin v Standard Telephones and Cables Ltd [1969] 1 WLR 1378; CPR Part 40 practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"It is especially useful for settlement terms extending beyond the ordinary form of a court order.","common_trap":"Confusing consensual settlement machinery with judgment or insolvency enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7afcdeb434894e9b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1006","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — conflicts between current clients","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor acts for two current clients whose interests become directly adverse in the same transaction. No regulatory exception can properly be satisfied.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The solicitor must not continue acting for both clients in the conflict matter and must address whether they can continue for either client consistently with confidentiality and professional obligations.","B":"The solicitor may always continue if both clients pay the fees.","C":"Client consent automatically cures every current-client conflict.","D":"The solicitor must disclose each client's confidential information to the other.","E":"Conflicts rules apply only to litigation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The SRA Code prohibits acting where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between current clients unless a narrowly defined exception applies and its conditions are satisfied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The SRA Code prohibits acting where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between current clients unless a narrowly defined exception applies and its conditions are satisfied.","B":"Incorrect. The solicitor may always continue if both clients pay the fees does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Consent alone is not a universal cure; the regulatory exception itself must be available and properly managed.","C":"Incorrect. Client consent automatically cures every current-client conflict does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Consent alone is not a universal cure; the regulatory exception itself must be available and properly managed.","D":"Incorrect. The solicitor must disclose each client's confidential information to the other does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Consent alone is not a universal cure; the regulatory exception itself must be available and properly managed.","E":"Incorrect. Conflicts rules apply only to litigation does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Consent alone is not a universal cure; the regulatory exception itself must be available and properly managed."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consent alone is not a universal cure; the regulatory exception itself must be available and properly managed.","common_trap":"Treating informed consent as sufficient without applying the Code's substantive conflict requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cd40c546a60b7509"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1007","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract formation — certainty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parties sign a document stating that a key price will be 'agreed between us later', with no objective mechanism for determining it if they fail to agree.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Courts always invent a price regardless of the parties' wording.","B":"The purported agreement may fail for uncertainty if an essential term is left to future agreement without an objective machinery capable of resolving it.","C":"An agreement to agree is invariably enforceable as a fixed-price contract.","D":"Certainty matters only to deeds.","E":"The missing price automatically becomes zero."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A binding contract requires sufficient certainty. An agreement to agree an essential matter in the future can be unenforceable where no objective mechanism permits the court to ascertain the parties' obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Courts always invent a price regardless of the parties' wording does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Courts interpret and sometimes imply machinery, but do not ordinarily make the parties' bargain for them.","B":"Correct. A binding contract requires sufficient certainty. An agreement to agree an essential matter in the future can be unenforceable where no objective mechanism permits the court to ascertain the parties' obligation.","C":"Incorrect. An agreement to agree is invariably enforceable as a fixed-price contract does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Courts interpret and sometimes imply machinery, but do not ordinarily make the parties' bargain for them.","D":"Incorrect. Certainty matters only to deeds does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Courts interpret and sometimes imply machinery, but do not ordinarily make the parties' bargain for them.","E":"Incorrect. The missing price automatically becomes zero does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Courts interpret and sometimes imply machinery, but do not ordinarily make the parties' bargain for them."},"primary_legal_authority":"May & Butcher Ltd v R [1934] 2 KB 17; Walford v Miles [1992] 2 AC 128.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Courts interpret and sometimes imply machinery, but do not ordinarily make the parties' bargain for them.","common_trap":"Assuming every incomplete bargain can be rescued by judicial selection of a reasonable term.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dd31afd3c00498b4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1008","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Exemption clauses — incorporation by notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A car park operator seeks to rely on an onerous exclusion clause printed only inside the premises after the customer has already entered and paid, with no earlier notice of it.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any displayed term binds regardless of when the customer first sees it.","B":"Exclusion clauses can be incorporated only by signature.","C":"The clause may not be incorporated because reasonable notice of a contractual term must ordinarily be given before or at the time the contract is made, with particularly onerous terms requiring suitably prominent notice.","D":"A term first disclosed after formation automatically varies the contract.","E":"Notice is irrelevant if the clause benefits a business."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Incorporation by notice requires reasonable steps to bring the term to the other party's attention before or at contracting; unusual or onerous clauses may demand especially clear notice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Any displayed term binds regardless of when the customer first sees it does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A party cannot ordinarily add an exclusion term unilaterally after contract formation.","B":"Incorrect. Exclusion clauses can be incorporated only by signature does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A party cannot ordinarily add an exclusion term unilaterally after contract formation.","C":"Correct. Incorporation by notice requires reasonable steps to bring the term to the other party's attention before or at contracting; unusual or onerous clauses may demand especially clear notice.","D":"Incorrect. A term first disclosed after formation automatically varies the contract does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A party cannot ordinarily add an exclusion term unilaterally after contract formation.","E":"Incorrect. Notice is irrelevant if the clause benefits a business does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A party cannot ordinarily add an exclusion term unilaterally after contract formation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Olley v Marlborough Court Ltd [1949] 1 KB 532; Interfoto Picture Library Ltd v Stiletto Visual Programmes Ltd [1989] QB 433.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A party cannot ordinarily add an exclusion term unilaterally after contract formation.","common_trap":"Confusing post-contract information with timely incorporation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"878d8d7664e82556"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1009","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — remoteness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D negligently causes physical damage of a kind that was reasonably foreseeable, although the precise sequence by which the damage occurred was unusual.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every detail of the causal sequence must have been foreseeable.","B":"Foreseeability is irrelevant to remoteness.","C":"D is liable only for the exact accident previously predicted.","D":"Damage is not necessarily too remote merely because the precise mechanism was unforeseeable, provided the relevant kind of damage was reasonably foreseeable.","E":"Any unusual sequence automatically breaks causation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Remoteness in negligence generally asks whether the kind or type of damage was reasonably foreseeable, not whether the defendant could predict the precise manner in which it occurred.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Every detail of the causal sequence must have been foreseeable does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Foreseeability operates at the level of the relevant kind of harm rather than every detail of the accident.","B":"Incorrect. Foreseeability is irrelevant to remoteness does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Foreseeability operates at the level of the relevant kind of harm rather than every detail of the accident.","C":"Incorrect. D is liable only for the exact accident previously predicted does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Foreseeability operates at the level of the relevant kind of harm rather than every detail of the accident.","D":"Correct. Remoteness in negligence generally asks whether the kind or type of damage was reasonably foreseeable, not whether the defendant could predict the precise manner in which it occurred.","E":"Incorrect. Any unusual sequence automatically breaks causation does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Foreseeability operates at the level of the relevant kind of harm rather than every detail of the accident."},"primary_legal_authority":"The Wagon Mound (No 1) [1961] AC 388; Hughes v Lord Advocate [1963] AC 837.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Foreseeability operates at the level of the relevant kind of harm rather than every detail of the accident.","common_trap":"Demanding foresight of the exact mechanism instead of the type of damage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"86639f2c5d844e2d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1010","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — public interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A journalist publishes a statement on a matter of public interest after taking substantial verification steps and genuinely concludes that publication is in the public interest.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Honest opinion necessarily, even if the statement is presented as fact.","B":"Absolute privilege for all journalism.","C":"Consent, regardless of whether the claimant consented.","D":"Contributory negligence.","E":"Publication on a matter of public interest, if the statutory requirements are satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 4 provides a defence where the statement complained of was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Honest opinion necessarily, even if the statement is presented as fact does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The defence focuses on subject matter and the reasonableness of the defendant's public-interest belief.","B":"Incorrect. Absolute privilege for all journalism does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The defence focuses on subject matter and the reasonableness of the defendant's public-interest belief.","C":"Incorrect. Consent, regardless of whether the claimant consented does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The defence focuses on subject matter and the reasonableness of the defendant's public-interest belief.","D":"Incorrect. Contributory negligence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The defence focuses on subject matter and the reasonableness of the defendant's public-interest belief.","E":"Correct. Section 4 provides a defence where the statement complained of was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defence focuses on subject matter and the reasonableness of the defendant's public-interest belief.","common_trap":"Assuming journalism receives absolute privilege or confusing factual public-interest reporting with opinion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a86c8b995fe0c082"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1011","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — standing","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A campaigning organisation with expertise in an environmental field challenges a public decision directly affecting the statutory scheme it monitors. The defendant argues that only a person suffering financial loss can seek judicial review.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Judicial review requires a 'sufficient interest'; standing is contextual and does not depend exclusively on personal financial loss.","B":"Only a person with a contractual right can seek judicial review.","C":"Campaign groups can never have standing.","D":"Standing is automatic for every member of the public regardless of the issue.","E":"A claimant must prove damages before permission."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Senior Courts Act requires sufficient interest. Courts assess standing in context, including the nature of the issue, the claimant's connection and expertise, and the public-law function being challenged.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Senior Courts Act requires sufficient interest. Courts assess standing in context, including the nature of the issue, the claimant's connection and expertise, and the public-law function being challenged.","B":"Incorrect. Only a person with a contractual right can seek judicial review does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Public-law standing is broader and more contextual than a requirement for private financial injury.","C":"Incorrect. Campaign groups can never have standing does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Public-law standing is broader and more contextual than a requirement for private financial injury.","D":"Incorrect. Standing is automatic for every member of the public regardless of the issue does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Public-law standing is broader and more contextual than a requirement for private financial injury.","E":"Incorrect. A claimant must prove damages before permission does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Public-law standing is broader and more contextual than a requirement for private financial injury."},"primary_legal_authority":"Senior Courts Act 1981, s 31(3); R v IRC, ex p National Federation of Self-Employed [1982] AC 617.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public-law standing is broader and more contextual than a requirement for private financial injury.","common_trap":"Importing private-law loss requirements into judicial-review standing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"efbb1d56488a40b5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1012","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — declaration of incompatibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A higher court concludes that primary legislation cannot be read compatibly with a Convention right using section 3.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The court must strike down the Act as void.","B":"A court empowered by section 4 may make a declaration of incompatibility, which does not invalidate the legislation or automatically remove its legal effect.","C":"The declaration automatically repeals the provision.","D":"Only the European Court of Human Rights can identify incompatibility under UK law.","E":"A declaration makes the government automatically liable in damages to everyone."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 4 permits specified higher courts to declare primary legislation incompatible where compatible interpretation is unavailable. The declaration does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The court must strike down the Act as void does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The HRA preserves parliamentary sovereignty while creating a formal judicial signal of incompatibility.","B":"Correct. Section 4 permits specified higher courts to declare primary legislation incompatible where compatible interpretation is unavailable. The declaration does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","C":"Incorrect. The declaration automatically repeals the provision does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The HRA preserves parliamentary sovereignty while creating a formal judicial signal of incompatibility.","D":"Incorrect. Only the European Court of Human Rights can identify incompatibility under UK law does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The HRA preserves parliamentary sovereignty while creating a formal judicial signal of incompatibility.","E":"Incorrect. A declaration makes the government automatically liable in damages to everyone does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The HRA preserves parliamentary sovereignty while creating a formal judicial signal of incompatibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, ss 3–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The HRA preserves parliamentary sovereignty while creating a formal judicial signal of incompatibility.","common_trap":"Treating a declaration as constitutional strike-down.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"238aa6a551f9263e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1013","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — ademption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will specifically gives 'my shares in X plc' to B. Before death, the testator validly sells all those shares and owns none at death. No replacement or statutory exception applies.\n\nWhich is the ordinary result?","options":{"A":"B automatically receives the sale proceeds regardless of the estate's circumstances.","B":"The executors must repurchase identical shares.","C":"The specific gift is adeemed and B ordinarily receives nothing under that gift.","D":"The whole will is revoked.","E":"B takes the residuary estate instead."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A specific legacy ordinarily fails by ademption where the specifically identified property is no longer part of the testator's estate at death, subject to recognised exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. B automatically receives the sale proceeds regardless of the estate's circumstances does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Specific gifts depend on the relevant property remaining in the estate at death.","B":"Incorrect. The executors must repurchase identical shares does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Specific gifts depend on the relevant property remaining in the estate at death.","C":"Correct. A specific legacy ordinarily fails by ademption where the specifically identified property is no longer part of the testator's estate at death, subject to recognised exceptions.","D":"Incorrect. The whole will is revoked does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Specific gifts depend on the relevant property remaining in the estate at death.","E":"Incorrect. B takes the residuary estate instead does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Specific gifts depend on the relevant property remaining in the estate at death."},"primary_legal_authority":"Ademption principles in the law of wills.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Specific gifts depend on the relevant property remaining in the estate at death.","common_trap":"Assuming the beneficiary automatically traces into disposal proceeds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc0e07adf90ed29e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1014","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — order of payment of debts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solvent estate contains several classes of assets and debts. The will does not contain a provision displacing the statutory order applicable to payment of debts.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Specific devisees always bear every debt first.","B":"Residuary beneficiaries can require creditors to wait until all legacies are paid.","C":"Debts disappear on death if the estate is solvent.","D":"Personal representatives must apply estate assets to debts and liabilities in the statutory order, rather than choosing arbitrarily which beneficiaries bear the burden.","E":"The personal representatives may allocate debts according to personal preference."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Administration of Estates Act establishes an order for applying assets toward funeral, testamentary and administration expenses, debts and liabilities, subject to the will and statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Specific devisees always bear every debt first does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Administration requires lawful marshalling of estate assets before distribution to beneficiaries.","B":"Incorrect. Residuary beneficiaries can require creditors to wait until all legacies are paid does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Administration requires lawful marshalling of estate assets before distribution to beneficiaries.","C":"Incorrect. Debts disappear on death if the estate is solvent does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Administration requires lawful marshalling of estate assets before distribution to beneficiaries.","D":"Correct. The Administration of Estates Act establishes an order for applying assets toward funeral, testamentary and administration expenses, debts and liabilities, subject to the will and statutory rules.","E":"Incorrect. The personal representatives may allocate debts according to personal preference does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Administration requires lawful marshalling of estate assets before distribution to beneficiaries."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 34 and Schedule 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Administration requires lawful marshalling of estate assets before distribution to beneficiaries.","common_trap":"Assuming representatives have an unrestricted discretion to shift debt burdens among beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a00f6038769cb7e2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1015","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Accounts — client account withdrawals","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor wants to withdraw money from client account to pay the firm's billed costs. The client has been given a bill or other written notification of the costs.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Once a bill is issued, the firm may take any amount it chooses from client account.","B":"Client money can never be used to pay billed costs.","C":"The whole client balance automatically becomes business money when a bill is sent.","D":"No written notification is relevant to withdrawal for costs.","E":"The firm may transfer the amount properly due for billed costs in accordance with the Accounts Rules, but must not withdraw more than is properly available for that purpose."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Accounts Rules permit withdrawal of client money for the firm's costs after a bill or other written notification has been given, but only to the extent properly due and in accordance with the rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Once a bill is issued, the firm may take any amount it chooses from client account does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Billing can authorise an appropriate transfer; it does not convert unrelated client funds into firm money.","B":"Incorrect. Client money can never be used to pay billed costs does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Billing can authorise an appropriate transfer; it does not convert unrelated client funds into firm money.","C":"Incorrect. The whole client balance automatically becomes business money when a bill is sent does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Billing can authorise an appropriate transfer; it does not convert unrelated client funds into firm money.","D":"Incorrect. No written notification is relevant to withdrawal for costs does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Billing can authorise an appropriate transfer; it does not convert unrelated client funds into firm money.","E":"Correct. The Accounts Rules permit withdrawal of client money for the firm's costs after a bill or other written notification has been given, but only to the extent properly due and in accordance with the rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 4.3 and 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Billing can authorise an appropriate transfer; it does not convert unrelated client funds into firm money.","common_trap":"Treating a bill as authority to appropriate the entire client balance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"32c2794411190748"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1016","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — duty to consider exercise of discretion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee holding a discretionary power simply adopts a permanent policy never to consider exercising it, regardless of beneficiaries' circumstances.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A trustee must genuinely consider from time to time whether and how to exercise a fiduciary discretion and cannot simply fetter it by refusing ever to consider its exercise.","B":"A trustee may permanently surrender every discretion without considering the trust purposes.","C":"Beneficiaries can dictate every discretionary decision.","D":"A discretionary power must always be exercised in favour of every beneficiary equally.","E":"Courts automatically exercise all trustee discretions instead."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Trustees must exercise fiduciary powers properly, considering relevant matters and the purposes for which the power was conferred. A rigid refusal to consider exercise can amount to a failure of duty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Trustees must exercise fiduciary powers properly, considering relevant matters and the purposes for which the power was conferred. A rigid refusal to consider exercise can amount to a failure of duty.","B":"Incorrect. A trustee may permanently surrender every discretion without considering the trust purposes does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A discretion entails genuine decision-making rather than mechanical abdication.","C":"Incorrect. Beneficiaries can dictate every discretionary decision does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A discretion entails genuine decision-making rather than mechanical abdication.","D":"Incorrect. A discretionary power must always be exercised in favour of every beneficiary equally does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A discretion entails genuine decision-making rather than mechanical abdication.","E":"Incorrect. Courts automatically exercise all trustee discretions instead does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A discretion entails genuine decision-making rather than mechanical abdication."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Hay's Settlement Trusts [1982] 1 WLR 202; fiduciary decision-making principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A discretion entails genuine decision-making rather than mechanical abdication.","common_trap":"Confusing freedom as to outcome with freedom not to consider the power at all.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9e89c1cc7044cf3d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1017","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — overriding interests and actual occupation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary has a proprietary interest in registered land and is in actual occupation at the time of a registered disposition. The interest has not been overreached.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Actual occupation can never affect priority in registered land.","B":"The interest may override the disposition under Schedule 3 paragraph 2, subject to the statutory exceptions concerning discoverability, enquiry and disclosure.","C":"Every person physically present automatically has a proprietary overriding interest.","D":"Overriding status depends only on whether the interest is registered as a charge.","E":"Actual occupation automatically creates ownership."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Certain proprietary interests of persons in actual occupation can override registered dispositions under Schedule 3 paragraph 2, but the statute contains important exceptions and occupation itself does not create the underlying interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Actual occupation can never affect priority in registered land does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The claimant needs both a proprietary interest and qualifying actual occupation, with the statutory exceptions considered.","B":"Correct. Certain proprietary interests of persons in actual occupation can override registered dispositions under Schedule 3 paragraph 2, but the statute contains important exceptions and occupation itself does not create the underlying interest.","C":"Incorrect. Every person physically present automatically has a proprietary overriding interest does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The claimant needs both a proprietary interest and qualifying actual occupation, with the statutory exceptions considered.","D":"Incorrect. Overriding status depends only on whether the interest is registered as a charge does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The claimant needs both a proprietary interest and qualifying actual occupation, with the statutory exceptions considered.","E":"Incorrect. Actual occupation automatically creates ownership does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The claimant needs both a proprietary interest and qualifying actual occupation, with the statutory exceptions considered."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Schedule 3, para 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The claimant needs both a proprietary interest and qualifying actual occupation, with the statutory exceptions considered.","common_trap":"Treating physical presence alone as either irrelevant or sufficient to create proprietary rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9eeba5e40c4c3a77"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1018","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Completion — undertakings to redeem mortgage","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller's registered title is subject to a mortgage that will be repaid from the completion monies. The buyer requires the property free from that charge.\n\nWhich conveyancing protection is commonly central to completion?","options":{"A":"The buyer should simply assume the lender will remove the charge eventually.","B":"The mortgage automatically disappears on exchange.","C":"A solicitor's undertaking to use completion funds to redeem the mortgage and procure discharge, supported by the relevant completion arrangements.","D":"An environmental search removes registered charges.","E":"The buyer must personally pay the seller's lender before receiving any contractual protection."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where a seller's mortgage is to be discharged on completion, the buyer commonly relies on the seller's solicitor's professional undertaking to redeem and procure discharge, within established completion practice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The buyer should simply assume the lender will remove the charge eventually does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The undertaking bridges the timing gap between completion payment and formal removal of the registered charge.","B":"Incorrect. The mortgage automatically disappears on exchange does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The undertaking bridges the timing gap between completion payment and formal removal of the registered charge.","C":"Correct. Where a seller's mortgage is to be discharged on completion, the buyer commonly relies on the seller's solicitor's professional undertaking to redeem and procure discharge, within established completion practice.","D":"Incorrect. An environmental search removes registered charges does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The undertaking bridges the timing gap between completion payment and formal removal of the registered charge.","E":"Incorrect. The buyer must personally pay the seller's lender before receiving any contractual protection does not fit the governing rule on these facts. The undertaking bridges the timing gap between completion payment and formal removal of the registered charge."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct, para 1.3; Law Society Code for Completion by Post where used; conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The undertaking bridges the timing gap between completion payment and formal removal of the registered charge.","common_trap":"Assuming transfer of purchase money itself clears the register without redemption machinery.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9f0d69745af6b035"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1019","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Secondary liability — assisting or encouraging","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally assists P to commit an offence, knowing the essential facts that make P's conduct criminal. P commits the offence with the required mens rea.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"D can be liable only if D personally performs the actus reus of the principal offence.","B":"Mere association always proves secondary liability.","C":"Secondary liability requires D to control P's actions completely.","D":"D may be liable as a secondary party if D intentionally assisted or encouraged the offence with the legally required knowledge or belief as to the essential facts.","E":"D is automatically innocent whenever P is the person who physically commits the offence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Secondary liability can arise where a defendant intentionally assists or encourages the principal offence while possessing the required state of mind concerning the essential facts or circumstances. Foresight is evidence, not the former doctrine of parasitic accessory liability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. D can be liable only if D personally performs the actus reus of the principal offence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. An accessory need not perform the principal act but must intentionally assist or encourage with the required mens rea.","B":"Incorrect. Mere association always proves secondary liability does not fit the governing rule on these facts. An accessory need not perform the principal act but must intentionally assist or encourage with the required mens rea.","C":"Incorrect. Secondary liability requires D to control P's actions completely does not fit the governing rule on these facts. An accessory need not perform the principal act but must intentionally assist or encourage with the required mens rea.","D":"Correct. Secondary liability can arise where a defendant intentionally assists or encourages the principal offence while possessing the required state of mind concerning the essential facts or circumstances. Foresight is evidence, not the former doctrine of parasitic accessory liability.","E":"Incorrect. D is automatically innocent whenever P is the person who physically commits the offence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. An accessory need not perform the principal act but must intentionally assist or encourage with the required mens rea."},"primary_legal_authority":"Accessories and Abettors Act 1861, s 8; R v Jogee [2016] UKSC 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An accessory need not perform the principal act but must intentionally assist or encourage with the required mens rea.","common_trap":"Equating physical commission with the only route to criminal responsibility, or treating mere presence as sufficient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e168f13023e2d28e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1020","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — credit for guilty plea","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant indicates an unequivocal guilty plea at the first stage of proceedings at which the plea is sought. The court later sentences the defendant.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A guilty plea can never affect sentence.","B":"The court must acquit because a plea saves trial time.","C":"Every guilty plea automatically halves the sentence.","D":"Credit depends solely on whether the prosecution agrees.","E":"The timing of the guilty plea is relevant to sentence reduction under the applicable guideline, with the greatest reduction ordinarily available for a plea indicated at the first stage."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Sentencing law requires the court to take a guilty plea into account, and the guideline structures the reduction principally by reference to the stage at which the plea is indicated, subject to its detailed provisions and exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. A guilty plea can never affect sentence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Early indication ordinarily attracts greater credit because it secures the benefits of a guilty plea at the earliest stage.","B":"Incorrect. The court must acquit because a plea saves trial time does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Early indication ordinarily attracts greater credit because it secures the benefits of a guilty plea at the earliest stage.","C":"Incorrect. Every guilty plea automatically halves the sentence does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Early indication ordinarily attracts greater credit because it secures the benefits of a guilty plea at the earliest stage.","D":"Incorrect. Credit depends solely on whether the prosecution agrees does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Early indication ordinarily attracts greater credit because it secures the benefits of a guilty plea at the earliest stage.","E":"Correct. Sentencing law requires the court to take a guilty plea into account, and the guideline structures the reduction principally by reference to the stage at which the plea is indicated, subject to its detailed provisions and exceptions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Act 2020, s 73; Sentencing Council, Reduction in Sentence for a Guilty Plea guideline.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Early indication ordinarily attracts greater credit because it secures the benefits of a guilty plea at the earliest stage.","common_trap":"Assuming credit is either unavailable or fixed at an arbitrary percentage in every case.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e996093631b0d210"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1021","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered dispositions — registrable transfer","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A registered freehold owner executes a transfer of the whole registered title to a buyer. The transfer has completed, but the buyer has not yet applied to HM Land Registry.\n\nWhat is the best statement about acquisition of the legal estate?","options":{"A":"The transfer operates at law immediately on execution, whether or not registered.","B":"The buyer acquires the legal estate only when the disposition is completed by registration.","C":"Registration matters only for evidential purposes.","D":"The seller retains both legal and beneficial ownership permanently.","E":"The transfer is void unless preceded by a land charge search."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","B":"Correct. A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 27(1)–(2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A transfer of a registered estate is a registrable disposition and does not operate at law until the registration requirements are met.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"55ec01a6d4178b91"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1022","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — priority period of official search","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer of registered land makes an official search with priority before completion. During the protected priority period, another registrable disposition affecting the seller's title is lodged after the search.\n\nWhat is the principal protection given by the buyer's priority search?","options":{"A":"It freezes the register permanently.","B":"It guarantees that no fraud can occur.","C":"If the buyer completes and applies within the priority period, the buyer's application takes priority over adverse entries lodged after the search, subject to the statutory scheme.","D":"It transfers the legal estate before completion.","E":"It removes all overriding interests."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","C":"Correct. An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules 2003; HM Land Registry priority-search regime.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An official search with priority protects the intended registrable disposition against competing applications lodged during the priority period, provided the protected application is lodged in time.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0467aa09841895cb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1023","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — prescription: nec vi, nec clam, nec precario","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner has used a track over neighbouring land openly and continuously for more than 20 years. The neighbour expressly gave permission for the use throughout that period.\n\nWhy is an easement by prescription unlikely to arise from that use?","options":{"A":"Prescriptive use must be as of right; use by permission is precario and therefore not as of right.","B":"A track can never be the subject of an easement.","C":"Prescription requires the dominant and servient land to have the same owner.","D":"Only five years' use is permitted before a licence becomes an easement.","E":"Prescription is available only for registered leases."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free."},"primary_legal_authority":"Prescription Act 1832 and common-law prescription principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Prescription depends on user 'as of right'—without force, secrecy or permission. Express permission prevents the use being precario-free.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c17d2b8f021cf534"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1024","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — excessive use after change of dominant land","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A house benefits from a right of way granted for access to the house. The owner acquires a separate adjoining parcel and begins using the right of way predominantly to serve a new commercial development on that separate land.\n\nWhat issue most directly arises?","options":{"A":"The easement automatically expands to serve any land later acquired by the dominant owner.","B":"The servient owner must accept every intensified use because the right of way exists.","C":"Use of an easement to benefit land outside the dominant tenement may be impermissible, particularly where the additional use is substantial.","D":"The easement is converted automatically into a lease.","E":"The dominant owner acquires the servient land by adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","C":"Correct. An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Harris v Flower (1904) 74 LJ Ch 127; Peacock v Custins [2001] EWCA Civ 1735.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An easement is appurtenant to the identified dominant land; it cannot ordinarily be used substantially to benefit separate land outside the dominant tenement.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"864f9a3dbd77c7bb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1025","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — clog on equity of redemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A mortgage agreement provides that, even after the borrower repays the loan in full, the lender will retain a permanent option preventing the borrower from recovering the property free of the lender's proprietary control.\n\nWhat equitable principle is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"Once a mortgage, always a mortgage: the borrower must retain a genuine right to redeem, and a collateral term cannot improperly clog the equity of redemption.","B":"Every collateral advantage to a mortgagee is automatically valid.","C":"Redemption is available only if expressly stated in the mortgage.","D":"Repayment automatically transfers the property to the lender.","E":"Equity of redemption applies only to unsecured loans."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable mortgage principles; Kreglinger v New Patagonia Meat [1914] AC 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem; a term that is inconsistent with or improperly fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4267388c33950428"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1026","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — certainty of term","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landlord purports to grant a lease 'for as long as the tenant wishes to remain in occupation', with no maximum duration and no other mechanism making the term certain.\n\nWhat is the central problem with the purported fixed-term lease?","options":{"A":"Rent has not been paid annually.","B":"A lease requires a term certain; an inherently uncertain duration can prevent the grant operating as the intended fixed-term lease.","C":"Leases may never last more than seven years.","D":"Only companies can take leases of uncertain duration.","E":"Registration cures all uncertainty as to duration."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","B":"Correct. A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Prudential Assurance Co Ltd v London Residuary Body [1992] 2 AC 386.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A lease must have a sufficiently certain maximum duration at its commencement; an indeterminate fixed term may fail the certainty requirement.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7abb3a5c55ea4718"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1027","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leasehold covenants — landlord consent and improvements","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A residential long lease contains a qualified covenant against making improvements without the landlord's consent. The tenant proposes a reasonable improvement and asks for consent.\n\nWhat statutory modification is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"The covenant is automatically void.","B":"The landlord may demand transfer of the freehold as a condition.","C":"A qualified covenant against improvements is subject to statutory rules under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, although reasonable conditions may be relevant.","D":"Consent can never be required for improvements.","E":"The tenant automatically acquires planning permission."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","C":"Correct. Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19(2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 19(2) of the LPA 1927 converts relevant qualified improvement covenants into a regime under which consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, with statutory provision for conditions.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5804384f03a2d1c7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1028","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Freehold covenants — annexation of benefit","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deed imposes a restrictive covenant for the benefit of specifically identified neighbouring land retained by the covenantee. Years later that benefited land is sold.\n\nWhich doctrine can enable the benefit to pass with the benefited land without a fresh express assignment on each sale?","options":{"A":"Statutory annexation can attach the benefit of a covenant to land where the statutory requirements are satisfied.","B":"The burden of every positive covenant automatically runs at common law.","C":"A restrictive covenant is always personal and can never benefit successors.","D":"The doctrine of adverse possession transfers the benefit.","E":"Only novation can pass the benefit of a land covenant."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 78; Federated Homes Ltd v Mill Lodge Properties Ltd [1980] 1 WLR 594.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 78 can operate to annex the benefit of a covenant to identified benefited land, allowing successors to enforce where the requirements are satisfied.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a7122d37e368cde2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1029","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — purpose under TLATA","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two co-owners bought a property specifically as a family home. One later seeks an immediate sale after the relationship breaks down, while young children continue to live there.\n\nWhen determining an application concerning sale, what is relevant under TLATA?","options":{"A":"The court must order sale whenever one trustee requests it.","B":"The court considers statutory factors including the intentions of the persons who created the trust and the purposes for which the property is held, as well as welfare and creditor factors where relevant.","C":"The existence of children permanently prevents sale.","D":"Only the property's market value may be considered.","E":"TLATA applies only to commercial property."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","B":"Correct. TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, ss 14–15.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"TLATA gives the court a structured discretion; the original intentions, continuing purposes of the trust, welfare of minors occupying as a home and interests of secured creditors can be relevant.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7080c696d1a4e969"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1030","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Unregistered land — puisne mortgage","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner of unregistered freehold land grants a legal mortgage that is not protected by deposit of title deeds. The mortgage is not registered as the appropriate land charge.\n\nWhat is the principal risk of failing to register the puisne mortgage?","options":{"A":"It automatically becomes a registered title.","B":"It is void against a purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth if the statutory land-charge registration requirement is not met.","C":"It becomes an overriding interest.","D":"It can bind only if the purchaser has actual notice.","E":"It converts into an easement."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","B":"Correct. In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Charges Act 1972, ss 2 and 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"In unregistered conveyancing, a puisne mortgage is registrable as a Class C(i) land charge; non-registration can make it void against the relevant purchaser.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"56cd5006d1d90700"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1031","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Three certainties — conceptual uncertainty of objects","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a discretionary trust for 'all my good friends', without providing any objective criterion for deciding who falls within that description.\n\nWhat is the main validity concern?","options":{"A":"Certainty of subject matter.","B":"Certainty of intention.","C":"Certainty of objects, because the class may lack a sufficiently certain conceptual definition.","D":"The perpetuity period automatically makes every friendship trust valid.","E":"A discretionary trust needs no ascertainable objects."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","C":"Correct. A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class."},"primary_legal_authority":"McPhail v Doulton [1971] AC 424; Re Baden (No 2) [1973] Ch 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A discretionary trust requires conceptually certain objects so that it can be said of any person whether they are or are not within the class.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cb427a972a9fa01a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1032","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Powers — fiduciary power and consideration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee holds a fiduciary power to distribute capital among a defined class. The trustee decides never to consider exercising the power because doing so would be inconvenient.\n\nWhat is the best statement?","options":{"A":"The trustee must exercise the power in favour of every object.","B":"A fiduciary power need not necessarily be exercised, but the trustee must periodically consider whether and how it should be exercised.","C":"The power automatically becomes a fixed trust.","D":"The trustee may permanently ignore the power without consideration.","E":"Every object has an equal proprietary share before exercise."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","B":"Correct. A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment."},"primary_legal_authority":"McPhail v Doulton [1971] AC 424; fiduciary-power principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A fiduciary donee of a power must consider from time to time whether to exercise it, although there is no duty necessarily to make an appointment.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b31ebb4ddf051b8d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1033","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee delegation — asset management functions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees wish to appoint a professional investment manager to exercise delegated asset-management functions.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the statutory framework?","options":{"A":"Trustees can delegate without terms or supervision.","B":"Delegation of asset-management functions is prohibited.","C":"Trustees may delegate qualifying functions but must comply with statutory safeguards, including an appropriate policy statement and review of the agent.","D":"Appointment of an agent permanently removes the trustees' duties.","E":"Only beneficiaries may appoint an investment manager."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 11, 15, 22 and related provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Trustee Act permits delegation of asset-management functions subject to safeguards designed to preserve responsible trustee oversight.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3d0bbea372b14028"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1034","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — duty of care and exclusion","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professionally drafted trust instrument expressly modifies the statutory duty of care for a particular trustee function.\n\nWhat should be checked before assuming the statutory duty applies unchanged?","options":{"A":"Whether Schedule 1 to the Trustee Act 2000 makes the statutory duty applicable and whether the trust instrument validly modifies or excludes it in the relevant context.","B":"The statutory duty can never be affected by the trust instrument.","C":"Professional trustees owe no duty of care.","D":"Only criminal law determines trustee care.","E":"A beneficiary's age determines whether a duty exists."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, s 1 and Sch 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care applies to specified functions and its operation must be considered alongside the terms of the trust instrument.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f4d2a7e2e6358efc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1035","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Breach of trust — equitable compensation and causation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee breaches duty in making an unauthorised transaction, but evidence shows that the same financial loss would have occurred even if the trustee had complied with the relevant duty.\n\nWhat issue is central to equitable compensation?","options":{"A":"Whether the beneficiary personally approved all investments ever made.","B":"Whether the breach caused the loss for which restoration or compensation is sought, applying the equitable principles relevant to the duty breached.","C":"Whether the trustee acted without remuneration.","D":"Whether the trust owns land.","E":"Whether the trustee is also a beneficiary automatically bars compensation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","B":"Correct. Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Target Holdings Ltd v Redferns [1996] AC 421; AIB Group (UK) plc v Mark Redler & Co Solicitors [2014] UKSC 58.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equitable compensation is not necessarily imposed for losses unrelated to the breach; the causal connection must be analysed in light of the nature of the fiduciary or trustee obligation.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4fda405952cf9115"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1036","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Knowing receipt — beneficial receipt","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A third party receives trust property into an account merely as a mechanical intermediary and immediately passes it on, never obtaining beneficial ownership.\n\nWhich element of knowing receipt is most directly in issue?","options":{"A":"Whether the recipient beneficially received trust assets.","B":"Whether the trustee was over 18.","C":"Whether the trust was created by deed.","D":"Whether the recipient is a solicitor.","E":"Whether the property was originally cash."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable."},"primary_legal_authority":"ITC Ltd v Hotels.com LP [2024] UKSC 17; knowing-receipt principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowing receipt ordinarily requires beneficial receipt of assets disposed of in breach of fiduciary duty plus the requisite knowledge making retention unconscionable.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e8ff4a80163e531e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1037","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Dishonest assistance — accessory need not receive property","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person deliberately assists a trustee's dishonest breach of trust but never receives any trust property personally.\n\nCan absence of receipt by itself defeat a dishonest-assistance claim?","options":{"A":"Yes, receipt of trust property is always essential.","B":"Yes, unless the assistant is also a trustee.","C":"No. Dishonest assistance is accessory liability based on assistance and dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not an element.","D":"No, because every assistant automatically becomes a trustee.","E":"Yes, unless the beneficiary first rescinds the trust."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","C":"Correct. Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs."},"primary_legal_authority":"Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan [1995] 2 AC 378; Ivey v Genting Casinos [2017] UKSC 67.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance does not require the defendant to receive trust property; the focus is assistance in a breach and objective dishonesty assessed in light of the defendant's knowledge or beliefs.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b266f97f310bfd8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1038","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Charitable trusts — public benefit","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust is expressed exclusively for a purpose falling within a statutory description of charitable purposes.\n\nIs falling within a listed description by itself sufficient to establish charitable status?","options":{"A":"Yes; public benefit is presumed for every listed purpose.","B":"No. The purpose must also satisfy the public-benefit requirement.","C":"Yes, if the settlor calls the trust charitable.","D":"No charitable purpose can benefit a restricted section of the public.","E":"Only poverty relief can be charitable."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","B":"Correct. Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Charities Act 2011, ss 2–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Charitable status requires both a purpose within the statutory descriptions and satisfaction of the public-benefit requirement; there is no general presumption of public benefit.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc3e2a14163fbc79"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1039","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Charities — political purposes","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust's sole purpose is to secure a change in the law through political campaigning, rather than to further an independently charitable purpose.\n\nWhat is the central difficulty?","options":{"A":"A trust with a purely political purpose aimed at changing the law is not charitable merely because the proposed change is said to benefit the public.","B":"All campaigning is prohibited for charities in every circumstance.","C":"Political purposes are automatically charitable under education.","D":"The trust is charitable if funded by more than one person.","E":"The trust becomes charitable after one year."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose."},"primary_legal_authority":"McGovern v Attorney General [1982] Ch 321; charity-law political-purpose principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A purpose whose object is political change is not itself charitable; this is distinct from permissible campaigning ancillary to an established charitable purpose.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b37070e7bf726f1b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1040","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Variation of trusts — unborn beneficiaries","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Adult beneficiaries all consent to a proposed variation, but the trust also contains contingent interests for unborn beneficiaries who cannot consent.\n\nWhy might court approval still be required?","options":{"A":"Adult consent automatically binds unborn beneficiaries.","B":"The court can, under the statutory variation jurisdiction, approve an arrangement on behalf of specified persons unable to consent where the statutory benefit requirements are satisfied.","C":"Unborn beneficiaries have no possible trust interests.","D":"The trustees alone can extinguish contingent interests at will.","E":"A variation is possible only after every contingent beneficiary is born."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","B":"Correct. The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Variation of Trusts Act 1958, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Variation of Trusts Act permits the court to approve arrangements for categories unable to consent, including unborn persons, subject to the statutory requirements.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9eec129a09431b56"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1041","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Actus reus — omissions and assumed responsibility","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult voluntarily assumes responsibility for caring for a dependent person and then deliberately fails to provide basic assistance despite being able to do so.\n\nWhy can an omission potentially found criminal liability?","options":{"A":"English criminal law never recognises liability for omissions.","B":"A duty to act can arise from voluntarily assuming responsibility for another's care.","C":"Only contractual duties can support omission liability.","D":"An omission automatically proves murder.","E":"Liability exists only if the parties are related by blood."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","B":"Correct. Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Stone and Dobinson [1977] QB 354.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Although there is no general duty to act, criminal liability can arise from an omission where a recognised duty exists, including a duty arising from voluntary assumption of responsibility.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c95b54dc8f873bda"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1042","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Causation — intervening medical treatment","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant inflicts a serious but non-fatal injury. At hospital, the victim receives negligent treatment and later dies.\n\nWhen is the medical treatment most likely to break the chain of causation?","options":{"A":"Whenever any medical mistake occurs.","B":"Only where the treatment is so independent and potent in causing death that the defendant's contribution is no longer an operating and substantial cause under the applicable causation principles.","C":"Whenever a doctor rather than the defendant is present at death.","D":"Never; medical treatment can never affect causation.","E":"Only if the defendant intended the victim to receive treatment."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","B":"Correct. Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Cheshire [1991] 1 WLR 844; R v Jordan (1956) 40 Cr App R 152.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Negligent treatment does not ordinarily break causation merely because it contributes to death; only exceptional treatment may render the original injury no longer an operating and substantial cause.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f3ed2b5058a94bf3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1043","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Mens rea — transferred malice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intentionally throws a stone at A intending to cause the injury required for an offence, misses A and instead causes the same type of injury to B.\n\nWhat doctrine may transfer the defendant's mens rea from A to B?","options":{"A":"Transferred malice.","B":"Strict liability.","C":"Automatism.","D":"Novus actus interveniens.","E":"Marital coercion."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Latimer (1886) 17 QBD 359.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where the intended and actual offences are legally compatible, the defendant's mens rea directed at the intended victim can transfer to the actual victim.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0dc330a04623b92e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1044","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Attempts — more than merely preparatory","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person forms an intention to commit an offence and buys ordinary equipment that could be used for it, but takes no step directly embarking on commission of the offence.\n\nWhat is the key statutory issue for attempt liability?","options":{"A":"Whether the person has merely thought about the offence.","B":"Whether, with intent to commit the offence, the person has done an act that is more than merely preparatory to its commission.","C":"Whether the completed offence actually occurred.","D":"Whether the equipment was expensive.","E":"Whether police were already investigating."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","B":"Correct. Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981, s 1(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Attempt requires the relevant intent plus conduct that has crossed the statutory threshold of being more than merely preparatory.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4d0a1ac696a5e3fc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1045","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Intoxication — voluntary intoxication and basic intent","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant voluntarily becomes intoxicated and is charged with an offence of basic intent. The defendant argues that intoxication prevented formation of the ordinary mens rea.\n\nWhat is the general significance of voluntary intoxication?","options":{"A":"It is a complete defence to every offence.","B":"For basic-intent offences, voluntary intoxication generally cannot be relied on in the same way as for a specific-intent offence to negate liability.","C":"It automatically converts the offence into negligence.","D":"It is relevant only to sentence and never liability.","E":"It creates a defence only where alcohol, rather than drugs, was consumed."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","B":"Correct. The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea."},"primary_legal_authority":"DPP v Majewski [1977] AC 443.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The law distinguishes specific- and basic-intent offences; voluntary intoxication is generally not a defence to basic-intent offending on the basis that intoxication prevented the ordinary mens rea.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"61b83a2e747d7cfd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1046","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Self-defence — mistaken belief","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant honestly but unreasonably believes that an immediate attack is about to occur and uses force based on that mistaken perception. The mistake was not induced by voluntary intoxication.\n\nHow is the necessity for defensive force generally assessed?","options":{"A":"Only by the facts as they objectively existed.","B":"By the circumstances as the defendant honestly believed them to be, although the reasonableness of the belief is evidence relevant to whether it was genuinely held.","C":"Any unreasonable mistake automatically creates strict liability.","D":"Self-defence requires the defendant to wait until actually struck.","E":"A mistake can be considered only if induced by intoxication."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","B":"Correct. For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008, s 76; R v Williams (Gladstone) [1987] 3 All ER 411.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"For self-defence, necessity is generally assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them, subject to the statutory/common-law rules; reasonableness bears on credibility.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"aab74e0651b7cf2a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1047","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Loss of control — qualifying trigger","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant charged with murder relies on loss of control solely because the victim was unfaithful in a relationship.\n\nWhat is the specific statutory problem with relying on sexual infidelity alone as the qualifying trigger?","options":{"A":"Sexual infidelity is expressly excluded from consideration as a qualifying trigger when relied on by itself.","B":"Sexual infidelity automatically establishes fear of serious violence.","C":"Loss of control applies to every homicide offence.","D":"The defence requires complete absence of any emotional reaction.","E":"Only financial disputes can qualify."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist."},"primary_legal_authority":"Coroners and Justice Act 2009, ss 54–55; R v Clinton [2012] EWCA Crim 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory loss-of-control defence excludes sexual infidelity as a qualifying trigger in its own right, although the factual context may require careful treatment where other qualifying triggers exist.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"69e4181c61d2e469"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1048","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Gross negligence manslaughter — grossness","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person owes a duty of care, breaches it and causes death. The prosecution proves ordinary negligence but no more.\n\nWhat additional element is required for gross negligence manslaughter?","options":{"A":"Any civil negligence causing death is automatically manslaughter.","B":"The breach must be so gross, having regard to the risk of death and the circumstances, as to justify criminal liability.","C":"The defendant must intend to kill.","D":"The victim must have consented to the risk.","E":"The defendant must obtain a financial benefit."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","B":"Correct. Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Adomako [1995] 1 AC 171; R v Misra [2004] EWCA Crim 2375.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Gross negligence manslaughter requires more than actionable civil negligence: the breach causing death must be exceptionally serious and criminally gross.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3e0af1a4ccefb9fb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1049","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Burglary — entry as trespasser","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A customer enters a shop during opening hours with permission to use the public sales area, then knowingly goes through a clearly marked staff-only door into a private office intending to steal.\n\nWhich burglary issue is most directly raised?","options":{"A":"A person who initially enters part of a building lawfully can nevertheless enter another part as a trespasser for burglary purposes.","B":"Permission to enter any part of a building permanently prevents burglary.","C":"Burglary requires the building to be a dwelling.","D":"Burglary can be committed only at night.","E":"Theft must be completed before entry."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 9; R v Walkington [1979] 1 WLR 1169.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Burglary can involve entry into a building or part of a building as a trespasser; limited permission to enter one area does not necessarily authorise entry into another.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1263c7de9685da5f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1050","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Robbery — force immediately before or at theft","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant uses force on a victim immediately before taking the victim's property, and uses the force in order to facilitate the taking.\n\nWhat feature distinguishes potential robbery from simple theft?","options":{"A":"The property must be worth more than a statutory minimum.","B":"Force or threat of force is used immediately before or at the time of stealing and in order to steal.","C":"The defendant must use a weapon.","D":"The victim must suffer physical injury.","E":"Robbery requires entry into a building."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","B":"Correct. Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Robbery is aggravated theft: the statutory definition requires force or threat of force immediately before or at the time of stealing and for the purpose of stealing.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"047c3b9d03eb4c55"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1051","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police station — adverse inferences and legal advice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A detained suspect asks for legal advice. Police delay access without lawful justification and continue questioning. The suspect remains silent and the prosecution later seeks an adverse inference from that silence.\n\nWhat is the important procedural protection?","options":{"A":"An adverse inference can always be drawn from silence before legal advice.","B":"Statutory safeguards restrict adverse inferences where the accused was not allowed an opportunity to consult a solicitor before questioning in the relevant circumstances.","C":"Requesting a solicitor is evidence of guilt.","D":"Police may permanently deny legal advice whenever an interview is urgent.","E":"Silence automatically proves the offence."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","B":"Correct. The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994, s 34 and related safeguards; PACE 1984, s 58.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The adverse-inference regime interacts with the right to legal advice; legislation protects a suspect from specified inferences where access to legal advice has not been afforded.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b85bac238265291c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1052","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"PACE — identification procedures","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A suspect disputes being the person shown on CCTV committing an offence. The police know the suspect's identity and the issue is visual identification.\n\nWhich procedural framework should the solicitor consider?","options":{"A":"PACE Code D governing identification procedures.","B":"The Civil Procedure Rules.","C":"The Land Registration Rules.","D":"The Companies Act written-resolution procedure.","E":"The hearsay rules are the only relevant framework."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, Code D.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PACE Code D regulates police identification procedures and safeguards where identification is in issue.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"457940e5f074789b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1053","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bail — failure to surrender","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant released on bail deliberately fails, without reasonable cause, to surrender to custody at the appointed time.\n\nWhat separate legal consequence may arise?","options":{"A":"Failure to surrender can itself constitute an offence under the Bail Act, separate from the original charge.","B":"The original charge is automatically dismissed.","C":"The defendant is automatically acquitted.","D":"No consequence can arise until conviction for the original offence.","E":"The court permanently loses jurisdiction."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bail Act 1976, s 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure to surrender, so breach of the surrender obligation can have consequences independent of the underlying allegation.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e9b0edcb0a4c96b0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1054","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Magistrates' court — allocation of either-way offences","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant pleads not guilty to an either-way offence. The magistrates must decide whether the case is suitable for summary trial before any Crown Court election by the defendant.\n\nWhat process is being undertaken?","options":{"A":"Mode of trial/allocation, applying the statutory allocation framework and relevant guidelines.","B":"A Newton hearing.","C":"A civil directions questionnaire.","D":"A confiscation hearing.","E":"A judicial review permission hearing."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; Crime and Disorder Act 1998; Allocation guideline.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Either-way offences are subject to allocation: magistrates consider whether their sentencing powers and the nature of the case make summary trial suitable before the defendant's election where applicable.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d509052b5e8cf5f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1055","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Crown Court — Newton hearing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant pleads guilty but disputes a factual basis advanced by the prosecution. The difference would materially affect sentence, and the prosecution does not accept the defendant's basis.\n\nWhat procedure may be required?","options":{"A":"A Newton hearing to determine disputed facts material to sentence.","B":"A second jury trial on guilt is mandatory.","C":"Automatic acquittal.","D":"A civil summary judgment application.","E":"A coroner's inquest."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Newton (1982) 77 Cr App R 13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A Newton hearing enables the sentencing court to resolve a material factual dispute following a guilty plea where the basis of plea is not accepted.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6dd556890ef9026a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1056","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Disclosure — unused prosecution material","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During preparation for trial, the prosecution holds unused material that does not form part of its case but may reasonably undermine the prosecution case or assist the defence.\n\nWhat is the prosecution's general disclosure obligation?","options":{"A":"Unused material can never be disclosed.","B":"Material meeting the statutory disclosure test must be disclosed to the accused, subject to the applicable statutory regime and public-interest procedures.","C":"Only material proving guilt is disclosable.","D":"Disclosure begins only after conviction.","E":"The defence must purchase the material from the prosecution."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","B":"Correct. The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The CPIA disclosure regime requires prosecution disclosure of unused material satisfying the statutory test, with separate procedures where public-interest immunity is asserted.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e62d8395dd7390ff"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1057","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Defence statement — Crown Court","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant pleads not guilty in the Crown Court after prosecution disclosure. The defence is considering whether to serve a defence statement.\n\nWhat is the general position?","options":{"A":"A defence statement is part of the statutory defence-disclosure regime in Crown Court proceedings and must address prescribed matters.","B":"A defence statement is prohibited in the Crown Court.","C":"It is merely an informal letter with no statutory significance.","D":"It can be served only after the trial ends.","E":"Serving one automatically amounts to a guilty plea."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996, ss 5–6A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The CPIA imposes a statutory defence-statement regime in Crown Court cases, requiring specified information about the nature of the defence and disputed matters.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6125b7777e5a4dd6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1058","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Special measures — witness eligibility","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A prosecution witness is a child and is likely to have difficulty giving effective evidence in the ordinary way.\n\nWhat should the parties and court consider?","options":{"A":"Whether statutory special measures are available to assist the witness in giving evidence.","B":"Children are never permitted to give evidence.","C":"The witness must give evidence only by written affidavit.","D":"Special measures are available only to defendants.","E":"The court has no power to alter how evidence is received."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Youth Justice and Criminal Evidence Act 1999, ss 16–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory special measures can assist eligible witnesses, including children, through measures designed to improve the quality of their evidence.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4d54c0604c9f8462"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1059","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — guilty plea reduction timing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intends to plead guilty and asks whether the timing of the indication can affect sentence.\n\nWhat is the best general advice?","options":{"A":"Timing is irrelevant to any reduction.","B":"The guilty-plea guideline gives the greatest reduction for a plea indicated at the first stage of proceedings, with reduced credit as the case progresses.","C":"A guilty plea automatically halves every sentence.","D":"Credit is available only after a trial has started.","E":"A guilty plea removes the court's sentencing discretion entirely."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","B":"Correct. The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Council, Reduction in Sentence for a Guilty Plea guideline.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The sentencing guideline links the level of reduction to the stage at which guilt is indicated, rewarding the earliest indication most strongly.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"633211b382b5625c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1060","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Appeals — magistrates to Crown Court","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant convicted and sentenced in the magistrates' court after a not-guilty plea wants the ordinary statutory appeal route challenging conviction and sentence.\n\nWhat is the principal route?","options":{"A":"An appeal to the Crown Court, which ordinarily proceeds by way of rehearing.","B":"Direct appeal as of right to the Supreme Court.","C":"A civil claim in the County Court.","D":"An application to HM Land Registry.","E":"Only judicial review is available."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not state the governing rule or consequence engaged by the facts. A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980, s 108.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defendant has a statutory route of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court against conviction following a not-guilty plea and/or sentence, with the Crown Court rehearing the matter.","common_trap":"Applying a familiar headline rule without identifying the narrower element, exception or procedural consequence tested by the scenario.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6abac2af192636e8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1061","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — notice and restrictions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is investigating a registered title. The proprietorship register contains a restriction requiring a specified consent before registration of a disposition.\n\nWhat is the principal function of the restriction?","options":{"A":"It makes the underlying interest a legal estate.","B":"It regulates whether a disposition may be registered unless the stated condition is satisfied.","C":"It guarantees priority to every equitable interest.","D":"It prevents the registered proprietor from signing a contract.","E":"It converts the title into unregistered land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A restriction controls the circumstances in which an entry may be made in the register following a disposition; it does not itself confer proprietary priority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Option A does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Restrictions regulate registration of dispositions where their conditions are engaged.","B":"Correct. A restriction controls the circumstances in which an entry may be made in the register following a disposition; it does not itself confer proprietary priority.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Restrictions regulate registration of dispositions where their conditions are engaged.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Restrictions regulate registration of dispositions where their conditions are engaged.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Restrictions regulate registration of dispositions where their conditions are engaged."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 40–47.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictions regulate registration of dispositions where their conditions are engaged.","common_trap":"Confusing a restriction with a notice protecting priority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5a03a314e94cef32"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1062","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — alteration and indemnity","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An innocent registered proprietor suffers loss after the register is altered to correct a qualifying mistake. The proprietor asks whether the land registration scheme can compensate that loss.\n\nWhich mechanism is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"An indemnity under the statutory land-registration scheme.","B":"A Part 36 payment.","C":"A statutory legacy.","D":"Automatic specific performance against the Land Registry employee.","E":"A resulting trust over every neighbouring title."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The LRA 2002 contains a statutory indemnity regime for specified losses arising from rectification or mistakes in the register.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The LRA 2002 contains a statutory indemnity regime for specified losses arising from rectification or mistakes in the register.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory scheme can provide indemnity where the conditions in Schedule 8 are met.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory scheme can provide indemnity where the conditions in Schedule 8 are met.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory scheme can provide indemnity where the conditions in Schedule 8 are met.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory scheme can provide indemnity where the conditions in Schedule 8 are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory scheme can provide indemnity where the conditions in Schedule 8 are met.","common_trap":"Assuming alteration of the register has no separate compensation regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"85187fc6e7bc2d49"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1063","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Unregistered land — root of title","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor investigates an unregistered freehold title. The seller supplies an old conveyance dealing with the whole legal and equitable interest and containing a recognisable description of the property, with nothing on its face casting doubt on title.\n\nWhat role can such a document play?","options":{"A":"It can serve as a good root of title if the applicable requirements, including the required age, are met.","B":"It automatically registers the property.","C":"It eliminates the need to investigate subsequent dealings.","D":"It is valid only if executed after exchange of the present sale.","E":"It is a land charge."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A good root is the starting document for deduction of an unregistered title and must satisfy the established characteristics and statutory period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A good root is the starting document for deduction of an unregistered title and must satisfy the established characteristics and statutory period.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A qualifying good root must deal with the whole interest, adequately identify the property and owner, and contain nothing casting doubt on title, while satisfying the applicable age requirement.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A qualifying good root must deal with the whole interest, adequately identify the property and owner, and contain nothing casting doubt on title, while satisfying the applicable age requirement.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A qualifying good root must deal with the whole interest, adequately identify the property and owner, and contain nothing casting doubt on title, while satisfying the applicable age requirement.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A qualifying good root must deal with the whole interest, adequately identify the property and owner, and contain nothing casting doubt on title, while satisfying the applicable age requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1969, s 23; unregistered conveyancing principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying good root must deal with the whole interest, adequately identify the property and owner, and contain nothing casting doubt on title, while satisfying the applicable age requirement.","common_trap":"Treating a root of title as proof that later title events need not be checked.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"526a32cde47f7f19"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1064","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Legal easements — formal creation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two freehold neighbours agree to create a permanent right of way capable of existing as a legal easement. They record the arrangement only in an informal signed note, with no deed.\n\nAssuming no other doctrine applies, what is the principal formality problem?","options":{"A":"A legal easement expressly created for an equivalent freehold duration ordinarily requires a deed.","B":"Every easement must be created orally.","C":"An easement can exist only by statute.","D":"A right of way is incapable of being an easement.","E":"The arrangement must be contained in a will."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An express grant of a legal easement generally requires creation by deed, subject to the land-registration requirements where applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An express grant of a legal easement generally requires creation by deed, subject to the land-registration requirements where applicable.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Legal interests that fall within LPA 1925 s 1(2), including easements, generally require a deed for express legal creation.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Legal interests that fall within LPA 1925 s 1(2), including easements, generally require a deed for express legal creation.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Legal interests that fall within LPA 1925 s 1(2), including easements, generally require a deed for express legal creation.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Legal interests that fall within LPA 1925 s 1(2), including easements, generally require a deed for express legal creation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 1(2), 52; Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Legal interests that fall within LPA 1925 s 1(2), including easements, generally require a deed for express legal creation.","common_trap":"Confusing a contract satisfying s 2 LP(MP)A 1989 with the deed required to create the legal interest.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6a11feced8e1312e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1065","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Proprietary estoppel — assurance, reliance and detriment","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner repeatedly assures a neighbour that the neighbour will receive a strip of land. Relying on that assurance, the neighbour incurs substantial expenditure improving the strip. The owner later seeks to deny any entitlement.\n\nWhich combination lies at the core of a proprietary-estoppel analysis?","options":{"A":"Assurance, reliance and detriment, assessed in determining whether it would be unconscionable to resile.","B":"A deed and registration only.","C":"Twenty years' adverse possession in every case.","D":"A criminal conviction for dishonesty.","E":"Proof of a concluded commercial contract is indispensable."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Proprietary estoppel focuses on an assurance or expectation concerning rights in property, reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable to withdraw.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Proprietary estoppel focuses on an assurance or expectation concerning rights in property, reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable to withdraw.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The court examines assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability; the remedy is then fashioned to address the equity.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The court examines assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability; the remedy is then fashioned to address the equity.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The court examines assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability; the remedy is then fashioned to address the equity.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The court examines assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability; the remedy is then fashioned to address the equity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thorner v Major [2009] UKHL 18; Guest v Guest [2022] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The court examines assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability; the remedy is then fashioned to address the equity.","common_trap":"Assuming proprietary estoppel requires a fully enforceable land contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ab847b9771af6ad2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1066","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — four unities","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two people seek to hold the equitable interest in land as joint tenants.\n\nWhich feature is traditionally required for a joint tenancy?","options":{"A":"The four unities of possession, interest, title and time.","B":"Different interests acquired at unrelated times.","C":"An express agreement never to occupy.","D":"Separate legal titles to physical halves of the land.","E":"A minimum of three co-owners."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A joint tenancy traditionally requires the four unities; absence of the necessary unities may point to a tenancy in common in equity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A joint tenancy traditionally requires the four unities; absence of the necessary unities may point to a tenancy in common in equity.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Joint tenancy requires unity of possession, interest, title and time, subject to the modern statutory context.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Joint tenancy requires unity of possession, interest, title and time, subject to the modern statutory context.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Joint tenancy requires unity of possession, interest, title and time, subject to the modern statutory context.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Joint tenancy requires unity of possession, interest, title and time, subject to the modern statutory context."},"primary_legal_authority":"Co-ownership principles; AG Securities v Vaughan [1990] 1 AC 417.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Joint tenancy requires unity of possession, interest, title and time, subject to the modern statutory context.","common_trap":"Confusing equal beneficial shares with the complete four-unities analysis.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"09aa18f4832869b3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1067","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — survivorship","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A and B beneficially own land as joint tenants. A dies without having severed the beneficial joint tenancy.\n\nWhat happens to A's beneficial interest?","options":{"A":"It passes under A's will.","B":"It passes automatically to B by survivorship.","C":"It escheats to the Crown.","D":"It is divided between A's creditors and B.","E":"It becomes a tenancy at will."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The right of survivorship operates on an unsevered beneficial joint tenancy, so the deceased joint tenant's interest accrues to the survivor rather than passing under the estate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Option A does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Jus accrescendi applies to a beneficial joint tenancy until severance.","B":"Correct. The right of survivorship operates on an unsevered beneficial joint tenancy, so the deceased joint tenant's interest accrues to the survivor rather than passing under the estate.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Jus accrescendi applies to a beneficial joint tenancy until severance.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Jus accrescendi applies to a beneficial joint tenancy until severance.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Jus accrescendi applies to a beneficial joint tenancy until severance."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 36; joint-tenancy principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Jus accrescendi applies to a beneficial joint tenancy until severance.","common_trap":"Applying succession rules to an interest extinguished by survivorship.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa290bb545e9c904"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1068","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Lease or licence — exclusive possession","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier is granted exclusive possession of a self-contained flat for a fixed six-month term at a rent. The written agreement is labelled 'licence'. No exceptional circumstances explain the arrangement.\n\nWhat is the best approach to classification?","options":{"A":"The label 'licence' is conclusive.","B":"The substance prevails; exclusive possession for a term at rent strongly indicates a tenancy, subject to recognised exceptions.","C":"A residential occupier can never be a tenant for less than one year.","D":"Only registration determines whether a tenancy exists.","E":"Payment of rent prevents a lease."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The court looks to substance rather than the label; exclusive possession for a term is central to the lease/licence distinction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. Option A does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Where the legal characteristics of a tenancy exist, parties cannot avoid them merely by attaching a different label.","B":"Correct. The court looks to substance rather than the label; exclusive possession for a term is central to the lease/licence distinction.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Where the legal characteristics of a tenancy exist, parties cannot avoid them merely by attaching a different label.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Where the legal characteristics of a tenancy exist, parties cannot avoid them merely by attaching a different label.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Where the legal characteristics of a tenancy exist, parties cannot avoid them merely by attaching a different label."},"primary_legal_authority":"Street v Mountford [1985] AC 809.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where the legal characteristics of a tenancy exist, parties cannot avoid them merely by attaching a different label.","common_trap":"Treating contractual terminology as conclusive despite the substantive rights granted.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"80478c08e6e44fc9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1069","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Forfeiture — waiver","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial tenant breaches a covenant. The landlord knows of the breach but subsequently demands and accepts rent accruing after the breach in circumstances recognising the lease as continuing.\n\nWhat issue arises for a right to forfeit based on that breach?","options":{"A":"The landlord may have waived the right to forfeit for that particular breach by unequivocally recognising the lease as continuing with knowledge of the breach.","B":"The lease automatically becomes freehold.","C":"Waiver can never occur through acceptance of rent.","D":"The tenant is automatically released from every future covenant.","E":"Forfeiture becomes a criminal remedy."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A landlord can waive a right of forfeiture for a particular breach by an unequivocal act recognising the continued existence of the lease after acquiring knowledge of the breach.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A landlord can waive a right of forfeiture for a particular breach by an unequivocal act recognising the continued existence of the lease after acquiring knowledge of the breach.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Waiver requires knowledge of the breach and an unequivocal act affirming the lease; its consequences depend on the nature of the breach.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Waiver requires knowledge of the breach and an unequivocal act affirming the lease; its consequences depend on the nature of the breach.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Waiver requires knowledge of the breach and an unequivocal act affirming the lease; its consequences depend on the nature of the breach.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Waiver requires knowledge of the breach and an unequivocal act affirming the lease; its consequences depend on the nature of the breach."},"primary_legal_authority":"Central Estates (Belgravia) Ltd v Woolgar [1972] 1 WLR 1048; forfeiture principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Waiver requires knowledge of the breach and an unequivocal act affirming the lease; its consequences depend on the nature of the breach.","common_trap":"Assuming acceptance of any payment has no relevance to waiver.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5b1aa87b64752092"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1070","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Restrictive covenants — burden in equity","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser acquires freehold land subject to a restrictive covenant imposed by a predecessor. The covenant benefits identifiable neighbouring land, and the purchaser has the priority-relevant form of notice/protection.\n\nWhich principle can permit the restrictive burden to bind a successor in equity?","options":{"A":"The rule in Tulk v Moxhay, provided the requirements for the equitable running of the burden are satisfied.","B":"The burden of every positive covenant runs automatically.","C":"Privity of contract alone binds all successors forever.","D":"Only adverse possession can transmit the burden.","E":"Restrictive covenants bind only the original covenantor."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The burden of a genuinely restrictive covenant can run in equity to successors where the established requirements, including benefited land and appropriate protection, are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The burden of a genuinely restrictive covenant can run in equity to successors where the established requirements, including benefited land and appropriate protection, are met.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Equity can enforce restrictive, but not generally positive, freehold covenant burdens against successors under the Tulk v Moxhay doctrine.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Equity can enforce restrictive, but not generally positive, freehold covenant burdens against successors under the Tulk v Moxhay doctrine.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Equity can enforce restrictive, but not generally positive, freehold covenant burdens against successors under the Tulk v Moxhay doctrine.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Equity can enforce restrictive, but not generally positive, freehold covenant burdens against successors under the Tulk v Moxhay doctrine."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tulk v Moxhay (1848) 2 Ph 774; LPA 1925; LRA 2002/Land Charges Act 1972 as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equity can enforce restrictive, but not generally positive, freehold covenant burdens against successors under the Tulk v Moxhay doctrine.","common_trap":"Assuming all freehold covenant burdens run with land in the same way.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8f44cfeaf4612d39"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1071","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Re Rose","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor executes the correct share-transfer form and delivers it with the share certificate to the company, having done everything personally required to transfer the shares. Registration is delayed for administrative reasons.\n\nWhich equitable principle may treat the gift as effective before registration?","options":{"A":"The rule in Re Rose.","B":"The rule against perpetuities automatically.","C":"Cy-près.","D":"Proprietary estoppel requires twenty years.","E":"The gift necessarily fails until registration regardless of the donor's completed acts."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under Re Rose, equity may treat a transfer as complete where the donor has done everything in their power necessary to transfer the property and only third-party steps remain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under Re Rose, equity may treat a transfer as complete where the donor has done everything in their power necessary to transfer the property and only third-party steps remain.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Re Rose exception can prevent the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift from defeating a transfer where the donor has completed all required acts.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Re Rose exception can prevent the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift from defeating a transfer where the donor has completed all required acts.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Re Rose exception can prevent the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift from defeating a transfer where the donor has completed all required acts.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Re Rose exception can prevent the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift from defeating a transfer where the donor has completed all required acts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Rose [1952] Ch 499.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Re Rose exception can prevent the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift from defeating a transfer where the donor has completed all required acts.","common_trap":"Assuming legal registration is invariably the only moment at which equity can recognise the completed gift.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3be14ba2a71df8f5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1072","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Strong v Bird","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A debtor intends immediately to forgive a debt owed by another person but does not validly release it. The intention continues unchanged until the debtor's death, and the debtor appoints that debtor-person as executor.\n\nWhich doctrine may perfect the imperfect release in appropriate circumstances?","options":{"A":"Strong v Bird.","B":"Tulk v Moxhay.","C":"Saunders v Vautier.","D":"Donatio mortis causa automatically.","E":"The rule in Hastings-Bass."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Strong v Bird may perfect an imperfect inter vivos gift or release where there was an immediate continuing intention and legal title later vests in the intended recipient as executor or administrator.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Strong v Bird may perfect an imperfect inter vivos gift or release where there was an immediate continuing intention and legal title later vests in the intended recipient as executor or administrator.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The doctrine requires an immediate intention that continues until the recipient obtains legal title through the donor's personal representation.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The doctrine requires an immediate intention that continues until the recipient obtains legal title through the donor's personal representation.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The doctrine requires an immediate intention that continues until the recipient obtains legal title through the donor's personal representation.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The doctrine requires an immediate intention that continues until the recipient obtains legal title through the donor's personal representation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Strong v Bird (1874) LR 18 Eq 315.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The doctrine requires an immediate intention that continues until the recipient obtains legal title through the donor's personal representation.","common_trap":"Treating any appointment as executor as sufficient without the required continuing intention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d7e8bab5f3aba5f4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1073","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Choithram","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares that property is given to a charitable foundation and is himself one of several trustees of that foundation, but fails formally to transfer title to all trustees before death.\n\nWhat principle may prevent the gift failing solely because legal title was not transferred to every trustee?","options":{"A":"Choithram may apply because it can be unconscionable for a settlor who is already one trustee to deny the trust after declaring the gift to the foundation.","B":"Milroy v Lord always invalidates the arrangement regardless of the settlor being a trustee.","C":"A charitable gift requires no intention.","D":"The property automatically becomes ownerless.","E":"Only Strong v Bird can ever perfect a trust."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Choithram recognises a limited exception where a donor declares a gift to a foundation of which the donor is already a trustee; the donor cannot insist that the trust fails merely for want of transfer to all trustees.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Choithram recognises a limited exception where a donor declares a gift to a foundation of which the donor is already a trustee; the donor cannot insist that the trust fails merely for want of transfer to all trustees.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The case is a specific qualification to the rule against perfecting imperfect gifts, not a general abolition of constitution requirements.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The case is a specific qualification to the rule against perfecting imperfect gifts, not a general abolition of constitution requirements.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The case is a specific qualification to the rule against perfecting imperfect gifts, not a general abolition of constitution requirements.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The case is a specific qualification to the rule against perfecting imperfect gifts, not a general abolition of constitution requirements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Choithram International SA v Pagarani [2001] 1 WLR 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The case is a specific qualification to the rule against perfecting imperfect gifts, not a general abolition of constitution requirements.","common_trap":"Treating Choithram as validating every imperfect gift.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"23abc88529d7e41a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1074","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Presumed resulting trust — purchase money","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A pays the entire purchase price for an investment asset but title is placed in B's name. There is no evidence of a gift and no applicable contrary presumption.\n\nWhat equitable presumption may arise?","options":{"A":"A presumed resulting trust in favour of A.","B":"A constructive trust always giving B half.","C":"A charitable trust.","D":"B necessarily owns beneficially because B has legal title.","E":"A purpose trust for the seller."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where one person provides purchase money and title is taken in another's name, equity may presume a resulting trust for the contributor, subject to evidence rebutting the presumption and modern statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where one person provides purchase money and title is taken in another's name, equity may presume a resulting trust for the contributor, subject to evidence rebutting the presumption and modern statutory rules.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A purchase-money resulting trust is a rebuttable evidential presumption about beneficial ownership.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A purchase-money resulting trust is a rebuttable evidential presumption about beneficial ownership.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A purchase-money resulting trust is a rebuttable evidential presumption about beneficial ownership.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A purchase-money resulting trust is a rebuttable evidential presumption about beneficial ownership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Dyer v Dyer (1788) 2 Cox Eq Cas 92; resulting-trust principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A purchase-money resulting trust is a rebuttable evidential presumption about beneficial ownership.","common_trap":"Equating legal title with conclusive beneficial ownership.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0b9b463f24ac31d5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1075","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — investment advice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees are considering a substantial investment decision under their statutory investment powers. They propose to proceed without considering whether professional advice should be obtained.\n\nWhat does the statutory framework generally require?","options":{"A":"Trustees must obtain and consider proper advice unless they reasonably conclude that obtaining it is unnecessary or inappropriate in the circumstances.","B":"Professional advice is forbidden.","C":"Advice is needed only if every beneficiary requests it.","D":"Obtaining advice transfers the investment decision to the adviser.","E":"Trustees may consider only expected financial return and no other statutory criterion."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Trustee Act generally requires trustees exercising investment powers to obtain and consider proper advice unless the statutory exception applies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Trustee Act generally requires trustees exercising investment powers to obtain and consider proper advice unless the statutory exception applies.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Investment powers are accompanied by duties concerning standard investment criteria, review and proper advice.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Investment powers are accompanied by duties concerning standard investment criteria, review and proper advice.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Investment powers are accompanied by duties concerning standard investment criteria, review and proper advice.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Investment powers are accompanied by duties concerning standard investment criteria, review and proper advice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 4–5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Investment powers are accompanied by duties concerning standard investment criteria, review and proper advice.","common_trap":"Assuming broad investment powers remove the procedural safeguards governing their exercise.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"05ae81af6d38a465"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1076","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — maintenance for minors","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust gives a minor a future interest in property. During minority, income is available and the trustees consider applying some of it for the child's education and maintenance.\n\nWhich statutory power is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"The statutory power of maintenance.","B":"The rule in Tulk v Moxhay.","C":"The criminal doctrine of necessity.","D":"The rule against double recovery.","E":"A land-registration priority search."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Trustees may have statutory power to apply income for the maintenance, education or benefit of a beneficiary during minority, subject to the trust terms and statute.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Trustees may have statutory power to apply income for the maintenance, education or benefit of a beneficiary during minority, subject to the trust terms and statute.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Section 31 provides a maintenance regime for income where its conditions apply.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Section 31 provides a maintenance regime for income where its conditions apply.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Section 31 provides a maintenance regime for income where its conditions apply.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Section 31 provides a maintenance regime for income where its conditions apply."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 31.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 31 provides a maintenance regime for income where its conditions apply.","common_trap":"Confusing maintenance from income with advancement of capital.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"24c4701055b10b8f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1077","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Saunders v Vautier — termination","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole beneficiary is absolutely entitled to the entire trust fund, is aged 18 and has full capacity. There is no other person with any beneficial interest.\n\nWhat may the beneficiary generally require?","options":{"A":"The beneficiary may invoke the Saunders v Vautier principle to require the trustees to transfer the fund and terminate the trust.","B":"The beneficiary must wait until the date selected by the settlor in every case.","C":"Only the court can ever terminate a trust.","D":"The trustees acquire the beneficial ownership.","E":"The fund automatically passes to the Crown."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An adult beneficiary with full capacity who is absolutely entitled to the whole beneficial interest can generally require the trust property to be transferred, notwithstanding a postponed enjoyment provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An adult beneficiary with full capacity who is absolutely entitled to the whole beneficial interest can generally require the trust property to be transferred, notwithstanding a postponed enjoyment provision.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The rule depends on all beneficial interests being represented by persons of full age and capacity who together are absolutely entitled.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The rule depends on all beneficial interests being represented by persons of full age and capacity who together are absolutely entitled.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The rule depends on all beneficial interests being represented by persons of full age and capacity who together are absolutely entitled.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The rule depends on all beneficial interests being represented by persons of full age and capacity who together are absolutely entitled."},"primary_legal_authority":"Saunders v Vautier (1841) 4 Beav 115.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The rule depends on all beneficial interests being represented by persons of full age and capacity who together are absolutely entitled.","common_trap":"Assuming a settlor's postponement always defeats an absolutely entitled adult beneficiary.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe41034c48a1532b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1078","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Tracing — mixed bank account and Re Hallett","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee wrongfully mixes trust money with personal money in one bank account and later makes personal withdrawals, leaving enough money to satisfy the trust claim.\n\nUnder the traditional Re Hallett presumption, how are the withdrawals treated?","options":{"A":"The trustee is presumed to spend personal money first, preserving trust money in the remaining balance so far as possible.","B":"Trust money is always presumed spent first.","C":"Mixing automatically destroys all proprietary claims.","D":"The beneficiary can claim only half the balance.","E":"Every withdrawal creates a criminal trust."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Re Hallett presumes a wrongdoing trustee spends their own money first, helping preserve the beneficiary's proprietary claim to the remaining fund, subject to later tracing refinements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Re Hallett presumes a wrongdoing trustee spends their own money first, helping preserve the beneficiary's proprietary claim to the remaining fund, subject to later tracing refinements.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The presumption prevents a fiduciary from choosing an appropriation that benefits the wrongdoer at the beneficiary's expense.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The presumption prevents a fiduciary from choosing an appropriation that benefits the wrongdoer at the beneficiary's expense.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The presumption prevents a fiduciary from choosing an appropriation that benefits the wrongdoer at the beneficiary's expense.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The presumption prevents a fiduciary from choosing an appropriation that benefits the wrongdoer at the beneficiary's expense."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Hallett's Estate (1880) 13 Ch D 696.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The presumption prevents a fiduciary from choosing an appropriation that benefits the wrongdoer at the beneficiary's expense.","common_trap":"Assuming mixing itself makes tracing impossible.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8cc680fb302920ae"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1079","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Tracing — lowest intermediate balance","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee pays £20,000 of trust money into an account containing personal funds. The balance later falls to £5,000 before the trustee deposits fresh personal money, raising it to £30,000.\n\nSubject to other claims, what limit does the lowest-intermediate-balance principle place on tracing the original trust money into that account?","options":{"A":"The beneficiary can ordinarily trace no more than the lowest intermediate balance of £5,000; later personal deposits are not automatically treated as restoring dissipated trust money.","B":"The beneficiary automatically owns the full £30,000.","C":"The claim is fixed at £20,000 even if the account reached zero.","D":"Tracing is impossible whenever money is mixed.","E":"The later deposit becomes trust money because it was made by a trustee."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Once trust money has been dissipated from a mixed account, later payments of the trustee's own money do not ordinarily replenish the proprietary trace; recovery is limited by the lowest intermediate balance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Once trust money has been dissipated from a mixed account, later payments of the trustee's own money do not ordinarily replenish the proprietary trace; recovery is limited by the lowest intermediate balance.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Tracing identifies surviving substitutes of the claimant's property and does not ordinarily treat later independent funds as restoration.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Tracing identifies surviving substitutes of the claimant's property and does not ordinarily treat later independent funds as restoration.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Tracing identifies surviving substitutes of the claimant's property and does not ordinarily treat later independent funds as restoration.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Tracing identifies surviving substitutes of the claimant's property and does not ordinarily treat later independent funds as restoration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Roscoe v Winder [1915] 1 Ch 62.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Tracing identifies surviving substitutes of the claimant's property and does not ordinarily treat later independent funds as restoration.","common_trap":"Treating later deposits as automatically recreating property that has already been dissipated.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"faf82a6bac335e0c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1080","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Fiduciaries — conflict authorisation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A fiduciary faces a transaction creating a real conflict between personal interest and duty. Before proceeding, the fiduciary makes full disclosure to the principal, who has capacity and gives properly informed consent.\n\nWhat is the significance of that consent?","options":{"A":"Proper fully informed authorisation can permit conduct that would otherwise breach the no-conflict rule.","B":"Conflicts can never be authorised.","C":"Disclosure itself automatically transfers property to the fiduciary.","D":"Consent is irrelevant unless given after litigation begins.","E":"Authorisation eliminates every unrelated fiduciary duty."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The strict no-conflict rule can be displaced by valid authorisation from the person or body entitled to give it after sufficiently full disclosure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The strict no-conflict rule can be displaced by valid authorisation from the person or body entitled to give it after sufficiently full disclosure.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Fiduciary loyalty rules prohibit unauthorised conflicts; informed consent can provide authorisation within its proper scope.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Fiduciary loyalty rules prohibit unauthorised conflicts; informed consent can provide authorisation within its proper scope.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Fiduciary loyalty rules prohibit unauthorised conflicts; informed consent can provide authorisation within its proper scope.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Fiduciary loyalty rules prohibit unauthorised conflicts; informed consent can provide authorisation within its proper scope."},"primary_legal_authority":"Boardman v Phipps [1967] 2 AC 46; fiduciary principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Fiduciary loyalty rules prohibit unauthorised conflicts; informed consent can provide authorisation within its proper scope.","common_trap":"Assuming the no-conflict rule is incapable of informed authorisation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9c6943ec297a71eb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1081","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Mens rea — intention: oblique intention","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant acts to achieve one objective while foreseeing a prohibited consequence as virtually certain, although that consequence is not the defendant's purpose.\n\nWhen may a jury be entitled to find intention as to that consequence?","options":{"A":"Where the consequence was virtually certain from the defendant's act and the defendant appreciated that virtual certainty.","B":"Whenever the consequence was merely possible.","C":"Only if the defendant later confesses.","D":"Only where recklessness is legally impossible.","E":"Whenever a reasonable person would have foreseen any risk."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Virtual certainty of the consequence, appreciated by the defendant, provides the evidential basis on which a jury may find oblique intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Virtual certainty of the consequence, appreciated by the defendant, provides the evidential basis on which a jury may find oblique intention.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Oblique intention is not equated with mere foresight of risk; Woollin uses the virtual-certainty direction.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Oblique intention is not equated with mere foresight of risk; Woollin uses the virtual-certainty direction.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Oblique intention is not equated with mere foresight of risk; Woollin uses the virtual-certainty direction.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Oblique intention is not equated with mere foresight of risk; Woollin uses the virtual-certainty direction."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Woollin [1999] 1 AC 82.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Oblique intention is not equated with mere foresight of risk; Woollin uses the virtual-certainty direction.","common_trap":"Confusing recklessness or objective foreseeability with intention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"31d5f5003480a4dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1082","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Recklessness — subjective risk","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant consciously recognises a risk that a prohibited result may occur but unreasonably goes ahead.\n\nWhat is the core modern test for recklessness?","options":{"A":"The defendant must be aware of a risk and, in the circumstances known to the defendant, it must be unreasonable to take it.","B":"Any risk that a reasonable person would have noticed is sufficient even if the defendant did not.","C":"Recklessness requires desire for the result.","D":"Recklessness is identical to negligence.","E":"The defendant must believe the result is virtually certain."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Modern recklessness is subjective as to awareness of risk, followed by assessment of the unreasonableness of taking that risk in the circumstances known to the defendant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Modern recklessness is subjective as to awareness of risk, followed by assessment of the unreasonableness of taking that risk in the circumstances known to the defendant.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defendant must actually foresee the relevant risk; inadvertence alone does not satisfy Cunningham/R v G recklessness.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defendant must actually foresee the relevant risk; inadvertence alone does not satisfy Cunningham/R v G recklessness.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defendant must actually foresee the relevant risk; inadvertence alone does not satisfy Cunningham/R v G recklessness.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defendant must actually foresee the relevant risk; inadvertence alone does not satisfy Cunningham/R v G recklessness."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v G [2003] UKHL 50.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defendant must actually foresee the relevant risk; inadvertence alone does not satisfy Cunningham/R v G recklessness.","common_trap":"Using a purely objective reasonable-person test for awareness of risk.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9090d0bb4aa3e5d0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1083","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Strict liability — statutory interpretation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A regulatory offence is silent about mens rea for one element. The prosecution argues that the element is one of strict liability.\n\nWhat is the starting point when construing the offence?","options":{"A":"There is a presumption that mens rea is required, capable of being displaced by the statutory language, purpose and context.","B":"Every statutory offence is automatically strict liability.","C":"Silence always means negligence.","D":"Mens rea is required only for common-law offences.","E":"Strict liability requires proof of intention."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The courts ordinarily begin with a presumption of mens rea, but Parliament can expressly or impliedly displace it in an appropriate statutory context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The courts ordinarily begin with a presumption of mens rea, but Parliament can expressly or impliedly displace it in an appropriate statutory context.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Strict liability is a question of statutory construction; silence alone does not automatically resolve the issue.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Strict liability is a question of statutory construction; silence alone does not automatically resolve the issue.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Strict liability is a question of statutory construction; silence alone does not automatically resolve the issue.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Strict liability is a question of statutory construction; silence alone does not automatically resolve the issue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sweet v Parsley [1970] AC 132.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Strict liability is a question of statutory construction; silence alone does not automatically resolve the issue.","common_trap":"Assuming all regulatory offences dispense with mens rea.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a8a1a779374975f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1084","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Coincidence — continuing act","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant inadvertently creates a dangerous continuing situation. After becoming aware of it, the defendant deliberately allows the situation to continue with the mens rea required for the offence.\n\nWhich principle may allow actus reus and mens rea to coincide?","options":{"A":"The continuing-act principle.","B":"Transferred malice only.","C":"The rule against perpetuities.","D":"Issue estoppel.","E":"The hearsay rule."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where the actus reus can properly be characterised as continuing, mens rea formed while it continues may coincide with the act for criminal liability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where the actus reus can properly be characterised as continuing, mens rea formed while it continues may coincide with the act for criminal liability.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A continuing act can satisfy contemporaneity where the required mental state arises before the actus reus has ended.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A continuing act can satisfy contemporaneity where the required mental state arises before the actus reus has ended.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A continuing act can satisfy contemporaneity where the required mental state arises before the actus reus has ended.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A continuing act can satisfy contemporaneity where the required mental state arises before the actus reus has ended."},"primary_legal_authority":"Fagan v Metropolitan Police Commissioner [1969] 1 QB 439.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A continuing act can satisfy contemporaneity where the required mental state arises before the actus reus has ended.","common_trap":"Assuming mens rea must always exist at the first instant of a continuing act.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"99aad0277c4fe876"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1085","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Coincidence — single transaction","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant forms the required mens rea and begins a sequence of acts designed to commit an offence. The prohibited consequence occurs at a later stage of the same planned sequence, after the defendant mistakenly thinks the objective has already been achieved.\n\nWhat doctrine may satisfy the coincidence requirement?","options":{"A":"The single-transaction principle can treat the connected sequence as one transaction for coincidence purposes.","B":"Mens rea is automatically extinguished after the first physical act.","C":"The offence can never be established.","D":"Only transferred malice applies.","E":"The consequence must occur in the same second as the initial act."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A sufficiently connected series of acts implementing a plan can be treated as one transaction, allowing the earlier mens rea and later actus reus to coincide.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A sufficiently connected series of acts implementing a plan can be treated as one transaction, allowing the earlier mens rea and later actus reus to coincide.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Contemporaneity is applied realistically to a connected criminal transaction rather than requiring artificial moment-by-moment coincidence.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Contemporaneity is applied realistically to a connected criminal transaction rather than requiring artificial moment-by-moment coincidence.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Contemporaneity is applied realistically to a connected criminal transaction rather than requiring artificial moment-by-moment coincidence.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Contemporaneity is applied realistically to a connected criminal transaction rather than requiring artificial moment-by-moment coincidence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thabo Meli v R [1954] 1 WLR 228.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contemporaneity is applied realistically to a connected criminal transaction rather than requiring artificial moment-by-moment coincidence.","common_trap":"Applying an unrealistically instantaneous coincidence requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5191e0726e6f66f9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1086","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Duress — threats and escape","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant claims to have committed an offence because of a serious threat, but had a safe and reasonable opportunity to escape or seek protection before committing it.\n\nHow does that opportunity affect duress?","options":{"A":"A safe avenue of escape can defeat the defence because duress requires the threat to leave no reasonable evasive course.","B":"It is irrelevant in every case.","C":"It automatically proves insanity.","D":"Duress applies whenever any threat is made, however remote.","E":"The defendant must commit the offence before seeking help."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Duress is unavailable where the defendant could reasonably have avoided the threat through a safe avenue of escape or protective action.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Duress is unavailable where the defendant could reasonably have avoided the threat through a safe avenue of escape or protective action.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defence is one of constrained choice and requires the threat to operate with sufficient immediacy and no reasonable escape.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defence is one of constrained choice and requires the threat to operate with sufficient immediacy and no reasonable escape.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defence is one of constrained choice and requires the threat to operate with sufficient immediacy and no reasonable escape.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The defence is one of constrained choice and requires the threat to operate with sufficient immediacy and no reasonable escape."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Gill [1963] 1 WLR 841; R v Hasan [2005] UKHL 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The defence is one of constrained choice and requires the threat to operate with sufficient immediacy and no reasonable escape.","common_trap":"Looking only at the seriousness of the threat while ignoring available escape.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c418b6f92eea58aa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1087","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Duress — voluntary association","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant voluntarily joins a group known to engage in serious criminal activity and knows that members use coercive threats to compel participation in offences. The defendant later claims duress after such a threat.\n\nWhat limitation is relevant?","options":{"A":"Foreseeable coercion arising from voluntary association with criminals can preclude reliance on duress.","B":"Voluntary association is never relevant.","C":"Membership automatically proves the substantive offence.","D":"Duress is available only to police officers.","E":"Any threat guarantees the defence."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Duress can be unavailable where the defendant voluntarily associates with criminals in circumstances in which coercion to offend was reasonably foreseeable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Duress can be unavailable where the defendant voluntarily associates with criminals in circumstances in which coercion to offend was reasonably foreseeable.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A defendant cannot necessarily rely on duress after voluntarily exposing themselves to a foreseeable risk of coercion from criminal associates.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A defendant cannot necessarily rely on duress after voluntarily exposing themselves to a foreseeable risk of coercion from criminal associates.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A defendant cannot necessarily rely on duress after voluntarily exposing themselves to a foreseeable risk of coercion from criminal associates.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A defendant cannot necessarily rely on duress after voluntarily exposing themselves to a foreseeable risk of coercion from criminal associates."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Hasan [2005] UKHL 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defendant cannot necessarily rely on duress after voluntarily exposing themselves to a foreseeable risk of coercion from criminal associates.","common_trap":"Assuming a later serious threat erases the relevance of prior voluntary association.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"699d87ee4d4cd456"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1088","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Secondary liability — intention to assist or encourage","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person knowingly provides practical assistance to another, intending that the assistance help the other commit an offence.\n\nWhat mental element is central to ordinary secondary liability?","options":{"A":"An intention to assist or encourage the commission of the offence, together with the legally required knowledge or belief concerning the essential facts.","B":"Mere association with the principal is always enough.","C":"The accessory must personally perform the complete actus reus.","D":"Negligence about any possible offence automatically suffices.","E":"The accessory must receive a financial benefit."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Secondary liability requires intentional assistance or encouragement, not mere foresight or association, with the requisite state of mind as to the essential circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Secondary liability requires intentional assistance or encouragement, not mere foresight or association, with the requisite state of mind as to the essential circumstances.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. After Jogee, foresight can be evidence of intent but is not itself the legal test for intention to assist or encourage.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. After Jogee, foresight can be evidence of intent but is not itself the legal test for intention to assist or encourage.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. After Jogee, foresight can be evidence of intent but is not itself the legal test for intention to assist or encourage.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. After Jogee, foresight can be evidence of intent but is not itself the legal test for intention to assist or encourage."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Jogee [2016] UKSC 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"After Jogee, foresight can be evidence of intent but is not itself the legal test for intention to assist or encourage.","common_trap":"Treating foresight or presence alone as conclusive secondary liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8befa6d53a0afa0f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1089","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Conspiracy — agreement","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two people genuinely agree to pursue a course of conduct that, if carried out as intended, will necessarily involve commission of a criminal offence.\n\nWhat is the core actus reus of statutory conspiracy?","options":{"A":"The agreement to pursue the criminal course of conduct.","B":"Completion of the substantive offence.","C":"Physical presence at the eventual offence.","D":"Receipt of proceeds.","E":"A written contract between the conspirators."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Conspiracy is an inchoate offence centred on the prohibited agreement; the contemplated substantive offence need not be completed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Conspiracy is an inchoate offence centred on the prohibited agreement; the contemplated substantive offence need not be completed.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory offence is complete upon the relevant agreement accompanied by the required mental state.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory offence is complete upon the relevant agreement accompanied by the required mental state.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory offence is complete upon the relevant agreement accompanied by the required mental state.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The statutory offence is complete upon the relevant agreement accompanied by the required mental state."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Law Act 1977, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory offence is complete upon the relevant agreement accompanied by the required mental state.","common_trap":"Assuming the planned offence must actually be carried out before conspiracy exists.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"15abefc39ab18aa5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1090","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Criminal damage — lawful excuse","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person damages another's property honestly believing that the owner would have consented to the damage if the owner had known the circumstances.\n\nWhich defence may be relevant?","options":{"A":"The statutory lawful-excuse provision concerning an honest belief in the owner's consent.","B":"Duress is the only possible defence.","C":"Mistake is irrelevant to criminal damage.","D":"Consent must actually have been given in writing.","E":"Lawful excuse applies only to property owned by the defendant."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For criminal damage, the statutory lawful-excuse provisions include an honestly held belief that the person entitled to consent had consented or would have consented in the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For criminal damage, the statutory lawful-excuse provisions include an honestly held belief that the person entitled to consent had consented or would have consented in the circumstances.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The relevant statutory belief is judged for honesty, subject to the precise terms of the Criminal Damage Act.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The relevant statutory belief is judged for honesty, subject to the precise terms of the Criminal Damage Act.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The relevant statutory belief is judged for honesty, subject to the precise terms of the Criminal Damage Act.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The relevant statutory belief is judged for honesty, subject to the precise terms of the Criminal Damage Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Damage Act 1971, s 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The relevant statutory belief is judged for honesty, subject to the precise terms of the Criminal Damage Act.","common_trap":"Requiring actual consent rather than considering the statutory honest-belief defence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ceb3966f963651f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1091","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"PACE — detention review","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A suspect remains detained at a police station without charge while enquiries continue.\n\nWhat safeguard governs continued detention at intervals?","options":{"A":"Detention must be subject to periodic statutory review by the appropriate review officer in accordance with PACE.","B":"Once authorised, detention cannot be reviewed before charge.","C":"Only the suspect can review detention.","D":"A civil judge must attend the station every hour.","E":"Review is required only after conviction."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"PACE imposes periodic reviews of detention to ensure that continued detention remains justified.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. PACE imposes periodic reviews of detention to ensure that continued detention remains justified.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Pre-charge detention is not an open-ended initial authorisation; statutory review safeguards apply.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Pre-charge detention is not an open-ended initial authorisation; statutory review safeguards apply.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Pre-charge detention is not an open-ended initial authorisation; statutory review safeguards apply.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Pre-charge detention is not an open-ended initial authorisation; statutory review safeguards apply."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, ss 40–42.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Pre-charge detention is not an open-ended initial authorisation; statutory review safeguards apply.","common_trap":"Treating the custody officer's initial decision as sufficient for unlimited detention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"abec70b6c3b1b015"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1092","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"PACE — detention time limits","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A suspect is arrested for an indictable offence and police wish to continue detention without charge beyond the ordinary initial period.\n\nWhat is the general legal structure?","options":{"A":"PACE imposes staged time limits, with progressively higher authorisation and, for further extensions, judicial involvement where statutory conditions are met.","B":"Police may detain without charge indefinitely.","C":"Every extension can be authorised by any constable.","D":"Time limits apply only after charge.","E":"A solicitor can unilaterally extend detention."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"PACE regulates pre-charge detention through escalating statutory time limits and authorisation requirements rather than permitting indefinite detention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. PACE regulates pre-charge detention through escalating statutory time limits and authorisation requirements rather than permitting indefinite detention.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Continued detention beyond ordinary limits requires compliance with the applicable PACE extension regime.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Continued detention beyond ordinary limits requires compliance with the applicable PACE extension regime.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Continued detention beyond ordinary limits requires compliance with the applicable PACE extension regime.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Continued detention beyond ordinary limits requires compliance with the applicable PACE extension regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, ss 41–44.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Continued detention beyond ordinary limits requires compliance with the applicable PACE extension regime.","common_trap":"Assuming investigative convenience alone permits indefinite detention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e218e42fd23f3e0a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1093","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police interview — appropriate adult","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A detained suspect is a juvenile and police propose to conduct a formal interview.\n\nWhat additional safeguard is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"An appropriate adult should attend in accordance with the PACE Codes, subject to the limited exceptions provided by the regime.","B":"Juveniles may never be interviewed.","C":"A jury must attend the interview.","D":"Only a parent can ever act as appropriate adult.","E":"Legal advice is prohibited when an appropriate adult attends."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The PACE Codes provide additional safeguards for juveniles and vulnerable suspects, including the role of an appropriate adult during interview and specified procedures.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The PACE Codes provide additional safeguards for juveniles and vulnerable suspects, including the role of an appropriate adult during interview and specified procedures.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The appropriate adult safeguard is distinct from, and does not replace, the right to legal advice.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The appropriate adult safeguard is distinct from, and does not replace, the right to legal advice.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The appropriate adult safeguard is distinct from, and does not replace, the right to legal advice.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The appropriate adult safeguard is distinct from, and does not replace, the right to legal advice."},"primary_legal_authority":"PACE Code C.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The appropriate adult safeguard is distinct from, and does not replace, the right to legal advice.","common_trap":"Treating the presence of an appropriate adult as optional or as a substitute for a solicitor.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fac08de98ac7e5d7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1094","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Charging — Full Code Test","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Investigators refer an ordinary prosecution decision to the prosecutor after the evidence has been gathered sufficiently for the usual charging test.\n\nWhat are the two principal stages of the Full Code Test?","options":{"A":"Evidential sufficiency followed by the public-interest stage.","B":"Proof beyond reasonable doubt followed by sentence.","C":"Civil liability followed by costs.","D":"Arrest followed automatically by conviction.","E":"Victim consent followed by jury approval."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Full Code Test asks first whether there is sufficient evidence to provide a realistic prospect of conviction and, if so, whether prosecution is required in the public interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Full Code Test asks first whether there is sufficient evidence to provide a realistic prospect of conviction and, if so, whether prosecution is required in the public interest.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The prosecutorial charging test is not the trial standard and includes both evidential and public-interest stages.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The prosecutorial charging test is not the trial standard and includes both evidential and public-interest stages.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The prosecutorial charging test is not the trial standard and includes both evidential and public-interest stages.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The prosecutorial charging test is not the trial standard and includes both evidential and public-interest stages."},"primary_legal_authority":"Code for Crown Prosecutors, Full Code Test.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The prosecutorial charging test is not the trial standard and includes both evidential and public-interest stages.","common_trap":"Confusing the realistic-prospect charging threshold with proof beyond reasonable doubt at trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"32163c50980c20a7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1095","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Plea before venue","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant appears before magistrates charged with an either-way offence.\n\nWhat procedural step ordinarily precedes allocation where the defendant is asked about plea?","options":{"A":"Plea before venue.","B":"A Crown Court sentencing hearing.","C":"A Newton hearing in every case.","D":"A civil allocation questionnaire.","E":"An appeal to the Court of Appeal."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For an either-way offence, the magistrates' court ordinarily conducts plea before venue; a guilty indication leads toward sentence, while a not-guilty indication leads to allocation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For an either-way offence, the magistrates' court ordinarily conducts plea before venue; a guilty indication leads toward sentence, while a not-guilty indication leads to allocation.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Plea before venue determines the procedural route before mode-of-trial allocation for a contested either-way case.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Plea before venue determines the procedural route before mode-of-trial allocation for a contested either-way case.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Plea before venue determines the procedural route before mode-of-trial allocation for a contested either-way case.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Plea before venue determines the procedural route before mode-of-trial allocation for a contested either-way case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980, ss 17A–17C.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Plea before venue determines the procedural route before mode-of-trial allocation for a contested either-way case.","common_trap":"Skipping directly to Crown Court election without the plea-before-venue stage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8f763208b235a120"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1096","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Case management — overriding objective","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At a criminal case-management hearing, the parties disagree about how procedural decisions should be approached.\n\nWhat overarching framework guides the court?","options":{"A":"The Criminal Procedure Rules' overriding objective of dealing with criminal cases justly, supported by active case management.","B":"The Civil Procedure Rules' Part 36 regime.","C":"The Companies Act model articles.","D":"Only the parties' private agreement.","E":"The court has no case-management responsibility."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Criminal Procedure Rules require participants and the court to further the overriding objective and support active, efficient case management.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Criminal Procedure Rules require participants and the court to further the overriding objective and support active, efficient case management.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Criminal procedure is governed by its own overriding objective and case-management duties.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Criminal procedure is governed by its own overriding objective and case-management duties.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Criminal procedure is governed by its own overriding objective and case-management duties.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Criminal procedure is governed by its own overriding objective and case-management duties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Procedure Rules, Parts 1 and 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Criminal procedure is governed by its own overriding objective and case-management duties.","common_trap":"Importing civil-procedure mechanisms as the governing criminal case-management framework.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"931ed492e6bad533"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1097","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Hearsay — business documents","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution seeks to rely on a business record containing an out-of-court statement as evidence of the truth of its contents.\n\nWhat should be considered first?","options":{"A":"Whether a statutory hearsay gateway, including the business-document provisions where applicable, permits admission and whether any exclusionary discretion applies.","B":"All business records are automatically inadmissible.","C":"Hearsay is unrestricted in criminal trials.","D":"Only the maker's consent matters.","E":"The document is admissible only if notarised."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Criminal hearsay is admissible only through recognised statutory or preserved gateways; business documents have a specific statutory framework where its conditions are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Criminal hearsay is admissible only through recognised statutory or preserved gateways; business documents have a specific statutory framework where its conditions are met.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 provides structured gateways rather than a blanket rule for or against business records.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 provides structured gateways rather than a blanket rule for or against business records.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 provides structured gateways rather than a blanket rule for or against business records.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 provides structured gateways rather than a blanket rule for or against business records."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, ss 114 and 117.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Criminal Justice Act 2003 provides structured gateways rather than a blanket rule for or against business records.","common_trap":"Assuming the commercial character of a document alone determines admissibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"54b654c7cb327733"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1098","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bad character — defendant's own bad character","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution wants to adduce evidence of a defendant's previous misconduct at trial.\n\nWhat is the correct starting point?","options":{"A":"The evidence must fall within one or more statutory gateways for a defendant's bad character and remain subject to applicable safeguards and exclusion provisions.","B":"Every previous conviction is automatically admissible for every purpose.","C":"Bad character can never be admitted.","D":"Only the defendant may introduce such evidence.","E":"The civil hearsay rules decide admissibility."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Evidence of a defendant's bad character is governed by the statutory gateways in the Criminal Justice Act 2003 and associated safeguards.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Evidence of a defendant's bad character is governed by the statutory gateways in the Criminal Justice Act 2003 and associated safeguards.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Previous misconduct is not simply free-standing propensity evidence; the prosecution must identify a lawful gateway and address exclusion where applicable.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Previous misconduct is not simply free-standing propensity evidence; the prosecution must identify a lawful gateway and address exclusion where applicable.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Previous misconduct is not simply free-standing propensity evidence; the prosecution must identify a lawful gateway and address exclusion where applicable.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Previous misconduct is not simply free-standing propensity evidence; the prosecution must identify a lawful gateway and address exclusion where applicable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, ss 98, 101–103.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Previous misconduct is not simply free-standing propensity evidence; the prosecution must identify a lawful gateway and address exclusion where applicable.","common_trap":"Treating any previous misconduct as automatically admissible.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f07608f18bd68a37"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1099","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Submission of no case to answer","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At the close of the prosecution case, the defence argues that the evidence is legally insufficient for the case to continue.\n\nWhat test is central to a submission of no case to answer?","options":{"A":"The Galbraith principles, including whether there is no evidence on an essential element or the prosecution evidence is so weak that a properly directed tribunal could not properly convict.","B":"Whether the defence has proved innocence beyond reasonable doubt.","C":"Whether the defendant has previous convictions.","D":"Whether the complainant wants the case dismissed.","E":"Whether sentence would be custodial."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Galbraith test governs whether the prosecution evidence is sufficiently capable of supporting a conviction to require the defence to answer the case.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Galbraith test governs whether the prosecution evidence is sufficiently capable of supporting a conviction to require the defence to answer the case.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A no-case submission concerns evidential sufficiency at the close of the prosecution case, not proof of innocence by the defence.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A no-case submission concerns evidential sufficiency at the close of the prosecution case, not proof of innocence by the defence.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A no-case submission concerns evidential sufficiency at the close of the prosecution case, not proof of innocence by the defence.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. A no-case submission concerns evidential sufficiency at the close of the prosecution case, not proof of innocence by the defence."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Galbraith [1981] 1 WLR 1039.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A no-case submission concerns evidential sufficiency at the close of the prosecution case, not proof of innocence by the defence.","common_trap":"Applying the ultimate trial burden to the defence at the no-case stage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"917052900dbcdb68"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-1100","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Crown Court appeal — conviction","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is convicted in the Crown Court after trial and wishes to appeal the conviction to the Court of Appeal.\n\nWhat is the central statutory ground on which the Court of Appeal allows an appeal against conviction?","options":{"A":"That the conviction is unsafe.","B":"That the defendant disagrees with the jury.","C":"That any procedural imperfection automatically voids the conviction.","D":"That the sentence is longer than expected.","E":"That the prosecution refuses to consent to the appeal."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Court of Appeal must allow an appeal against conviction if it thinks the conviction is unsafe; permission and procedural requirements also apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Court of Appeal must allow an appeal against conviction if it thinks the conviction is unsafe; permission and procedural requirements also apply.","B":"Incorrect. Option B does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Unsafe conviction is the statutory appellate criterion; not every error necessarily renders a conviction unsafe.","C":"Incorrect. Option C does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Unsafe conviction is the statutory appellate criterion; not every error necessarily renders a conviction unsafe.","D":"Incorrect. Option D does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Unsafe conviction is the statutory appellate criterion; not every error necessarily renders a conviction unsafe.","E":"Incorrect. Option E does not satisfy the specific rule tested here. Unsafe conviction is the statutory appellate criterion; not every error necessarily renders a conviction unsafe."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Appeal Act 1968, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unsafe conviction is the statutory appellate criterion; not every error necessarily renders a conviction unsafe.","common_trap":"Assuming any disagreement or procedural defect automatically satisfies the appellate test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9e386da12ff391b9"}]);
